/* Machine translations of Logik Philippi (uncorrected OCR), in the manner of Guyer and Wood: see translation-briefs.js. Not the translators' own work. */
window.KANT_TR = window.KANT_TR || {};
KANT_TR['philippi/guyer'] = {"philippi-s01-001":["Everything in the world conforms to certain rules and laws. Even those things that seem to alter entirely without rule are bound to certain laws. That it often occurs to us, however, as if certain things happened without any rules, stems either from this, that we cannot see into with our understanding the rules according to which they happen, or because their laws are not the laws of the greatest perfection, according to which they, in our opinion, ought to happen. Thus, e.g., the alterations of the weather seem to happen without rules and laws. Nonetheless the weather conditions alternate constantly according to determinate laws; which we, however, on account of our limited reason, do not see into. A vicious man seems to act without rules; meanwhile he arranges all his actions precisely according to the rules that vice prescribes to him. There is accordingly no doubt that all things in the world act according to certain rules. But as all things are regular, so we also find it in the human understanding. Everyone uses the same according to certain rules, everyone thinks, judges etc. etc. according to certain rules. But it is here well to be noted:",["OCR slips silently corrected: wechsein → wechseln; Lasterhaffter → Lasterhafter; the stray space before the semicolon after 'ab'.","Meinung = opinion; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Vernunft = reason; Verstand = understanding; bestimmt = determinate; Zweifel = doubt.","'einsehen' is rendered 'see into' throughout.","The passage ends in a colon; the sentence it introduces is in the next passage."]],"philippi-s01-002":["In some subjects the exercise precedes the rules, in others, by contrast, the rules precede the exercise. That is: some subjects act according to certain laws without being conscious of them; others do regular actions, but are conscious thereby of the rules according to which they do something.",["bewust = bewußt, 'conscious' (Bewußtsein = consciousness).","Ausübung = exercise (not in the glossary)."]],"philippi-s01-003":["It is just so with the understanding. Some use it, but are not conscious of the rules according to which they use it. Others make use of it, but also know at the same time the laws and rules according to which they make use of it. From this one sees that the use of the understanding is twofold:",["Gebrauch = use; 'brauchen' and 'sich bedienen' are both rendered 'use' / 'make use of' respectively."]],"philippi-s01-004":["1. The natural use, when one is not conscious of the rules of the understanding. Thus, e.g., a child learns to walk. It observes in this countless many rules, so that Borellus, who wrote *de motu animalium et infantium*, applies all mathematical art in order to show all the rules that a child exercises when it begins to walk. For all that, however, the child is not conscious of the rules that it exercises. Just so little children speak and observe therein uncommonly many rules, but they do not know at all that they observe them.",["OCR slips corrected: Bev → Bey; bewüst → bewußt.","The Latin title is given as printed in the transcript.","beobachten = observe (Beobachtung = observation); 'wissen' = know."]],"philippi-s01-005":["Thus the understanding too is subject to certain rules, according to which it acts, even then when it is not conscious of them, even then when it errs, although laws that are of lesser perfection.",["The final clause ('obgleich Gesetzen die von minderer Vollkommenheit sind') is elliptical and ungrammatical as it stands (dative 'Gesetzen'); kept as it stands, not completed.","Also = 'thus'."]],"philippi-s01-006":["The understanding is the faculty of general concepts, it now works partly in that it abstracts general rules and judgments from individual cases or experiences, as e.g. in morals, jurisprudence, physics or the beautiful sciences. This kind of understanding is called the *sensus communis* (*sens commun*) or the common understanding. The common understanding becomes a sound understanding (*le bon sens*) when it is correct; here experience and effect precede the rules.",["OCR slip corrected: Regehi → Regeln.","'theils' occurs without a following second 'theils'; kept as 'partly'.","allgemein = general; einzeln = individual; Art = kind; Wissenschaften = sciences; Erfahrung = experience; Wirkung = effect (the verb 'wirken' = 'work').","'hier geht ... vor' follows a semicolon-like break in the original, without a full stop before 'hier'."]],"philippi-s01-007":["The understanding that acts according to certain general laws, of which it is conscious, is the scientific learned understanding.",["'so er sich bewußt ist' is rendered 'of which it is conscious' ('so' = relative 'which'); the construction is loose in the German."]],"philippi-s01-008":["2. The artificial use is that according to which one is conscious of the rules of the understanding.",["bewust = bewußt (OCR/spelling)."]],"philippi-s01-009":["Note. This has been sent ahead in order now to be able to give correct *definitiones* of the common and learned understanding.",["Not. = Nota/'Note' (abbreviation expanded).","Definitiones is Latin in the German; kept in italics."]],"philippi-s01-010":["The skill of the understanding in the natural use is the common understanding.",["Fertigkeit = skill (not in the glossary).","gemein = common."]],"philippi-s01-011":["The skill of the understanding in the artificial use is the learned, artificial or higher understanding.",[]],"philippi-s01-012":["All exercises of the understanding, be it of the common or learned, all exercises and cognitions of the understanding are general, thereby they distinguish themselves from the sensible cognitions, which are individual.",["Erkenntnißen = Erkenntnissen, 'cognitions'; sinnlich = sensible; allgemein = general; einzeln = individual."]],"philippi-s01-013":["But the common understanding takes in its exercises an entirely different way from the learned. The common understanding cognizes the general only *in concreto*, or draws the general from individual propositions, which are grounded on experience. The higher understanding cognizes the general *in abstracto* or draws individual ones out of general propositions. E.g. If someone has given someone something to keep, but it should get lost, shall now the depositor alone suffer the damage, or shall the one to whom the *depositum* was entrusted compensate him for it? If one puts this question to common reason and demands from it a general rule for such occurrences, it cannot give one immediately, but appeals first to experience. It takes an individual example before itself and assesses the same through experience, thereupon it draws from experience a general proposition and rule which it then applies to other cases. In such a way most of the rules in jurisprudence have arisen.",["OCR slips corrected: gantz/Weg kept; daßelhe → daßelbe; einzeles/einzele kept as 'individual'; Jurisprudentz = jurisprudence.","erkennen = cognize; beurteilen = assess; Satz = proposition; Weg = way.","'Deponent' is rendered 'depositor'; 'Depositum' kept in Latin italics as *depositum*.","'wenn ... verlohren gienge' is subjunctive: 'should get lost'."]],"philippi-s01-014":["The *principia* the common understanding thus borrows from experience. The distinctness, however, it takes from particular individual cases. If one considers the common understanding from this point of view, one sees that it surpasses the sciences in distinctness, in obviousness and evidence. For everything that it presents is taken from experience and touches the senses. (But as regards extensiveness; the sciences have a preeminence.)",["Deutlichkeit = distinctness (deutlich = distinct); Augenscheinlichkeit = obviousness; Evidentz = evidence (Latinate, not marked in the German).","besonder = particular; einzeln = individual; Weitläufftigkeit = extensiveness (not in glossary); Vorzug = preeminence.","The semicolon inside the parenthesis is as in the transcript."]],"philippi-s01-015":["This obviousness and distinctness, however, which the common understanding has, is entirely lacking to the learned use of the understanding or to the learned understanding; for it considers the things *in abstracto* separated from all sensibility.",["'Diese Augenscheinlichkeit und Deutlichkeit ... fehlet' has a singular verb with a compound subject, as in the German ('is lacking').","Betrachtung = consideration (betrachten = consider)."]],"philippi-s01-016":["It is accordingly entirely opposed to the beautiful sciences that speak to our senses, and if one constantly abstracts and gives oneself over to remote subtleties; then they vanish entirely, which the time of the Scholastics proves.",["'Er' (masculine) refers back to the learned understanding (Verstand); rendered 'It'. 'sie' is taken as the beautiful sciences; the German leaves this open.","Scholastiker = Scholastics; Subtilitäten = subtleties."]],"philippi-s01-017":["The laws and rules of our understanding are either subjective or objective.",["subiectiue / obiectiue are the transcript's spellings (Latinate adjectives); rendered 'subjective' / 'objective'."]],"philippi-s01-018":["The subjective rules of the understanding or those rules according to which we actually make use of our understanding, psychology deals with. Psychology occupies itself in general with our soul, thus also with the forces of the same, consequently also with the understanding. But it considers the forces and their laws subjectively, i.e. without regard to the object with which these forces occupy themselves, and to the rules that must be observed thereby.",["Kräfte = forces (Kraft); Object = object (Latinate Object, not Gegenstand); Seele = soul.","wirklich = actually; abhandeln = deal with."]],"philippi-s01-019":["The rules, however, according to which we ought to make use of our understanding, is logic. It therefore presents the objective laws of the use of reason.",["Singular verb 'ist' with plural subject 'Die Regeln' is as in the German.","Logick = logic; Vernunfft = reason."]],"philippi-s01-020":["Metaphysics is to be sure also a science of the laws of reason, but only of pure reason, which is not mixed with sensibility and takes its *principia* merely from reason and not from experiences. It therefore considers our cognitions as we would have them if we had no body. Logic, on the other hand, borrows its *principia* partly from reason, partly from experiences.",["rein = pure; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Körper = body; Erkenntniße = cognitions."]],"philippi-s01-021":["Note. Physics takes the *principia* merely from experience; morals has its *principia* from reason.",["Not. = Nota/'Note' (abbreviation expanded)."]],"philippi-s01-022":["The logic of common and sound reason, otherwise it is also called natural wit, is distinguished from the logic of learned reason. The first presents its rules from concrete propositions, from experience, the other will demonstrate them from general grounds. The first is nothing but only a *critic*, the other is a *doctrine*.",["Witz = wit; Gründe = grounds; darthun = demonstrate.","'Critic' and 'Doctrin' are printed in the Latinate forms; the latter is rendered 'doctrine' (Lehre in the glossary is a different German word).","Future 'wird ... darthun' retained."]],"philippi-s01-023":["What is a doctrine?",["Doctrin: see previous note."]],"philippi-s01-024":["An interconnection of general rules through which one learns a certain exercise of the understanding is called a doctrine. But the faculty of assessing through an inner sensation whether a certain product of the understanding is good or bad is critique. All fine arts, e.g. poetry, aesthetics etc. etc., accordingly admit of no doctrine, but only of a critique; for taste cannot be learned through rules. One cannot teach anyone to have witty ideas. The rules that one has in aesthetics serve only to assess a product of taste. They are drawn from sensibility and do not directly help to make a product perfect, but sometimes indirectly through trying out.",["Doctrin = doctrine (Lehre elsewhere is also 'doctrine'); Critic = critique; beurtheilen = assess throughout; Empfindung = sensation; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; vollkommen = perfect.","'Probiren' rendered literally as 'trying out'."]],"philippi-s01-025":["The logic of common and healthy reason serves as a *catharcticon*, as a means to purge the understanding of errors and prejudices. — But the logic of learned reason is an aid, an *organon* of the sciences.",["Catharcticon and Organon are given in the transcript in Greek-derived form and kept in italics.","Wissenschafft = science; Mittel = means; Hülfsmittel = aid."]],"philippi-s01-026":["*Logica naturalis*, which is the science of the rules of the common and natural understanding. Common reason is necessary to determine the ends, in that it teaches us the *principia dijudicativa*. For learnedness teaches us only to choose skillful means for obtaining the ends. Under its territory belong the fine arts, because here everything depends on taste.",["Gebieth rendered 'territory' (not 'domain', which is the glossary equivalent of Umfang). 'Erhaltung' rendered 'obtaining'; it could also be 'preservation'.","'Ihr' in 'Unter ihr Gebieth' is ambiguous in the German (common reason / learnedness) and is left as 'its'. The OCR 'nothwendig.' is read as 'nothwendig'."]],"philippi-s01-027":["*Logica artificialis*. The logic of learnedness is the science of the rules of the correct use of the learned understanding.",[]],"philippi-s01-028":["Now, after the necessary ideas have been sent ahead, one can give a correct definition.",["Definition is the German word here (Erklärung in the next passages); both are rendered 'definition'."]],"philippi-s01-029":["Logic is a philosophy about the general laws of the correct use of the understanding and of reason.",["Stray 't' after 'Vernunft' in the OCR dropped. 'über' rendered 'about'."]],"philippi-s01-030":["Now we want to dissect every concept in this definition and set it out, but beforehand to note this:",["Erklärung rendered 'definition' to match the preceding passage (glossary also allows 'explanation'). Zergliedern = dissect."]],"philippi-s01-031":["*Logica* comes from λόγος (*ratio*) and is otherwise also called *Philosophia rationalis*; not because it philosophizes through reason but because it philosophizes from and about reason. The rational is here not the form, but the object, for the form can be rational, whatever the object or the matter may be, e.g. in physics the object is bodies, in mathematics magnitudes: in both the form is rational, in both one philosophizes through reason. Thus one can philosophize about the world, about human beings, about folly and unreason, in short, about every object, the form is always rational.",["Object and Gegenstand are both rendered 'object' (Object in the first part, Gegenstand in the last). OCR 'Aöyos' read as λόγος; 'Physich' as 'Physick'; 'philoßophiren' as 'philosophiren'.","'Das rationale' is given in roman type in the German and not italicized here, since it is German-inflected."]],"philippi-s01-032":["We said in the definition: logic philosophizes about the general laws of the correct use etc. etc.",["OCR 'tue' read as 'die'."]],"philippi-s01-033":["The laws that determine how the understanding is to think and judge correctly are called the objective laws. But the laws according to which our understanding actually judges are called (as we already cited above) the subjective laws.",["urtheilen = judge (v.); würklich = actually."]],"philippi-s01-034":["Whence does it come that our understanding does not act according to the objective laws?",[]],"philippi-s01-035":["That our understanding does not judge and think as it ought, or that it does not act according to the objective laws, stems merely from sensibility. If we also had the slightest degree of understanding, we would nevertheless never judge falsely. The lack of restriction would make our judgments merely faulty.",["Einschränkung rendered 'restriction' to keep it apart from Schranke = limitation. 'wenn wir auch' is rendered 'if we also', which preserves the German's possible 'even if' reading."]],"philippi-s01-036":["That we so often err and judge and think falsely, that is made by sensibility, under which we understand everything that is in the human being outside of reason. So often, then, as we err, so often our judgments do not follow the understanding but sensibility, and our judgments then occur not according to the objective but subjective rules.",["OCR 'Obicctiven', 'Subiectiven Pegeln' read as 'Objectiven', 'Subjectiven Regeln'; stray '»' dropped. 'ausser der Vernunft' = 'outside of reason' (also 'besides')."]],"philippi-s01-037":["NB. We know the rules that our understanding should exercise and yet we transgress them. Whence does it come, then? The cause is that we do not study properly the subject of our understanding, with which psychology deals. It is wonderful that, since so many scholars are always writing how one should be, the fewest nevertheless show how one actually is. They prescribe rules how one is to think, judge, and even in writing this they do not perfectly observe them. One asserts that there are faults with the subject in the whole of nature. If a bomb is thrown at a magazine and does not fall there, the fault is not to be sought in the subject: the bomb has moved quite exactly according to the laws of nature in gravity, but not according to the wish and intention of the shooter who threw it.",["The OCR '^\\ ir' read as 'wir'; 'Schützens' read as 'Schützen'. 'Subject' stands for Subject in both senses used here (of the understanding; of the bomb-throw).","'dass beim Subjecte Fehler in der gantzen Natur seyen' is obscure and kept literal: 'with the subject' (beim) could also be 'in the case of the subject'. 'wunderbar' = 'wonderful' (also 'strange')."]],"philippi-s01-038":["The rules that I need for the correct use of the understanding, I seek and take from the understanding. Reason says the rules according to which it is to direct itself.",["'sich richten' rendered 'direct itself' (also 'conform')."]],"philippi-s01-039":["Logic is supposed to be a means of preparation for the other sciences. It must therefore border most closely on common reason and be composed quite comprehensibly. Were it to be highly philosophical, one would have to use another logic as an *organon* in order to understand that one; as, e.g., he must already have logic in his command who wants to understand Crusius's logic.",["begreiflich = comprehensible (cf. begreifen = comprehend). 'inne haben' rendered 'have in his command'. Organo (Latin dative) rendered '*organon*'."]],"philippi-s01-040":["We said in the definition: logic is a philosophy. Why can one not define it through a science? Because 1. the word science is too general. 2. because logic is a part of philosophy.",["erklären rendered 'define' to match Erklärung = 'definition'."]],"philippi-s01-041":["If one investigates the origin of logic, it seems to be a paradox that one takes the rules of the understanding that are set forth in logic from the understanding itself. Only this paradox vanishes, if one considers that one is not conscious of them.",["'bewust' (bewußt) = conscious. 'ihrer' = 'of them' (the rules, by grammar)."]],"philippi-s01-042":["1. All logic must arise from the analysis (dissection) of the common understanding, e.g. the common understanding thinks, judges, infers. If one shows how the thoughts follow one from the other, how the understanding is moved to take something to be true, how it infers unknown propositions from known ones etc. etc.; then one can sketch from this an interconnection of rules, which then represents a logic.",["schlüßen rendered 'infer' (Schluß = inference); Sätze = propositions; Zusammenhang = interconnection; Zergliederung = dissection; vor wahr halten = take to be true."]],"philippi-s01-043":["2. Logic is grounded on critique, i.e. the sum total of the rules of the common understanding. Through analysis I begin to bring the rules of which I am conscious only into certain artificial formulas, according to which, as according to a certain prescription, I begin to criticize. For in dissection we already observe certain rules, without being conscious of them, or being able to become conscious of them. Logic is therefore a theory of cognition of reason. A theory is a sum total of such doctrines that, independently of experience, get proofs from reason *a priori*.",["OCR 'der ich mir bewust bin' read as 'die ich mir bewust bin' ('which'); 'Irmbegriff' read as 'Inbegriff'. 'nur' could attach to 'bringen' or to 'die Regeln...bewust bin'; kept in the German position.","Vernunfterkenntniß rendered 'cognition of reason'. 'a priori' could attach to 'from reason' or to 'get proofs'; left as in the German."]],"philippi-s01-044":["In experience the judgments *in concreto* are not made in accordance with the rules *in abstracto*, but the rules *in abstracto* arise from those *in concreto*. Accordingly logic is a theory from the dissection of reason and a critique, i.e. the sum total of the rules of the common understanding, in which it agrees with aesthetic (from αἰσθάνομαι), except that logic is a demonstrable, but aesthetic an indemonstrable critique. Therefore it is also called a theory of all cognitions of reason, because it contains *dogmata* and theorems that can be taught, and no theory can be given of taste because here everything depends on individual sensations. For this cannot prove its rules why they are so and not otherwise; they all stand under the common understanding. If a poem is composed exactly according to all the rules of aesthetic and poetics and yet does not please; then one will sooner believe that all rules are false than that such a piece would be beautiful.",["The Greek etymon is garbled in the OCR ('aio~0dvonou'); αἰσθάνομαι is a conjectural reading.","'Diese' ('For this cannot prove its rules') is left ambiguous in its referent (taste / aesthetic / critique). Lehrsätze = theorems; Empfindung = sensation."]],"philippi-s01-045":["The healthy understanding distinguishes itself thereby, that the judgments *in abstracto* are taken from the judgments *in concreto*.",["'dadurch, daß' kept as 'thereby, that'. Urtheile = judgments."]],"philippi-s01-046":["Logic is supposed to be an organon of the sciences, but not of common reason. Its utility is this:",["Wissenschafften = sciences (*scientia*); gemeinen Vernunfft = common reason (gemein = common)."]],"philippi-s01-047":["1. It serves as a universal *catharcticon* and is indispensable to common reason, in that it purifies the latter of errors and prejudices. Just as the art of all medicine amounts to this, not to give health, but only to remove the ills that are harmful to health: so too logic can give no understanding, but only improve what we have corrupted. If we were not so inclined to errors, we would not hold logic to be so indispensable either. Philosophy and sciences require a higher degree of sound understanding; logic determines the boundaries of the same with regard to the purification and safeguarding against errors.",["OCR: 'cüe Logic' read as 'die Logic'; 'Irrt Immer' read as 'Irrthümer'; the space before the semicolon in 'Verstandes ;' removed.","Grentzen = boundaries (Grenze); Verstand = understanding; gesunden Verstandes = sound understanding; Grad = degree.","'desselben' (\"of the same\") is ambiguous in reference between 'Verstandes' and the preceding phrase; kept as 'of the same'.","'Arzeney' rendered 'medicine'; 'beßern' = 'improve' (bessern)."]],"philippi-s01-048":["2. Through logic we can criticize the products of the human understanding in the sciences, although one cannot improve them.",["The opening quotation mark in the OCR is treated as a stray mark and omitted.","kritisiren = criticize; verbessern = improve."]],"philippi-s01-049":["One can proceed in criticism",["The OCR 'sh<-h kann' is garbled; probably 'Man kann' or 'Ich kann' (\"I can\"); rendered here as 'One can' on a doubtful reading.","Critick = criticism."]],"philippi-s01-050":["1. as an amateur (when we ground our judgment on the use of common understanding, and express ourselves without technical terms), who can judge of certain things that he indeed does not understand but yet is fond of, whether they are right or not, but cannot express this criticism of his formally. E.g. when someone who does not understand music hears another play and gives his judgment whether it sounds unpleasant or not.",["Liebhaber = amateur (lit. 'lover'); Kunstwörter = technical terms; Urtheil = judgment; Gebrauch = use; gemeinen Verstandes = common understanding.","verstehen = understand; 'versteht ... nicht' kept literal."]],"philippi-s01-051":["2. as a connoisseur, when one understands the thing one is judging and can express the fault one finds in the thing to be criticized through certain received judgments, formulas, canons (and this is the final end of logic), although one does not say how it ought to be better. E.g. when one criticizes a definition and adduces that it offends against the rule: *Definitio ne sit latior suo definito*",["Kenner = connoisseur; beurtheilen = judge/assess; Endzweck = final end (End + Zweck); Definition = definition (Latin-derived; Erklärung would be the glossary's 'definition' too).","The sentence ends without a period in the source; Latin rule kept in Latin."]],"philippi-s01-052":["Thus terminology belongs to the criticism of art, so that one knows how to express oneself artfully; in aesthetics one teaches an artful criticizing.",["Kunstcritic = criticism of art; künstlich = artfully; kunstmässiges = artful (lit. 'in accordance with art').","Aesthetick = aesthetics."]],"philippi-s01-053":["3. As a master, when one can produce the product that one criticizes oneself and make it better, and is the inventor of such formulas and rules as are generally received on his authority. Such judges of art were Wolff and Leibniz.",["Meister = master; Kunstrichter = judges of art; Ansehen = authority; 'Wolf' spelled as in source (Wolff).","'Product ... selbst verfertigen' kept in German order where possible; 'selbst' attaches to 'verfertigen'."]],"philippi-s01-054":["A cognition is logically perfect when it is perfect according to the rules of logic.",["Erkenntniß = cognition; vollkommen = perfect (Vollkommenheit = perfection)."]],"philippi-s01-055":["Logic is of two kinds 1. the theoretical.",[]],"philippi-s01-056":["2. the practical.",[]],"philippi-s01-057":["The theoretical is that which shows the conditions under which a cognition is logically perfect. It contains for this *principia rationalia*. The practical shows the means by which we can fulfill the conditions of logical perfection *per principia empirica*. Just as morals is theoretical, which shows the criteria of ethical perfection, and practical, which shows the means to attain the same.",["OCR 'prmcipia' corrected to 'principia'.","Bedingungen = conditions; Mittel = means; Criteria = criteria (Kriterium); sittlich = ethical (also 'moral' for Moral); Vollkommenheit = perfection.","'die die Bedingungen zeigt': 'die' (that which / the one that) kept ambiguous as in German; 'zur selbigen' = 'to the same'."]],"philippi-s01-058":["In theoretical logic we occupy ourselves merely with the concepts of that which is true or false, but in practical logic only rules are taught, which we must observe if we want to distinguish the true from the false, means are assigned to us as to how we have to proceed in this, and it is a practical discipline that teaches the subjective rules of human reason.",["Begriffen = concepts; Regeln = rules; Mittel = means; Vernunft = reason.","'von dem, was wahr oder falsch ist' kept literal (the German could mean 'of what is true or false')."]],"philippi-s01-059":["In such a way one has two kinds of *principia* of logic 1. *rationalia* 2. *practica*. The latter are still very much lacking. In the so-called practical logics there are many questions, but the resolution is for the most part tautological. Logic is a philosophy; consequently what is to be said of philosophy in general can be inserted here.",["'mehren-theils' read as 'mehrentheils' (for the most part); 'Auflösung' = resolution.","Philosophie = philosophy (distinct from Weltweisheit in the glossary).","'zweierley' = 'two kinds'; 'die lezteren' = 'the latter' (plural, with principia)."]],"philippi-s01-060":["Definition: *Philosophia est scientia rationalis de rerum qualitatibus.*",["Latin kept in Latin. 'Defin.' expanded as 'Definition'."]],"philippi-s01-061":["By rational cognition it is distinguished from other cognitions that are historical or empirical.",["OCR period after 'Erkenntnissen' read as a comma.","rationale Erkenntniß = rational cognition; historisch = historical; empirisch = empirical."]],"philippi-s01-062":["NB. The knowledge of nature comprehends 1. the history of nature, what experience teaches. 2. the mathematics of nature, what concerns magnitudes, 3. philosophical cognition.",["Naturkunde = knowledge of nature (Kunde, not Wissen/Erkenntniß); begreifft = comprehends (begreifen, glossary 'comprehend').","Grössen = magnitudes; Erfahrung = experience."]],"philippi-s01-063":["Historical cognition has nothing to do with reason, but at most only with the understanding. Thus one can even cognize philosophy historically. When a thunderstorm arises, then understanding must be there in order to consider everything that goes on; but if one is to describe a thunderstorm historically: then the understanding can do nothing on its own, otherwise it would be fiction and not history. Philosophy is not a cognition of common and sound reason, but a science of the same.",["Verstand = understanding; Vernunfft = reason; Wissenschafft = science; Betrachten = consider; erkennen = cognize.","'von selbst nichts vor sich thun' rendered 'can do nothing on its own'; 'vor sich' left unrendered as idiomatic. 'derselben' (\"of the same\") ambiguous between 'Vernunfft' and the whole phrase.","Erdichtung = fiction."]],"philippi-s01-064":["Which cognition is of such a degree that it becomes philosophical? Every cognition, insofar as it judges what can be cognized *a posteriori*, is common cognition. But if one judges universally *a priori*, one already has a philosophical cognition. If a tailor were to complain about the oppressive times, lack of money etc. etc., he would have a common cognition. But if he says the general complaint lies in luxury; then he already begins to philosophize. For this judgment he takes merely from his reason. A mutilated beggar already judges philosophically when he believes that although a limb is missing from him, nothing has yet gone from his own self. People must judge in the moral expressly according to reason. If the whole world were full of vice, some would nevertheless cognize the ethical not from experience, no, from reason.",["OCR 'pbilosophiren' read as 'philosophiren'; 'erglau lit' read as 'er glaubt'; 'urthcilen' as 'urtheilen'.","Grad = degree; urtheilen = judge; Erkenntniß = cognition; glauben = believe; Selbst = self; das Sittliche = the ethical.","'Wenn ein Schneider ... klagen würde' kept in subjunctive; 'abgegangen' rendered 'gone' (literally 'departed')."]],"philippi-s01-065":["Common understanding shows itself among simple people nowhere more than in the judging of the ethical. But it takes its judgments only from cases *in concreto*; but the scholar draws them from abstractions.",["OCR 'nirgend!' read as 'nirgend'; 'abstract is' read as 'abstractis' (kept as 'abstractions' for Latin 'ex abstractis').","Beurtheilung = judging (judgment); geringen Leuten = simple (lowly) people; gemeine Verstand = common understanding."]],"philippi-s01-066":["Philosophical cognition is therefore distinguished from common cognition in that it arises *a priori*, but common cognition *a posteriori*.",["unterschieden = distinguished; entsteht = arises."]],"philippi-s01-067":["Philosophy deals with *de rerum qualitate*. All sciences distinguish themselves from mathematics and philosophy.",["The source reads 'handelt de rerum qualitate'; the Latin is left as it stands, with 'deals with' rendering 'handelt'.","Wissenschafften = sciences; 'unterscheiden sich von' kept literal."]],"philippi-s01-068":["These two are indeed alike according to form; for they are both sciences of the learned understanding: but according to matter they are distinguished.",["OCR 'Wissenschaff ten' joined.","Form = form; Materie = matter; gelehrten Verstandes = learned understanding."]],"philippi-s01-069":["The object of mathematics is always identical, the object of philosophy heterogeneous.",["Gegenstand and Object are both rendered 'object' (Gegenstand in the first clause, Object in the second)."]],"philippi-s01-070":["In all cognition one must distinguish matter and form. The former is called the object of science, and the latter the *modus cognoscendi*.",["Object = object; Wissenschaft = science; 'Jene ... diese' = 'the former ... the latter'."]],"philippi-s01-071":["Whoever buys a country estate considers it quite differently *philosophice*, according to the constitution of the land, the meadows, the fields etc. etc., and quite differently *mathematice*, namely according to the magnitude, according to the number of hides, buildings etc. etc. Philosophy and mathematics are heterogeneous, for their objects are very different.",["Beschaffenheit = constitution; Grösse = magnitude; Anzahl = number; Hüben = hides (units of farmland; 'Hube').","Objecte = objects; 'heterogeneisch' = heterogeneous."]],"philippi-s01-072":["Yet there is also a hidden difference in the form, namely the use of the method of mathematics is distinguished from that of philosophy. In mathematics I consider only magnitude and this is nothing other than a manifold repetition of the very same through attention to the relation. The principles of mathematics can be regarded as *principia empirica peregrina*; mathematics considers the coordination, the taking together of the ideas.",["Gebrauch = use; Grösse = magnitude; Verhältniß = relation; Grundsätze = principles; Ideen = ideas.","'vielfältige' = manifold (adjective here, not the substantive 'das Mannigfaltige'); 'eben desselben' = 'of the very same'.","'Zusammennehmung' rendered 'taking together'; Coordination kept as 'coordination'."]],"philippi-s01-073":["Philosophy considers the *genesis*, the subordination of concepts, e.g. when two play, who will win? The mathematician weighs the multitude of grounds, the philosopher their weight. In philosophy one asks what the relation of cause to effect is. The mathematician can therefore also get on well without too great an understanding.",["'Genesin' = accusative of Latin/Greek 'genesis'; Menge = multitude; Gründe = grounds; Wichtigkeit = weight (importance); Begriffe = concepts; Ursache = cause; Wirkung = effect; Verstand = understanding.","'ohne gar zu großen Verstand' kept literal."]],"philippi-s01-074":["All mathematical definitions and all concepts are actual and infallible. (In philosophy they are previously determined and necessary.) For the mathematician sets himself something and explains it as he wants and needs it. As, e.g., Wolff explains light, space, air etc. etc. in mathematics. But to the philosopher something is given. Whoever in philosophy wanted to define something as he needs it for his system; *ille Gryphis iungere equis*. Wolff did it so.",["The parenthesis is as in the source. 'wirklich' = actual; 'bestimmt' = determined; 'notwendig' = necessary; 'erkläret' rendered 'explains' (Erklärung = explanation/definition).","'ille Gryphis iungere equis': Latin left as printed (a Virgilian tag; the source reading is kept without completion or emendation).","'Wolf' spelled as in source."]],"philippi-s01-075":["Philosophy is",["The passage breaks off in mid-sentence; not completed."]],"philippi-s01-076":["1. *Philosophia habitualis*, a skill of being able to philosophize.",["Fertigkeit = \"skill\" (not in the glossary); philosophiren rendered \"philosophize\"."]],"philippi-s01-077":["2. *Philosophia disciplinaris*, an interconnection (system) of propositions. (The former could also be called the subjective philosophy, in that it must determine the subject itself, the latter the objective, insofar as it teaches the form of philosophizing; i.e., how one ought to think.)",["Zusammenhang = interconnection; Sätze = propositions (Satz also \"principle\", \"sentence\").","OCR \"Philosoplne\" corrected silently to \"Philosophie\".","\"Jene\" / \"diese\" are rendered \"the former\" / \"the latter\"; the German does not name the referents, and \"the latter\" could take the nearer noun."]],"philippi-s01-078":["Is there then a ready and correct philosophy? If there were, that would be a subjective philosophy. (A scientific cognition of reason, if I know how to judge the cases *in concreto*, *a priori*). But now one has only a mere possible knowledge, an objective philosophy of how one ought to use the understanding.",["Erkenntnis = cognition; Wissen = knowledge; Verstand = understanding; beurteilen = judge.","\"Vernunfterkenntniß\" = \"cognition of reason\"; \"scientifische\" = \"scientific\".","\"lauter mögliches Wissen\": \"lauter\" rendered \"mere\"; the German is compressed and the appositive \"eine objective Philosophie\" is kept as it stands.","\"Wenn die wäre\": \"die\" could refer to the ready philosophy or be demonstrative; left as \"If there were\"."]],"philippi-s01-079":["Other sciences can be learned through the teaching of others, in that we merely imitate everything. In history I can devise nothing myself; I must follow others; in mathematics too. Pascal, already in his tender childhood, could make all geometrical figures, and when his books were taken from him, he made them again from his own head.",["Wissenschaft = science; Belehrung = teaching.","\"machte er sie ... nach\": \"them\" (sie) is ambiguous between the figures and the books; the German is equally open."]],"philippi-s01-080":["But if someone also reads all philosophical books, he is still only a repository of these books, provided he thinks only according to them and not himself. He has merely an epitome in the understanding of the propositions read. He knows them merely historically; that is no philosopher. Hence a philosopher also cannot in the proper sense be called a scholar, in that philosophy cannot be taught. For 1. there is no philosophy that would be reliable. 2. If philosophy is taught to someone, and he would grasp these propositions ever so well, that would still be no philosopher, who only imprints the propositions of the other in himself.",["kennen = know (be acquainted with); Satz = proposition; Verstand = understanding (\"im eigentlichen Verstande\" = \"in the proper sense\").","\"Gelehrter\" = scholar; \"gelehret\" = taught."]],"philippi-s01-081":["That is a philosopher who possesses the skill of philosophizing. Such a one must have genius.",["Fertigkeit = skill."]],"philippi-s01-082":["Genius is an original spirit, who brings forth perfect products without imitation. The original distinguishes itself from others, is characteristic; and an original spirit is 1. one who has none beside him 2. who has his dexterity not through imitation, but through himself. Genius is an original spirit.",["Geist = spirit (also \"mind\"); Originalgeist = \"original spirit\".","Geschicklichkeit = \"dexterity\" (not in glossary).","\"karacteristisch\" = \"characteristic\"."]],"philippi-s01-083":["NB. Original is not always an archetype, it only means one who does not imitate. The English, original spirits, who wrote from themselves without imitation, have often put forward repugnant and false propositions only in order to be original. There are sciences 1. of learning, in which one imitates one's teacher in school; but also 2. of genius.",["Urbild = \"archetype\".","\"Original\" at the start is neuter/adjectival noun; \"der nicht nachahmt\" is masculine, as rendered.","Satz = proposition."]],"philippi-s01-084":["Habitual philosophy is of this kind, can therefore not be learned; I only learn to *evolve* it. Philosophical books serve only as an occasion to express one's thoughts *methodo philosophica*. A philosophical head begins to examine for himself the propositions that the teacher gives him and often prefers his opinions to them. He must not learn the definitions, but be able to invent them himself. For mathematics not so much genius is required. One need only go by the books. In philosophy, however, there are no infallible books. A philosophy that would be infallible would be of inestimable worth. Meanwhile one must learn the method of how one can judge such books.",["\"euoluiren\" = Latin *evolvere* with German ending; rendered \"evolve\" in italics.","Erklärung/Definition: \"Definitionen\" = \"definitions\"; Meinung = opinion; Satz = proposition; beurteilen = judge.","\"von der Art\": rendered \"of this kind\" (Art = kind)."]],"philippi-s01-085":["*Denominatio*. Philosophia from <piÄ£00 and (7096s. Some say the name is not appropriate, in that philosophy is a seat of wisdom. But this term is very good and it is probably not credible that it should have been invented by chance. In general one often finds in such names the remotest concepts, which have maintained themselves very long and have been approved by many scholars. Human wisdom is indeed nothing but knowing and inquiry.",["The Greek (φιλέω and σοφός presumably) is garbled by OCR (\"<piÄ£00 and (7096s\"); left as it stands, not restored.","Benennung = term; Begriff = concept; Wissen = knowing/knowledge.","\"Sitz der Weißheit\" = \"seat of wisdom\"."]],"philippi-s01-086":["If philosophy is regarded as a piece of erudition and not of wisdom, then there are many cognitions of understanding and of reason, of which one could ask what use they then would have?",["Gelehrsamkeit = \"erudition\"; Erkenntnis = cognition; Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason.","\"Verstandes und Vernunft Erkenntniße\" kept as a compound with the first genitive."]],"philippi-s01-087":["Science of the world has nothing in itself but theory and strives only after logical perfection. It takes its name from the world, because its *principia* are borrowed from the world. If we take philosophy from our illumination, that is enthusiasm: if we take it from the illumination of others, that is revelation.",["\"Weltwissenschafft\" = \"science of the world\" (Welt + Wissenschaft); related to Weltweisheit = philosophy.","Benennung = term, rendered \"name\" here since it is the verb \"Benennung herhmen\"; Vollkommenheit = perfection.","\"Begeisterung\" = \"enthusiasm\"; \"Offenbarung\" = \"revelation\"."]],"philippi-s01-088":["All philosophers have as their end only to impart the sciences or skills to the listeners in the use of reason. One should guard against the luxury of reason, pure speculation.",["Zweck = end; Gebrauch = use; Vernunft = reason; rein = pure.","\"Geschicklichkeiten\" = \"skills\"; \"im Gebrauch\" could attach to \"beizubringen\" or to \"Geschicklichkeiten\"; left ambiguous."]],"philippi-s01-089":["Through philosophy the boundaries of all cognition must be drawn in advance.",["Grenze = boundary; Erkenntnis = cognition.","\"vorgezeichnet\" = \"drawn in advance\" (also \"prescribed\")."]],"philippi-s01-090":["2. Philosophy insofar as the science serves for the promotion of the essential wisdom.",["Weltweißheit = philosophy (glossary Weltweisheit).","OCR \"(\\t>v\" is garbled (probably \"der\"); the article before \"wesentlichen\" is uncertain, rendered \"the\"."]],"philippi-s01-091":["That is true wisdom through which we learn to recognize 1. our great vocation 2. the means to reach the end. Some sciences of the human understanding relate only to the skill and dexterity of finding out means in order to arrive at any end one likes. Philosophy shows both. Locke has described the vocation of the human understanding, Hume its limitations.",["erkennen = recognize; Bestimmung = vocation; Mittel = means; Zweck = end; Schranke = limitation.","\"Hurae\" in OCR corrected silently to \"Hume\".","\"Wissenschaffen\" in OCR corrected to \"Wissenschaften\"."]],"philippi-s01-092":["Metaphysics and morals make up true wisdom. The former shows the vocation of the understanding, the other that of the will. Physics is no dexterity. The philosopher is a *theoreticus*, the CTO90S (wise) is a *practicus*.",["The Greek word (presumably σοφός) is garbled in OCR (\"CTO90S\"); left as it stands.","Bestimmung = vocation; Verstand = understanding.","\"Geschicklichkeit\" = \"dexterity\"."]],"philippi-s01-093":["All philosophy can be divided into the practical and the theoretical. The former is called morals and contains the rules of the perfection of all free conduct. The latter is logic and contains the rules of logical perfection, accordingly also *principia rationalia*.",["Weltweißheit = philosophy; Einteilung = division; Vollkommenheit = perfection.","\"Verhalten\" = \"conduct\"."]],"philippi-s01-094":["All sciences presuppose an ignorance and a state",["\"Staat\" = \"state\" in the political sense (not Zustand); no final punctuation in the source."]],"philippi-s01-095":["*Historia philosophiae*.",[]],"philippi-s01-096":["All sciences begin from the time when human beings stood under the protection of the authorities, from human beings who had leisure and for whose welfare provision was made publicly. When one is in need and must provide scantily for one's maintenance, one asks nothing about the sciences; but when one is idle, one becomes curious. The inequality that one is lord and the other servant has arisen from violence. The comfort of some and the exertion of others, who had to nourish those idle ones; this was the beginning of the sciences. In what estate then did they arise? Those of whom one believed that they had to teach were the priests: they were the first who had to invent them and make a profession of them. For without religion no state can be. At that time, however, they still had no doctrine of religion. One raised questions and held oneself entitled to put them to the priests, as those who were regarded as ones who were to teach. These therefore had, in order to be able to answer the questions, to make themselves prepared for answers. The first science that arose in this way was morals; after this arose pneumatology.",["Wissenschaft = science; Lehre = doctrine; Staat = state; Stand = estate.","\"hält sich berechtiget dieselbe ... vorzulegen\": \"dieselbe\" (singular) after plural \"Fragen\" kept as \"them\"; the sentence is syntactically awkward in the German and is left so.","\"sich auf Antworten gefaßt machen\" = \"make themselves prepared for answers\"; \"daß sie lehren sollten\" attaches to the priests as rendered, but \"dafür angesehen wurden\" is literal.","\"Pneumatologie\" kept as \"pneumatology\"."]],"philippi-s01-097":["Besides the Egyptians, Persians, Chaldeans, and Indians, no other peoples have had systems of religion.",["\"Indianern\" rendered \"Indians\"."]],"philippi-s01-098":["But finally a revolution of the understanding ensued. This happened when one began, independently of the priestly estate, to pursue sciences and to philosophize. The peoples among whom this first happened were the Chinese and Greeks",["Verstand = understanding; Wissenschaft = science.","OCR \"pliilosophiren\" corrected silently to \"philosophiren\".","No final punctuation in the source."]],"philippi-s01-099":["The Jews had no need of philosophy. In China neither the Bonzes nor the Lamas philosophize, but the Mandarins, since the former adhered to their particular system.",["OCR \"Aveder\" corrected silently to \"weder\".","\"jene\" rendered \"the former\"; it could refer to Bonzes and Lamas as rendered, or be read otherwise; German does not specify.","besonder = particular."]],"philippi-s01-100":["There have been peoples who philosophized speculatively independently of religion, and the Greeks and Chinese did this.",["\"Spekulativisch\" = \"speculatively\"."]],"philippi-s01-101":["China is the most cultivated among all oriental peoples and is to be regarded as the source of the sciences. The priests in both countries wrote no book at all about religion.",["\"Qvelle\" = \"source\" (spelling modernized silently).","\"beiden Ländern\" = \"both countries\"; the German does not name which two, and the passage's context is not translated."]],"philippi-s01-102":["Egypt has been, from ancient times, a very accurately organized state. Since there were now many tranquil and idle estates there, who had nothing to do but to see how they could distinguish themselves from the people; so the sciences had to sprout forth. But that the sciences did not come to perfection, happened because they were not allowed to step beyond a certain prescription. Even if a great genius was found; so it had to produce nothing that was beyond that prescription. It had to go not beyond the boundary that its wretched religion set for it. Confucius wrote 550 years before Christ's birth first a morality. It contains principles according to which princes are to be educated, which were also generally accepted in esteem, and through which he brought about the mandarins' beneficent and just government. This morality now stands in the place of a system of religion. It could not properly spread, because it was combined with the government: just as Egyptian philosophy was fettered by religion. And so they both stood under constraint; constrained, one cannot philosophize. Where philosophy is subjected to the fundamental laws of the state, it can never attain perfection.",["Wissenschaften = sciences (scientia); Vollkommenheit = perfection; Grenze = boundary; Grundsätze = principles; Verbindung/verbunden = combination/combined.","Stände rendered 'estates'; the German could also be read as 'ranks' or 'classes'.","'im Ansehen allgemein angenommen' is kept ambiguous: Ansehen could mean esteem, repute or regard; 'esteem' is chosen.","The punctuation ' ; so muste' and the repeated 'so' clauses are kept as in the German."]],"philippi-s01-103":["Mathematics gives through its order, evidence, precision to the understanding a model and sketch, of how near it comes to the same. This science has begun with the art of writing, and the Egyptians are supposed to have invented it but only *empirice*. Among the Greeks it first attained its perfection.",["'wie nahe er demselben kömmt': er (understanding) and demselben (the model, or mathematics) are left as ambiguous as in the German.","Wissenschaft = science (scientia); Verstand = understanding; Evidenz = evidence."]],"philippi-s01-104":["Of the philosophy of the Egyptians nothing has come down to our times.",[]],"philippi-s01-105":["Among the Chaldeans the first writing was probably invented. Their best-known philosopher is Zoroaster, among the Persians Zerdust, who is perhaps the same, although there are just as many Zoroasters as Hercuteses. He taught 2 *principia*, a good one, which he called Hormisdas, and an evil one, which he called Ahriman. Leibniz believes that the latter is the German Herrmann, who under the reign of Hormisdas made an expedition to Persia, and that the Persians are a German nation, which the many German words in the Persian language also seem to confirm. He is said to have established the religion of the Persians at the times of Darius Hystaspes. The book in which the same is composed is called Zendavesta, which Perron brought to Europe and is of a great age.",["'dieser' is kept ambiguous (grammatically the nearer antecedent is Ahriman); likewise 'welches' in the last sentence may refer to the book or to Zendavesta.","'Herkuleße' is rendered 'Hercuteses' by mistake of form; read 'Hercules-es' i.e. plural of Hercules as printed ('Herkuleße'). The spelling 'Hercuteses' should be read 'Herculeses'.","'welcher' after Zerdust: whether Zoroaster or Zerdust is the antecedent is left as in the German."]],"philippi-s01-106":["The sciences of genius flourish especially in small free states that lie next to one another. Freedom awakens the spirit and kindles emulation: and so was Greece constituted, (England still has some analogy with these Greek city-states). To the citizens of this country all estates stood open. As an abuse in small states does not remain long, in that one criticizes and laughs at the other, whereas in large states the same continually endures: so also in Greece, when an abuse arose in a city the same was soon carded through by others and thereby stopped. Hence it comes that the propositions they had in religion and otherwise are clothed with such wit that one still cites them even now in similar cases in writings, in that one can hardly invent wittier similes, as e.g. the sirens, the story of Pandora's box and of such kind are. On the whole the Greeks can be regarded as the most talkative, frivolous and most curious of peoples. Their talkativeness shows itself in the unfaithfulness in the narration of occurrences. There are the fewest histories that are not stuffed full with a multitude of invented circumstances. Their frivolity was the sequel of their freedom and one sees it clearly in the mockery and laughter of many and in the indifference of all toward the narratives which constituted the system of their religion. Their curiosity was so great that when they publicly exhibited something that they made through art or genius the common man ran up and reasoned about it. If someone had found something admirable or useful, it was enough for him to see his name resplendent on public columns; an honor that many deserving generals have wished for in vain. It was also not unfair that one left products of taste to the judgment of common understanding. Homer sang his poems to common people and Molière read his comedies to his maid, and he found her taste entirely agreeing with that of the spectators.",["OCR slips silently corrected: Wißcnschaften, m (in), Nacheif erung, ldeinen (kleinen), Moliere.","'eingestehet' is doubtful (possibly 'eingestellet'); rendered 'stopped'.","Geist = mind; spirit: 'spirit' chosen. Sätze = propositions (also sentences, principles). Begebenheiten = occurrences; Folge = sequel; Beurteilung = judgment; gemeiner Verstand = common understanding.","'raisonnirte' (French-derived) rendered 'reasoned about it'.","'Wissenschaften des Genies' kept literal; 'durchgehechelt' (literally 'hackled, combed through') rendered 'carded through'."]],"philippi-s01-107":["Pythagoras traveled to India, in order to learn there the philosophy of the Indians. All that one encounters among them resembling philosophy is the doctrine of metempsychosis, and a kind of Stoic system. Brahma, their putative lawgiver and originator of this doctrine, has left it behind in a book, which can be regarded as their law book, and is called Vedas, which is still older than the Persians' Zewa-Vesta. Their philosophy was thus a kind of mystical morality. They still have now a university at Benares.",["'welches' (after Veden) may refer to the book or to Veden; left ambiguous.","Lehre = doctrine; 'Veden' rendered 'Vedas'; 'Zewa-Vesta' kept as spelled (cf. Zendavesta in the preceding passage)."]],"philippi-s01-108":["The Asiatics are very rich in images (which seems to be the ground of all superstition and of the little growth that the sciences have had in this part of the world), just as the French are rich in sudden thoughts. In the seven sages only the maxim-rich wit was encountered, among all these sages perhaps Thales alone was merely a scholar and Solon the only sage.",["'Thaies' read as Thales (OCR).","Einfälle rendered 'sudden thoughts' (not 'ideas', which is reserved for Idee).","'nur ein Gelehrter' could attach to 'der einzige Thales' as 'merely a scholar' or 'only a scholar'; the German is left open.","Weisen = sages; Grund = ground; Wissenschaften = sciences."]],"philippi-s01-109":["With Confucius the philosophy of the Chinese began, who namely brought it into a great form, in which it afterwards constantly remained, without new discoveries having been added.",["'der' after 'Chineser' is kept as in the German (relative to Confucius)."]],"philippi-s01-110":["The Greek philosophers can be brought under two classes, into those before the introduction of larger schools, and into those who introduced various schools. To the former belong the 7 philosophers, if they deserve the name. At that time the civil constitution of the republics began to form itself, and those who distinguished themselves on such occasion received the name sages.",["OCR 'Sclnüen.' read as 'Schulen,'.","Weltweisen rendered 'philosophers' (Weltweisheit = philosophy in the glossary); Weise = sage.","Verfaßung = constitution."]],"philippi-s01-111":["Learning is distinguished from wisdom in that the former is almost merely speculative, and the latter contains more practical rules and ends of humanity. Socrates called the first Philodoxes, the others however philosophers. Their whole philosophy consisted of sayings of sense and of thought, which were esteemed the more the more striking they were, and of images in the taste of the Asiatic Greeks, which is on the whole a sure mark of little talent.",["OCR 'Plülodoxen' read as 'Philodoxen'; 'Sinnund Denksprüche' read as 'Sinn- und Denksprüche'.","Gelehrsamkeit = learning; Weisheit = wisdom; Zwecke = ends; Merkmahl = mark (nota).","'die ersten ... die andern': the referents are left as in the German."]],"philippi-s01-112":["The history of Greek philosophy can be divided into the schools of the practical philosophers, who however properly did not constitute a sect, but only taught morality, to which Diogenes the Cynic, Zeno, Socrates are to be counted, and into the contemplative and speculative or theoretical philosophers. These separate themselves again into various classes.",["OCR '»Secte aus machten' read as 'Secte ausmachten'.","Eintheilung = division."]],"philippi-s01-113":["The dogmatists, who assert a complete certainty in the propositions of reason. *Dogmata* are called truths of reason, and one is dogmatic when one does not seek the propositions of reason but holds them to be already found. Against these the skeptics set themselves.",["The first sentence has no finite verb in the German; kept as it stands.","Vernunftsätze = propositions of reason; Gewißheit = certainty; 'Scepticer' rendered 'skeptics'."]],"philippi-s01-114":["The dogmatists distinguish themselves",["The passage breaks off; continued in the next passages."]],"philippi-s01-115":["1. into those who, from *principia* of sensibility and",["Breaks off in mid-sentence; continued in the next passage."]],"philippi-s01-116":["2. into those who philosophized from *principia* of reason.",[]],"philippi-s01-117":["Into the νοούμενοι and φαινόμενοι. Heraclitus made the distinction. The νοούμενοι asserted the senses were false, they showed not how the things were, but only how they were touched by the things. They believed that philosophy restricts itself to the *intelligibilia*, and that all objects of the senses, which were as it were in a constant flux, belonged only to philodoxy, but were not objects of reason. Democritus, Leucippus, Epicurus defended the opposite, that the whole philosophy restricts itself to the *sensibilia*.",["The Greek in the OCR ('vooOnsvoi', '9aiv6|j£voi', 'voouuevoi') is badly garbled; the readings νοούμενοι and φαινόμενοι are conjectural restorations.","Gegenstände = objects; Sinne = senses; Philodoxie rendered 'philodoxy'.","'die ganze Philosophie' is kept without added 'the whole of'."]],"philippi-s01-118":["Aristotle considered the *mundus sensibilis* and *insensibilis*. He said that the intellectual concepts were indeed higher than the sensitive, but yet had grown up with them on one stem.",["OCR 'scnsibilem' read as 'sensibilem'; 'Sensitiue' (Latin-form adjective) rendered 'sensitive'.","Begriffe = concepts; 'intellectuelle' = intellectual."]],"philippi-s01-119":["Others say, like Plato, that the intellectual concepts come from an inner intuition of the deity.",["Begriffe = concepts; Anschauung = intuition; 'innera' read as 'innern' (OCR).","'intellectuelle' kept as the cognate 'intellectual'."]],"philippi-s01-120":["The objects of pure reason are all moral and metaphysical cognitions, just as the objects of sensibility are *phaenomena*.",["'(Jegenstände' read as 'Gegenstände' (OCR): object. Erkenntniße = cognitions. Phaenomena kept in Latin/Greek form as printed."]],"philippi-s01-121":["All philosophers of older and newer times have precisely the same matter or objects of their philosophy, only in their form are they distinguished.",["'(Jegenstände' read as 'Gegenstände' (OCR). Materie = matter; Form = form.","The German has no connective before 'nur ihrer Form nach'; the comma-splice is kept."]],"philippi-s01-122":["The dogmatists in turn distinguished themselves into various schools, such as the Ionic, which began with Thales, one of the 7 sages, who had fetched his wisdom from Egypt 2, the Eleatic 3, the Italic, whose head was Pythagoras 4, the Platonic. All of these divided the world into *mundum sensibilem* and *intelligibilem*. The concepts of the sensible world, they said, we receive by means of the senses from outer objects. The concepts of the intellectual world arise from our understanding. The latter concepts Pythagoras called number, Plato ideas, and Aristotle the form, under which they understood everything that is cognized through the understanding.",["The numerals 2, 3, 4 are as in the OCR text (apparently residual enumeration); '1' is absent. 'Thaies' read as 'Thales'; 'nante' = 'nannte'.","Verstand = understanding; Verstände (dative) likewise. erkannt = cognized. Numerus kept as in the German (Latin form, 'number').","'sagten sie.' punctuation as in the source (period after 'sie')."]],"philippi-s01-123":["Socrates was no *dogmaticus*; his doubt was no decisive doubt. There follow",["Passage breaks off; continued by the next passage ('2. die Dogmatici ...')."]],"philippi-s01-124":["2. the *dogmatici* who accept merely *principia sensibilia*. The head of these was Epicurus. He is the only philosopher of antiquity. For one judges philosophy not according to the propositions but only according to the method, but in this he is the foremost of antiquity.",["Sätzen = propositions; beurtheilt = judges (beurteilen); Epikur rendered 'Epicurus'.","'darinn aber' — 'in this' could refer to method or philosophy; left as in German."]],"philippi-s01-125":["His first rule was this: since our cognitions arise from the senses, one must name the original.",["Erkenntniße = cognitions. The sense of 'das Original nennen' is obscure and is kept literal; possible OCR/transcript corruption."]],"philippi-s01-126":["2. One should avoid all mystical, oratorical expressions, and accept no proposition that one could not show in concrete cases and make sensible.",["Ausdrücke = expressions; Satz = proposition; sinnlich machen = 'make sensible'."]],"philippi-s01-127":["Through the first he banished all begged propositions from his philosophy and through the second all chimerical concepts and drew from it the principle that what is not an object of our outer sense is problematic, and one would have no ground to accept it straightaway, that on the contrary everything that one is to take to be true must be able to be shown in experience and laid before outer sensibility.",["'verbanne te' read as 'verbannte' (OCR). Grundsatz = principle; Gegenstand = object; Grund = ground; für wahr halten = take to be true; Erfahrung = experience.","'daß dagegen ...' stands in the German as a further clause after the comma; kept."]],"philippi-s01-128":["Thus he demanded to derive all cognitions from sensible grounds. It is a pity that this Epicurus has become so hated by posterity, partly through the misconduct of his pupils, partly through the misunderstanding of his propositions, from which his enemies burdened him with the most abominable vices. One judges him according to how Lucretius his pupil described him and does not recall the bold freedom that poets take in their style of writing. Would not a Haller, if one wanted to judge him according to his writings, deserve to be called a heretic.",["'sem Schüler' read as 'sein Schüler' (OCR). Erkenntnisse = cognitions; Sätze = propositions; beurtheilen = judge.","Final sentence ends with a period in the source though it is a question in form; kept."]],"philippi-s01-129":["He sought only to bring back the *dogmatici* who were swarming about in sheer ideal worlds. Whence does it rain? One says providence ordains it so. Yes, but then all philosophy ceases. To the *materia substratum* of philosophy the world is given and not supernatural things. But what one cannot derive from natural grounds, that one should not posit in philosophy.",["'Materia Substratum' kept in Latin; Welt = world; setzen = posit; Gründe = grounds.","'Zur Materia Substratum ... ist gegeben' kept in German word order roughly."]],"philippi-s01-130":["He accepted as principles that whose laws of effect are known to us.",["'dessenWirkungsgesetze' read as 'dessen Wirkungsgesetze' (OCR). Principien = principles; Gesetze = laws; Wirkung = effect."]],"philippi-s01-131":["The immortality of the soul, of which he did not see the ground through the senses, he neither affirmed nor denied.",["affirmirt / negirt = affirmed / denied (bejahen)."]],"philippi-s01-132":["Pleasure (*voluptas*) he held to be the point of reference of his philosophy. By it he understood not at all the outer, low sensible delights. His moderate life proves it. In this he is of course certainly wrong, but he is by no means on that account to be accused of gross vices.",["'massiges' read as 'mäßiges' (OCR). Vergnügen = pleasure."]],"philippi-s01-133":["One must seek virtue because one finds happiness in it, he says.",["Glückseeligkeit = happiness."]],"philippi-s01-134":["This is his mistake, that he wanted to assert that our pleasures were not effects of the mind but of sensibility. Thereby he does not, however, posit gross animal lust as the end; but left to each the mental pleasure. This is a philosophical judging of the *phaenomenon*, not a morals. E.g. joy he derived from the warming of the heart and the harmony of the blood, etc. etc.",["Geist = mind; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Zweck = end; Beurtheilung = judgment/assessment (rendered 'judging' to keep the verbal sense).","'Phaenomeni' genitive of Latin phaenomenon; 'eine Moral' rendered 'a morals' to keep the article."]],"philippi-s01-135":["His principle was distinctness and as much on that account as on account of the principle of happiness or of pleasure (considered in the ground these two concepts are one and the same. That we call happiness which is suitable to our inclinations) he can be called the philosopher of the Graces, who strews the path of virtue with roses.",["Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Begriffe = concepts; Principium kept as 'principle'. 'Grundsatz' also = principle (one English word for Principium/Grundsatz)."]],"philippi-s01-136":["If one wanted to judge him according to the portrayal that Lucretius has left us of him, we would act just as incorrectly as if we were to judge Haller's system of belief from his writings.",["Glaubenssystem: Glaube = belief. 'Lukrez' here, 'Lucretius' above, as printed."]],"philippi-s01-137":["Epicurus asserted that cognition through the senses alone is capable of a science and is certain.",["behauptete = asserted; Wissenschafft = science; Erkenntniß = cognition."]],"philippi-s01-138":["Plato, on the contrary, held only intellectual cognitions to be a science and truths.",[]],"philippi-s01-139":["All truth and all cognition came, according to him, from the ideas which the soul receives from an inner intuition of the deity and copying-off of this archetype.",["Anschauung = intuition; Urbild = archetype; Abkopirung = copying-off."]],"philippi-s01-140":["Both believed that between the intellectual and the sensitive there was an essential difference. The one was very mystical, obscure, enthusiastic; the other distinct, sensible, natural.",["'Dieser ... jener' = 'this one ... that one' (the latter/former); referents are ambiguous in context, and I render 'the one ... the other' accordingly, leaving the order to the reader. deutlich = distinct."]],"philippi-s01-141":["Aristotle said the understanding is a method of making use of sensibility; death is the end of all sensibility, he made the positive principle *Nihil est in intellectu quod non antea fuit in sensu*.",["Verstand = understanding. 'Tod ist das Ende aller Sinnlichkeit' sits oddly in the context; possibly corrupt transcript, kept as is."]],"philippi-s01-142":["Socrates, who sought to copy the archetype in himself, said that the human being has no greater object than the human being.",["Mensch = human being; Gegenstand = object."]],"philippi-s01-143":["He sought to teach the pupils a moderation in deciding and the doubt of postponement, and plucked at the proud dogmatists through ironies, but without his intention thereby gave occasion to scepticism. For some of his pupils took merely these ironies for their philosophy, attacked all dogmatic propositions partly with malicious mockery, partly with fallacies. Pyrrho, the originator of this sect, whose motto was the modest *non liquet*, had this not at all as his intention. He taught only that one should not accept and decide the propositions of philosophy right away but at first doubt them until one is infallibly convinced of them. Out of this his successors made a categorical doubt, that everything is uncertain.",["'kernen' not present here; 'nante' etc. none. Absicht = intention; Sätze = propositions; überzeugt = convinced (Überzeugung); Trugschlüsse = fallacies (lit. deceptive inferences).","'Scepticismo' dative/ablative Latin form kept as 'scepticism'. 'Aufschubs' = of postponement."]],"philippi-s01-144":["Strange as this sect also is and useless as it also seems to be; yet it has indirectly produced great use. For they have served as a whetstone of human cognition and reason, they called themselves *Academici*.",["Nutzen = use (benefit); Erkenntniß = cognition; Vernunft = reason. 'nanten' = 'nannten'.","Switch from singular 'sie' (sect) to plural kept as in the German."]],"philippi-s01-145":["It behaved neutrally, did not want to let itself be convinced at all by arguments that could really convince it, in short they were freethinkers. They demanded that their pupils should themselves doubt the propositions of their teachers.",["Singular 'Sie ... verhielt' then plural 'sie waren' as in the German."]],"philippi-s01-146":["The Romans made very much of this philosophy. In more recent times Hume has, however, written in a transfigured form in such a way of thinking.",["Denkungsart = way of thinking; 'jedoch' placed as in German after the verb."]],"philippi-s01-147":["The Cynic sect had as its object simplicity and innocence. They considered the natural human being.",["'Objeet' read as 'Object' (OCR): object. Betrachtung = consideration (betrachten = consider)."]],"philippi-s01-148":["Rousseau has endeavored to sketch the nature of the human being. Every original is unintelligible: even he who finds it does not understand it. So it goes with him also with some.",["'Roußeau' = Rousseau. The last sentence ('So geht es ihm auch bey einigen') is obscure and kept literal; 'ihm' referent uncertain."]],"philippi-s01-149":["The Stoics drew up their philosophy upon enthusiastic civic virtue. This gave them the air of the sublime.",["Bürgertugend = civic virtue; Miene = air."]],"philippi-s01-150":["What could be more charming to the Romans, whose deity was the republic and whose religion was civic virtue, than this philosophy.",["'kernen' not here; sentence ends with period though interrogative in form, as in the source."]],"philippi-s01-151":["The Romans begot no original philosophers, all sectarians. They borrowed not the theory but the practice from Aristotle and Plato. Their true philosophy made up their jurisprudence; and in this they certainly showed much understanding. Their law one should not hold for a matter of memory; it is capable of a mathematical method and still now serves as a rule in all states. In the XIth century their *Leges XII. Tabular.* fell into oblivion. (After the destruction of the Roman empire all sciences ceased.) But they revived after the Pandects were found at Amalfi, at Naples. To these one has perhaps to thank a great influence on human cognition.",["'kernen' read as 'keinen' (OCR), so 'no'. 'X I . Seculo' read as 'XIten Seculo'; 'Tahular.' read as 'Tabular.' (OCR). 'Zerstöhrung' = destruction.","Verstand = understanding; Wissenschaften = sciences; Erkenntniß = cognition. 'Seculo' kept as in the text. Pandecten = Pandects.","'Sie lebten' — plural subject (the laws/sciences) is ambiguous; kept as 'they'. 'Diesen' = 'to these' (the Pandects or the laws)."]],"philippi-s01-152":["As Christendom spread, Plato came to the fore. In his philosophy everything is ideal; the understanding, separated from all sensibility, is left to itself; and the propositions of religion are to be considered in just the same way. Hence the Platonic philosophy seemed to the Christians to be able to serve best for the explanation of the truths of religion. But one did not find what one sought; on that account Plato fell, and Aristotle, who in his philosophy combines the understanding with sensibility, came into his place. The enlightenment of religion, the work of the Reformation, was what lowered him. All sciences are in it combined with religion. As long as one had to think under constraint in the latter, wit and all the arts remained silent; once one got freedom and light in it, the sciences arose. The heathen religion was the occasion for the formation of most of the sciences and arts, especially the beautiful ones. For since it extended merely to sensibility, it could not but awaken the sciences of sensibility. Michelangelo admits that a single block, without head and arms, which had been carved by the Romans and was supposed to represent Hercules, was his school, where he learned his art. They surpassed the beauty of nature. An Apollo, a statue of ancient Rome that has been preserved down to now, cannot be considered without wonder at the skill of the artist. Their poetry, oratory, etc. etc. reached the highest perfection. Demosthenes, Homer, Cicero and Virgil will always remain the archetypes.",["OCR corrections made silently: 'get rennet' = getrennet (getrennt); 'Demosthenes' for 'DemostheneSj'; 'Bildsävde' read as Bildsäule (the OCR form is garbled; 'statue' is rendered).","'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility; 'Verstand' = understanding; 'Erklärung' = explanation; 'Vollkommenheit' = perfection; 'Wissenschafften' = sciences (Wissenschaft).","'in ihr' (\"in it\") in 'Alle Wissenschafften sind in ihr der Religion verbunden' is left as in the German; the antecedent is unclear (Religion or Reformation), and 'der Religion' may be dative with 'verbunden'.","'Witz' = wit; 'Urbilder' = archetypes; 'Erhellung' = enlightenment (literally 'illumination'). 'Aristotel' is the transcript's form of the name, rendered 'Aristotle'.","'Plato ... fell' (fiel) and 'came to the fore' (kam empor) kept literal."]],"philippi-s01-153":["Will not some of ours too serve posterity as archetypes and be preserved? No; for the present languages are subject to changeability. Gellert will become incomprehensible to posterity, like Hans Sachs and many of the newest French books, such as Montaigne.",["'Geliert' read as Gellert and 'Hans Sachse' as Hans Sachs (OCR/orthography). 'Urbilder' = archetypes.","'als Montaigne' is kept as 'such as Montaigne'; the German 'als' could also be read 'like', and the sense of the comparison is left open."]],"philippi-s01-154":["It is almost not probable that, besides the languages already dead, others should still die out; the most probable cause of this seems to be the unchangeability of religion, and that we will have no new holy books. Through the Reformation the sensible, which had crept in beside the Christian religion, was separated off; it received more of the spiritual, and Plato therefore became a favorite philosopher.",["'Un Veränderlichkeit' read as Unveränderlichkeit; 'kerne' read as keine.","'wahrscheinlich' = probable (Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability).","'sie erhielt' (\"it received\"): 'sie' is taken to refer to the religion; 'erhielt' could also mean 'maintained'; the German is ambiguous and the English keeps 'received'.","'das Sinnliche' = 'the sensible'; 'geistiges' = 'the spiritual' (Geist = mind; spirit)."]],"philippi-s01-155":["Finally, with the flooding in of the barbarians, the complete decline of all sciences and arts ensued. Meanwhile Europe gained a new form among them. Through the predisposition to the good that they had, and through the spirit of freedom that they brought with them to Europe. The Arabs, after they had overrun Africa, began first to explain Aristotle, who was most suited to their character, and to translate him into Arabic. To them one also ascribes the invention of universities. Samarkand in Tartary and Cordoba in Spain have been their most famous universities.",["'Durch die Anlage ... brachten.' is a sentence fragment in the German and is kept as such.","'unter denselben' (\"among them\") could also be read 'under them'; left ambiguous as far as English allows.","'Ueberschwemmung' and 'überschwemmet' (flooding / overrun) are the same root; 'überschwemmen' rendered 'overrun' in the second occurrence.","'Gestalt' = form (not Form)."]],"philippi-s01-156":["On the other hand those Latin and Greek authors will always remain archetypes, because they are written in unchangeable languages: for the unchangeability of religion, whose holy doctrines are written in the Greek and Latin language, draws after it the unchangeability of these languages.",["'Lehren' = doctrines (Lehre). 'Urbilder' = archetypes. 'Autores' kept as 'authors'.","'deren' (\"whose\") refers grammatically to religion."]],"philippi-s01-157":["With the revival of the sciences Aristotle came into esteem, in which he maintained himself until the papal authority received a blow through the Reformation, whereupon the esteem of Plato rose again, in which he maintains himself still now. Since the philosophy of the latter is merely speculative, consequently stands in a very close combination with religion, which is almost entirely spiritual, and this is for the human being the most interesting, everything else has received a certain form from it.",["'Wiederauf lebung' read as Wiederaufflebung (Wiederaufleben). 'Verbindung' = combination.","'deßelben' (\"the latter\") refers to Plato, as the nearest antecedent; 'bey welchem' / 'in welchem' (\"in which\") refers to esteem (Ansehen).","'diese' (\"this\") is ambiguous between religion and the combination; left as 'this'.","'Ansehen' = esteem in the first and third occurrences; 'authority' for 'päbstliche Ansehen'; one German word, two English renderings."]],"philippi-s01-158":["The more spiritual a religion is, the greater is the lack of beautiful arts, and everything therefore contains a sad and melancholy form. Religion has also brought the old languages, and with them at the same time the monuments of the great mind of the ancients, down to our times.",["'Geist' = spirit/mind; 'geistiger' rendered 'spiritual'; 'des großen Geists der Alten' rendered 'great mind'.","'enthält' (\"contains\") kept literal; 'Gestalt' = form."]],"philippi-s01-159":["The brooding understanding and corrupted taste maintained Aristotle for so long. Each explained him according to his aims and sought out for himself all sorts of subtleties, with which he attacked other propositions. Aristotle's philosophy was the most perfect and, as if instilled from heaven, infallible.",["'Aiistotel', 'Alistoteis' read as Aristotel / Aristoteles (OCR). 'Absichten' = aims; 'Sätze' = propositions; 'vollkommenste' = most perfect (Vollkommenheit = perfection).","'Subtilitäten' = subtleties. 'er' in 'womit er' is ambiguous (each / Aristotle); left as 'he'."]],"philippi-s01-160":["The actual cause of the long esteem of Aristotle was the despotic government of the Pope.",["'Ansehen' = esteem; 'Aristotels' rendered 'of Aristotle'."]],"philippi-s01-161":["The lazy and slavish swarm of monks, shut off from the world, sought their scholarship merely in the most utterly useless speculations, in which they found Aristotle very fruitful. With cunning they picked the subtleties out of him and sought in such a way to distinguish themselves from the rabble; for in religion they dared not do it, since the thunder of the Castel Sant'Angelo immediately pursued them; rather they had to seek to confirm the people in ignorance of religion and themselves also therein. Hence philosophy, and indeed only the Aristotelian, was the single means by which they could distinguish themselves from the people; and there they went on rooting about in their philosophy, which consisted of nothing but distinctions and ridiculous subtleties; while the ignorance of others threw seraphic names at them.",["'Engelsburg' rendered 'Castel Sant'Angelo' (literally 'Angel's Castle'); 'Sublilitäten' read as Subtilitäten.","'suchten' is plural with the singular subject 'Schwarm'; kept as 'sought'.","'Mittel' = means; 'Unwissenheit' = ignorance; 'Distinctionen' = distinctions.","'sich selbst darinn zu bestärken' kept as 'themselves also therein', the German being elliptical (to confirm themselves in it)."]],"philippi-s01-162":["Finally the dawning light of religion drove away these dark heads. The academies, the new societies of the learned, the free forms of the cities had meanwhile very much to do to cultivate again the devastated field of scholarship. Aristotle had, through his decisive and general pronouncements, which each also accepted as such, done uncommon harm; just as afterwards Leibniz and Wolff erred precisely thereby. For no one any more took pains to investigate certain propositions; for they stood already entirely settled in Aristotle, and there one dared not doubt. Thus Aristotle was the hindrance and the ruin of philosophy, yet he was so still more with regard to the beautiful sciences. Since the Scholastics abstracted from all sensibility and let the understanding always swarm about so; taste and all beautiful arts had to vanish. For nothing is more opposed to them. Taste demands evidentness to the eye and to see all propositions in sensible cases *in concreto*; the Scholastics wanted to know nothing of this and kept tossing themselves about only with their abstract propositions.",["OCR corrections silently made: 'allgememe' = allgemeine; 'Scholastüter' = Scholastiker; 'abstralnrten' = abstrahirten; 'Hessen' = liessen; 'niuste' = musste; 'Er der Geschmack' read as 'Der Geschmack' (the 'Er' taken as an OCR or transcript slip).","'Wolf' rendered 'Wolff'. 'so wie es nachher Leibnitz und Wolf eben dadurch versehen': 'versehen' taken as 'erred/failed'; doubtful reading.","'jeder so angenommen': 'each also accepted as such' - the subject and sense are compressed in the German and are kept so.","'Augenscheinlichkeit' = evidentness (literally 'appearance to the eye'); 'Sätze' = propositions; 'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility; 'Verstand' = understanding.","'ihnen' in 'nichts ist ihnen mehr entgegen' may refer to taste and the arts or to the Scholastics; the English 'them' keeps the ambiguity (plural: 'arts')."]],"philippi-s01-163":["The moderns have in some sciences no advantage over the ancients, e.g. in logic, metaphysics and morals, although their treatments in these are to be valued higher than the product that they actually delivered.",["'Logic Metaphysic und Moral' rendered 'logic, metaphysics and morals'. 'Neuern' = moderns; 'Alten' = ancients.","'Bearbeitungen' = treatments; 'Product' = product."]],"philippi-s01-164":["In natural science and jurisprudence the moderns have an actual advantage. The ancients mixed morals and natural right with one another, now one has not only distinguished the two, but also separated them from public law.",["'Naturkunde' = natural science (literally 'knowledge of nature'); 'Rechtswissenschaft' = jurisprudence (literally 'science of right'); 'Recht der Natur' = natural right; 'Staatsrecht' = public law (literally 'state right').","'Wissenschaft' elsewhere 'science'; here the compound is rendered 'jurisprudence'.","'wirklichen' = actual (Wirklichkeit = actuality)."]],"philippi-s01-165":["The moderns have an essential advantage over the others. One now looks at the philosophical sciences with a quite different eye. In metaphysics one now studies not dogmatic propositions as formerly, but one's pure understanding and its laws. In logic one has a quite different method of investigation; it is that in which one studies one's own subject, the laws of the understanding as well as of the sensibility. The method of the modern philosophers is for the most part skeptical, whereas the ancients so greatly affected the dogmatic. But what has contributed the most to the improvement of philosophy? That one has applied oneself with great earnestness to the doctrine of nature, and in this one has also taken a great step ahead of the ancients. The cause that physics has so increased is that one has consulted experiences and investigated nature through experiments. The ancients neglected these interesting sciences. They created for themselves, through their fantastical understanding, ideal worlds, from which they got nothing, and did not want to get to know the one in which they were. It is wondrous that the Greeks did not esteem the observations and experiences of nature. Their astonishing inconstancy was to blame for it. They did not even have a geography. (Meanwhile they were excellent as regards the doctrine and the classification of concepts.)",["OCR 'wir' read as 'wie'. 'Naturlehre' = doctrine of nature (Lehre = doctrine); 'Physik' = physics.","'Sätze' = propositions; 'Subject' = subject; 'Erfahrungen' (plural) = experiences; 'Versuche' = experiments.","'diejenige' (feminine) is left as 'the one', its referent unstated as in the German."]],"philippi-s01-166":["Galilei began to elevate the doctrine of nature, as he investigated and recorded the laws of gravity. As one saw what use the experiments created, one continued them zealously, and still now they are the objects of the endeavors of whole learned societies.",["'Naturlehre' = doctrine of nature; 'Gegenstände' = objects (Gegenstand).","'Schwere' rendered 'gravity'; 'Nutzen' = use."]],"philippi-s01-167":["Among the fortunate incidents that have brought advantage to the doctrine of nature belong the inventions pertaining to it, of the microscopes and telescopes, of the barometer and the air pump.",["'Vorfälle' = incidents (not the glossary's Begebenheit/Ereignis). 'dahin einschlagenden' rendered 'pertaining to it'."]],"philippi-s01-168":["As regards the method of philosophy, Wolff is the one to whom one must attribute a preeminent merit in this. One should weigh his works if one wants to form a concept of the extensive understanding of this man. The distinctness, the exactness of the explanations, the order of the proofs, all of this which he introduced into philosophy, makes Wolff's name worthy of remembrance; and although one now seeks to bring everything again into confusion already, in that one holds it to be very burdensome to subject oneself to such a compelling method, still the German heads are already so accustomed to it that this way of teaching will not easily be extinguished; and this gives hope that in Germany sooner than elsewhere new inventions will be made in philosophy.",["'Deutlichkeit' = distinctness; 'Erklärungen' = explanations (also 'definitions' in the glossary); 'Beweise' = proofs; 'Begriff' = concept.","'Lehrart' rendered 'way of teaching'; 'Verstande' = understanding."]],"philippi-s01-169":["Meanwhile he has done reason a great disadvantage by deciding everything dogmatically, defining everything and assuming it as undoubted. He believed that philosophy is capable of a mathematical method and also actually introduced it.",["OCR 'führe te' read as 'führte'. 'Vernunfft' = reason."]],"philippi-s01-170":["Locke is the one who has chiefly labored to invent genetically the origin of all human cognition and the limitations of it. He philosophizes subjectively.",["OCR 'Erkennt niß' and 'phüosophirt' corrected. 'Erkenntniß' = cognition; 'Schranken' = limitations (Schranke); 'erfinden' rendered 'invent'."]],"philippi-s01-171":["Crusius has an antiphilosophical method that ruins all philosophy at the ground. He gives things as subjective laws that are often only effects of the understanding and not laws. He has nothing but figments of the brain. He throws away all means of examination.",["'zu Grunde richtet' is an idiom (ruins); 'Grund' is visible in the German, rendered 'at the ground'.","'Wirkungen' = effects; 'Mittel' = means; 'Prüfung' = examination; 'Hirngespinste' = figments of the brain."]],"philippi-s01-172":["On the history of logic",["Heading; continues in the next passage ('insbesondere')."]],"philippi-s01-173":["in particular.",[]],"philippi-s01-174":["Logic is a far later part of philosophy, because it is a critique of the human understanding, although it is now taught first at academies. It is more a technical means to name the procedure of the understanding with technical terms. It serves to bring our concepts under certain genera and to indicate the errors where they have been committed. It would have arisen even later if one had laid at the ground in philosophy actual inventions and not figments of the brain. But one has grounded logic on the common use of the understanding. The oldest logics are not so much dogmatic disciplines as rather individual general theorems. The logic of the Pythagorean school was nothing other than a mysticism to the disadvantage of sensibility, in which, according to the judgment of it and of the Platonists, no truth but merely illusion is to be found, who explained all concepts intellectually, and regarded the whole world as a hieroglyphic book that contained the signs for the concepts that were in the understanding of man: whereas through the mere understanding we cognize only general properties of things. On these followed the logic of Epicurus which was merely negative and consisted of few propositions. Its final end was to protect human reason from errors and confusion.",["OCR corrected: 'bennen' read as 'benennen', 'wirkliehe' as 'wirkliche', 'Piatoniker' as 'Platoniker'.","Preserved: the relative clauses 'bey welcher ... sey' and 'die ... erklärten' have uncertain antecedents (school/Platonists); the German is left as loose as it stands.","'Gattungen' = genera (Gattung); 'Lehrsätze' = theorems; 'Eigenschaften' = properties (also glossary's Beschaffenheit); 'Endzweck' = final end; 'Kunstwörtern' = technical terms; 'Erkennen' = cognize.","'Gebrauch' = use; 'Schein' = illusion; 'Critic' = critique."]],"philippi-s01-175":["In the method of making use of reason is written",["Doubtful: 'ist geschrieben' is odd and may be a transcript slip; rendered as it stands. The passage breaks off, continuing in the next."]],"philippi-s01-176":["1. the mystical logic which was made by Euclid of Megara on account of sorceries. He taught to prove and to refute two opposed propositions in the same way. From this there arose a kind of sham philosophers, the dialecticians, great disputation-heroes.",["'Affterphilosophen' rendered 'sham philosophers'; 'Art' = kind; 'Sätze' = propositions; 'zween' = two.","'Disputirhelden' rendered literally 'disputation-heroes'."]],"philippi-s01-177":["The Greek people was delighted by nothing more than when it saw two scholars quarreling before it. Hence many sought the skill and cunning to give everything an illusion, and with these weapons to attack the other.",["'Schein' = illusion; the phrase 'einen Schein geben' could also be rendered 'give a semblance'."]],"philippi-s01-178":["The dark times of the papacy were also full of such dialecticians. Since their logic was nothing but an *organon* of talkativeness, to be able to ensnare the other through subtle captious questions.",["OCR 'Maren' read as 'waren'. The 'Da'-clause is left as the grammatically incomplete sentence it is ('Since/As').","'Organon' italicized as a foreign word."]],"philippi-s01-179":["The second logic of antiquity is the dogmatic one a. the *Canones* of Epicurus. This one presented the doctrines that are grounded on",["Breaks off mid-sentence; continues in -180. 'Canones' kept in Latin, italicized."]],"philippi-s01-180":["experience. Naturally such a logic had to, through the",["Fragment of a sentence continued in -181 and -182."]],"philippi-s01-181":["confirmation of the cognitions, through sensible cases, to the understanding",["Fragment; 'Erkenntnisse' = cognitions; 'sinnliche' = sensible. The verb ('schaffen') comes in the next passage."]],"philippi-s01-182":["create great use. Considered at bottom, however, it was only an",["'Im Grunde betrachtet' rendered 'Considered at bottom' (Grund visible). Breaks off; continues in -183."]],"philippi-s01-183":["*organon* of physics. b. Aristotle sketched how one can assess not only the cognition of experiences but also that of the pure understanding.",["OCR 'l>.' read as 'b.' (following the 'a.' of -179). 'beurtheilen' = assess (glossary: assess; judge); 'Erkenntniß' = cognition; 'rein' = pure."]],"philippi-s01-184":["But he fell into nothing but subtleties in such matters that were entirely",["Breaks off; continues in -185 ('bekannt waren')."]],"philippi-s01-185":["known.",["'bekannt' rendered literally as 'known'."]],"philippi-s01-186":["The Academics departed from the doctrine of Plato. Among them arose the sect of the Skeptics who were, as it were, *Antilogici*, contradictors. Instead of giving a logic or a science for the cognition of reason, they said no cognition at all was to be attained. Meanwhile they served to hold back the pride of the deciding and chattering dogmatists through their *non liquet*. Instead of this modest doubt by which they as it were plucked the dogmatists by the sleeve, they afterwards adopted a dogmatic doubting, which is a non-thing.",["OCR corrected: 'lind' read as 'und'. 'Wiedersprecher' = contradictors (the German 'Wieder-' spelling normalized).","Preserved: 'Wissenschafft zur Erkenntniß der Vernunft' is ambiguous ('cognition of reason' / 'cognition by reason').","'Unding' rendered literally 'non-thing' (Ding = thing). 'Antilogici' and 'non liquet' are Kant's Latin."]],"philippi-s01-187":["The Scholastics showed in what follows, in regard to the dogmatists, a likeness and made, through useless whims of Aristotle, logic useless.",["OCR 'Aelmlichkeit' read as 'Aehnlichkeit'.","Ambiguity: 'des Aristotels Logik' may be read 'Aristotle's logic' (the object) or 'whims of Aristotle' with 'Logik' the object; the English keeps the German order but takes the second reading, which cannot be fully preserved."]],"philippi-s01-188":["They believed that through subtle distinctions they would procure great use for religion. Disputing was their chief work. Therefore one can never get on better than when one diligently distinguishes and thereby as it were throws dust in the other's eyes so that he cannot see the truth.",["'Distinctionen' = distinctions; 'Nutzen' = use. 'distinguiret' rendered 'distinguishes'."]],"philippi-s01-189":["In the Reformation the minds were very prepared for true cognition.",["'Gemüther' = minds (Gemüt); 'Erkenntniß' = cognition."]],"philippi-s01-190":["Before this, however, Petrus Ramus in Paris had already begun to declare war on Aristotle. He posted a public writing that he wanted to refute Aristotle, in whatever propositions it might be. Thereby he lifted that blind and servile tyranny and awakened the minds from their somnolence. He wrote a logic whose first part treated *de inventione* and the other *de iudicio*. (One says proverbially, he lacks the *secunda Petri*; that is, the *judicio*.)",["OCR corrected: 'ni' read as 'in', 'wekte' as 'weckte'. 'hob' (aufheben) rendered literally 'lifted' (could also be 'removed'). 'Schlafsucht' = somnolence.","'Sätze' = propositions; 'Gemüther' = minds; 'Judicio' kept as Latin and italicized."]],"philippi-s01-191":["Melanchthon improved logic. Malebranche has through his *Recherches de la Vérité* established much use. All these doctrines of reason served only to abolish the empty knowledge, and to drive away the dazzling shows.",["Garbled OCR: 'Logü-c' read as 'Logic'; 'Vernunft -leinen' read as 'Vernunftlehren' (doctrines of reason), a conjecture; 'Blendwelke' read as 'Blendwerke'.","'Wissen' = knowledge; 'Nutzen' = use; 'Blendwerke' rendered literally 'dazzling shows'."]],"philippi-s01-192":["The following have made an essential step therein:",["Breaks off at a colon; the continuation is in the next passage. 'Folgende' rendered literally."]],"philippi-s01-193":["Leibniz and Wolf not so much through new laws and discovery of auxiliary means for coming to truth as rather through the introduction of distinctness and order. He leaves no concept unexplained; he admits no propositions that are indeed universally known but indemonstrable, and seeks to derive everything from one principle, the *princ. contr.* However, he set it up too high. It is not possible to explain everything and to give correct proofs of everything. Meanwhile Wolf demands it. And certainly it is desirable that he has sketched out for philosophy so great a plan and its highest perfection, even if one cannot reach it. It is good that one should be careful to bring all things to the greatest distinctness That he accepts only one proposition, and demands to prove everything from it, is also good; the human being can never be deceived more than when he accepts something as a principle; that he also proves known propositions is in accordance with thoroughness; and one has no ground to become indignant about it, like Cicero, who, when he heard someone speak of the excellence of virtue, went out and said: *Stulte quis unquam vituperavit.*",["The passage begins mid-sentence (the subject and verb are in the preceding passage); translated as it stands. A stray 'I' after 'der' is dropped as an OCR/apparatus mark.","Deutlichkeit = distinctness (deutlich = distinct); Begriff = concept; Grundsatz = principle, Satz = proposition; Beweis = proof; Vollkommenheit = perfection; erklären rendered 'explain' (Erklärung = explanation; definition).","'durehgehends bekannt aber indem onstrable' read as 'durchgehends bekannt aber indemonstrable'; 'princ. contr.' left abbreviated, as in the source; 'vnquam' read as 'unquam'; 'un< 1 sprach' read as 'und sprach'.","No full stop after 'Deutlichkeit zu bringen' in the source; kept. 'Er' in 'Er läßt' etc. is left unresolved (Wolf is presumably meant, but the text does not say)."]],"philippi-s01-194":["The demand of the mathematical method is not the most unreasonable. For although the object is different from mathematics, and the understanding also proceeds here quite differently than in philosophy, it is still good if one reaches even only a part; just as in morals one has the most perfect morality as end. It accustoms heads to taste for distinctness, order and subordination.",["Object = object (Latinate Object, as printed); Zweck = end; vollkommen = perfect.","'Sie' (It) could refer to the demand or to the method; left as ambiguous as the German."]],"philippi-s01-195":["Wolf teaches indeed how to dispose; how one should send the principles ahead and let the explanations follow etc. etc. The Germans will not forget this thoroughness, and surpass other nations, who in their philosophies like to discourse.",["disponiren rendered 'dispose' (to arrange); Erklärung = explanation (or definition, per the glossary).","'discuriren' read as 'discurriren', rendered 'discourse'.","The final clause is syntactically loose in German; the grammatical connection of 'übertreffen' to its objects is kept as it stands."]],"philippi-s01-196":["Lock has taken the most essential step to pave ways for the understanding. He has given entirely new criteria. He philosophizes subjectively, whereas Wolf and all before him philosophized objectively. He has investigated the *genesin*, the descent and the origin of concepts. His logic is not dogmatic, but critical. Wolf asks: what is a spirit? Lock: where does the idea of spirit in my soul come from? It has never seen a spirit; whence come these thoughts?",["OCR corrections: 'WTege' = Wege; 'subjeetiue' = subjectiue; 'objectiue philosophirtcn' = philosophirten; 'allerwesenthchsten' = allerwesentlichsten.","Geist rendered 'spirit' (glossary: mind; spirit). 'Sie' (It) refers grammatically to Seele (soul); 'Genesin' is the Latin/Greek accusative, kept in italics.","Begriff = concept; Idee = idea; Criteria = criteria."]],"philippi-s01-197":["Lambert's Organon, a work that has many merits regarding human cognition. It is a fault among the scholars, in that they prescribe the conditions under which a cognition is perfect; thus they believe they have given means to attain perfection. All practical logics are constituted thus. They are tautological and instead of giving means to the resolution of the questions, they give the same questions again.",["'Erkennt -niß' rejoined as Erkenntniß = cognition; Bedingung = condition; Mittel = means; Vollkommenheit = perfection; vollkommen = perfect.","Auflösung rendered 'resolution'; 'tavtologisch' = tautologisch."]],"philippi-s01-198":["But no other way is feasible in logic either. The exercise of the rules cannot be taught, for I can again do it only through rules.",["'geht nicht an' rendered 'is not feasible' / 'cannot be'; the German is idiomatic and slightly loose. Ausübung = exercise."]],"philippi-s01-199":["Through attention logic will become serviceable for *dijudication*.",["OCR 'Diiudication' read as 'Dijudication' (Latin dijudicatio); kept as a foreign word in italics. The reading is conjectural."]],"philippi-s02-001":["*Tractatio ipsa Logice s.* Considerations.",["Latin kept as printed ('Logice'); 's.' is presumably 'sive', not expanded in the text. Betrachtung = consideration."]],"philippi-s02-002":["1. We do not treat in logic of the origin of cognition, whether it comes from the senses or not, as Epicurus and Aristotle asserted the former, or according to Plato and Pythagoras from pure reason.",["'Wh-' read as 'Wir'. behaupten = assert; rein = pure; Vernunft = reason; Erkenntniß = cognition.","'das erstere behaupteten' / 'oder ... aus der reinen Vernunft': the second verb is elided in the German and left so in English."]],"philippi-s02-003":["2. Logic will treat of the laws of the understanding, not of those according to which one proceeds, but of those according to which one should proceed. Thus not subjective but objective laws of reason, that is such to which every finite reason is subject, will be the object of logical considerations. For we consider here the human understanding in general. Thus here there cannot be treated subjective laws, which have only a private validity, and depend on the nature of each subject; those belong in psychology; but objective ones, which have a universal validity. We trace the procedure of reason in general; just as in morals there are not investigated the rules according to which one acts, but those according to which one should act. Meanwhile it belongs to true logic that the rules be investigated according to which we actually proceed. Helvetius has begun to do this.",["OCR corrections: 'verfähret' kept as verfahret; 'subjeetiue/objeetiue' = subjectiue/objectiue; 'weiden' not occurring here; 'dependiren' rendered 'depend'; 'Heluetius' = Helvetius.","Gesetz = law; Gültigkeit = validity; Gegenstand = object; Betrachtung = consideration; betrachten = consider; wirklich = actually."]],"philippi-s02-004":["3. In logic we consider not the determinations of things and the relations of things, but only the determinations of concepts and their relations.",["Sache and Ding would both be 'thing'; here Sache is printed. Bestimmung = determination; Verhältniß = relation."]],"philippi-s02-005":["Since we now consider in logic the relations and determinations only of concepts without application of these to actual objects; so we see that logic can be applied to all sciences and extends over all cognitions. Were logic to have an object of things, a science that has another object would not belong under it. But now, since it has no actual thing as object, it is a general science. Just as arithmetic has no real object, but teaches only the concepts of numbers and their relations, and for that reason can be applied to all magnitudes, may they occur in whichever science they like. Logic is accordingly of great usefulness.",["'erstickt' is an OCR misreading, taken as 'erstreckt' (extends); the original reading is uncertain.","Gegenstand and Object both rendered 'object'; Wirkliche/wirklich = actual; reell = real; Wissenschaft = science; Größe = magnitude; Zahl = number.","The 'Weil ... so' construction and the 'Just as' sentence are kept as in the German; the latter has no main verb of its own beyond 'lehret ... läßt'."]],"philippi-s02-006":["If we cognize many things, we cognize little in them; if we cognize few things, we cognize much in them.",["erkennen = cognize; 'an ihnen' rendered 'in them'."]],"philippi-s02-007":["It is a particular weakness of our understanding that what we gain at one end we lose again at the other.",["besonder = particular; Verstand = understanding."]],"philippi-s03-001":["On learned cognition in general.",[]],"philippi-s03-002":["In many things we cognize little, but in few we can cognize much. Just as from the Pico on Tenerife one sees very many objects but only quite obscurely, and on the contrary, if one takes only one object before oneself, one cognizes it very distinctly.",["dunkel = obscure; deutlich = distinct; Gegenstand = object. 'Teneriffa' given in modern spelling."]],"philippi-s03-003":["*ad* §10",[]],"philippi-s03-004":["The author strives in vain to explain the representation. That which I want to explain, of that I must have a representation. If I wanted therefore to explain a representation, I would have to have a representation of the representation, or I would have to have a representation that is no representation. I can have a representation only of cognitions. Cognition is something objective, the subjective in it is sensation. E.g. of the sweet and the sour we have no cognition, but only a representation that something must be there, by which we are so affected.",["'niüste' read as 'müste'. Z. E. = e.g. Vorstellung = representation; Erkenntniß = cognition; Empfindung = sensation.","'das subjective dabey' rendered 'the subjective in it' ('dabey' = with/in connection with it); 'afficirt' = affected."]],"philippi-s03-005":["Certain things represent something, but of us it is said, we represent something to ourselves.",["The German plays on 'vorstellen' and 'sich etwas vorstellen'; kept literally. 'Gewiße' = certain."]],"philippi-s03-006":["There are certain means of cognizing something. To recognize these means again is impossible.",["'wieder zu erkennen' rendered 'recognize ... again' (erkennen = cognize; recognize, per the glossary); it could also be 'cognize again'. Mittel = means."]],"philippi-s03-007":["Logic teaches only how we ought to employ representations and concepts of which we are already conscious. The origin of the same is however not presented in it.",["'mcht' read as 'nicht'. verwenden rendered 'employ'; bewust = conscious (Bewußtsein = consciousness); Ursprung = origin."]],"philippi-s03-008":["Consciousness is as it were an inner intuition.",["Anschauung = intuition."]],"philippi-s03-009":["The sum of the representations of which we are not conscious is, with regard to the others, the greatest and most powerful.",["Summe rendered 'sum'; 'in Ansehung der anderen' = 'with regard to the others'. 'so wir uns nicht bewust seyn' is a relative clause with 'so'."]],"philippi-s04-001":["§12",[]],"philippi-s04-002":["In every cognition we will consider",["'weiden' read as 'werden'. The sentence continues in the next items (1. matter, 2. form)."]],"philippi-s04-003":["1. the matter",["'cüe' read as 'die'. Materie = matter."]],"philippi-s04-004":["2. the form. Such a division is very suitable to the understanding. It lies deeply grounded in the understanding and one can set many propositions in a great light through such a distinction.",["Form = form; Eintheilung = division; Satz = proposition."]],"philippi-s04-005":["The matter is that which is given. *Obiectum*. The form the relation which is found between that, *modus cognoscendi*.",["'zwischen dem' is left as a bare demonstrative ('between that'); the referent is not given in the text and is possibly elliptical or corrupt. 'Obiectum' kept in the source spelling (Latin)."]],"philippi-s04-006":["The matter is the object of cognition (*obiectum*) and cannot be altered.",["Gegenstand = object; verändern rendered 'alter'."]],"philippi-s04-007":["The form is the *modus cognoscendi*, e.g. let spirit be the matter of the representation. This object is always the same; but the form can be altered. The common man represents it to himself as a vapor, which the names [garbled] *spiritus* indicate, whereas the learned think a spirit quite differently.",["The Greek (or other) words in 'n:,~) äus|ios Spiritus' are too garbled in the OCR to read; rendered as '[garbled]' and the stray '|' not carried over. 'Spiritus' follows the source.","'ihn' refers to Geist (masculine); rendered 'it'. gemein = common; Geist rendered 'spirit' (glossary: mind; spirit). Z. E. = e.g."]],"philippi-s04-008":["If I consider a worm through the *Microscopium*, the form of this worm changes; the object remains the same. Philosophy differs from common understanding only according to form; the matter remains the same for both.",["Object = *Object* (Latinate); 'object' also renders Gegenstand elsewhere in these passages. Verstand = understanding; gemein = common.","'Microscopium' kept in Latin italics as in the source. The original has a full stop before 'die Materie' (lower-case); rendered with a semicolon, a minimal adjustment."]],"philippi-s04-009":["All philosophy concerns only the form, that we consider an object piece by piece and become more conscious of the matter contained therein.",["Object = *Object*; bewust (bewußt) = conscious. The 'daß' clause is left in apposition to 'form' as in the German."]],"philippi-s05-001":["§13",[]],"philippi-s05-002":["If we are conscious of a cognition, then the concept becomes clear. We are in no way unconscious of any cognitions. For as soon as we say we have a cognition, we must be conscious of it; and a cognition of which one is not conscious takes place not at all. If, therefore, there is talk of unconscious concepts, these are supposed to indicate as much as concepts of which one is not immediately conscious, but of which one can well become conscious through inferences.",["Erkenntniß = cognition; Begriff = concept; Schlüsse = inferences; 'findet gar nicht statt' rendered literally as 'takes place not at all'.","OCR 'unbe wüsten' read as 'unbewusten'."]],"philippi-s06-001":["§ 14",[]],"philippi-s06-002":["If we are conscious of a representation considered as a whole; then we are also always conscious of certain parts of this whole, though not of all. Every representation is very composite. In general the representations are either",["Theile = parts. 'zusammengesetzt' rendered 'composite'. The passage breaks off before the list that follows."]],"philippi-s06-003":["1. *compositae* or",[]],"philippi-s06-004":["2. *Simplices*. Most are *compositae*.",[]],"philippi-s06-005":["In that composite representation we are conscious either of the whole and also at the same time of the parts found therein, and then our concept is distinct; or we are conscious only of the whole without the parts, and then our concept is indistinct or confused.",["deutlich = distinct; undeutlich = indistinct; verworren = confused."]],"philippi-s06-006":["A simple cognition, since it has no parts, can therefore be clear and yet not distinct, nor confused either.",["einfach = simple; klar = clear. 'auch nicht verworren' is ambiguous in construction as in the German; kept as is."]],"philippi-s06-007":["The distinct therefore goes to composite representations.",["'gehet auf' rendered literally as 'goes to'."]],"philippi-s06-008":["I hear a noise, that is certainly a composite representation, but a confused one. If I come closer, and begin to distinguish the voices in the din and finally also the words of these voices, then that is a distinct representation.",["Lerm = Lärm, read as 'din'."]],"philippi-s06-009":["Distinct cognition is that in which there is order, indistinct that in which disorder prevails, i.e. where the part-representations are not clear. Order consists in this, where the manifold has its proper place. The simpler our representations are, the less confused they are. It can be distinct in what we intuit, if we represent each part of it clearly, distinct in representation and in thinking if I analyze what I think, a concept is required for distinctness.",["Mannichfaltige = manifold; anschauen = intuit; Deutlichkeit = distinctness; theil Vorstellungen = part-representations.","The last sentence is syntactically loose in the source (subject of 'Es kann deutlich seyn' unspecified); preserved. 'zergliedere' = analyze (dissect)."]],"philippi-s06-010":["Distinctness in sensibility is brought forth through the *synthesis*, in the understanding through the *analysis*.",["Synthesin / Analysin are Greek accusative forms in the source; rendered as *synthesis*, *analysis*. Sinnlichkeit = sensibility."]],"philippi-s06-011":["I obtain empirical distinctness through a continued attention to objects of the senses, the distinctness of understanding through an attention to the actions of my understanding.",["Gegenstände = objects. OCR 'am0' read as 'auf' (or 'an'); doubtful, translated as 'to'."]],"philippi-s06-012":["A rational cognition is that which agrees with reason, but a cognition of reason also at the same time arises from reason; it would therefore be better if our author had said, instead of rational, cognitions of reason.",["vernünftig = rational; Vernunfterkenntniß = cognition of reason; stimt = agrees. 'unser Autor' = Meier."]],"philippi-s06-013":["§ 15",[]],"philippi-s06-014":["The relation of ground and sequel of things does not belong in logic at all, but only that of concepts. In logic we will only cognize how one cognition contains the ground of another. If the soul is a spirit, then it cannot be destroyed through death. Here I do not consider the real of the spirit, but only the concept and the relation of it to the concept of indestructibility. If the soul ceases with death, then it ceases to be a spirit.",["Grund = ground; Folge = sequel; Sachen = things; Reelle = the real; Geist = spirit.","OCR 'Erkennt niß' rejoined. 'Erkennen' rendered 'cognize'."]],"philippi-s07-001":["§ 17 et 18",[]],"philippi-s07-002":["Rational cognition is not opposed here to the irrational; because then it might mean as much as cognition in accordance with reason, and in this sense the rational is not taken here, but rational cognition is here opposed to the historical, and indicates as much as cognition sprung from reason. But when a cognition can be regarded as sprung from reason is hard to say. If a cognition considers the particular from the universal; then it has sprung from reason. But if one draws the universal from the particular or also considers the particular by itself; then the cognition one attains thereby is historical. E.g. one sees that the cold in May is useful to the plants of the earth. That is a historical cognition. Thus one can learn even philosophy historically, if one makes the propositions of another known to oneself in order, without having insight into how these propositions are drawn from universal principles. Experimental physics is historical; for it goes only to individual cases. But as soon as one explains it through universal propositions, it becomes rational.",["vernünftig = rational; Vernunft = reason. OCR 'Yernünfftige', 'liier', 'ausderVernunfft', 'mochte' silently corrected (mochte read as 'möchte').","besondere = particular; allgemein = universal (German 'allgemein'); einzele = individual; Sätze = propositions; Grundsätze = principles; erklären = explain.","'Philosophie' = philosophy (Weltweisheit not used here). 'Erdgewächsen' rendered 'plants of the earth'."]],"philippi-s07-003":["Cognitions of reason are distinguished from the historical not according to matter, but only according to form.",["Vernunffterkenntniße = cognitions of reason."]],"philippi-s07-004":["A child learns universal morals in the catechism historically.",[]],"philippi-s07-005":["Cognitions that relate to universal things can become historical, if one judges them not *in universali ex abstracto* but *in particulari in concreto*; and conversely, an object of experience can still become a cognition of reason, if only the form is changed. Thus it has gone with physics.",["beurtheilen = judge (Beurtheilung = judgment); Gegenstand = object; Erfahrung = experience. OCR 'Erkennt niß' rejoined. Latin phrases kept in italics."]],"philippi-s07-006":["In all cognitions, if they are to be aesthetically perfect, 4 pieces are required, namely sensation, power of judgment, spirit and taste.",["Stücke = 'pieces'; Empfindung = sensation; Urteilskraft = power of judgment; Geist = spirit (glossary also allows 'mind'); Geschmak = taste; vollkommen = perfect."]],"philippi-s07-007":["§ 19",[]],"philippi-s07-008":["Cognition of understanding is distinguished from the sensible, so that sensible cognition is that which considers things insofar as they touch the senses; cognition of reason, however, goes to that which does not touch sensibility, but is universal and generated in the understanding. Sensible cognition therefore extends to individual, cognition of understanding to universal things. Here is a convenient place for the distinction of the science of the beautiful from the science of the understanding.",["Verstandeserkenntniß = cognition of understanding; Vernunffterkenntniß = cognition of reason (the source shifts terms mid-sentence; kept). sinnlich = sensible; Sinne = senses; Wissenschaft = science.","'rühren' rendered 'touch'; 'erstreckt sich' = extends."]],"philippi-s07-009":["The agreement of cognition with the subjective laws is the aesthetic, with the objective laws is the logical perfection.",["Gesetze = laws; Vollkommenheit = perfection; 'Uebereinstimmung' = agreement. The elliptical 'ist die ästhetische' is preserved, with 'perfection' only at the end, as in the German."]],"philippi-s07-010":["Besides the fact that a cognition agrees with the object, I can also still take care how the cognition affects the subject.",["Object = *Objecte*; afficirt = affects (Latinate form *afficiren*). 'acht geben' = 'take care' (literally 'give heed')."]],"philippi-s07-011":["The cognition agrees with the subject, if it puts all powers of the mind into an easy and free play and my cognition becomes at the same time strong and great.",["Gemüthskräfte = powers of the mind (Gemüth = mind, Kraft = power). Subiect = Subject."]],"philippi-s07-012":["The science which contains the first grounds of distinction of the beautiful is aesthetics. We have a natural power of distinction by which we distinguish whether something is beautiful or not. This is called judgment or critique.",["Unterscheidungsgründe = grounds of distinction; Unterscheidungskraft = power of distinction; Beurtheilung = judgment; Critick = critique; Aesthetick = aesthetics."]],"philippi-s07-013":["The critique which presents the rules of the judgment of the sensible is aesthetics. The critique which presents the rules of the judgment of cognitions of understanding is logic.",["Beurtheilung = judgment; Regeln = rules; 'des Sinnlichen' = 'of the sensible'; 'der Verstandes erkenntniße' = 'of cognitions of understanding' (the German article 'der' may be read as genitive plural or as a slip for 'des'; preserved)."]],"philippi-s07-014":["We have a twofold way of distinguishing things or our cognition has a twofold relation:",["Art = way; Verhältniß = relation; unterscheiden = distinguish."]],"philippi-s07-015":["1. we judge of the thing what it is.",["urtheilen = judge (v.)."]],"philippi-s07-016":["2. how the thing pleases. The former relates to the object, the other to the subject.",["Object = *Object*; Subject = *Subject*."]],"philippi-s07-017":["A scholar followed Homer in all the journeys described in the Odyssey according to the map, and found therein the greatest correctness and exactness. Here he had an object, without cognizing how it pleases.",["Gegenstand = object; 'Carte' = map; 'erkennen' = cognize."]],"philippi-s07-018":["In a cognition we consider either in general",["Passage continues in the next two items."]],"philippi-s07-019":["1. what the thing is, that is the constitution of it, or",["Beschaffenheit = constitution."]],"philippi-s07-020":["2. what relation this object has to our subject. That makes up the worth of the thing. The worth therefore consists in the relation of the thing to thinking beings. E.g. 1.) I see the moon, consider its body, investigate its magnitude, distance from us, motion. There I do not look to the relation of the moon to our subject. 2. I investigate its worth which it has for thinking beings. If there were no thinking being, it would have no worth, just as the jewel has no worth except when it comes into the possession of another.",["Object = *Object*; Größe = magnitude; Wesen = being; Werth = worth; Verhältniß = relation. 'Juvel' read as 'Juwel' (jewel).","'Z. E.' rendered 'E.g.'. The numbering '1.)' and '2.' is kept as in the source."]],"philippi-s07-021":["These two kinds of cognition and representations are essentially distinguished. Through the one we judge what the thing is, even if it does not relate to thinking beings or has no worth; but in the other I decide what the object is worth in relation to thinking beings. And through what do I decide that? What is the standard of worth? The feeling of pleasure and displeasure or the sensation. By this one judges whether an object is agreeable or disagreeable, beautiful or ugly, and that constitutes the worth.",["Erkenntnißarten = kinds (*Arten*) of cognition (*Erkenntniß*); Gegenstand rendered 'object' throughout (Object, in s07-023, also 'object').","'urtheilen' = judge (v.); 'Gefühl' = feeling; 'Empfindung' = sensation; 'gilt' rendered 'is worth' (literally 'counts/is valid'); 'Werth' = worth.","'Nach dieser richtet man' kept with 'one'; 'dieser' could refer to 'Gefühl' or 'Empfindung' (feminine both); left ambiguous ('this' → 'By this')."]],"philippi-s07-022":["But the constitutions of the objects are cognized through the understanding. This determines what is true or false, good or evil.",["Beschaffenheiten = constitutions (glossary: 'constitution; property'); Verstand = understanding; erkannt = cognized; bestimmt = determines.","'Dieser' (masc.) refers to Verstand."]],"philippi-s07-023":["We are therefore capable of essentially different cognitions 1. of certain cognitions that concern the constitutions and 2. of cognitions that concern the worth of the objects. If this is so, then we also have two entirely different sources from which we draw these cognitions: one from which we take all cognitions of the constitutions of things, and that is the understanding: another from which we take the cognitions of the worth of things, and that is, in a word, sensibility. Just as the understanding can cognize nothing of the worth, so sensibility can cognize nothing of the constitutions of the objects. The word sensibility signifies here as much as the feeling of pleasure and displeasure, and the worth, the liking or disliking of a thing. It is therefore no object, but, as has often been said, only the relation of the object to the subject.",["'Gegenstände' and 'Objecte'/'Object' both rendered 'object'.","'zwar' left untranslated-in-effect in 'We are therefore ... capable' (the sentence has no following 'aber'; construction preserved as it stands).","'fähig' = capable (cf. Fähigkeit = capacity); 'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility; 'bedeutet' = signifies.","'Er' (masc.) in the last sentence refers back to 'Werth'; kept as 'It'."]],"philippi-s08-001":["§22",[]],"philippi-s08-002":["If a cognition is so constituted that it agrees with general rules of the understanding, then it is logically perfect. If a cognition is so constituted that it agrees with general rules of taste or of pleasure and displeasure, then it is aesthetically perfect. Logical perfection is an agreement of the cognition with the object. The aesthetic, an agreement of the cognition with the feeling. The former is truth, the latter beauty. What pleases us, pleases us either",["Vollkommenheit = perfection; Geschmak = taste; 'Zusammenstimmung' = agreement; 'ästhetisch'/'aesthetische' both rendered 'aesthetic(ally)'.","OCR 'Erkenntmß', 'Zusammenstimmimg' silently corrected. Passage breaks off in mid-sentence ('entweder') and is continued in the next passages."]],"philippi-s08-003":["1. immediately, or",["unmittelbar = immediately."]],"philippi-s08-004":["2. mediately. Money, e.g., pleases a rational person mediately. It pleases him only as a means to what pleases.",["mittelbar = mediately; Mittel = means; 'Vernünfftigen' = rational person (substantivized adjective, from *vernünftig*)."]],"philippi-s08-005":["It is sad that what should please us immediately pleases us only mediately. Virtue, indeed the Godhead itself, pleases mortals only mediately. The former pleases merely because of its agreeable accompaniment, the latter because of its benefactions. Perhaps this is our whole case; and indeed it is.",["'Fall' rendered literally as 'case'; 'Gottheit' = Godhead; 'Jene'/'diese' = 'the former'/'the latter'."]],"philippi-s08-006":["A good dish pleases us 1. immediately because of the good taste 2. mediately as a means of health. A bitter medicine is immediately disagreeable, but mediately it pleases. The beautiful pleases immediately. Something pleases mediately insofar as it is a means to another end that pleases us. A flower piece can please mediately and immediately at once, if the flowers also have a usefulness.",["Zweck = end; 'Blumenstück' = flower piece (a painting or arrangement; German is not specific); Nutzbarkeit = usefulness."]],"philippi-s08-007":["What now pleases immediately pleases either one and not many others, or it pleases all universally. What pleases immediately only one has a private validity and is called agreeable: but what pleases universally has a universal validity and is called beautiful.",["Gültigkeit = validity (cf. gültig = valid); 'allgemein' rendered 'universal(ly)' here and 'general' in the 'Regeln'/'Sätze' contexts, where it modifies rules/propositions; the same German word, two English words.","'gefället' (sic) = 'pleases'."]],"philippi-s08-008":["Through the agreeable I express that something is an object merely of my pleasure, it may arouse pleasure or displeasure in others. How wrong it is, then, if one thinks that what is agreeable to us must also be agreeable to others. Would not someone who believes that the dish which tastes agreeable to him must taste just the same to all without exception be just as laughable as the Greenlanders who, when one talks to them of paradise, immediately ask whether there are also seals there, whose blubber is their most delicious food. The agreeable is therefore different in different subjects. And what is the cause? What is agreeable, gratifies. Gratification is an agreeable sensation; therefore the agreeable is a sensation. Since now sensation consists in the way and manner in which sensibility is modified by an object: but sensibility is in a certain way almost different in all human beings; so their sensation is also different and consequently also the agreeable, because it is sensation. From this we conclude: what pleases in sensation gratifies, what gratifies has a private validity, and thus what pleases in sensation has a private validity.",["'Durch angenehm' = 'Through the agreeable' (adjective used as noun, but possibly 'through \"agreeable\"', i.e., the word mentioned); left as in the German's lack of quotation marks, rendered as noun.","'er mag ... erwecken' = 'it may arouse' (concessive, 'whether it arouses pleasure or displeasure'); kept literal and asyndetic.","'Art und Weise' = 'way and manner' (Art = way/manner in glossary); 'modificirt' = modified; 'Vergnügen' = gratification; 'vergnügt' = gratifies.","'folgern' (here 'folgen' in the text, 'folgern' read as OCR/orthographic variant: 'Hieraus folgen wir') = 'we conclude'.","'Thran' = blubber (train oil); 'Seehunde' = seals."]],"philippi-s08-009":["As through the agreeable I express that which pleases me alone, so through the beautiful I express that which pleases everyone universally. Agreeable had a private validity; beautiful has a universal validity. Agreeable is a sensation; beautiful is a judgment. Agreeable goes to the subject; beautiful, however, to the object. This latter is to be proved. The proof will follow below.",["'Beurtheilung' = judgment (glossary: Beurteilung = judgment); 'Beweis' = proof; 'hatte' (sic) kept as past tense 'had' though perhaps a slip for 'hat'.","'gehet auf' = 'goes to' (literal; 'is directed to' avoided)."]],"philippi-s08-010":["Judgments that relate to the subject can have different relations. Judgments that relate to the object have equal relations in the subject, though not of equal degree. The former consider the agreeable, the latter the beautiful, or what pleases universally. We have, to be sure, until now called without restriction what pleases universally beautiful; but now we want to distinguish it more precisely.",["Urtheile = judgments; Verhältnisse = relations; 'Grade' = degree; 'distinguiren' rendered 'distinguish'.","'gleiche Verhältnisse' = 'equal relations' ('gleich' could be 'same' or 'equal'; left as 'equal', parallel with 'gleichem Grade')."]],"philippi-s08-011":["What pleases universally is what necessarily pleases everyone. What pleases universally in such a way pleases either",["OCR 'wras noth wendiger weise' silently corrected to 'was nothwendiger Weise'. Breaks off before the list in the next passages."]],"philippi-s08-012":["1. according to general propositions of sensibility and is called beautiful; or",["Sätze = propositions; 'allgemein' here rendered 'general' (see earlier note)."]],"philippi-s08-013":["2. according to general propositions of the understanding and of reason, and is called good.",[]],"philippi-s08-014":["That which pleases universally according to general propositions of sensibility can only be seen into *a posteriori* through experience.",["'einsehen' = 'see into' (literal); *a posteriori* in Latin; Erfahrung = experience."]],"philippi-s08-015":["Only through experience can one, e.g., discover the food that pleases everyone or at least many. Of him who, instructed by much experience, knows how to choose what pleases all, one says that he has taste. Whoever, then, cannot hit upon what pleases everyone is without taste, and, if he wants to attain it, must pay attention to observations and experiences.",["Beobachtungen = observations; 'Erfahrungen' plural = experiences; 'Acht haben auf' = 'pay attention to'; 'Geschmack' = taste."]],"philippi-s08-016":["He who chooses only what pleases him, but not others, has no taste at all.",[]],"philippi-s08-017":["Taste is a judgment according to universally valid laws.",["allgemeingültigen = universally valid; Gesetzen = laws."]],"philippi-s08-018":["What pleases according to laws of sensibility is beautiful.",[]],"philippi-s08-019":["What pleases according to laws of the understanding and of reason is good. We have not only a sharing sensation (sympathy) but also a communicating inclination, and distrust our cognitions. Hence scholars always make their thoughts universally known. Other human beings are, as it were, controllers of our judgments.",["OCR 'eme' and 'Erkenntnißen' silently corrected ('eine', plural of Erkenntniß). 'theilnehmende Empfindung' = 'sharing sensation'; 'mittheilende Neigung' = 'communicating inclination'.","'Controleurs' = 'controllers' (French-derived, kept close to the German)."]],"philippi-s08-020":["Nature has given taste especially to sociable people. People without taste always have something stubborn about them and concern themselves only with their well-being, which, moreover, they often posit in things that are disgusting to others. The sociable know how to distinguish through sensation what pleases everyone, and that is true taste. How? do they distinguish it through sensation and not through experience? It is true, one cannot guess from one's sensation what pleases others; for subjects are different. Yet if one observes much experience, the relation of the object to the senses finally becomes so constituted that one contingently hits upon what pleases everyone. So this happens not properly through sensation, but through experience.",["OCR 'semer' → 'seiner', 'Verhält -niß' → 'Verhältniß' silently corrected.","'setzen' = posit (glossary); 'zufälliger weise' = 'contingently' (zufällig = contingent; could also be 'by chance').","'Wohlbefinden' = 'well-being'; 'störrisches' = 'stubborn'; 'Gesellige' = 'the sociable'."]],"philippi-s08-021":["It is of great importance to distinguish taste in objects of sensation from taste in objects of appearance. That which pleases me in appearance, does that please others too? This important question we affirm, and certainly not without ground.",["Erscheinung = appearance; 'bejahen' = affirm; Grund = ground; 'Wichtigkeit'/'wichtig' = importance/important.","'Gegenständen' = objects."]],"philippi-s08-022":["What pleases one in the appearance pleases all. For here sensation does not matter; in appearances things are not considered according to the matter of sensibility or of sensation, but only according to the form, that is, according to the way in which my subject would sensibly cognize the object, even if it caused no sensation in me. Sensation and appearance are distinguished as matter and form. The matter of sensibility is sensation, the form is appearance. A thing can please in the appearance, even if it is not an object of sensation. It then pleases on account of its form, that is, on account of the harmonious relations of all the sensible representations that I get of the thing and, taken together, call the object.",["Sache and Ding are both rendered \"thing\".","Gegenstand and Object are both rendered \"object\".","gefallen = \"please\"; Verhältniß = \"relation\" (not in the glossary).","betrachten = \"consider\"; erkennen = \"cognize\"."]],"philippi-s08-023":["Thus, for example, a regularly erected palace pleases the Spaniard walking about with a hungry stomach, although he gets nothing from it for his sensation.",["Sache/Ding distinctions do not arise here; \"Pallast\" is modernized to \"palace\"."]],"philippi-s08-024":["In music the melodic, or the sound of the tones, is the matter; the form of the same, however, consists in the harmonious alternation of these tones. As regards the matter or the sound, this instrument can be more agreeable to one, that to another: for here it depends on sensation, which is different in different subjects. But as regards the form of music, a concert that is harmonious must sound good to all. The matter of a flower is its smell, the form its shape.",["\"sejm\" read as \"sein\" (OCR).","Musik/Music both rendered \"music\"; Concert rendered \"concert\"."]],"philippi-s08-025":["Taste has universally valid laws, which are taken from the nature of sensibility. A priori, however, they cannot be pleased. We can criticize taste only according to rules a priori.",["\"Gesehmak\" read as \"Geschmack\" (OCR).","\"gefällt werden\" is odd as it stands (\"be pleased\"); the reading is doubtful and perhaps corrupt, and I have translated it literally.","\"A priori\" is placed first as in the German, so its attachment is left open; the second occurrence is italicized as Latin.","allgemein giltig = \"universally valid\"."]],"philippi-s08-026":["The norm contains the rules according to which something ought to happen; the norm of taste is encountered in actual works of taste, and is thus a model, not a precept.",["Norm = \"norm\"; Muster = \"model\"; Vorschrift = \"precept\"."]],"philippi-s08-027":["For the distinct insight into these important propositions we cite once more: The objects in the appearance are not considered subjectively, as they touch the subject with a feeling of pleasure or displeasure; but as they are cognized through the subject as objects of sensibility. If the objects of the appearance are thus cognized such that the impressions of them on our sensibility have an exact relation to one another and, taken together, make up a harmonious whole; then we call this harmonious whole, which we regard as the object itself, beautiful. But if between the impressions that an object makes on our sensibility there is no relation or only a weak one, so that, taken together, they form no whole or an imperfect one, then we say of the object that we understand under the *complexu* of the sensible impressions that it is not beautiful, and indeed in the first case less beautiful than in the other. There one has now analyzed the beautiful according to its essential concepts!",["\"subiectiue\" read as \"subjectively\"; \"deutlichem\" as \"deutlicher\"; \"aus machen\" as \"ausmachen\" (OCR/old spelling).","*complexu* is Latin (ablative) in the German text, kept in italics.","Satz = \"proposition\"; Gegenstand and Object both \"object\"; zerlegen = \"analyze\"."]],"philippi-s08-028":["But does one constantly judge of the beautiful in the described way? We would do so, if charm, that is, the taking part in it and the appropriation of it, did not hinder us. This makes it that we commonly prefer what belongs to us, even though it is less beautiful, to what has far more beauties but does not belong to us.",["Reiz = \"charm\"; Antheilnehmung = \"taking part\"; Zueignung = \"appropriation\" (none in the glossary).","urtheilen = \"judge\" (v.)."]],"philippi-s08-029":["Then we assess the beautiful mixed with sensation. Thus it can come about that a garden that belongs to me pleases me better than a stranger's garden, which is nevertheless far more beautiful than my own. For what is lacking in beauty in my own, the charm or the taking part in it, that I possess it, replaces. There are cases of assessing the beautiful without sensation.",["The text reads \"Denn \\\\a> dem Meinigen\"; the garbled characters are too uncertain to read (perhaps \"was\"), and I have translated what can be made out.","beurteilen = \"assess\"; Theilnehmung = \"taking part\"."]],"philippi-s08-030":["Winkelmann shows that the masculine build of the body is much more beautiful than the feminine. Charm makes it that one prefers the latter to the former.",["\"Winkelmann\" kept as printed. \"diesen jenem\" rendered \"the latter ... the former\"."]],"philippi-s08-031":["A prospect of a beautiful region from my window I hold to be more beautiful than the far more beautiful region that another has. For what mine lacks in beauty the charm that I possess it replaces. If I therefore assess the beautiful mixed with charm, then what belongs to me can indeed appear to me more beautiful than other, actually more beautiful things.",["\"ersetzt den Reiz\" is as printed: grammatically Reiz is the object, though the sense is that charm is the subject; left as it stands.","wirklich = \"actually\"; Sache = \"thing\"."]],"philippi-s08-032":["The judgments of the beautiful go to the object, therefore they have a universal validity. In judgments about an object two cannot contradict one another. What one judges of the object has a universal validity. If, therefore, one says, this thing is beautiful; the other, it is ugly: then at least one must have said falsely.",["Gültigkeit = \"validity\"; allgemein = \"universal\"; widersprechen = \"contradict\"."]],"philippi-s08-033":["The judgments of the agreeable go to the subject and have a private validity. Here two can often contradict one another. If one says, this food tastes only agreeable, and the other, it is disgusting to me, both can be right.",["\"nur angenehm\" is as printed and left as it stands."]],"philippi-s08-034":["Of the first judgments, which have a universal validity, it is to be noted that they have their universality only according to rules of sensibility and in the appearance, not in concepts.",["\"erstem\" read as \"ersten\" (OCR)."]],"philippi-s08-035":["We want to sketch quite briefly, in interconnection, what we have said so far about the beautiful and the agreeable.",["Zusammenhang = \"interconnection\"."]],"philippi-s08-036":["We have two capacities, power of cognition and feeling. Through the former we learn what a thing is, through the other, how a thing pleases or does not please, that is, how the thing relates to our feeling of pleasure or displeasure.",["Fähigkeit = \"capacity\"; Erkenntnißkrafft = \"power of cognition\" (Kraft = \"power\"); \"sieh\" read as \"sich\" (OCR).","Lust/Unlust = \"pleasure\"/\"displeasure\"."]],"philippi-s08-037":["To have pleasure and displeasure is something other than to cognize what a thing is.",[]],"philippi-s08-038":["If the judgment is so constituted that it goes merely to one < or is valid only for one, then the judgment does not go to the object; but if something pleases all, then it must go to the object. Fashions are thus pleasing not to objects but to subjects. The uncovering of the head is with us a sign of politeness, with the Turks it is the uncovering of the feet. The Circassian women, because they please all nations, must please as objects, their beauty must lie in themselves, in their object. Of what pleases only one, the ground of the satisfaction lies in the subject; of what pleases all, the ground of the satisfaction must lie in the object. Our adjudication and distinction of the thing according to the relation to our pleasure and displeasure takes place",["The text is garbled in places: \"< m 1er nur vor einen gilt\" read as \"oder nur vor einen gilt\" (the \"<\" is kept); \"hegen\" as \"liegen\"; \"Tseherkessinnen\" as \"Tscherkessinnen\"; \"Lmsere Düudication\" as \"Unsere Dijudication\" (doubtful).","\"Hauptentblössen\" rendered literally as \"uncovering of the head\".","Wohlgefallen = \"satisfaction\"; Grund = \"ground\"; the passage breaks off after \"geschieht\" and is left unfinished.","\"Moden ... Objecten\": Object rendered \"object\", as is Gegenstand."]],"philippi-s08-039":["1. from subjective grounds, which are valid only for one",["\"subiectiven\" read as \"subjectiven\"."]],"philippi-s08-040":["2. from objective grounds, which are valid for all.",[]],"philippi-s08-041":["Sensibility is the way in which an object pleases a subject. If we assess a thing according to the relations of pleasure and displeasure from subjective grounds; then we distinguish the agreeable and the disagreeable, the gratifying and the disgusting. But if we assess the relation of a thing to our pleasure and displeasure from objective grounds; then we distinguish the beautiful from the not-beautiful. What is beautiful pleases in the thing itself, and is agreeable in the thing itself. But what pleases in the universal relation I do not consider as it is agreeable. If I say that something pleases, then what pleases there is the matter, but the universality the form.",["\"\\\\ ir\" read as \"wir\"; \"eckelhaffte\" as \"ekelhafte\" (OCR/old spelling).","Art = \"way\"; beurteilen = \"assess\"; Sache = \"thing\"; Materie/Form = \"matter\"/\"form\"."]],"philippi-s08-042":["What pleases universally pleases in a twofold way: 1. according to laws of sensibility; that is beautiful and pleases in the appearance; 2. according to laws of the understanding; that is good and pleases in concepts.",["Art = \"way\"; Gesetz = \"law\"; Verstand = \"understanding\"."]],"philippi-s08-043":["Virtue cannot fall into the senses, and pleases only in concepts. Virtuous actions can please in the appearance. Virtue pleases, but does not gratify. Therein consists the whole fall of mortals: what pleases them in concepts, that does not gratify them in sensibility; and what pleases them in sensibility, that does not please them in concepts. The fashion pleases the Italians in sensibility, that ladies, wherever they drive, take their *Cavaliere Servente* along. But whom does it please in concepts",["The passage breaks off after \"Begriffen\" with a stray \"\\\\\" (OCR), omitted; the final sentence is left without a question mark.","*Cavaliere Servente* is Italian, italicized as foreign; Frauenzimmer rendered \"ladies\".","Sinne = \"senses\"; \"wol\" = \"wohl\", rendered \"whom ... then\" omitted; left as \"But whom does it please\"."]],"philippi-s08-044":["With sensibility the matter is that which pleases, or sensation; the form, how this or that thing pleases, or the appearance.",["Bey der Sinnlichkeit: 'with' chosen for 'bey'; the German could also be read 'in the case of'.","Materie = matter; Form = form; Empfindung = sensation; Erscheinung = appearance."]],"philippi-s08-045":["That in the beautiful which pleases in relation to sensation is called charm. Charm is thus that which sensation is, namely the way my organs are touched by the object: it will accordingly also have in common with sensation that it is not universally valid. Charm must not be mixed up with the essential beauty that consists solely in the form of the object, otherwise one can often find oneself deceived in the judgment of the beautiful. Thus one deceives oneself in the judgment of the beauty of a woman through charm. Deceived by charm one holds the whole second sex to be more beautiful than the male.",["Reiz = charm (not in the glossary); used consistently.","Gegenstand and Object are both 'object' here (Gegenstande / Objects).","allgemein gültig = universally valid; gemein haben = have in common.","Beurtheilung = judgment; Frauenzimmer = 'woman' (literally 'women's quarters', an old term for a woman).","OCR 'offtin' read as 'oft in'; 'vermischet', 'bestehet' are old spellings."]],"philippi-s08-046":["In music harmony is the proper beauty and for the understanding; melody, however, the charm or the sensation. The former is universally valid and unchangeable. The latter, charm, is different according to the difference of the subjects. Melody is nothing other than sensations expressed through tones. According as a subject is more or less inclined to such sensations as the melodic of a music expresses, accordingly it is more or less charming for the subject. Blinded by charm many a one can hold a mediocre, indeed probably bad music to be very beautiful; but another does not think so.",["allgemeingültig = universally valid; Verstand = understanding; Empfindungen = sensations.","'ja wol schlechte' rendered 'indeed probably bad'; 'wol' could also be read 'well' or 'perhaps'.","OCR 'Subjeet' read as 'Subjekt'."]],"philippi-s08-047":["The beautiful form has universal charm, that is, true beauty pleases everyone all the time, and why? Because the ground of the satisfaction lies in the object. Sensation or charm is changeable and different. Why? because the ground of the satisfaction lies in the subject.",["Wohlgefallen = satisfaction (not in the glossary); gefallen = please; Grund = ground.","OCR 'veränderlieh' read as 'veränderlich'; 'hegt' read as 'liegt'; 'Subjeet' as 'Subjekt'."]],"philippi-s08-048":["I say that charm is changeable. When the organs become dull, things lose their charm. One sees it in old people, who for just that reason judge more correctly about beauty.",["OCR 'siebet' read as 'sieht'.","urtheilen = judge (v.)."]],"philippi-s08-049":["The matter of a sensible object requires to be sensed sensibly, the form of it to be assessed by taste. We thus come to taste. The laws of sensibility are very much in agreement; from this it follows that taste has universal rules. Many a one holds it to be a fashion; and how many other explanations does one have of it? I do not know whether one does not explain it correctly if one assumes it to be a critique of that which pleases universally; so that he has taste who knows how to choose what pleases universally. But what then critiques that which pleases universally? Is it the understanding? No, but the sensibility practiced through experiences. We shall see below that that which agrees according to the universal rules of the understanding does not please at all according to rules of sensibility; and conversely. Truths please the understanding, the more so the deeper, the more hidden they are and the more effort and exertion it must apply in order to understand them and this is just that which is most unbearable to sensibility. The ground of the satisfaction in propositions of reason lies, if I am not mistaken, in the increase of insights, and in the perfection of them in products of taste, in the ease of being able to take up a multitude of manifold impressions, to order them without effort, distinguish them, sense them vividly and survey them in the whole. The laws of our sensibility, we said above, are very much in agreement with the laws of the sensibility of others, and from this the basic rules of taste are drawn. What sort of laws are these?",["sinnlich empfunden: 'sensibly sensed' keeps the repetition of the stem; 'sinnlichen' is an OCR correction ('sinnhehen').","beurtheilt = assessed (beurteilen); erklären/Erklärung = explain/explanation; verstehen = understand; Verstand = understanding.","Critick / kritisiret: 'critique' and 'critiques' (verb) follow the German's own coinage.","Vernunftsätzen = propositions of reason; Einsichten = insights (not in glossary); Eindrücke = impressions; Menge = multitude; mannigfaltiger = manifold (adj.).","The construction 'liegt ... in der Vermehrung der Einsichten, und Vollkommenheit derselben bei Producten des Geschmacks, in der Leichtigkeit ...' is kept as it stands; the attachment of 'bey Producten des Geschmacks' is ambiguous.","'empfinden' in the final clause is rendered 'sense' (the same word as 'sensed' above); Empfindung = sensation.","'umgekehrt' = 'conversely'; 'wo ich nicht irre' = 'if I am not mistaken'."]],"philippi-s08-050":["Our sensibility is in constant activity and also wants to be so constantly. From this basic law of sensibility one draws the rule of taste: if an object is to please sensibility, then manifoldness must be put into it, so that it gets material with which it can occupy itself: The mind is active with the form of all objects. It gives up the materials, and wants to have them formed through the object.",["Stoff = material; Materialien = materials (both 'material'; Materie is 'matter').","Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness; Gemüth = mind; Gegenstand = object.","OCR 'Stof' read as 'Stoff'; 'be}r' read as 'bey'.","'sie' ('it') in 'damit sie Stoff bekomme' refers to sensibility; the pronoun 'it' in the last sentence is left as in German and may refer to the mind."]],"philippi-s08-051":["Everything that hinders this activity of sensibility is annoying and unpleasant to it. From this flows the rule: let one endeavor to put into the manifold symmetry, harmony and clarity and in general graspability, so that sensibility can grasp the object without effort, distinguish the impressions of it easily and sense them. Taste therefore demands manifoldness, contrast, harmony, ease, clarity and a gradual transition from one up to the *oppositum* of it, the leap confuses sensibility. An object in which all this is put in a graspable relation is essentially beautiful and pleases universally. A large object such as a building pleases if symmetry is put into it; it eases the view of the building.",["Faßlichkeit = 'graspability', fassen = 'grasp', chosen to keep clear of begreifen = comprehend.","das Mannigfaltige = 'the manifold' (n.); Mannigfaltigkeit = 'manifoldness'.","Oppositum is Latin in the German; the comma before 'der Sprung' is kept.","Verhältniß = relation; wesentlich = essentially; allmälig = gradual.","OCR 'Smnlichkeit' read as 'Sinnlichkeit'."]],"philippi-s08-052":["The chords in music please because of the easy relation that is between their tones. The octave as 1 to 2; the octave to the fifth as 1 to 3. *vid.* Euler p. 16. Everything that expands, eases and makes more distinct the representation of our sensibility, pleases.",["'viel. Eulerum' is an OCR reading; taken as 'vid. Eulerum' (Latin, 'see Euler'). Doubtful. 'Eulerum' is kept as an accusative form; rendered 'Euler'.","Verhältniß = relation (here a numerical ratio; the same English word is kept). The ratios are given as in the text.","Vorstellung = representation; deutlicher = more distinct; erweitert = 'expands' (not Ausdehnung)."]],"philippi-s08-053":["There are thus certain common laws of sensibility with regard to form and these will be, if I am not mistaken, those that I have gone through up to now.",["gemein = common; Gesetze = laws; Ansehung = 'regard'.","OCR 'sejm' read as 'seyn'."]],"philippi-s08-054":["But charm must be quite separated off. These common laws of taste are drawn from concrete cases; but can at any rate also go to the object, only the subjects must be capable of it. If a human being does not know the laws of sensibility, then the beautiful cannot please him either: for objects of the senses must be assessed according to laws of sensibility; such a human being then judges according to charm. When an Iroquois whom one had led around all of Paris was asked what had pleased him best after all, he said the cookshop. What pleases because it is beautiful pleases quite differently from that which pleases because it is agreeable. When Homer and a multitude of people were listening to a poet and the dinner bell was rung, everyone ran away; the Homer alone stayed with him. The poet praises this one as a true connoisseur of beauty, who does not let himself be blinded by charm like the rabble. But hardly had he learned that the dinner bell was ringing, for he was deaf; when he too went away.",["weiß = knows (wissen); fähig = capable; beurtheilt = assessed; urtheilt = judges.","Kenner = 'connoisseur' (from kennen); Menge Volks = 'multitude of people'.","'der Homer' keeps the article of the German. 'lasse' is subjunctive in the German; rendered by the plain present.","Correlative 'kaum ... so' rendered 'hardly ... when'; the semicolon is kept.","Garküche = 'cookshop'; Tischglocke = 'dinner bell'; angenehm = agreeable."]],"philippi-s08-055":["The self-interested have no taste; for they look only at what is charming to them; whereas the essential of the beautiful is that one look at what pleases universally.",["Eigennützigen = 'the self-interested'; 'sehe' is subjunctive in the German, rendered 'look'.","Das Wesentliche = 'the essential'."]],"philippi-s08-056":["If one considers the relation of an object to sensibility, then one looks at charm; if one considers that to taste, then that is beauty.",["Betrachtet = considers; 'dasjenige' rendered 'that', referring back (ambiguously) to the relation.","OCR 'Sinnhchkeit' read as 'Sinnlichkeit'."]],"philippi-s08-057":["I note further that understanding belongs to taste.",["Verstand = understanding."]],"philippi-s08-058":["One assesses the beautiful not according to the degree of satisfaction but according to universal laws of sensibility.",["beurtheilt = assesses; Grad = degree; Wohlgefallen = satisfaction."]],"philippi-s08-059":["One can e.g. build a house that blocks someone's view. If it has true beauty, then this person too will hold it to be beautiful, however little charm it may have for him.",["z. E. = 'e.g.'; 'vor schön halten' = 'hold to be beautiful'."]],"philippi-s08-060":["I conclude this treatise with some considerations on the beautiful:",["OCR 'Schlüsse' read as 'schließe'; Betrachtungen = considerations; Abhandlung = treatise."]],"philippi-s08-061":["I. In solitude the human being is very indifferent to the beautiful. There they look only at their well-being, only at charm. What touches his sensation agreeably, he makes the main aim.",["The shift from singular (Mensch, er) to plural (sie) is kept as in the German.","Empfindung = sensation; Hauptabsicht = main aim (Absicht = aim); Wohlbefinden = well-being."]],"philippi-s08-062":["When I am alone, I like it better in the forest; but in company better in the garden. The beautiful form seems to be merely for company. But whence does it come that in solitude taste vanishes? In solitude we look only at our private liking. This must not happen in judgment according to laws of taste. There we must orient ourselves by the universal liking, however little charming and pleasing it may be to us.",["'an Beurtheilung' is kept as 'in judgment'; the preposition 'an' is odd and may be an OCR or transcript slip. Doubtful.","Gefallen (noun) = liking; gefallen = please; gefällig = pleasing; Beurtheilung = judgment.","OCR 'Einsamiveit' read as 'Einsamkeit'."]],"philippi-s08-063":["When we are alone, we never attend to the beautiful. In the country little regard is paid to the beautiful. Those who are concerned with it are either those who still have an impression of city life, which however also goes out with time, or those who have frequent intercourse with respected people. A human being on a desert island would prefer the slightest of feeling to all taste and in the end lose it entirely. Hardly would he, if he had a piece of cloth for a coat, cut from it a French *Taille*. Comfort would be his chief aim.",["attendiren: the Latinate verb is rendered 'attend'. aufs Schöne gesehen is rendered 'regard is paid to the beautiful', which is looser than the German.","Taüle read as Taille (OCR/spelling), left in French and italicized; the German gives no further gloss. Hauptabsicht: Absicht = aim.","ihn (in 'ihn zuletzt ganz verlieren') is left as 'it'; the German pronoun refers back to Geschmack."]],"philippi-s08-064":["Taste is a harbinger of sociability; and sociability the nourishment of taste. There is a certain delicacy of taste, and in it it does not depend so much on judgment as on sensation or well-being. This delicacy of taste is a fruit of sociability.",["Geselligkeit = sociability; Beurtheilung = judgment; Empfindung = sensation; Wohlbefinden = well-being.","kommt es nicht so sehr an auf is rendered literally as 'it does not depend so much on'."]],"philippi-s08-065":["II. Sociability is the cause and motive of taste. Sociable people acquire so refined a feeling that they feel a far greater pleasure when something pleases others than when they treat themselves to something. They are more concerned with the well-being of others than with their own. The so-called vanity is inseparable from taste; and this name one is accustomed commonly to give, with spiteful mockery, to the conduct of sociable people. But is he then hateful who seeks to make himself agreeable to others, and feels a pleasure when he can afford others a satisfaction? Rather is vanity, when it stays within the limitations that virtue sets for it, praiseworthy.",["'IL' in the OCR read as the numeral 'II.'; the '1' after the question (OCR for '?') read as a question mark.","Bewegungsgrund = motive; Schranken = limitation; Eitelkeit = vanity.","Wohlgefallen erzeigen is rendered 'afford a satisfaction'; gefällig = 'agreeable'; gefallen = 'please'.","sich selbst was zu gut thun: 'treat themselves to something' is idiomatic."]],"philippi-s08-066":["The agreeable concerns the well-being of a private person; the beautiful concerns the appearance of the thing and is the proper object of taste. Yet one often also makes use of taste with regard to what pleases in enjoyment and in sensation; that, however, one must judge empirically. One thus has a taste not only of judgment but also of feeling, if one can hit upon from one's feeling that which pleases universally or at least many in sensation. Many a one knows, e.g., well what sort of dish tastes good to many.",["Erscheinung = appearance; Gegenstand = object; Beurtheilung = judgment; beurtheilen = judge; Empfindung = sensation; Gefühl = feeling.","allgemein is rendered 'universally' (it could also be 'generally'); the same choice is kept throughout.","was für ein Gericht vielen schmeckt: 'tastes good to many' supplies 'good'; the German has only schmeckt."]],"philippi-s08-067":["That is then a mark of a fine taste, if one can through feeling even distinguish what pleases universally. The sociable one, who has participating passions, has such a fine taste.",["Marke (modern Merkmal) = 'mark' (nota).","theilnehmende Leidenschaften is rendered 'participating passions'; 'sympathetic' would be an alternative.","allgemein = 'universally' (alternatively 'generally')."]],"philippi-s08-068":["If one wants to be agreeable, one must choose that which pleases others, III. Young people have more feeling than taste and old people more taste than feeling.",["'III.' stands in the text mid-line and is kept as it stands; the comma before it is in the German.","gefällig = agreeable."]],"philippi-s08-069":["The causes of this are these: 1. taste is acquired through experience 2. where there is much feeling, taste is lacking. For what has a relation to taste pleases in universal relations and concerns the understanding. What has a relation to feeling pleases in private relation. Whoever seeks that which pleases in private relation will not find that which pleases universally, and whose organs are strongly assailed, as happens in constant feeling, has a dull taste.",["ün read as 'in' (OCR). Verhältniß = relation; Verstand = understanding; Erfahrung = experience.","im allgemeinen Verhältnisse: allgemein = 'universal' (alternatively 'general').","wer ... und wessen Organe: the syntax is loose and is kept so; 'whose organs are strongly assailed ... has' follows the German construction.","angegriffen is rendered 'assailed'."]],"philippi-s08-070":["The effort of some authors goes merely to feeling, not to taste. It is also certainly easier to depict objects strongly sensibly than to order a plan of ideas so that one surveys them almost with a glance. To the first belongs only imagination, only sensation; to this understanding and judgment. One must have experiences and know how to compare things with one another, if one wants to have taste.",["Gegenstände = objects; sinnlich = sensible (here adverbial, 'sensibly'); Einbildungskraft = imagination; Idee = idea; Beurtheilung = judgment; Ding = thing.","Zum ersten ... zu diesem: 'the first' and 'this' follow the German demonstratives literally."]],"philippi-s08-071":["An object is beautiful insofar as order, unity, symmetry, proportion is to be encountered in it and it pleases in the appearance, not insofar as it has an influence on our sensibility.",["Gegenstand = object; Einheit = unity; Erscheinung = appearance; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility.","Ebenmaaß = 'symmetry'; Proportion is left as the cognate.","The singular verb 'is' after four nouns follows the German."]],"philippi-s08-072":["Besides what pleases us in objects there is yet another ground of liking, namely whether it also belongs to us from afar. That is charm, and that begets vanity. The reflection on the appropriation of the object is already in charm. When a human being has great merits, one always recognizes some ties in which he stands with us, so that one may besides the liking over his merits also feel a charm, which in a certain way belongs to us. Those seven cities had this as their target, which contended about Homer's fatherland. One always gladly boasts of that to which we have a claim; that is vanity. The essential beauty is the form; from this the charm, which agrees with laws of inclinations and passions, must be distinguished.",["Wohlgefallen = 'liking'; Reiz = 'charm'; Grund = ground; Reflexion (a Latinate word, distinct from Überlegung) = 'reflection'; Zueignung = 'appropriation'; Gesetz = law.","Verbindungen rendered 'ties' to avoid the technical 'combination' (Verbindung) and 'connection' (Verknüpfung). Augenmerk rendered 'target' (not Absicht or Zweck).","Jene sieben Städte ... die ... stritten: German word order kept, so the relative clause trails.","A full stop is lacking in the German after 'Eitelkeit' (OCR); none is supplied beyond the German's own."]],"philippi-s08-073":["If someone inherits a beautiful palace in a foreign place, and he comes to the place and catches sight of it for the first time, he looks at it 1. as a traveler passing through without charm; then it pleases him in the appearance and he then sees the true beauty alone 2. He considers it as a property of his relative; that already belongs to charm. 3. As his own property which he will now take into possession; here is feeling.",["siebet read as 'sieht'; dami read as 'dann' or 'damit'. The reading of 'dami' is uncertain and 'then' is chosen.","Erscheinung = appearance; Betrachtung/betrachten = consideration/consider; Reiz = charm; Gefühl = feeling.","Anverwandten = 'relative' (kinsman); Eigenthum = 'property'."]],"philippi-s08-074":["There is a difference between beautiful objects and between beautiful cognitions or representations.",["Object = object; Erkenntniß = cognition; Vorstellung = representation. 'between ... and between' repeats the German zwischen ... und zwischen."]],"philippi-s08-075":["The representation can be beautiful, although the object is ugly. The object can in turn be beautiful and the representation of it ugly.",["Vorstellung = representation; Object = object; wiederum = 'in turn'."]],"philippi-s08-076":["In Holland one loves grotesque pictures. The representations are whimsical, the objects are beautiful.",["'Avunderlich' in the OCR read as 'wunderlich'; rendered 'whimsical' ('odd' would also do).","Bilder = pictures; Vorstellungen = representations; Objecte = objects."]],"philippi-s08-077":["The representation is called image, insofar as it is laid before the senses.",["Abbildung = 'image' (alternatively 'depiction'). Sinne = senses; Vorstellung = representation."]],"philippi-s08-078":["Just as representations can displease us whose object pleases us in itself; so descriptions and representations can please us, although their object displeases us. One can make it so that the cognition even charms of an ugly object. Thus, e.g., of the Dead Sea, of the ice zones and of desolate nature, objects that in themselves displease us, agreeable and pleasing representations can be made, namely representations that shake and move us in an agreeable way. One can even represent very agreeably that which is abominable: indeed even mix charm into representations of such a kind.",["gar reizet is rendered 'even charms' (gar could also be 'quite'); von einem häßlichen Gegenstande = 'of an ugly object', with 'of' kept as the German's von.","an sich = in itself; Erkenntniß = cognition; Gegenstand/Gegenstände = object(s); Art = way/kind ('in an agreeable way', 'of such a kind').","Missing full stop after 'mißfällt' in the German; the sentence is carried on as printed. 'Man kann machen, daß' is kept literal."]],"philippi-s08-079":["Poets give themselves the trouble, the easy trouble, of representing charmingly things that are otherwise all too moving for us and whose charm they should rather damp. Certainly easy trouble! If only they would seek to clothe in charm that which is the true essential beauty, and which toward us now has lost all charm, I mean virtue.",["ohnedem = 'otherwise' (anyway); rührend = 'moving'; gegen uns = 'toward us' (could also be 'in comparison with us').","Dinge = things; wesentlich = essential; Tugend = virtue."]],"philippi-s08-080":["We can mix a charm into descriptions of abominableness, not only when we depict it quite true to life and represent it movingly; but through smiling and beautiful descriptions.",["recht nach dem Leben = 'quite true to life' (literally 'rightly after life'). Abscheulichkeit = abominableness.","The sentence stops at 'beautiful descriptions' as in the German."]],"philippi-s08-081":["I consider with a cheerful mien the sublime, the splendid mockery with which Wieland praises vice. Just as no abuse, no cursing is more sensitive than mockery, especially that over which one still makes merry: so also an object never becomes more abominable than when one represents it in this way. Instead of scolding and cursing vice, as is usual, and thereby putting the reader or listener into a melancholy sad movement. one should represent it with beautiful ironic descriptions, with sublime narratives and then all at once show virtue in its sublimity, beauty and fullest splendor. That would have an uncommon impression. What should disturb us? the evil of the other if cheerfulness can be brought to us thereby.",["ungeineinen read as 'ungemeinen' (OCR); rendered 'uncommon'. Eindruk = impression.","The full stop after 'Bewegung' in the German (mid-sentence, 'Anstatt daß ... sollte man') is kept as printed.","besonders diejenige worüber man sich noch lustig macht: the antecedent of 'that' (Verspottung) is left as in the German.","Uebel = 'evil' (could be 'ill'); Gegenstand = object; erhaben = sublime; Erhabenheit = sublimity."]],"philippi-s08-082":["Music furnishes no objects at all, nor descriptions of them. The proportion, which in no way of beauty is so exact and so manifold as in music, it is that which constitutes the beauty of the same. Yet in it there is not so much appearance as a multitude of sensations and charm. Every tone is similar to certain expressions of the passions. Perhaps the pleasure in music arises from the similarity that the tones have with sensations which one gladly recalls. The proportion of music lies partly in the tones, partly in the beat; the unity in the theme of the music or in the principal tone, which is carried through.",["'die1': the superscript 1 is an OCR/apparatus residue and was dropped.","'Gegenstände' = objects (Gegenstand); 'Art' = way (of beauty); 'Erscheinung' = appearance; 'Empfindung' = sensation; 'Ausdruck' = expression; 'Einheit' = unity.","'ist es die die Schönheit derselben ausmacht' is rendered with the German's emphatic 'it is that which'; 'derselben' ('of the same') is ambiguous between music and proportion, and is kept so.","'Reiz' = charm (not in glossary; 'charm' used consistently); 'Tact' = beat (doubtful: could also be 'measure/time')."]],"philippi-s09-001":["On the good in concepts.",["'Begriffen' = concepts (Begriff)."]],"philippi-s09-002":["Our judgments of taste always concern the subject. For in the beautiful we looked to the relation of the thing to the subject. We did not consider what the thing is, but only how it appears. In the judging of the good it is entirely different, there we look to the true constitution of the object, and this is a subject-matter of the understanding. Good is that which agrees according to the laws of the understanding; therefore whoever wants to judge the good must know the laws of his understanding.",["'Urtheile' = judgments; 'Beurtheilung' = judging (Beurteilung); 'beurtheilen' = judge/assess (rendered 'judge'); 'Beschaffenheit' = constitution; 'Object' = object (Latinate, same English as Gegenstand elsewhere); 'Verstand' = understanding; 'Gesetze' = laws.","'Vorwurf' = subject-matter (not in glossary; literally something 'cast before' one).","'kennen' = know (in 'kennen' of the laws); 'betrachten' = consider (Betrachtung)."]],"philippi-s09-003":["In logic we do not speak of objects, whether they are good or evil, beautiful or ugly, but of concepts.",["'Gegenständen' = objects (Gegenstand); 'Begriffen' = concepts."]],"philippi-s09-004":["The agreement of our representation with the object is otherwise called truth; (we will speak of it below) this is the essential logical perfection. Only truth is not the ground of aesthetic perfection. Something can be beautiful even if it is not to be encountered in all of nature; rather this consists in the greatest degree of universal pleasing. One represents the things here not as they are, but as they ought to be, if they are to please quite completely. If I concede too much to logical perfection, then I become dry; if I concede too much to the aesthetic, then I become a poet in the rational, or a novelist in the historical, like Voltaire in his Charles the Twelfth.",["'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Vollkommenheit' = perfection; 'Grund' = ground; 'Grad' = degree; 'Object' = object.","'Man steht hier die Sachen vor' read as 'stellt ... vor' (OCR/orthographic slip: 'steht'); translated 'represents'.","'Allein' = 'Only' (adversative); 'complet' kept as 'completely'; 'Carl dem Zwölften' = Charles the Twelfth (Voltaire's Histoire de Charles XII)."]],"philippi-s09-005":["In § 21 the author speaks of learned cognition.",["'Erkenntniß' = cognition; 'gelehrt' = learned. 'Autor' = Meier (identification not in the passage)."]],"philippi-s09-006":["Philosophy itself is no learned cognition. The philosopher works; the scholar writes.",["'Philosophie' (not 'Weltweisheit') = philosophy; 'Gelehrte' = scholar (learned person)."]],"philippi-s09-007":["Learnedness is distinguished from common cognition *materialiter* or *formaliter*. *Materialiter* through the object, *formaliter* through the *modum cognoscendi*; *materialiter* it is not different, in that it can have one object with common cognition, but *formaliter*, in that the scholar cognizes the object better. All learnedness is either historical or rational; it has either objects of history or objects that are thinkable only through reason. These two ways of learnedness are distinguished only according to form. A historical object can be cognized rationally and a rational one historically. The rational science that does not go to objects of the senses is either mathematics or philosophy. For it has as object either the magnitudes, and that is mathematics, or the realities, and that is philosophy. (The word learnedness properly comprehends only historical cognition.) To what, then, do the beautiful sciences belong? Do they belong to learnedness or not?",["'Gelehrsamkeit' = learnedness; 'gemein' = common; 'Art' = way; 'Gegenstand'/'Object' both = object; 'Grössen' = magnitudes; 'Realitäten' = realities; 'Wissenschaft' = science; 'Sinne' = senses.","'begreifft' = comprehends (begreifen); 'schönen Wissenschaften' = beautiful sciences (belles lettres sense not imposed).","'Mathematick'/'Mathematik' both = mathematics."]],"philippi-s09-008":["A science of the beautiful there is not at all, because no first rules can be given, which yet ought to be so in a science. The doctrine of taste is no doctrine, no *Doctrin*, but only a critique. To criticize means to compare a particular case with the whole domain of sensibility. The rules of critique are more explanations of the *Phaenomenorum* than precepts of actual objects. Just as it is also in medicine. In aesthetics one learns to criticize, and this helps indirectly also toward the production of the beautiful. In the rational sciences the mathematician, and in the common sciences the historian, are alone scholars. The philosopher is no scholar; for philosophy cannot be taught and a scholar is yet one to whom something has been taught. A narrative of the endeavors of human reason can well be imparted to someone; but that is no philosophy.",["'Lehre' = doctrine; 'Doctrin' kept as Latinate variant (also 'doctrine'); 'Umfang' = domain; 'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility; 'Erklärungen' = explanations; 'besondern' = particular; 'Gegenstände' = objects; 'Vernunft' = reason.","'Phänomenorum' and 'Präcepte' are German-Latin hybrids; the former kept in Latin italics, the latter rendered 'precepts'.","OCR: 'Venranfft' read as 'Vernunfft'; 'Philo-Bophie' read as 'Philosophie'.","'Gelehrter ... der dem etwas gelehret worden' kept literal ('one to whom something has been taught')."]],"philippi-s10-001":["On perfection § 22",["'Vollkommenheit' = perfection."]],"philippi-s10-002":["Theoretical perfection according to *quantitaet* is extensiveness and fruitfulness. According to *qualitaet* perfection is either logical or aesthetic.",["'quantitaet', 'Qualitaet' left in Latin form, italicized (Quantität/Qualität)."]],"philippi-s10-003":["Logical perfection has a relation to other cognitions; practical, to the will.",["'Erkenntniße' = cognitions. 'practische' stands elliptically for 'practical perfection'; the ellipsis is kept."]],"philippi-s10-004":["The harmfulness that a truth can have is always contingent.",["'zufällig' = contingent."]],"philippi-s10-005":["Aesthetic perfection has solely a relation to my taste.",["'Geschmak' = taste."]],"philippi-s10-006":["Objective logical perfection is the agreement of a cognition with the laws of the understanding. The subjective aesthetic, the agreement of the cognition with the laws of sensibility.",["'Uebereinstimmung' = agreement; the second sentence is elliptical ('perfection' not repeated)."]],"philippi-s10-007":["We have two capacities, understanding and sensibility. This goes only to how the subject is touched through the object. It is the only force of the soul that goes immediately to the object.",["'Fähigkeiten' = capacities; 'Krafft' = force; 'Seelen' = soul (genitive; Seele not in glossary).","'Diese' ('This') is grammatically feminine and so could refer to 'Sinnlichkeit' (the nearer) ; kept as 'This'.","'rühren' = touch (also 'move/stir'; 'Rührung' elsewhere)."]],"philippi-s10-008":["Aesthetic perfection is also called beautiful perfection.",[]],"philippi-s10-009":["We have already remarked that the beautiful is to be separated from charm. The beautiful concerns only the form, the contemplation; charm, however, the sensation and the alteration of our state. Beauty is to be preferred; for in it what matters is the judging, which is not necessary for charm. Further, beauty pleases every time; charm, however, wears out. The laws of sensibility always remain the same and are universal, in charm human beings are not to be compared at all. Many strive after constant stirring; they want always to have only a shaking beauty; they want to have their nerves always excited. This way of motion belongs to coarse senses insensible to the essentially beautiful. A human being of fine taste demands true beauty, stripped of charm; that pleases him.",["'Reiz' = charm; 'Rührung' = stirring; 'Form' = form; 'Empfindung' = sensation; 'Zustand' = state; 'Beurtheilung' = judging; 'notwendig' = necessary; 'Art' = way.","OCR: 'aUemal' read as 'allemal'; 'dieselbe' kept as in the text (read 'dieselben').","'Beschauung' = contemplation; 'Motion' left as 'motion'; 'Sinne' = senses."]],"philippi-s10-010":["The agreement of cognition with the object was logical perfection and the means to it truth. That with the subject, aesthetic perfection and the means to it, a diligent observation how an object or the cognition of the same stirs our and other subjects.",["'Mittel' = means; 'Beobachtung' = observation; 'Erkenntniß' = cognition; 'Object' = object; 'Subjekt' = subject.","The last clause is elliptical and syntactically loose in the German ('unser und anderer Subjekte'); kept as it stands. 'rühret' = stirs."]],"philippi-s10-011":["Ease agrees with the subjective laws; but not with the objective or laws of the understanding; consequently it is not a logical perfection. The objects of cognition are often very difficult; so that one needs much exertion to have insight into them properly. Thus all metaphysical abstract truths are very difficult to understand, because they please only in concepts.",["'Gegenstände' = objects; 'verstehen' = understand; 'Begriffen' = concepts; 'einsehen' = have insight into (not in glossary).","'daß man ... braucht' follows the German 'so that'; 'gehörig' = properly."]],"philippi-s10-012":["Novelty gives nourishment to the mind, which strives to expand itself and always to be busy. It is a property not of the object, but of the subject. The softness of the green color, the shining of the red, the stinging out of the violet do not lie in the colors, but in our eye, which through the impressions that the colors make is capable of this or that modification.",["'Gemüth' = mind; 'Eigenschafft' = property (glossary 'Beschaffenheit' is also 'property'; here a different German word); 'Eindrücke' = impressions; 'fähig' = capable (Fähigkeit).","'das hervorstechende' kept literal ('the stinging out'; also 'the prominent'); 'hegen' read as 'liegen' (OCR/orthography)."]],"philippi-s10-013":["We often hold the relation of an object to our subject to be a certain property of it. So it goes with colors and many other objects of the senses.",["'Verhältniß' = relation; 'Eigenschafft' = property; 'Gegenständen' = objects; 'Object' = object.","'für ... halten' = hold ... to be."]],"philippi-s10-014":["In logical perfection we will bring everything to concepts; in the aesthetic, everything to appearances. If we want to give a logical perfection, then we consider everything in the universal, *in abstracto*. If we want to give an aesthetic one, then we consider everything as it were present, *in concreto*. Logical perfection rests on the understanding and is determined by it, the aesthetic on sensibility and determines it. This must often help out that one, on account of our weakness, since we are so much bound to the sensible, to make the matter distinct. In morals one seeks, e.g., to show the universal in examples. Virtue pleases in concepts, but one must, so that it may also please in appearance, make it sensible through examples. For this history is required, to which one therefore has to apply oneself.",["'Begriffe' = concepts; 'Erscheinungen' = appearances; 'Verstand' = understanding; 'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility; 'bestimmt/bestimmet' = determined/determines; 'deutlich' = distinct; 'betrachten' = consider.","'Diese muß jener ... aushelfen': 'Diese' (the aesthetic) must help out 'jener' (the logical) — the German dative is kept ambiguous-looking; I render 'This must often help out that one'.","'sinnlich' = sensible; 'Exempel'/'Beispielen' both = examples; 'Historie' = history."]],"philippi-s10-015":["Thus aesthetic perfection is, as it were, the vehicle of the logical. I realize for myself, so to speak, the universal concepts. All intuition, all sensibility makes up the true perfection of aesthetics; but truth is the purpose of logical perfection.",["Vollkommenheit = perfection; Anschauung = intuition; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Begriff = concept; Zweck = purpose (glossary).","OCR 'Volllvommenheit' silently read as 'Vollkommenheit'. 'Vehikul' rendered 'vehicle'. 'Aesthetick' rendered 'aesthetics'."]],"philippi-s10-016":["Our intuitions are all sensible. If we are not touched by an object, then we cognize it only through the understanding *in abstracto*. God's understanding is intuiting, not touched by objects: its intuitions are all intellectual through the understanding. They are immediate intuitions through the same. Our understanding can attain to no evidence of an object if it is not made sensible, if the universal (abstr.) is not shown in the particular case (*in concreto*). We attain the universal through the particular: God not so.",["Gegenstand = object; erkennen = cognize; Verstand = understanding; anschauend = intuiting; Evidentz = evidence; besonder = particular.","OCR 'shid' read as 'sind'. 'gerührt' rendered 'touched'. 'durch denselben' ('through the same') could refer to the understanding or to God's understanding; kept as is. 'intellectual' is German 'intellectual' as printed.","'in abstracto' and 'in concreto' are Latin in the source; 'abstr.' abbreviation retained."]],"philippi-s10-017":["Aesthetic perfection is often conducive to the logical, contrary [to it]: for the aesthetic rests on sensibility, the logical on abstraction from sensibility.",["The German 'der logischen entgegen öfters beförderlich' is ambiguous: 'entgegen' may be a preposition governing 'der logischen' ('contrary to the logical, often conducive') or may go with 'beförderlich'. I rendered it with the bracketed 'to it' marking the first reading only minimally; the German has no such word and is awkward.","OCR 'Smnlichkeit' read as 'Sinnlichkeit'."]],"philippi-s10-018":["What serves sensible intuition promotes logical perfection, but what goes to the impression does it damage.",["Eindruck = impression; 'Abbruch thun' rendered 'do damage'. OCR 'diendt' read as 'dient'.","'auf den Eindruck geht' kept literal ('goes to the impression')."]],"philippi-s10-019":["The aesthetic combines many cognitions, the logical separates them.",["verbindet = combines (Verbindung = combination); Erkenntnis = cognition. 'absondern' rendered 'separate'."]],"philippi-s10-020":["The authors who go solely to impressions have the lowest rank.",["Eindruck = impression."]],"philippi-s10-021":["Whoever wants to convince someone of a proposition must take great care not to stir up the sensations, and to scatter beauties into it, because he arouses a mistrust that he only wants to persuade us.",["überzeugen = convince (Überzeugung = conviction); überreden = persuade (Überredung = persuasion); Satz = proposition; Empfindung = sensation.","'drein' ('into it') has no clear antecedent; kept vague."]],"philippi-s10-022":["Sciences have clarity but not intuition.",["Wissenschaft = science; Klarheit = clarity; Anschauung = intuition."]],"philippi-s10-023":["There is a great harmony between the intuition of the understanding and that of sensibility. E.g. there is a great harmony between the proof of the theorem of Pythagoras, which the geometer intuits with the understanding, and the figure of the same, which he intuits sensibly. The only difference is that one surveys in the figure with a glance that which one has clearly cognized through a series of thoughts or words.",["Beweis = proof; Theorema rendered 'theorem' (Lehrsatz in the glossary, but Latin 'Theorematis' stands here). OCR 'denn»' read as 'den' (relative 'which'); 'Sinnhchkeit' read as 'Sinnlichkeit'.","'desselben' ('of the same') refers to the theorem or the proof; ambiguity kept.","'Geometra' rendered 'geometer'."]],"philippi-s10-024":["Many an understanding cannot see the universal except in particular cases, and that is the common understanding, which cognizes the abstract *in concreto*. Yet in moral judgments even the most unlearned understanding has abstract concepts.",["einsehen = see (into); gemein = common; Urtheil = judgment; Begriff = concept.","OCR 'abstracte' kept as 'abstract'."]],"philippi-s10-025":["If we do not have skill to show the universal *in concreto*, if we do not know how to combine aesthetic sensibility with intellectual concepts; then there is lacking to us that evidentness and intelligibility which makes so many writers useless and unintelligible.",["Augenscheinlichkeit = evidentness (cf. Evidenz = evidence); Verständlichkeit = intelligibility. 'Geschicklichkeit' = skill.","Singular 'macht' follows the German, where the relative clause has a singular verb with the two nouns."]],"philippi-s10-026":["Skill contains the possibility of the production of various products.",["Geschicklichkeit = skill; Möglichkeit = possibility; Producte rendered 'products'."]],"philippi-s10-027":["The sensible form is also a true perfection and logical perfection steps forth in its company. Sensibility does not consist in confusion, that is, the understanding can judge the things even if the sensibility is very great.",["Verwirrung = confusion; beurtheilen = judge/assess (beurteilen); OCR 'Sinnhchkeit' read as 'Sinnlichkeit'.","'in ihrer Begleitung' - 'its' could refer to the sensible form or to true perfection; kept as is."]],"philippi-s10-028":["All distinctness comes from the working-over by the understanding. If we consider propositions *in concreto*, then they are indeed confused, but that does not lie with sensibility and the understanding soon knows how to order them and to bring them into a fitting form. It takes only the materials from sensibility, the form it gives them itself. The reading of a Fontenelle and association with the unlearned and the eager-to-know will teach us to make general propositions sensible and to clothe all concepts in sheer appearances. And if the form of sensibility agrees with the form of the understanding or with logical perfection: then that is the genuine beauty, the essential aesthetic perfection. Here no charm, no feeling comes into consideration, no matter, only the form. If the form of intuition agrees with that of the understanding; if the former is helpful in expressing the perfection of the understanding; if the appearance of the thing makes the concept of the understanding easier; then that is the essential beauty. A sensible cognition pleases if it has manifoldness and unity, if the representations are so ordered that one does not hinder the other, but its greatest perfection is when it is conformable to the understanding.",["Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Materialien = materials (Materie = matter); Form = form; Erscheinung = appearance; Betrachtung = consideration; Gefühl = feeling; Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness; Einheit = unity; Vorstellung = representation.","'lecture' is in Latin letters in the source (French/Latin 'lecture'), rendered 'reading'. 'Reitz' = charm. 'Wißbegierigen' rendered 'eager-to-know'.","'sie' in 'sie zu ordnen' refers to propositions (or confused things); 'er' = the understanding."]],"philippi-s10-029":["The sensible representations were formerly regarded as a hindrance to the understanding and rejected as such. But although sensible cognition is initially confused and can also never have the perfection of the understanding; one can yet say that all concepts of the understanding first attain their perfection when one makes them sensible, and that therefore sensible cognition is a necessary supplement to the understanding.",["Vorstellung = representation; Erkenntnis = cognition; notwendig = necessary. 'Ergänzung' = supplement.","'als dann erst' kept as 'first ... when'; 'als solche' = 'as such'."]],"philippi-s10-030":["It is a deficiency of our understanding that it does not see an abstract concept distinctly and must therefore go back to concrete propositions. Were it perfect, it would not have need of this detour and would cognize things immediately. Through the understanding one cognizes mediately through general marks. But our understanding is not intuiting, but only discursive.",["deutlich = distinct; Merkmal = mark (nota); unmittelbar = immediate; mittelbar = mediate; anschauend = intuiting; discursiv = discursive.","'einen abstracten Begriff nicht deutlich einsiehet' kept in German order: 'distinctly' could attach to 'see' only."]],"philippi-s10-031":["It is a proper artifice and great skill to make concepts sensible. In philosophy one must first think through a proposition abstractly and afterward make it sensible. Thus one can transform general maxims into sentiments, if one applies to a single case what those say *in abstracto*. This serves to make the truth more comprehensible and to represent it emphatically.",["Begriff = concept; Satz = proposition; Philosophie rendered 'philosophy' (Weltweisheit is not the German here). 'Sentiments' and 'Maximen' kept as in the German.","'abstracte' stands adverbially or as modifier of 'Satz' ('abstract a proposition'); the German is ambiguous, rendered 'abstractly'.","'faßlicher' = 'more comprehensible' (not begreifen)."]],"philippi-s10-032":["The perfection of the magnitude of cognition consists in the multitude that I cognize.",["Größe = magnitude; Menge = multitude; erkennen = cognize. OCR 'derMenge' read as 'der Menge'. Passage as printed ends without punctuation."]],"philippi-s10-033":["With regard to quality, cognitions are either true or false.",["Qualitaet kept as 'quality'; Erkenntnis = cognition."]],"philippi-s11-001":["On imperfection § 23",["Unvollkommenheit = imperfection (cf. Vollkommenheit = perfection)."]],"philippi-s11-002":["Imperfection arises either from lack or from conflict or privation.",["Mangel = lack; Widerstreit = conflict; Beraubung = privation. The 'oder ... oder' lists three items though 'entweder' suggests two; kept as printed."]],"philippi-s11-003":["Ignorance is an imperfection of lack, error an imperfection of conflict. In ignorance I see the missing of the cognition: in error the false of the same. Ignorance is lack, error sickness. Error not only procures nothing, but also robs.",["Unwissenheit = ignorance; Irrthum = error; Erkenntnis = cognition.","'ein das Fehlende' – 'ein' is probably OCR/original for 'ein' (sees in); kept 'see the missing'. 'derselben' = 'of the same' (cognition)."]],"philippi-s11-004":["Imperfection is thus: 1. *imperfectio deficiendi* 2. *imperfectio priuandi*.",["Latin as printed ('priuandi' = privandi)."]],"philippi-s11-005":["The logical imperfection of lack is ignorance; that of privation consists in errors.",["Mangel = lack; Beraubung = privation; Unwissenheit = ignorance; Irrthum = error."]],"philippi-s11-006":["That is *defectus*; this *vitium*.",["'Jene ... diese' = 'that ... this' (the former ... the latter), kept literal."]],"philippi-s11-007":["Aesthetic imperfection of lack, where something is missing, is dryness. That of privation, ugliness. Ugliness is thus something positive, not a mere absence of beauty but also the existence of that which is contrary to beauty. Vice is not only a lack of virtue, but also something positive. What diminishes money is just as positive as what increases it; the former leads the money from us to others, the other from others to us. Something can be not beautiful and yet also not ugly, e.g. the artificial. It does not please us when we see that someone has labored artificially with great effort at something that he could very easily have made in another way. We pity him that his art has failed.",["Trockenheit = dryness; Häßlichkeit = ugliness; Schönheit = beauty; Daseyn = existence (Dasein); Tugend = virtue.","'das Gekünstelte' rendered 'the artificial' (something done with artifice); 'laster' (OCR lower case) read as 'Laster' = vice. OCR 'ims' read as 'uns'.","'wir bedauren' = 'we pity'; 'woran gekünstelt hat' kept awkward."]],"philippi-s11-008":["The lack of all kinds of logical perfection is shallowness. The degree of logical perfection determines itself through thoroughness.",["Seichtigkeit = shallowness; Gründlichkeit = thoroughness (from Grund); Grad = degree; bestimmen = determine; Art = kind."]],"philippi-s11-009":["Ignorance is the lack of cognition in general.",["überhaupt = in general."]],"philippi-s11-010":["It is no reproach to a cognition that it is merely learned and that it has merely logical perfection. For it is sometimes expressly required that all aesthetic perfection go into exile, as e.g. in algebra.",["'exulire' is Latin (exulare) in the source; rendered 'go into exile' and not italicized as a technical term. 'expreß' = expressly."]],"philippi-s11-011":["If, however, logical perfection is accompanied by that of aesthetics in agreement; then this raises that considerably, in that it makes the concepts of the understanding intuitive and easier. In the sensible a great manifoldness was met with: through this the intuition is extended and from it confusion arises. If we therefore concede too much to aesthetic perfection, then, since we want to be agreeable, we become shallow.",["anschauend = intuitive (anschaulich in glossary); Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness; Anschauung = intuition; Verwirrung = confusion; seicht = shallow.","OCR 'entstellt' read as 'entsteht'; 'aesthetiscken' read as 'aesthetischen'.","'diese jene' = 'this ... that' (the latter ... the former); referents of 'this' and 'that' left as in German. 'ausgebreitet' rendered 'extended' for lack of a glossary entry (Ausdehnung = extension is a different word); alternatively 'spread out'."]],"philippi-s11-012":["If we take away all sensibility and aesthetic perfection from a cognition, then the soul remains empty of representations and taste is lacking. We therefore become, since we want to be thorough, dry! *Si breuis esse volo obscurus fio.* (Here it soon goes as with politeness or polish)",["Seele = soul; Vorstellung = representation; Geschmack = taste; gründlich = thorough; trocken = dry.","Latin quotation (Horace, Ars poetica) kept as printed ('breuis' for 'brevis'); the parenthesis is the transcriber's. 'Höflichkeit oder Geschliffenheit' = 'politeness or polish'."]],"philippi-s11-013":["How, then, is it distributed here? There are sciences whose ultimate end is merely pure reason. Here thoroughness is the chief end, and everything beautiful drops out here. Meanwhile there must be no ugliness there. Of such constitution are metaphysics and pure morals. Practical morals is capable of all beauty. We must first learn abstract truths; afterwards collect all beauties by reading the authors. These last must be the followers of those.",["Wissenschaften = sciences (Wissenschaft); Endzweck = ultimate end, Hauptzweck = chief end (both from Zweck); Beschaffenheit = constitution.","\"Nachtret erinnen\" is broken in the OCR; read as Nachtreterinnen (feminine plural, agreeing with the feminine \"Schönheiten\"). \"followers\" is a tentative rendering of that reading.","\"die Moral\" is rendered \"morals\" for consistency."]],"philippi-s11-014":["Beauty is manifold, if we assume it in general for the sensible; some of it is useful to logical perfection, others harm it. The beauties of form are in accordance with the perfection of the understanding and are very useful to it. The beauties of feeling, of the matter of sensibility, the tender, the laughing, are directly opposed to it and harmful. Whoever strives much after stirring in a cognition cannot bring much perfection of understanding into it. Thus in short: What goes to feeling hinders logical perfection, but the beautiful of form is useful to it; yet even this is sometimes not advantageous.",["Singular verbs after \"einige\" (\"nützet\", \"schadet\") are the German; \"others harm\" is the nearest English.","Rührung = stirring (\"emotion\" would be an interpretation); Gefühl = feeling; Verstandesvollkommenheit = perfection of understanding.","\"die\" in \"auch die\" (last clause) is kept as \"this\"; its antecedent (the beauty of form) is not stated."]],"philippi-s11-015":["In order to grasp something through the understanding I must abstract. In order to learn the beautiful I must show the abstract in concrete cases. That conflicts. The abstract, in that one separates and isolates something from many other cognitions, is dry. Just as one seeks through the microscope to separate everything else from an object, so that one may cognize the one all the more distinctly. Through this dryness I therefore deprive the soul of certain objects. It is bound and suffers a self-denial. But it is a property of all finite beings that they have a desire always to be busy.",["Gegenstand = object (also in the next passages); erkennen = cognize; deutlich = distinct.","Eigenschafft = property; the glossary also gives \"property\" for Beschaffenheit.","The sentence \"So wie man ... erkenne\" has no main clause in the German; kept as it stands.","\"Mikroscopium\" is rendered \"microscope\"."]],"philippi-s11-016":["If one gains in intuition, one loses this again in the estimation of reason. The liveliness, the heaping together of objects gives intuition. But just this is detrimental to the understanding. It then cognizes of the many objects only very little and confusedly.",["Anschauung = intuition; Vernunftbeurtheilung = \"estimation of reason\" (Beurteilung = estimation; the glossary also gives \"judgment\").","\"dieses\" (\"loses this again\") has no stated antecedent; kept as \"this\"."]],"philippi-s11-017":["Logical perfection demands abstraction, I am to separate: aesthetic association, I am to heap objects together according to a symmetry: the former with the aim of cognizing distinctly, the latter with the aim of cognizing intuitively. The poet, the more objects he heaps together, in proportion, the more stirring he becomes. A concert is so much the more agreeable the more instruments sound together.",["Ebenmaaß = \"symmetry\" (not in the glossary); Proportion = proportion.","\"anschauend zu erkennen\" = \"to cognize intuitively\" (anschauend, a participle, rendered as an adverb); Absicht = aim.","Aßociation = association."]],"philippi-s11-018":["In the unification of these two perfections one should be cautious. There are cognitions that do not belong to taste although we reckon everything to taste. A mathematical demonstration of a mechanical theorem can have beauty; but the beauty lies in the simplicity. One rejoices that a truth that one held to be so involved and difficult one has insight into so distinctly all at once. There are beauties that can be had insight into only through the understanding, although the understanding otherwise does not judge the beautiful. There are beauties that it only has insight into, that depend neither on taste nor on fashion: and those are the eternal beauties.",["einsehen = \"have insight into\" (Einsicht = insight); richten = \"judge\" (not beurteilen).","Lehrsatz = theorem; Simplicität (Latinate) and einfach both = \"simple\"/\"simplicity\".","\"Mode\" = fashion; \"dependiren\" = \"depend\".","Word order of \"die man für so verwickelt ... einsiehet\" is kept; the sentence is awkward in the German."]],"philippi-s12-001":["Perfections of cognition",["Erkenntniß = cognition."]],"philippi-s12-002":["Truth is the general perfection. No cognition can be cognition if it is not true.",["generale = general."]],"philippi-s12-003":["Truth can go",[]],"philippi-s12-004":["1. to the object.",["Object (Latinate) = object; Gegenstand elsewhere is also \"object\"."]],"philippi-s12-005":["2. to the subject.",[]],"philippi-s12-006":["In the fable truth is not in so far as the narrative is actual, but only in relation to the subject in so far as it is most easily and most exactly comprehended by the same. Subjective tru-There is a real difference between perfection with regard to quantity; this rests on the magnitude and extensiveness of cognition and between perfection of quality, which rests on the exactness of cognition. The former, the great cognition, the polyhistor seeks, the thorough head on the other hand seeks to make cognition perfect as to quality.",["OCR: \"Eabel\" read as Fabel; \"derQuantität\" and \"Weitläuf tigkeit\" repaired.","\"Subjective Wahr-\" is a broken fragment (probably a catchword or stray heading) before \"Es ist\"; translated as it stands.","wirklich = actual (first sentence) and \"real\" (\"wirklicher Unterschied\"): one German word, two English words.","\"die Erkenntniß der Qualität nach vollkommen zu machen\" is garbled or elliptical; the rendering \"cognition perfect as to quality\" follows the likely sense of the word order. begreifen = comprehend; Größe = magnitude."]],"philippi-s12-007":["The means of logical perfection is distinctness; that of the aesthetic is what makes intuition easier and more manifold.",["Mittel = means; Deutlichkeit = distinctness; mannigfaltig = manifold (adjective)."]],"philippi-s13-001":["§ 29",[]],"philippi-s13-002":["Certainty is the degree of truth and is in both. In the logical I look to the certainty of the object, in the aesthetic to that of the subject. How is one to unite these two? Which are the means? Which must go first, logical or aesthetic perfection, taste or understanding? Everything is subjected to the understanding. This assesses the thing according to its constitution, what it is; but taste according to the appearance, how the thing stirs the subject. Logical perfection is therefore the essential one and to be preferred, e.g. in history logical perfection consists in the correct disposition, in distinctness and certainty; distinctness is a means of truth; the aesthetic, however, in this, that I embellish, as Voltaire in Charles XII; it serves to make agreeable, but teaches nothing.",["Gewißheit = certainty; beurtheilen = assess; Beschaffenheit = constitution; Erscheinung = appearance.","\"Dieser\" (understanding) and \"die aesthetische\" (perfection) are supplied by gender only; kept as pronouns and adjective.","\"Disposition\" kept as in the German (Latinate)."]],"philippi-s13-003":["Logical perfection is the only one under which it is permitted to make a cognition aesthetically perfect.",[]],"philippi-s13-004":["Here there are two wrong paths",["Abwege = \"wrong paths\" (literally paths leading away)."]],"philippi-s13-005":["The rules of sensibility consist",["Sinnlichkeit = sensibility."]],"philippi-s13-006":["1. in sensation.",["Empfindung = sensation."]],"philippi-s13-007":["2. in intuition.",["Anschauung = intuition."]],"philippi-s13-008":["Every human being whose mind is moved or whose feeling is stirred or excited is no longer capable of reflecting. From this it is clear that the stirring is contrary to logical perfection. For one must be entirely calm if one is to assess an object correctly. But logical perfection can make use of the beautiful form in order to awaken attention, i.e. of expressions, clothing, simplicity of the sublime objects. Through stirring one accomplishes nothing. The understanding remains empty and gets nothing to think. The nerves are only agreeably shaken, and when this is past one knows nothing. It is just as when in a company a violent laughter arises. The moment it goes away from it and one looks at another, and does not know what he has laughed about.",["Gemüth = mind; Gefühl = feeling; Rührung = stirring; \"capable\" and \"reflectiren\" (reflect) are Latinate in the German.","\"Den Augenblick gehet sie davon\": \"sie\" could be the company or the laughter (or the stirring); kept as \"it\", ambiguous.","Ausdrücke = expressions; Einkleidung = clothing; Gesellschafft = company; weiß = know (Wissen)."]],"philippi-s13-009":["Truth can be",[]],"philippi-s13-010":["1. aesthetic in the appearance and the means to it liveliness",["OCR: \"Erscheinungund\" split as \"Erscheinung und\". Lebhafftigkeit = liveliness."]],"philippi-s13-011":["2. logical in the concepts, and the condition, distinctness.",["Begriffen = concepts; Bedingung = condition."]],"philippi-s13-012":["Logical distinctness arises from abstraction, aesthetic liveliness from association.",["Aßociation = association."]],"philippi-s14-001":["§30",[]],"philippi-s14-002":["The perfection of a cognition in so far as it refers to the will of the human being and stands in relation with it is practical perfection. Those perfections were theoretical; the logical speculative and the aesthetic not speculative: both theoretical and without relation. This is practical in so far as it goes to the choice of the will. Without this perfection the cognition is idle, it may well have sequels, but without relation to the will. A logically perfect and not practical cognition is nothing but speculation. An aesthetic and not practical perfection of cognition tickles the understanding. Cognitions are idle (sterile) not because they supply us no thoughts, but because they have no influence on our activity. The practical perfection is the most important; those that are not practical we can do without.",["OCR: \"ihrn\" read as ihm/ihr (the will); \"Hefern\" as liefern; \"kützelt\" as kitzelt; \"Sinnhchkeit\" etc. repaired.","Willkühr = \"choice\" (not in the glossary); Wille = will. Verhältniß/Relation both = \"relation\"; Folgen = \"sequels\" (Folge); Thätigkeit = activity.","\"müssig (steril)\" kept with the parenthesis; \"Spekulation\" = speculation."]],"philippi-s14-003":["Logical perfection in and for itself is separated from practical perfection; hence one cannot ask in the former how does this relate to my doing. I ask only after the rules of insight. I want only to learn insights, of whatever things it may be.",["\"es sey von welchen Dingen es wolle\" kept literally (impersonal \"it\"); Ding = thing; Einsicht = insight."]],"philippi-s14-004":["A cognition is practical if it has an influence on our choice and indeed",["Passage breaks off; continued by the following items. Willkühr = choice."]],"philippi-s14-005":["1. Subjectively, in so far as it has incentives of sensibility; in so far as it actually moves us.",["OCR: \"Subiectiue\" and \"Sinnhchkeit\" repaired. Triebfedern = \"incentives\" (not in the glossary). wirklich = actually."]],"philippi-s14-006":["2. objectively, in so far as it has grounds by which we cognize how it should happen. Objective practical cognition shows how we should proceed, subjective practical cognition indicates the incentives according to which we do actual actions. To subjective practical cognition there belongs therefore the doctrine of the affects. Morals has to do with objective practical grounds. The orator must act according to subjective practical rules. Often one must deceive.",["\"wie sie geschehen solle\": \"sie\" may refer to the cognition or to something unstated (e.g. the action); kept as \"it\".","Gründe = grounds; Lehre = doctrine; Affekten = affects; wirkliche Handlungen = \"actual actions\" (wirklich)."]],"philippi-s14-007":["All these perfections so far are now taken together in the author.",["Vollkommenheit = perfection."]],"philippi-s14-008":["Logical perfection is seldom mixed with the aesthetic, because they do harm to one another. One brings forward probability because it is easy for us. On the other hand, something can be thorough, convincing and not aesthetically perfect with it, as, e.g., in metaphysics.",["Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability (OCR 'Wahrscheinlich -keit' joined). 'überzeugend' (cf. Überzeugung = conviction) rendered 'convincing'.","'dabey' rendered 'with it'; the German does not say what it refers to (the something, or its being convincing)."]],"philippi-s14-009":["A cognition can be practically perfect and yet logically imperfect, e.g. a proof of the existence of God.",["Erkenntniß = cognition; Beweiß = proof; Daseyn = existence (Dasein)."]],"philippi-s14-010":["A cognition can be objectively practical and not subjective. Most sermons are constituted thus. They always show what one ought to do; how one ought to do it even with pleasure, but it remains nevertheless without benefit. I cannot do it; I do not see it sensibly. I would gladly do it; indeed I wish to have pleasure in it.",["OCR 'sinnlieh' corrected to 'sinnlich'. 'beschaffen' = constituted (cf. Beschaffenheit). Nutzen = benefit."]],"philippi-s14-011":["One keeps on admonishing continually. One always says one should be thus and so. The listener wants it too, he wishes it, but can bring nothing to actuality. Attention should therefore be paid to how one could bring forth, from the grounds of the understanding, also incentives of sensibility, how one could clothe the presentation with pleasure and add to the objective practical grounds also subjective practical grounds. Logical cognition gives insight; aesthetic not at all. Logical cognition must be laid as the ground; first one must learn something actual, and then know how to clothe beautifully the real thing learned. Those cognitions that we need are so easy to comprehend that the scholar has no greater insight into them than the common understanding with respect to *praxis*, e.g. the cognition of God and all practical cognition. Practical perfection is very brief; and all our practical moral cognitions can be brought to very few formulas. In contrast, the plan of speculative cognitions is immeasurable. These speculative cognitions have only a conventional value, e.g. in society. There is something imitated according to universal laws. The customary is what agrees with something universal yet contingent, or what agrees in this, that we adapt ourselves to one another. If one imitates the customary, that is not beautiful, it serves only to prevent aversion; the customary does not belong to the beautiful. The beautiful should always be uncustomary; *vid.* Thomas Abbt.",["Wirklichkeit = actuality; wirklich = actual; real(e) = real. Verstand = understanding; gemeine Verstand = common understanding. Triebfedern = incentives (not in glossary). Einsicht = insight (not in glossary).","'in Absicht auf' rendered 'with respect to'; Absicht is otherwise 'aim'. 'Praxin' is the Latin accusative of *praxis*.","'dem Gelehrte reale' / 'das Gelehrte reale': syntax obscure ('real' may be an adjective of 'das Gelehrte' or an adverbial addition); rendered 'the real thing learned'.","allgemein = universal; gebräuchlich = customary; Gebräuchliche = the customary. 'vid.' kept in Latin."]],"philippi-s14-012":["Something can be usual and not customary.",["gewöhnlich = usual; gebräuchlich = customary."]],"philippi-s14-013":["The beautiful is a matter of genius and absolutely not a matter of imitation. It is just the opposite of all imitation. Imitation can serve as no ground of the beautiful. The authors who have written according to independent and not fashionable laws serve only to test one's taste by them. There were often fashionable laws that authors followed, as e.g. Lohenstein, Voiture, that are entirely opposed to the laws of beauty. They go constantly only toward stimulating the feeling and making a noise in the mind. But to feeling no understanding belongs; to taste understanding belongs: this is therefore an *analogon* of the understanding. Animals also have feeling but not taste; for they have no understanding. In short, what is contemptible, what is disgusting, is imitated. One becomes in the end so accustomed through imitating that one can only make according to models and bring forth nothing out of oneself.",["'die' in 'die den Gesetzen der Schönheit ganz entgegen sind' could refer to the laws or to the authors; 'that' preserves this. 'Sie' in the next sentence: the authors.","'reitzen' (Reiz = charm) rendered 'stimulating'. 'eckelhafftist' in OCR read as 'eckelhafft ist'. Gemüth = mind; Gefühl = feeling; *analogon* is Latin in the source."]],"philippi-s14-014":["Nothing is more repugnant, nothing more contemptible than what has been imitated. What does not arise from myself cannot be imputed to me. The chief condition of all beauties is ease; it is, according to rules of sensibility, a ground of satisfaction. Everything must present itself, as it were, of itself and quite easily to our sensible comprehensibility. But how anxious, how painfully sought out do the beauties of imitation not look. One can already see it in it, with what anxiety and constraint the writer seeks to please, and one pities him that he displeases precisely thereby.",["Gemächlichkeit = 'ease'; Wohlgefallen = 'satisfaction' (not in glossary); Faßlichkeit = 'comprehensibility'. OCR 'ängstüch' read as 'ängstlich'.","'ihr' in 'Man sieht es ihr schon an' refers to the feminine Nachahmung (imitation), rendered 'it'."]],"philippi-s14-015":["The good is the essential ground of the beautiful. Satisfaction is only an accidental one, and the grounds of it contingent.",["OCR 'eßentiale' read as 'essentiale'; 'accidentaler' implies Grund understood. zufällig = contingent; Wohlgefallen = satisfaction."]],"philippi-s14-016":["If now the beautiful is long contrived, so that it is to please; then this is ridiculous, because it is contingent. One should have applied the effort to the good. The beautiful must be uncontrived, or at least it must seem not to be contrived.",["gekünstelt = contrived. 'lang' is as read; it may be an OCR or transcript slip, left as it stands."]],"philippi-s14-017":["The petit-maître, master in trifles, does not keep to use. Yet everything agrees together in him toward what pleases.",["Petitmaitre kept as in the source, spelled 'petit-maître'. Gebrauch = use (glossary); it is related to gebräuchlich = customary in the neighboring passages."]],"philippi-s14-018":["Nothing causes more vexation than the sought-out, where the painful sweat of anxiety and the gloomy rubbing of the brow shines forth. But where a beauty seems to have arisen of itself without the slightest effort; that pleases. Lightness is always very dear to us. Painful order is not light but heavy. Whoever writes in a sought-out way writes as little according to taste as he who dresses and adorns himself anxiously does it according to taste. Lightness, loftiness, self-command, a certain kind of disorder and negligence, in that one does not heed trifles in order to be all the more exact in what is more important: that is pleasing. When one hears a sermon or is present at a play, and one notices that something is sought-out, then it no longer pleases us at once. An anxious rhymester displeases in the highest degree; but with what pleasure one reads Pope's verses where the rhymes seem to have flowed naturally and from the matter itself. Simplicity is opposed to the sought-out and contrived. All beauty must have simplicity, without it there can be no true beauty. Everything is agreeable that seems to have cost no effort and seems to have arisen of itself. Simplicity is meanwhile not opposed to prolixity and manifoldness. To true beauty this also belongs.",["Einfalt = simplicity (cf. einfach = simple); Einfältigkeit appears in the next passage. Gesucht = sought-out; Mannigfaltigkeit = manifoldness; Weitläufigkeit = prolixity (not in glossary).","OCR 'Nachlaß igkeit' read as 'Nachlässigkeit'; 'Schau spiel' as 'Schauspiel'. 'Wo ... hervorscheint' rendered 'shines forth' as in the source."]],"philippi-s14-019":["Naivety is yet something other than simplicity. It consists in simple-mindedness, insofar as it is characteristic and has arisen from the object and cannot be taught. Thus unpainted honesty in the human being is naivety. One always rejoices when one catches sight of nature. Art is always suspect, that it deceives and betrays us.",["Einfalt = simplicity; Einfältigkeit = simple-mindedness (the difference is not interpreted). Objekt = object. 'am Menschen' = 'in the human being'."]],"philippi-s14-020":["It is unpleasant when one wants to spread still more charm over objects that already in themselves by nature have far too much charm; when one burdens simple nature, which has the greatest agreeablenesses quite easily, with foreign beauties gathered together by art, in order to make it still more agreeable and charming. Into this fault, into this turgidity of beauties, the present age of German poets has fallen. Meadows, gardens, voluptuousness itself, things that in nature have all too much charm, one strives in competition to make still more charming. One should rather give the charm to the things that are deprived of all charm, like the essential beauty, virtue. That would produce benefit. But that is also not as easy as it is to stun the grounds of approval through cold reason, and to stifle them through tickling charm. Let them give charm, to the contempt of all charm. Everything seeks to destroy the charm of human society. Now step forth, poets, and restore the half-lost charm. But just as it is easy to flatter a tyrant, but great art is required to tell him the truth so that he does not take it amiss; so it is also easy to flatter sensibility with charm, but very hard to represent to it the reflections that the calm understanding makes about it so that it does not take it amiss. Certainly great art belongs to representing to sensibility even only the true judgments of the understanding, so that it does not become vexed, let alone to making them charming.",["Reiz = charm; reizend = charming; Billigung = approval; Reflectiones kept as 'reflections' (Überlegung = reflection otherwise).","'Laßt sie Reiz geben, der Verachtung alles Reizes': 'sie' has no clear antecedent, and 'der Verachtung' is dative; rendered literally, sense doubtful (possibly corrupt or elliptical).","'ihr'/'sie' in the last sentences refer to Sinnlichkeit (sensibility); 'es' in 'daß er es nicht übel nimmt' refers to the truth. Urtheile = judgments."]],"philippi-s14-021":["On the other section",["'andern' could be 'second' or 'other'; rendered 'other'. Heading only."]],"philippi-s14-022":["On the extensiveness of learned cognition",["Weitläuftigkeit: rendered \"extensiveness\"; the German can also mean prolixity."]],"philippi-s14-023":["He whose goods do not suffice for his needs is poor. Among the learned there is found paltriness, for the desire for knowledge is not sated.",["Armseeligkeit (and armseelig in s14-025) is \"paltriness\"/\"paltry\", kept distinct from arm = \"poor\". Wißbegierde is not in the glossary; rendered \"desire for knowledge\".","\"Der dessen Güter\" is read as \"Der, dessen Güter\"; the relative pronoun is left as in the German."]],"philippi-s14-024":["The paltriness of our philosophical cognition comes from this, that one acts proud of one's wisdom.",["OCR \"phüosophischen\" corrected to \"philosophischen\"."]],"philippi-s14-025":["One calls someone poor in proportion to his station. A prince can possess many goods and yet be poor as prince. Whoever has as much cognition as he needs to attain his end is not poor. But if there is presupposed a cognition that is possible for the whole faculty of the human being, and the human being, who has a calling to it, does not seek to attain as much as is possible for him; then the cognition is paltry, because it does not reach the degree that it could and should reach.",["OCR \"armseehg\" read as \"armseelig\".","Beruf = \"calling\" (not in the glossary). Vermögen = \"faculty\"; Zweck = \"end\"; Grad = \"degree\"."]],"philippi-s14-026":["Ignorance is",["Unwissenheit = \"ignorance\"."]],"philippi-s14-027":["1. of things. When the objects are unknown; then one cannot assess them. All logical, practical and aesthetic cognition is lacking. That is *ignorantia quoad materiam*.",["Sachen = \"things\", the same English word as for Dinge (later \"grounds of things\"). Object = \"object\". beurteilen = \"assess\".","Latin phrase left in Latin."]],"philippi-s14-028":["2. Of form, of the connection of cognitions with the understanding, with taste and with *praxis*. If one does not know the grounds or the combination, that is *ignorantia quoad formam*.",["Verknüpfung = \"connection\"; Verbindung = \"combination\". Praxi is the Latin dative of *praxis*, rendered in the nominative form here. Kennt = \"know\"."]],"philippi-s14-029":["Formal ignorance is either",[]],"philippi-s14-030":["1. Ignorance of the grounds of cognitions.",[]],"philippi-s14-031":["2. Ignorance of the grounds of things. E.g. In history, to prove that something is, one must know grounds of cognition, and to prove why something is, grounds of things.",["The German is elliptical (\"muß man Gründe der Erkenntniß und zu beweisen warum etwas sey, Gründe der Dinge wissen\"), and the English keeps the construction. Historie = \"history\"."]],"philippi-s14-032":["There are cognitions with the grounds of cognition. Thus Copernicus cognized our solar world and had grounds of the cognition. He showed what happens there.",["Erkenntniß and Erkenntnisses (genitive) are both rendered \"cognition\". Sonnenwelt = \"solar world\"."]],"philippi-s14-033":["But there are also cognitions with the grounds of things. Thus Newton cognized the Copernican system, but also at the same time showed the grounds of things, whence the heavenly bodies move thus, through his system of gravitation; *vid.* Euler.",["OCR \"tue\" read as \"die\". \"Eulerum\" is the Latin accusative (\"see Euler\"), rendered \"Euler\"."]],"philippi-s14-034":["In order, therefore, to drive out formal ignorance one must",[]],"philippi-s14-035":["1. seek out grounds of cognition.",[]],"philippi-s14-036":["2. Grounds of things. These latter are often unknown to us. Philosophy occupies itself with them; but grounds of cognition one demands also of common cognition. With all cognition one asks first whether it is also true, and then, what ground does it have. The geniuses of human beings are different and one can judge them by this. Some ask after the grounds of things. The greatest part of human beings is very indifferent toward the grounds of cognition; they do not investigate whether it is also actually true. Hence comes the general credulity. One ponders, one researches how this or that, which one finds recounted, was possible, and does not let it occur to oneself to ask whether it also actually happened. Against this one must be well on guard. One should ask first what ground the narration has and then one should seek to spy out the grounds of things.",["OCR \"wras\" read as \"was\"; \"fürzusehen\" read as \"vorzusehen\" (sich vorsehen, \"be on guard\").","wirklich = \"actually\"; gemein = \"common\"; allgemein = \"general\"; Theil = \"part\".","\"bey beurtheilen\" (\"hier bey\") is rendered \"by this\" and is ambiguous in the German as to what is the standard."]],"philippi-s15-001":["§43",[]],"philippi-s15-002":["An ignorance can be unavoidable and necessary. Physically unavoidable is the ignorance of that which we do not cognize through our senses, e.g. what sort of creatures are on the moon; how the state after death, how that after the world, will be. And just this is what one asks after most eagerly and most often. Such questions after impossible cognitions are a business in which mediocre scholars amuse themselves, who just thereby only show how much they do not know.",["\"wie derjenige nach der Welt seyn werde\": \"that\" (derjenige) refers back to Zustand. The German is kept elliptical.","Gelehrte = \"scholars\" (elsewhere \"the learned\"). Sinne = \"senses\"."]],"philippi-s15-003":["But not through *tentiren*, not *per principia empirica*, but from general grounds to determine the boundaries of the cognition of the understanding and of reason and to see distinctly in which cases our understanding must necessarily be ignorant, that is true philosophy. That is what metaphysics does, which has for object pure understanding and its limitations. It is a study of the subject; hence it attains its end completely if through rules of the understanding it can show how far pure understanding extends, and if it explains the laws of it. With it we learn to see our necessary ignorance from the nature of reason. O an excellent science! That is indeed true wisdom, that we learn our vocation and the degree of perfection that is suited to us and of which we are capable. That teaches morals, this metaphysics. These sciences will also be brought to perfection; through metaphysics nothing at all will be invented.",["*tentiren* is a Germanized Latin verb (\"to attempt\"), left as in the text. OCR \"0\" read as \"O\"; \"Subjekt ;\" spacing normalized.","einsehen = \"see\" (not in the glossary), used consistently; Grenzen = \"boundaries\" and Schranken = \"limitations\". Weltweisheit does not occur: Philosophie = \"philosophy\".","Bestimmung = \"vocation\" here; \"determination\" is the other possible rendering. \"Perfection\" (Latinate, later) = Vollkommenheit and *Perfection* both rendered \"perfection\".","erfunden = \"invented\" (\"discovered\" is also possible). \"Jenes ... dies\" is rendered literally as \"That ... this\"."]],"philippi-s16-001":["§44",[]],"philippi-s16-002":["On the horizon of learned cognition.",[]],"philippi-s16-003":["One must begin from the large in every cognition, thereby arises combination with the small and all parts. If one learns geography, one begins from the globe. We will designate the boundaries logically through the standard of reason, practically through the end of the will.",["Maaßstab = \"standard\" (literally a measuring-rod). Verbindung = \"combination\"."]],"philippi-s16-004":["This belongs preeminently to logic.",[]],"philippi-s16-005":["The horizon of human cognition is the domain of the cognitions that are suited to human perfection. If cognitions agree in magnitude and quality with the rules of perfection and at the same time are suited to the boundaries of the understanding; then these are what constitute the horizon of human cognition, beyond which one cannot and should not go. The sciences are bounded either through the faculty of the human being, so that some cannot be cognized by him, because they exceed his faculty; or through the vocation and end of the human being, so that some may not be cognized by him, because they do not belong to his end.",["Umfang = \"domain\"; Größe = \"magnitude\"; begränzt = \"bounded\"; Bestimmung = \"vocation\" (alternative: \"determination\").","\"dürfen nicht\" = \"may not\" (are not permitted to); \"können nicht\" = \"cannot\"."]],"philippi-s16-006":["The sphere of the cognition of the human being aesthetically determined, is in accord with delusion, but not with the end of the human being.",["determinirt = \"determined\". Wahn = \"delusion\" (not in the glossary). The sentence is left as elliptical as the German."]],"philippi-s16-007":["If the boundaries of cognition are congruent with the boundaries of perfection; then the district that they enclose is the horizon determined for the human being. This horizon of human cognition will therefore be",["OCR \"Vollkonimenheit\" read as \"Vollkommenheit\". congruiren = \"are congruent\". Bezirk = \"district\"."]],"philippi-s16-008":["1. logical, insofar as it is determined through the relation of cognition to faculty.",[]],"philippi-s16-009":["2. practical, insofar as it is determined through the relation of cognition to the end, to the will, to moral perfection. Logical, if I assume the domain of the faculty of the human being, that is his capacity, as the standard. But because not everything belongs to our end that would be possible to acquire through our faculty, there must still be added the determination of the horizon according to the worth, the goodness (*Bonität*), the utility and the agreement of the cognition with the end; that is the practical determination of the horizon. The wise one sets the boundaries of his horizon not through his faculty, but through his rational end, not dynamically, but teleologically. He consults his perfection and draws his horizon only around the cognitions that are suited to it.",["Fähigkeit = \"capacity\"; Vermögen = \"faculty\"; Bestimmung = \"determination\" here. Bonität is a Germanized Latin word (*bonitas*), glossed \"goodness\".","\"zu Rath ziehen\" = \"consult\". \"Der Weise\" = \"the wise one\"."]],"philippi-s16-010":["Perfection is theoretically according to the magnitude of the faculty, practically according to the degree of perfection.",["The German is left as it stands; the repetition of \"perfection\" is in the text."]],"philippi-s16-011":["There are degrees of cognitions, beyond which we can do nothing more, 1, understand 2, see 3. comprehend.",["verstehen = \"understand\"; einsehn = \"see\" (see note on einsehen above); begreifen = \"comprehend\". The syntax of \"nichts mehr können, 1, verstehen ...\" is obscure and is preserved; whether the list names the degrees or what we can do is left open."]],"philippi-s16-012":["The circle of the objects that I can cognize is called the logical horizon of cognitions. *Congruentia cognitionis cum perfectione humana* (congruence of cognition with human perfection).",["The Latin phrase is kept in Latin; the English in parentheses is added only as a gloss. OCR \"Obiecte\" read as \"Objecte\"."]],"philippi-s16-013":["When one speaks of the horizon of human cognition and I am to determine it, then I must distinguish the horizon of the cognition of the whole human race from that of the cognition of a single human being. If I speak of the horizon of the cognition of the human race, then I understand by it the domain of the cognitions that are possible to human beings, and indeed *alternative* not *collective*. Then the horizon is very great, e.g. one can be great in history, the other in mathematics and so on, and so on. If I now sum up all these cognitions of different subjects, then a true sum, but not a whole, comes out, because the parts are not combined. Like the lands of the earth, so the understanding of human beings bears different fruits. Provision is made for human beings in respect of understanding as in respect of inclinations. Carefully and kindly providence has so distributed the capacities as well as inclinations, that all kinds of the sciences and arts are well filled. If one counts all this together, what a multitude! Those who take together what many others understand, and unite it in themselves, are called *Polyhistores*. Such a one was Leibniz. The horizon of the human race is not possible to determine.",["Erkenntniß = cognition; Umfang = domain; verstehen/Verstand = understand/understanding; Menge = multitude; Vorsicht = providence; Geschlecht = race.","Fähigkeiten = capacities (Fähigkeit); the glossary's separate 'capacity' for Vermögen is not at issue in this sentence.","*alternative*, *collective* are Latinate adverbs; italicized as foreign words. 'verbunden' = combined (Verbindung = combination).","'Subjekt' rendered 'subject'; 'Polyhistores' left in Latin form, italicized."]],"philippi-s16-014":["But how will one determine the horizon of a single human being? To be able to do this one must see 1. of what degree 2. of what kind of cognition he is capable; thus his faculty and his end. Human beings do not at all acquire cognitions in accordance with their horizon; for they do not know their faculty, not their future inclinations and thus also not the utility that they will draw from this or another cognition. Here we see, then, that we cognize the horizon in general well, but that of a single one with difficulty.",["OCR 'l' after 'bestimmen' read as a question mark.","Vermögen = faculty; Zweck = end; Grad = degree; Art = kind; fähig = capable; Nutzen = utility.","'welches Grades ... welcher Art der Erkenntniß er fähig ist' is kept with its double construction as in the German."]],"philippi-s16-015":["I. To be determined is the horizon in general logically, what the human being can cognize. Nothing is more ridiculous than to exert oneself to do something, since the end is indeed not possible to our faculty. If we have more than once experienced our powers through trials, then we see that it was not possible for us. Then we trust our powers too much that what they do not reach is also beyond the powers of human beings: for we often see that much that was impossible for us has been invented by others. A science that draws for us the boundaries of cognition with certainty is difficult; for from empirical grounds one cannot determine that, because ever more is invented. One must therefore do it from grounds *a priori*. To determine the boundaries of historical cognition is not possible, as of all sensible cognitions, but which degree of cognition is possible and which objects belong to it is possible. Metaphysics shows, namely, which objects belong to the intellectual and which degree can be attained.",["OCR slips corrected silently (möglieh, Mensehen, the stray period after 'war').","Kräfte/Kräfften = powers (Kraft); Vermögen = faculty; Grenzen = boundaries; Gründe = grounds.","Objecte = objects; here one English word for Object (Latinate) as for Gegenstand elsewhere.","'dann sehen wir daß es uns nicht möglich war' and the following 'Dann trauen wir ...' are kept as they stand; the logical connection is obscure in the German."]],"philippi-s16-016":["Metaphysical prattlers make use of this science on infinite questions, to remain owing no answer. For them it is a treasure chamber from which they fetch decisive answers to all questions. But this is philosophy, when through a correct cognition of the boundaries of reason I prove that these and other questions cannot be solved at all; to be sure it does not teach anything positive but it proves that it is no problem of reason. It pursues the question to its origin, and then sees how it arose, and whether it is not *applicable* to the natural powers. That is the most sublime of speculations. The circle of cognition of a human being can thus be drawn, so that he has insight that it is impossible for him to cognize this or that in accordance with the constitution of his understanding. Such a metaphysics holds us back not only from the useless but also from error. The greatest errors of theologians and moralists come from the fact that metaphysics was held to be a dogmatics of all propositions elevated above sensibility, all pure intellectual. Spinoza was not in his conduct as he was in theory. Merely the perverse opinion of the capacity of reason brought him to such strange figments of the brain.",["'auf unendliche Fragen' could attach to 'bedienen' or to 'keine Antwort schuldig zu bleiben'; the English keeps the placement.","Philosophie = philosophy (not Weltweisheit); Problem = problem (Latinate, as Aufgabe in the glossary).","'auf gelöset' read as 'aufgelöst'; 'Irrthüiner' as 'Irrthümer'; 'Metaphysick/Dogmatick' rendered 'metaphysics/dogmatics'.","'alles pure intellectuale' left as an unconnected apposition; Satz = proposition; Fähigkeit = capacity; Beschaffenheit = constitution.","Hirngespinste rendered literally as 'figments of the brain'."]],"philippi-s16-017":["Logically we determine the horizon through the faculty.",["Vermögen = faculty."]],"philippi-s16-018":["Practically through the ends.",["Zwecke = ends."]],"philippi-s16-019":["II. The practical horizon can be determined",["Passage breaks off here; the next passages continue it."]],"philippi-s16-020":["1. pragmatically according to utility.",[]],"philippi-s16-021":["2. morally according to wisdom.",[]],"philippi-s16-022":["The practical horizon can thus be determined 1. according to rules of prudence 2. according to rules of wisdom. According to these Rousseau exerted himself to determine it.",["Klugheit = prudence; Weißheit = wisdom."]],"philippi-s16-023":["Horizon of cognition is like the optical field of vision. Every human being has a piece that he surveys. All human beings on the earth have a very great horizon in the sciences. There human beings summed together cognize very much, but that cannot produce any special utility; if one has these scattered sciences together; that one can produce utility.",["'sonderlichen' rendered 'special' (cf. besonder).","'wenn einer ... der kann' kept with its punctuation; 'einer' may be read as 'someone' or 'one (person)'."]],"philippi-s16-024":["I. The horizon of historical cognitions is not possible to determine exactly, although to a certain extent. What comes newly added, of the old is forgotten. The old falls ever more into oblivion. Only by name is a Nabopolassar etc. etc. known. One makes compendia out of the great histories, these swell up again gradually so high that our grandchildren will have to make compendia. Thus the old is always drawn together and cut out. The human being cannot grasp everything. History creates only objects for rational cognition.",["Gegenstände = objects (Gegenstand).","'ausgeschaltet' rendered 'cut out'; 'Kompendia' = compendia.","'Was von neuem hinzukommt, das vergißt sich vom Alten' is awkward in the German and kept so."]],"philippi-s16-025":["2. The horizon of rational cognition",["Fragmentary heading; no verb."]],"philippi-s16-026":["a. of mathematics, which the brevity of human life determines. One therefore reasonably believes that not much more will be invented. For one spends one's lifetime before one knows what has already been invented, before one comes to the point from which one sets out. Hence a man who lived about 200 years and had cognized everything in mathematics could make more inventions therein than others who live an ordinary time in 12000 years. Commonly just as one comes to the standpoint where he can go out for inventions, he dies; the other must begin again from the beginning, he cannot continue where that one left off.",["'den bestimmt die Kürze' : 'den' is the accusative object (the horizon), the brevity the subject; kept.","Erfindungen = inventions; erkennen = cognize."]],"philippi-s16-027":["b. The horizon of philosophy is determined by metaphysics.",["OCR 'Metaplrysik' read as 'Metaphysik'. Philosophie = philosophy."]],"philippi-s16-028":["This would be a consideration of the logical horizon; but the practical horizon is much smaller. Rousseau sought to determine it. There is much there that we may not know, indeed that we must not know. But why do we have faculties for it? Rousseau says: Nature gave it to us so that we should not have need of it; but if we stray, it is to serve us as a means to help ourselves again: like an emetic and all medicinal plants that nature has given us, so that we should not have need to use them.",["OCR 'l' after 'Vermögen dazu' read as a question mark.","'nicht wissen dürfen' / 'nicht wissen müssen' rendered literally ('may not' / 'must not'); the German negated dürfen/müssen can also mean 'need not'.","Betrachtung = consideration; Vermögen = faculties; Mittel = means."]],"philippi-s16-029":["To the rational horizon belong only the objects that give forth concepts from themselves and occasion a reflection.",["Gegenstände = objects; Begriffe = concepts; Reflexion = reflection (Latinate; Überlegung is the glossary's other 'reflection')."]],"philippi-s16-030":["The more the sciences are restricted to a small number, the greater the perfection to which they can attain. The sciences find rich worth, the more they become general, and can be grasped through a playing wit.",["OCR 'Wissen-Schäften' read as 'Wissenschaften'.","'allgemein' rendered 'general'; restringirt = restricted; Vollkommenheit = perfection."]],"philippi-s17-001":["§45",[]],"philippi-s17-002":["The author says of a cognition that it is beyond our horizon, only insofar as it is logically determined, or insofar as the cognition exceeds our capacity, e.g. what our soul was before birth, where it was, that is beyond the horizon. What fashions are there on Saturn? As our eye here has certain boundaries and as astronomy has them, in that the senses do not suffice any further: so the understanding too has them. Milton seems to have set foot in the *Limbum vanitatis* (the rim of vanity).",["OCR 'l' after 'Saturn für Moden' read as a question mark.","Fähigkeit = capacity; Grenzen = boundaries; Verstand = understanding; zureichen rendered 'suffice'.","*Limbum vanitatis* kept in the accusative as in the text; Kant's parenthetical gloss kept."]],"philippi-s17-003":["When we see how small our cognition is here, it makes us anxious to remain in our true world, we swing ourselves up into ideal worlds. It is good to keep to the horizon, and it is also possible to determine it precisely. There are not many capacities that do not conduce to our happiness and benefit. What has no *respect* to our happiness, for that we also do not have much capacity. When the human being departs from his vocation, he wavers like the ass in Aesop standing between 2 bundles of hay. What need has he to wander about with his lustful eye in that which cannot be cognized by him.",["Erkenntniß = cognition; Fähigkeit = capacity; Bestimmung = vocation (glossary offers 'determination' or 'vocation'; 'vocation' chosen for the sense of 'aus seiner Bestimmung geht'); determiniren = determine.","'respicirt' is a Latinate German verb (respicere); rendered *respect* (v.) in italics as a foreign-derived word; 'Heubünden' = bundles of hay.","'ist uns ängstlich ... zu bleiben': the construction is impersonal and left as in the German; the comma splice before 'wir schwingen' is preserved.","'idealische Welten' rendered 'ideal worlds'."]],"philippi-s18-001":["§46 §47",[]],"philippi-s18-002":["The author names only the practical determination of the horizon, when he declares what is outside the horizon. What is harmful and useless is beneath its horizon, according to the term of the author. When the human being, who has obligation to so many things, does the useless, he neglects what is his. But what the useful in a cognition is, is hard to determine. It must be determined 1. morally according to rules of the understanding and 2. pragmatically according to those of prudence.",["Benennung = term; Bestimmung = determination; bestimmen = determine; Verstand = understanding; Erkenntniß = cognition.","'unter seinem Horizont': 'seinem' could refer to the harmful/useless or to the author's horizon; left as 'its horizon' following the German's ambiguity is not fully preservable in English.","Verbindlichkeit = obligation; Klugheit = prudence."]],"philippi-s18-003":["Beginners cannot see in advance the remote use. How could Archimedes and Euclid know that the conic sections would serve for the explanation of the course of the heavenly bodies? Only experience makes it distinct.",["Erklärung = explanation; deutlich = distinct; Erfahrung = experience.","'Nutzen' rendered 'use' here, though Gebrauch is also 'use' in the glossary; both Nutzen and Gebrauch stand for English 'use'. 'Weltkörper' = heavenly bodies (lit. world-bodies)."]],"philippi-s18-004":["A cognition that lets us feel the sharpness of the understanding, that is mathematics. It does not serve civil life; that sounds unworthy and miserly, for one would have to do without it; in evidence it serves, since it shows us the sharpness of the understanding.",["'denn müßte man ihrer entbehren' is obscure (possibly 'then one would have to do without it'); kept literal.","Evidenz rendered 'evidence'; 'in der Evidenz' is left as is. Schärfe = sharpness."]],"philippi-s18-005":["The cognition of morality and ethics has the most exact universal grounds. There the understanding has particular insight; an honor and happiness of humanity, but also a duty of the same, to strive after the cognition of moral laws. The usefulness of cognition is hard to settle. A mathematician invents at random and often his inventions bring inexpressible benefit.",["Sittlichkeit = morality; Moral = ethics (both German words rendered distinctly; 'Moral' also could be 'morals'). Gründe = grounds; Gesetze = laws.","'aufs Gerathe wohl' = at random (lit. 'on the chance'). Nutzen = benefit/use; Nützlichkeit = usefulness.","'allergenaueste allgemeine Gründe': 'allgemein' = universal."]],"philippi-s18-006":["Beneath the horizon. Cognition of nature is to be cognized in all regions. Indeed, if only the other necessary cognitions would permit it, one would encounter the text for morals itself therein. The effects of human folly and of human vices are not worth being cognized by us. What then does the history of heretics serve for? Insects, even dried, still adorn the cabinet, but how do Averdens's chimeras adorn, even though in French bindings? Indeed it serves only to lead some to imitating reveries, others to mockery. What? are we to learn to excoliate our hatred? — Many remnants of the old philosophy are dreams that the dreaming ancients produced. They would laugh, the ancients, if they were to see that we wanted to learn there.",["OCR 'Naturerkennt niß' and 'menschliehen' silently corrected. 'Averdens' (probably a name, perhaps Averroes? or Arnold's) is doubtful; kept as printed. 'Franz -bänden' read as 'Franzbänden' (French bindings).","'exkoliren' (Latin excolere) rendered 'excoliate' to keep the foreign word; 'Wirkungen' = effects; 'Philosophie' = philosophy (Weltweisheit not used).","'Insekten zieren ... aber wie ... zieren?' is syntactically broken in the source; kept as it stands. 'Kezerhistorie' = history of heretics."]],"philippi-s18-007":["Up to here on the horizon with regard to the human race in general; now on the same in respect of age, sex, station in life.",["Geschlecht = 'race' in the first occurrence (menschliches Geschlecht) and 'sex' in the second; one German word, two English words. Absicht = respect here, not 'aim'; Lebensstand = station in life."]],"philippi-s18-008":["I. A child has a different horizon than a man. About after 40 years, when the human being continues to think, he sees things quite differently than before. Then he is no longer so sensitive to charm. The inclinations vanish in him. Before he decided the good according to inclinations, but now he decides it through other marks, he cognizes it in itself. New insights he no longer has, but more application of insights. Now the question arises: are the cognitions to be arranged according to the horizon of the age in which the human being now finds himself, or according to that of the future age, e.g. How many and which cognitions belong for a child of 6 years; should one arrange its horizon according to the future horizon of the man or according to the age in which it is, according to what it is now capable of cognizing, toward which its taste now goes. Rousseau asserts the latter 1. the child then enjoys its life according to its relation and is 2. also prepared for the future horizon 3. It passes its time in contentment and fearlessness toward the future. Such a child may die if it wishes. It has rejoiced in its life. If a human being here conducts well his post, which he now has, he can precisely thereby be well prepared for the future one. But if he strives only after the future, he conducts his post badly and passes his life in unrest. So it goes also with children. But what acquaintance is appropriate to the child's taste and benefit, that is hard to say.",["Merkmal = mark; erkennen = cognize; Kenntnisse = acquaintance (glossary Kenntnis = acquaintance/knowledge), Erkenntnisse = cognitions; behaupten = assert; Einsichten = insights.","'erkennt es an sich selbst' could mean 'cognizes it in itself' or 'in himself'; the English 'in itself' leans on 'an sich'; ambiguity partly kept.","'Application' kept as 'application' (Applikation); 'Verhältniß' = relation; 'Posten' = post; 'Beruf' not in this passage. 'Alter' = age."]],"philippi-s18-009":["II. Horizon of sex. There it is obvious that that of the other sex circumscribes only such acquaintance as is suited to its taste and calling. A calling appropriate to the female sex is the honor of the same. The sexes have two different departments; to each belongs what is its own. Charm is lost when one occupies itself with what belongs in the department of the other.",["OCR 'IL' read as 'II.'; Geschlecht = sex; Kenntnisse = acquaintance; Beruf = calling; Reiz = charm.","'derjenige des andern Geschlechts' is obscure; kept literal."]],"philippi-s18-010":["III. Horizon according to difference of stations and ways of life. It is deplorable that a human being of high birth believes he may have only a small horizon. Distinguished people possess country estates, since they now have such desirable time and opportunity, they should cultivate precisely the sciences preferentially and not pass their life with feeding the cattle and catching the hare. In France and England one places nobility not so much in birth as in the way of thinking and in the soul. The way of life is a true ground of the determination of the horizon.",["Stände = stations; Lebensarten = ways of life; Art = way; Wissenschafften = sciences (scientia); Denkungsart = way of thinking; Grund = ground; Bestimmung = determination."]],"philippi-s18-011":["Sciences of skill in conducting oneself, and others, serve only for insinuation, for recommendation. The principal sciences are those that can benefit the state through interconnection.",["Geschicklichkeit = skill; Zusammenhang = interconnection; 'Insinuation' kept as the Latinate word. 'durch den Zusammenhang' is obscure; kept literal."]],"philippi-s18-012":["Remarks. What is above our horizon is not precisely above the horizon of all. Hence one should learn the history of the learned. What is within my horizon is not therein yet in the horizon of all. Hence comes the faulty instruction, that a teacher thinks the pupil must have the horizon that he has. To this belongs very much acuteness, that one know how to put oneself into the standpoint of another. Something can be outside the horizon of others that is nevertheless beneath our own.",["Anmerkungen = remarks; Scharfsinnigkeit = acuteness. 'Was innerhalb meinem Horizont ist, ist darin noch nicht in dem Horizont aller' is awkward in the German and kept so.","'so doch unter dem Unsrigen ist' kept literal; 'ausserdem' read as 'außer dem'."]],"philippi-s18-013":["Much acquaintance with the world is required if one wants to know the fitting application of the sciences and to know how far an acquaintance befits this or that station. Is it indeed fitting for the Turkish emperors to learn handicrafts? Could they not have a nobler pastime. The acquaintance with the value of the sciences is necessary for a prince to know. It is a misfortune that the human being in youth cannot choose what is appropriate to him and other human beings cannot hit upon it.",["Kenntniß der Welt = acquaintance with the world (Welt = world); Kenntniß = acquaintance; Wissenschaften = sciences; Stand = station.","'noth wendig' (OCR split) read as 'nothwendig' = necessary; 'Zeitvertreib' gender as in source (das/ein)."]],"philippi-s18-014":["The evil for the horizon of the human being comes partly from the difficulty in the choice, partly from that in the deep final end.",["Endzweck = final end (Zweck = end); 'bey dem tiefen Endzweck' is obscure ('deep' for 'tiefen'); kept literal. Uebel = evil."]],"philippi-s18-015":["A theoretical teacher must orient himself to the most capable; but a practical one also to the most incapable of all.",["fähigsten / unfähigsten = most capable / most incapable (Fähigkeit = capacity)."]],"philippi-s18-016":["As we do not make our horizon the measure of others, so we must always seek to determine our own more closely. The human being must not outline his horizon right away; for he does not know his future capacities and inclinations, nor the calling to which they will draw him. Finally he must fix it and then not alter it without necessity. Those who apply themselves now to this, now to that, will become perfect in none, bungling in everything.",["Fähigkeiten = capacities; vollkommen = perfect (Vollkommenheit = perfection); fuscherhafft = bungling; Maaßstab = measure; Beruf = calling.","'auch den Beruf nicht' read as 'nor the calling'; punctuation in source is defective ('Fähigkeiten und Neigungen nicht auch den Beruf')."]],"philippi-s19-001":["§50",[]],"philippi-s19-002":["Here the author speaks of the ignorance that is blameless. This goes to the things to whose cognitions we have no faculty and which do not belong to our calling and also procure us no real benefit. Such an ignorance is, with regard to the proper cognitions, their soul. Why should one trouble oneself about cognitions that do not belong to us, do not benefit us. E.g., the ignorance of a scholar in the science of the kitchen, in the method of deception of the merchants, will be blameless. Ignorance becomes praiseworthy when one rejects unnecessary or disadvantageous, unworthy, harmful cognitions, with the aim that one can occupy oneself instead with useful ones. But an ignorance can be *respective* praiseworthy, in view of certain circumstances, ages etc. etc., where it deserves not only no blame, but also praise.",["'sejm' is read as 'seyn' (OCR slip); 'zu gehören' is read as 'zugehören' ('belong to').","Erkenntnis = cognition; Wissen/Wissenschaft = knowledge/science; Beruf = 'calling'; Absicht = 'aim' in 'in der Absicht', but 'in Absicht auf' is rendered 'in view of' (idiomatic).","*respectiue* is Latin-form in the German text and is kept in italics, spelled 'respective'.","'Betrugmethode' (sic) is rendered literally as 'method of deception'."]],"philippi-s19-003":["I § 52",["The initial 'I' is as printed in the OCR text; it may be a stray mark."]],"philippi-s19-004":["Socrates said he had found through much research that he still knows nothing. This profound pronouncement is bandied about by shallow heads, without their having had insight into it. A very great deal belongs to knowing one's ignorance. There are two ways of the consciousness of ignorance.",["'Bewußt -seyn' read as 'Bewußtseyn' (consciousness); Art = 'way'; einsehen = 'have insight into'."]],"philippi-s19-005":["1. in that one measures oneself against the objects and thereby sees",["Gegenstand = 'object'. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s19-006":["that one does not know them.",["kennen = 'know' (be acquainted with)."]],"philippi-s19-007":["2. or *materialiter* in that one does not know the objects themselves.",["'materiahter' is read as *materialiter* (OCR slip); kept as Latin in italics."]],"philippi-s19-008":["But to have insight that our knowledge is nothing; that is scientific. To have insight into the bounds of one's cognition, the domain of the same, and thereby to cognize that I do not know this; that is very profound philosophy. The not-knowing is here scientific with regard to the bounds of cognition, which one must know in order to have insight into it. To have insight into one's ignorance systematically and with acuity, up to what degree one can go : oh, a great deal belongs to that. We can have insight into a lack only by having a cognition of opposite positions. E.g. One cannot know darkness except through light. Blind people, when they had been operated on, were afraid of the black night, whereas previously they had not been afraid of it, because they did not know it.",["'scientifisch' and 'wissenschaftlich' both = 'scientific'; kennen = 'know' and wissen = 'know' (both 'know'); Philosophie = 'philosophy' (Weltweisheit would also be 'philosophy').","'gegenseitigen' could be 'opposite' or 'reciprocal'; rendered 'opposite'.","'o dazu gehört viel' is doubtful in the OCR ('o' may be a slip, e.g. for 'so'); rendered as it stands.","'sehr tiefe' read as such; 'Scharfsinnigkeit' = 'acuity'."]],"philippi-s19-009":["Non-being is no object; but the sublation of the positive is something objective. The remotion of light is darkness; there I must have known the light, if I want to know that it is removed and thereby darkness is there. The savage does not know his lack, for he has never had the abundance. Just so one cannot know the bounds of cognition, except that one know all knowledge. Thus the astronomer will cognize his ignorance far more than the common man. Among all theoretical sciences none can humble more than astronomy.",["Object = 'object' (Latin-derived form, same English word as Gegenstand); 'objectives' = 'objective'.","Aufhebung = 'sublation' (literal choice); Remotion/removiret kept Latinate ('remotion', 'removed').","'als daß man alles Wissen kennt' is syntactically awkward and is kept so ('except that one know all knowledge')."]],"philippi-s19-010":["First it elevates us, then it lowers us to the deepest.",[]],"philippi-s19-011":["That which we have to esteem highly in ourselves is the capacity to be able to distinguish the moral and to be able to determine the bounds of our wishes.",["Fähigkeit = 'capacity'; Grenze = 'bound'; 'das Sittliche' = 'the moral'."]],"philippi-s19-012":["That is a great benefit of the sciences that they show us our ignorance.",["Nutzen = 'benefit'; Wissenschaft = 'science'."]],"philippi-s20-001":["§ 54",[]],"philippi-s20-002":["One can choose for one's horizon sciences according to skill, prudence, wisdom. He has skill who has good means to arrive at arbitrary ends. Prudence is the faculty of choosing for oneself ends that are only commensurate with the inclinations. Wisdom is the faculty of choosing for oneself ends of perfection.",["'behebigen' read as 'beliebigen' ('arbitrary').","Vermögen = 'faculty'; Mittel = 'means'; Zweck = 'end'; Vollkommenheit = 'perfection'; 'angemessen' = 'commensurate'."]],"philippi-s21-001":["§ 53",[]],"philippi-s21-002":["Plato distinguished philosophy and philodoxy. A philodox is he who acquires for himself all skill to answer all possible questions, they may have ever such tasteless ends. The philosopher in the academic understanding is such a philodox, but the true philosopher is he who seeks to make himself capable of cognizing only that which is commensurate with true perfection and with the highest vocation of man.",["'Pliilosoph' read as 'Philosoph' (OCR). 'im akademischen Verstände': Verstand = 'understanding' kept for consistency; the German phrase is idiomatic ('sense').","Bestimmung = 'vocation' here (glossary alternative 'determination'); 'Quaestionen' = 'questions'; 'abgeschmackt' = 'tasteless'."]],"philippi-s21-003":["Polyhistory is a historical cognition of the whole horizon of human beings. Vanity is a great impetus to polyhistory, because historical cognition is the easiest. He discovers what has been thought without himself thinking.",["'Menschen' rendered 'human beings' (plural); 'Polyhistorie' = 'polyhistory'."]],"philippi-s22-001":["§ 55",[]],"philippi-s22-002":["If a cognition contains nothing that does not belong to the end, then it is commensurate (*adaequat*)",["'Erkennt niß' repaired as 'Erkenntniß'; the stray period after it is dropped. 'abgemessen' = 'commensurate'."]],"philippi-s23-001":["§ 56",[]],"philippi-s23-002":["We can study 1. for the sake of taste, when one is taken by the charm of the sciences 2. for the sake of vanity, that one may only come into repute. In and for itself cursory study is not entirely to be rejected. One should first run through the field of the sciences cursorily, before one learns them thoroughly. If we study synthetically, that is, from the parts to the cognition of the whole, we will not get on well: but we will be more fortunate in studying if we proceed analytically, after consideration of the whole, to the parts of it. Hence when we read through a book, it is necessary that we first represent to ourselves the whole outline, then proceed to the main parts, and then to the subordinate parts.",["The stray apostrophe before '2.' is dropped. 'Geschmak' = 'taste'; 'Betrachtung' = 'consideration'; 'Theile' = 'parts'; 'vorstellen' = 'represent'.","'ists' = 'ist es'."]],"philippi-s24-001":["§ 62",[]],"philippi-s24-002":["The extensiveness of learned cognition rests",["Weitläufftigkeit = 'extensiveness'; the sentence continues in the following items."]],"philippi-s24-003":["1. on the multitude of objects that I cognize, that is *quantitas extensiua*.",["Menge = 'multitude'; Latin kept in italics with the manuscript spelling."]],"philippi-s24-004":["2. on the degree, how much I cognize of each of these objects: that is *quantitas intensiua*.",["Grad = 'degree'."]],"philippi-s25-001":["§ 63",[]],"philippi-s25-002":["The main science must have a relation",["Beziehung = 'relation'; the sentence continues in the following items."]],"philippi-s25-003":["α. to the capacities.",["'a.' read as α; Fähigkeit = 'capacity'."]],"philippi-s25-004":["β. to the inclinations.",["'ß' read as β."]],"philippi-s25-005":["γ. to the duties and the vocation of the subject; to the place that one occupies in the world. According to these rules one should choose one's main science; and this one must do, for among all cognitions there should be unity and a ground, to which I relate them all.",["'y' read as γ; 'Kegeln Avähle' read as 'Regeln wähle' and 'eiiinimmt' as 'einnimmt' (OCR). The OCR 'die\"' is read as 'die'.","Bestimmung = 'vocation' (alternative 'determination'); Stelle = 'place'."]],"philippi-s26-001":["§ 65",[]],"philippi-s26-002":["A pedant is one who may indeed possess skill and cognition, but does not know how to apply them, because he does not rightly know the world. There are thus pedants in all estates, among courtiers, soldiers, finery-pedants, there are pedants of taste, indeed pedants among scholars in metaphysics; when one applies a subtlety to an object that is nevertheless of no benefit. The learned pedant is the most tolerable, because one can still learn something from him although it is brought up at the wrong time. Hence in order to get rid of pedantry one must be in a position to be able to pass a sufficient judgment, in which cases the judgments fit. Cicero's *esse videatur*, the so-called *fallacia caussae non caussae*, almost the whole of syllogistic, the multitude of terminologies belong to pedantry. Pedantry is opposed to *Petitmaitrerey*, which is full of shallowness. This takes place among peoples among whom the womenfolk give the tone, e.g. among the French. But quite different is charlatanry. The pedant errs from ignorance, because he lacks insight to peddle out his proprieties rightly. The charlatan, however, errs on purpose; he has the intention toward others of peddling out his insight, whether it fits or not, in order to attain fame.",["OCR repairs: 'Metaplrysik' = 'Metaphysik'; 'Pedantere}T' = 'Pedanterey'; 'loß' = 'los'; 'Theüen' not present here; 'Putzpedanten' rendered 'finery-pedants'.","*Petitmaitrerey* (petit-maître-ry) is left untranslated; 'esse videatur' (Cicero's phrase) and 'fallacia caussae non caussae' are Latin and kept in italics.","'Schicklichkeiten' is as printed ('proprieties'); 'Geschicklichkeiten' ('skills') may be intended; doubtful.","Urtheil = 'judgment'; Stand = 'estate'; Einsicht = 'insight'; Absicht = 'intention'; Vorsatz = 'purpose' ('on purpose')."]],"philippi-s27-001":["The III. Section. §66",[]],"philippi-s27-002":["Extensiveness is an extensive magnitude, when I cognize how much is posited; this magnitude is regarded as a whole and counted. The intensive magnitude is the degree of the cognitions; it is regarded as a ground, when the parts have many sequels, and I ask how much is posited through the thing. This magnitude is called fruitfulness and importance. The subjective cognitions consist in the degree of distinctness.",["'wie viel gesetzt' is elliptical in the German (no verb) and is kept so; 'Gröse/Grösse' = 'magnitude'; 'gesetzt' = 'posited'.","'Folgen' = 'sequels' (Folge in the glossary); Deutlichkeit = 'distinctness'; Wichtigkeit = 'importance'."]],"philippi-s28-001":["§ 74",[]],"philippi-s28-002":["The author speaks of the vulgar. In and for itself nothing is vulgar or base, rather the actions of human beings, the misuse of the powers of the human being, make it vulgar or base. For one cannot say that a worm in nature is contemptible, but in the actions of human beings something becomes base. There are many indecent things, e.g., the casuistic morals that was written by a Jesuit, in which the author deals with how a clergyman is to conduct himself in all cases of the married state, is an indecent piece of writing. Rules are written there about cases that will never occur.",["Pöbelhafft = 'vulgar'; niederträchtig = 'base'; 'Kräfte' rendered 'powers' (Kraft = force; power, glossary).","OCR corrections: 'pöbelhaff t' → pöbelhafft; 'emem' → einem.","'die casuistische Moral ... ist eine unanständige Schrifft': the sentence is kept as one, with the relative clause between subject and predicate as in the German."]],"philippi-s29-001":["§91",[]],"philippi-s29-002":["The faculty of judging [Beurtheilung], of having insight into the true worth of things, we attain late, when experience, wisdom and prudence are found in us sufficiently. The faculty of speculation, however, we attain sooner.",["Vermögen = faculty; Beurtheilung = judging (glossary 'judgment'; here the verbal noun, 'faculty of assessment' is the alternative).","Weißheit = wisdom; Klugheit = prudence."]],"philippi-s30-001":["The Fourth Section. On the Truth of Learned Cognition.",["Erkenntniß = cognition."]],"philippi-s30-002":["The first among all logical questions is this: what is truth? yet it is so difficult to give an answer to it that does not run back in a circle, that this is still up to now one of the most difficult things in philosophy. The rules must already be laid down as ground, about which one is to pass a judgment. I am to investigate the rules of truth and must, in doing this, myself proceed according to the rules of truth, cf. Jacobi in his miscellaneous writings.",["Philosophie = philosophy (Weltweisheit not used here).","'viel.' read as the abbreviation 'vid.' (cf.), probably an OCR/transcription slip; doubtful.","'Zu Grunde gelegt' = 'laid down as ground' (Grund = ground).","Urtheil fällen = 'pass a judgment'."]],"philippi-s30-003":["We are to explain truth, we want to do this not directly but through comparison of it with falsity. Among the ancients the skeptics also asked after truth in order to put the dogmatists into confusion and refuted them through their dialectic; of this more below. I will here set truth opposed not to error, but to falsity. The theme of truth is the object. I here compare truth and falsity and there I establish the following:",["OCR corrections: 'f rügen' → fragen; 'Sceptiker' rendered 'skeptics'.","erklären = explain (also 'define'); Object = object; 'vest' = fest.","Irrthum = error; Falschheit = falsity."]],"philippi-s30-004":["I. Truth and falsity is only in judgments, not in concepts. Whoever does not judge, his cognition I can call neither true nor false: only through judging are truth and falsity revealed. But if a concept includes a contradiction in itself, then it must surely be false. No, not even then. Falsity is when one is deceived. Concepts cannot deceive me, unless judgments are added.",["OCR: 'hinzulvommen' → hinzukommen. 'Wiederspruch' = Widerspruch = contradiction.","'Truth and falsity is' keeps the German singular verb (ist)."]],"philippi-s30-005":["The concept of a corporeal spirit is not false. An impossible or contradicting concept can itself have true consequences. E.g., a corporeally thinking being would be divisible. And by drawing true consequences from the impossible, I afterwards have insight into the impossibility. If the concepts of the inferences were false, then one could have no insight into any impossibility. Spinoza's error consisted in judgment, not in concepts. In short: falsity is only there where one judges and not there where one does not judge.",["Geist = spirit; Wesen = being; Folgen = consequences (Folge = sequel; sequence in glossary, 'consequence' = Folgerung; choice made for the plural here).","Schlüsse = inferences. 'wiedersprechender' = contradicting."]],"philippi-s30-006":["II. Upon what do truth and falsity rest in every judgment? Upon the cognition's agreeing with the object. Good, but how can I see that my cognition agrees with the object? I can compare the object only insofar as I know it. I cannot compare the object itself with my cognition, but only the cognition of the object with the cognition of the same object, and if they agree then I say the cognition is true. Three sides have three angles. Against this the ancients set the dialectic, in which they wanted to despise all marks of truth. It runs thus: If someone whom you do not know gives out something as true, and he appeals, in order to confirm his statement, to another whom you also do not know; then the question is whether I can be certain that what he states is true? No; for I can only accept something as true insofar as it agrees with the object. Here, however, I do not know the objects or those people. It is just so with cognition. The one cognition of the object, of which I do not know whether it is true, appeals to another cognition of the object of which I also do not know whether it is true, and yet if they agree, I say that it is true. This dialectic is no mere objection but an argument, and serves to cognize the proper nature of truth. Truth is the agreement of the cognitions of the object with themselves. For what we call objects are only our cognitions. Do we then have universal rules of agreement? Yes, and we want to go through them.",["OCR: 'clrey' → drey; 'verachten' (verachten, 'despise') kept as printed though 'verwerfen'/'verachten' may be a misreading; doubtful. 'auf -nehmen' → aufnehmen.","Gegenstand and Object both 'object'. kennen = know; erkennen = cognize; Merkmal = mark.","übereinstimmen / stimmen / Zusammenstimmung: 'agree' / 'agreement'.","'mit sich selbst' = 'with themselves' (referring to Erkenntnisse); 'Aussage' = 'statement'."]],"philippi-s30-007":["All judgments spring from understanding and reason. If we want to judge what is illusion or truth, we must do it according to the laws of reason. Sensibility can do nothing here. The judgment that has a private validity indicates only the particular relation of the object to my subject; and that is illusion. The judgment that is universally valid concerns truth. For a judgment which necessarily holds for all agrees with the object. And the universal validity of cognition is just what we seek; and through which power of the soul? Through the understanding. Sensibility, the way we are affected, shows us only illusion; but the understanding, when it judges unmixed with sensibility, shows us truth unmixed with illusion. The understanding is therefore alone the power to find that which is universally valid, which agrees with all judgments.",["OCR: 'Kraff t' → Kraft; 'Mir' → wir (we are affected). Krafft = power (Kraft = force; power); Seele = soul.","Schein = illusion; Art = way; allgemeingültig = universally valid; Privatgültigkeit = private validity.","'vor alle' = 'for all' (für alle)."]],"philippi-s30-008":["Truth is the agreement of cognition with the common laws of reason.",["gemein = common."]],"philippi-s30-009":["If we are to cognize truth, then we must have certain marks of truth and these are",["Sentence breaks off before its list; continued in the following passages."]],"philippi-s30-010":["1. the agreement with the highest laws of the understanding in general.",["überhaupt = 'in general'."]],"philippi-s30-011":["2. that according to universal laws a posteriori, in that I observe whether the human understanding really agrees.",["'diejenige' refers to a feminine noun (Zusammenstimmung) understood from item 1; rendered 'that'.","*a posteriori* kept in Latin."]],"philippi-s30-012":["According to the first I say: what is true according to the universal laws of the understanding, is good for everyone; and according to the other: what agrees with everyone's judgment of the understanding, that is true. So there are two ways of cognizing truth. The mathematician proceeds according to the first and seeks to make all propositions agree with the common rules of reason. Then he proves his proposition and boldly defies that all must give him assent. The ground that all must give assent is the agreement of cognition with the basic laws of the understanding. If we were to see that some doubt, we would do so too. We have therefore a double mark of truth, one *a priori*, and the other *a posteriori*. This rests on the principles: *Consentientia uni tertio consentiunt inter se*; and *Consentientia inter se consentiunt uni tertio*. The trial *a posteriori* whether a proposition which one wants to have made according to universal rules of the understanding also finds assent in everyone's understanding, one cannot do without, e.g., The doctrine of monads is not accepted by the English. If this doctrine is according to the common laws of the understanding, then it must also be convincing to the English. Thus the application of a proposition of reason *a posteriori* is highly necessary. It is the test whether the proposition agrees with the common laws of the understanding or not. Even if I should err, others will not, and will thereby discover the error to me.",["OCR: 'erstem' → ersten; 'Ueberemstimmung' → Uebereinstimmung; 'Wü' → Wir; 'vni' → uni; 'Vernunfftsatzes' = Vernunftsatz. 'gut für jedermann' rendered 'is good for everyone'; reading 'gut' as 'good' (i.e., 'fine') is a possible alternative, as in 'Gut, aber' elsewhere.","Satz = proposition (in Grundsätzen: Grundsatz = principle); Merkmal = mark; Lehre = doctrine; überzeugend = convincing (überzeugen, Überzeugung = conviction).","Beifall = assent; Probe = test; Versuch = trial; Irrthum = error.","'die Lehre von den Monaden': 'The doctrine of monads' kept; 'Engelländern' = the English.","Sentence 'Den Versuch ... kann man nicht entbehren' keeps the German object-first order."]],"philippi-s30-013":["The diameter is related to the periphery as 100 to 314. Since all geometers accept this proposition; so it must be true and it also is. The proof can be given through geometry. There are",["'Weü' read as 'Weil' (OCR). Satz = proposition; Beweiß = proof. The passage breaks off at 'Es giebt', continued in the next passage."]],"philippi-s30-014":["1. objective criteria of truth, these are *a priori cognoscibile*.",["Latin kept as printed in the source; 'cognoscibile' is as in the text (no emendation)."]],"philippi-s30-015":["2. Subjective criteria — these one cognizes *a posteriori*.",["erkennt = cognizes (cognoscere)."]],"philippi-s30-016":["The objective criteria consist in the general laws of reason, according to which the agreement of the cognitions is judged. The agreement according to the laws of the understanding consists in propositions of identity. The conflict of the cognitions according to the laws of the understanding is the proposition of contradiction. Those are the objective criteria of truth and they are so alone; but they are not sufficient, i.e. the agreement of the cognitions of the subject with the cognition of others, or the agreement of the cognition of the subject in one state with that in another. After we have fulfilled all objective marks; we are nevertheless still uncertain in the application, whether it then also agrees with the general laws of the subject. Thus one does it in calculating. General marks one cannot give in the application. If I also give a rule of application, then there must yet again an instruction be added how I am to apply this rule.",["'objcctiven' in the first line is an OCR slip for 'objectiven'.","'Kennzeichen' and 'Merkmal' are both rendered 'mark'; 'hinreichend' is rendered 'sufficient' (as zureichend); 'Widerstreit' = conflict.","The sentence 'sie sind aber nicht hinreichend d. i. Die Uebereinstimmung ...' is kept as it stands; its syntax is obscure and has not been repaired.","The antecedent of 'es' in 'ob es denn auch mit den allgemeinen Gesetzen des Subjects übereinstimmt' is left unresolved."]],"philippi-s30-017":["Of the correctness in the application there are no general rules. One can therefore have it from nothing but subjective grounds. The subjective grounds serve to verify the exercise of the general rules. I must set up tests, thus:",["Grund = ground; Proben anstellen = 'set up tests'; 'sie' (have it/them) — 'it' follows Richtigkeit (feminine singular); the German pronoun 'sie' could also be read as plural ('them'), the ambiguity is not resolved."]],"philippi-s30-018":["I. I compare my cognition that I have of the object, with the cognition of other objects, which is already accepted as true and convincing. Thereby we have a guiding thread to apply the general rule. The subject compares the judgments that it passes, at another time in another state. To prevent falsity one must take time, to be able to consider the judgments from another side from another point of view at different times. Falsity does not appear so immediately. From haste in judging come the many errors. That makes a great difference in the application of the common rules of the understanding and of reason, when we look at something at different times from different points of view: we will thereby often discover errors that were at first entirely hidden from us.",["Gegenstand = object; überzeugend = convincing (cf. Überzeugung = conviction); Urtheile gefällt = 'judgments that it passes' (Urteile fällen).","Gesichtspunct = point of view; gemein = common; Falschheit = falsity; Fehler = errors; Uebereilung = haste."]],"philippi-s30-019":["II. The second criterion is the agreement of the cognition of our subject with that of other subjects. In general that is true which is valid for everyone; what has only a private validity is illusion. If I have proceeded according to general rules in cognition; then it is still necessary that I try whether this my cognition completely agrees with the judgment of others. For if it does not agree; then I can be assured that my cognition is not framed in agreement with the general rules of the understanding. Mathematics itself one must test in such a way; and if the mathematician proves newly invented propositions, without first learning whether others also accept them as true; then this security and this confidence arises from the observation that every human being's reason agrees with the reason of the mathematician, and that mathematics is conform to the rules of the common understanding.",["Schein = illusion; Gültigkeit = validity; Beobachtung = observation; 'conform' is as in the German ('conform sey').","'stimmend abgefasset' rendered 'framed in agreement'; 'Sicherheit' = security; 'Zutrauen' = confidence."]],"philippi-s30-020":["From this it follows that the subjective grounds must be added to the confirmation of the objective.",["'der objectiven' is left without a supplied noun (grounds? criteria?); the German does not specify."]],"philippi-s30-021":["General validity is required for truth.",["Allgemeingültigkeit rendered 'general validity' for consistency with allgemein = general."]],"philippi-s30-022":["We must not be indifferent when others do not give us approval. It is a logical experiment when I test my judgments by the heads of others. If someone does not believe my propositions, I can presume that there lies somewhere in them an error; because they are not generally valid. The point of distinction of truth is the general understanding and not solely and alone mine.",["Unterscheidungspunkt = 'point of distinction'; Beifall = approval; Experiment as in the German; 'meinige' (mine) has no noun supplied, so 'understanding' is only implied."]],"philippi-s30-023":["Whence does it come that all human beings who are devoted to reflection, are ready and inclined to communicate their insights to others? Is it the vanity of being praised? No, one even censures, if a scholar does not make his knowledge known, and this vanity one will never suppress. The cause is, the human understanding finds itself in its insights in need of testing its cognition on other heads. Without this experience it does not trust itself to take them to be true. It is conscious that it is not capable of seeing the errors itself; therefore it leaves it to others. Thus the human understanding is already by nature to be considered as participating. It has the drive to hold its judgments against the judgments of others, and not alone to stop at the higher propositions of reason.",["'comniuniciren' read as 'communiciren'; 'be}?\"' read as 'bei' (OCR garbled); 'obern' = higher (could be 'upper'/'supreme').","Nachdenken rendered 'reflection' (also the glossary rendering of Überlegung); Wissen = knowledge; für wahr halten = 'take to be true'.","'sie' after 'traut er sich nicht' has no clear antecedent in the German (insights/cognition); rendered 'them'.","theilnehmend = 'participating' (sharing in)."]],"philippi-s30-024":["Objections of others, or also the bad effect that my proposition has in conviction, ought to make me attentive. If objections are made to our judgments; then we will test our own propositions accordingly and seek to make them agreeing. If our propositions are not convincing, then we will already see that something is lacking in them, at least in distinctness, and we will improve them.",["Einwürfe = objections; 'Effekt' (foreign word in the German) = 'effect' (not Wirkung); Deutlichkeit = distinctness; 'daß es ihnen woran fehlt' rendered 'that something is lacking in them'."]],"philippi-s30-025":["Thus one must gladly hand over one's judgments to others for sifting. It belongs to the eternal laws that the human being have freedom to make known to others the propositions about which he speculates. Unless it be when one is to make something of oneself; that can have only a private validity. A human being on a desert island surely gives up all speculation.",["'Es sey dann wenn man von selbst etwas machen soll' is obscure; 'von selbst' could mean 'of oneself/by oneself'; left ambiguous. 'Mensch' = human being.","'sichten' = sifting."]],"philippi-s30-026":["Falsity is either",[]],"philippi-s30-027":["1. *materialis* or",[]],"philippi-s30-028":["2. *formalis*.",[]],"philippi-s30-029":["Formal falsity is when a cognition contradicts itself or does not fit.",["'sich nicht paßt': the reflexive 'sich' is ambiguous (itself / with itself); rendered without object. Widerspruch = contradiction."]],"philippi-s30-030":["Material falsity occurs most frequently, so that even true propositions can have it. When one presents and states a common cognition, which everyone knows, as if it had been invented by our understanding. One deceives oneself in the hope. One hopes to get a learned profound answer, and one gets back only what one already knows. What is the cause of the generation of plants? *Vis plastica*. Here I am deceived. It is just as if someone hands over to me a Polish copper groschen boiled in tartar, when I had lent him an ordinary groschen. I get back what is mine, but am deceived in my hope. I ask the scholar and he gives back to me, clothed round, what I asked. In explanations of causes and in solutions of problems there is so much word-peddling and tautologies, that one must be astonished. How do I make a definition that lays everything before me, in which everything is united as in a quintessence? Seek so many marks until taken together they make up the whole concept. That is just what I asked.",["'Wortkrärnerey' read as 'Wortkrämerey' (OCR); 'Tavtologien' = tautologies; 'Weinstein' = tartar.","Erklärung = 'explanation' (in 'Erklärungen der Ursachen') but 'Definition' = 'definition'; Auflösung = solution; Problem = problem; Ursache = cause.","Merkmal = mark; Begriff = concept; 'Hofnung' = hope. 'Vis plastica' kept in Latin."]],"philippi-s30-031":["One should take care against such tautologies. The beginning of wisdom is *stultitia caruisse*.",["'für' read as 'vor' (OCR/orthography); Latin 'Stultitia caruisse' kept as printed. Weißheit = wisdom (not Weltweisheit)."]],"philippi-s30-032":["No science is so full of tautologies as morality. The greatest men have this fault. If you want to be virtuous, tame your affects. But how do I bring it about that I change my taste and tame the affects? It is just as if a watchmaker answers his lad who asks him how one is to make watches: you must bring it about that the wheels mesh with each other with their teeth.",["'Kerne' read as 'Keine' and '\\\\ ie' as 'wie' (OCR). Wissenschaft = science; Moral = morality; Affect = affect; Bursche = lad."]],"philippi-s30-033":["The many tautologies in morality come from this, because we immediately give approval to the laws of virtue and morality, concede them at once, when the philosopher tells them to us even only half and unproven.",["Moral = morality; Beifall = approval; Gesetz = law."]],"philippi-s30-034":["One must pay attention to the falsity of the form even if the propositions are true.",["Form = form; Satz = proposition."]],"philippi-s30-035":["If someone proves the existence of God or a moral proposition, then every human being is already inclined to give him approval, even if he proves badly, and as it were to make him a gift of the proof.",["Daseyn = existence; Satz = proposition; Beweis/beweisen = proof/prove."]],"philippi-s30-036":["Doubting the form of cognition is distinguished from doubt about the matter, and the falsity of the form can and must not be regarded as the falsity of the matter. There are some sciences that are raised above common understanding; but there are others where the philosopher can prove it with common understanding and where his scientific grounds are very frail; yet he chooses the latter in order to have the appearance as if he had insight into the thing quite differently from common understanding.",["Materie = matter; Erkenntniß = cognition; Wissenschaft = science; gemeiner Verstand = common understanding.","die letzten (literally 'the last'): the antecedent is ambiguous (the frail scientific grounds, or the second kind of science); 'the latter' keeps this open.","Ansehen is rendered 'appearance' (it can also mean standing or repute); Erscheinung would be a different word. einsehe = 'have insight into'; Sache = 'thing' (not Ding)."]],"philippi-s30-037":["The marks and grounds of distinction of the true and the false. A cognition is false if it contradicts itself. Is it also false if it contradicts other cognitions? Yes, if it contradicts the true ones, then it is false, but that cannot be the supreme mark of the false, because here I presuppose that I already have a mark of the true and the false.",["Merkmal = mark; Unterscheidungsgründe = grounds of distinction; wiederspricht (= widerspricht) = contradicts.","den wahren: 'the true ones' supplies only the plural 'ones' for the elided noun (Erkenntnissen)."]],"philippi-s30-038":["The matter is very subtle. Meanwhile we want to accept it. What, e.g., contradicts the principles of experience, that is false.",["Materie = matter (here possibly the subject matter at hand); Grundsatz = principle; Erfahrung = experience.","annehmen rendered 'accept'; 'assume' is also possible."]],"philippi-s31-001":["§96",[]],"philippi-s31-002":["If all sequels of a cognition are true, then the cognition itself is true. But if only a single sequel is false, then the cognition is false. If many sequels of a cognition are true, then it is probable; but to have insight into all sequels is very difficult. From the truth of the sequels the truth of the cognition can be concluded only through approximation; but it has no logical evidence. So it is with the Copernican system and all other hypotheses whose correctness one concludes from the multitude of true sequels.",["Folge = sequel, following the glossary (in logic it would ordinarily be 'consequence', which the glossary reserves for Folgerung).","schließen/schliessen = conclude; wahrscheinlich = probable; Evidenz = evidence; Menge = multitude; einsehen = 'have insight into'.","OCR 'Kopernikamsche' corrected to Kopernikanische. 'So ist das ... System' is rendered as the German stands, with a loose connection to what precedes."]],"philippi-s31-003":["Many physical theorems held for a long time, because all the sequels that one knew were true, until a single experiment refuted them.",["Lehrsatz = theorem; kannte (kennen) = knew; Folge = sequel (see the preceding passage)."]],"philippi-s31-004":["These are the 2 ways *a posteriori* of concluding from the truth or falsity of the sequels to that of the grounds. To cognize the truth one must drive out the falsity. If we want to conclude from the truth of the sequels to that of the cognition; then that is difficult, for we must then know all the sequels. Supposing for now that all sequels of which I am conscious are true, the cognition is still not perfectly certain, according to the strictness of the logical form. The proof and inference is called apagogic, where I conclude from the sequels of the cognition to the truth of the same. I take the opposite and refute it by seeking out for myself a single false sequel, then my opposite proposition is proved. This apagogic way of proof is customary in mathematics and very evident. It is something particular that we have more conviction in having insight into the false than the truth. We perceive the conflict in the whole of nature sooner than the truth. It becomes thereby, as it were, a *hiatus* and a gap in the order of things. Hence one prefers the apagogic proofs to the direct ones.",["OCR 'be wüste' read as bewußte ('of which I am conscious'); 'vorjetzt' = 'for now'. 'AeBeAveiß' read as Beweiß.","Gewiß/gewiß = certain; vollkommen = perfectly (Vollkommenheit = perfection); Beweis = proof; Schluß = inference; schließen = conclude; Ueberzeugung = conviction; wahrnehmen = perceive.","Widerstreit = conflict; Hiatus is Latin in the German. 'Es wird ... ein Hiatus' kept literal, subject 'it' unresolved.","mehr Ueberzeugung haben das Falsche einzusehen: the construction is loose and kept so."]],"philippi-s31-005":["Since logical perfection consists in the agreement of the cognitions with the object, the aesthetic, however, in the agreement of the cognition with the subject; so truth is the essential perfection and the necessary essential condition under which a cognition can be perfect. In truth one has to look",["Passage breaks off after 'zu sehen'; the following items (1., 2.) continue it. Uebereinstimmung = agreement; Gegenstand = object; Subject = subject; Vollkommenheit = perfection.","die aesthetische: the noun (perfection) is elided in the German and left so."]],"philippi-s31-006":["1. to the agreement of the cognition with the object",["Gegenstand = object; auf continues 'zu sehen' of the preceding passage."]],"philippi-s31-007":["2. to the means of arriving at it; to that belongs distinctness etc. etc.",["Mittel = means; Deutlichkeit = distinctness."]],"philippi-s31-008":["If the degree of truth is sought that is compatible with the subjective laws; then it is indeed not in accordance with the requirement of the understanding but in accordance with the requirement of wit. That is aesthetic truth, the truth according to laws of sensibility, e.g. if the sky is represented as a blue covering, God as a majestic monarch; then that is aesthetically true, but not logically. Yet aesthetic truth too must be in accordance with the logical. Untruth eradicates all charm. One sees it in false similes. In poets one finds such aesthetic truth and one must also assess them accordingly. Haller, Lucretius, one must therefore assess not so much logically as aesthetically. The poet expresses generally what is frequent. He expresses himself *hardi*. The painfulness of restricting oneself to a particular proposition is not fitting for taste. What everyone holds to be true is true according to the aesthetic rules. In these there is nothing constant. They change and direct themselves according to the judgments of human beings.",["OCR '-\\\\ ird' read as wird; 'Erf orderung' as Erforderung (requirement).","Himmel can be 'sky' or 'heaven'; 'sky' chosen. Witz = wit; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; beurtheilen = assess; Urtheil = judgment; Gleichniß = simile.","sie in 'man muß sie auch darnach beurtheilen' could refer to the poets or to the truth; 'them' keeps the ambiguity.","*hardi* is French (bold), kept in italics as in the source; Peinlichkeit rendered literally as 'painfulness' (punctiliousness is the sense it carries).","Hallern, Lukrez: Hallern is dative in form (Albrecht von Haller); no verb or conjunction is supplied."]],"philippi-s31-009":["Objectively every cognition is either false or not; there is no third. But subjective cognition can be either true or false, or neither true nor false. It can have neither certain marks of truth nor of falsity i.e. be groundless. A cognition that is uncertain or groundless according to logical laws can be grounded enough according to practical laws. I.e. it can be capable of guiding the human being so as if it were true",["Passage ends without punctuation in the source. gewisse Merkmale: 'certain' may mean 'sure' or 'particular'; the ambiguity is kept.","ungegründet = groundless; unsicher = uncertain; Merkmal = mark; Gesetz = law; Mensch rendered 'human being' (singular, as in the German)."]],"philippi-s31-010":["§ 100",[]],"philippi-s31-011":["The author speaks of total truth and falsity.",["Autor = author (i.e. Meier, in the textbook context)."]],"philippi-s31-012":["All our cognitions are either wholly true or partly true, or wholly false or partly false. A cognition can contain sheer truth, but I do not have the cognition complete, something is still lacking in it. Then I must not say that it is practically true, e.g. when I extract the square root of 2: there I may approximate as long as I like; I never get it out wholly, something is always still lacking in it. Meanwhile what I get out is true. So it is also constituted with the relation of the diameter to the periphery. There is nothing false in it, but some little parts are still always lacking in it. Meanwhile the difference is to be reckoned as nothing. The moon is 59 l earth-diameters distant from the earth. Something is still lacking in it. What is thought with less of the truth than the total truth is true: what is thought more beyond the total truth of the cognition is false. If I give the true cognition not wholly but only a part of it; then that is no falsity that contradicts the remaining cognitions, but a lack or a missing piece of the measured-off true cognition.",["OCR 'se}^' read as seyn; '59 l' is garbled (possibly a fraction, e.g. 59½) and is left as printed.","lauter Wahrheit = 'sheer truth' (not rein); komplet = complete; Theil = part; Theilchen = 'little parts'.","Dann muß ich nicht sagen: 'must not' could also be read as 'need not'; the German is ambiguous and the English keeps the ambiguity.","Verhältniß = relation (also 'ratio'); beschaffen = constituted; wiederspricht = contradicts; abgemessenen = 'measured-off'."]],"philippi-s31-013":["We now come to total falsity, and here we fairly ask: Can a human judgment ever be wholly false? No. For only the understanding judges, even when it judges falsely. Falsity arises from the busyness of sensibility and of other powers of cognition, which couple with the understanding that judges and mix into its judgments. If it were possible that sensibility could sometimes judge, then totally false judgments would also be possible, but now, since nothing in the human being can judge except one and the same understanding; all judgments are partly true. There is no judgment that would not be particularly true. There must always be a ground why the understanding judged so. E.g. someone said there were many gods. It is false, but yet not wholly false. There is yet one God; and if someone said: there is no God: then one must consider it in the connection. The judgment is false, but in the form it can be partly true and have illusory grounds. He can never make his understanding believe something. Because sensible proofs, which satisfy all his objections, cannot be given to him, or also because in those he knows he finds no sufficient evidence; so he begins to doubt, afterwards to deny: but he will never prove that a God is not.",["OCR \"Aväre\" read as \"wäre\".","Urtheil = judgment; urtheilen = judge; Verstand = understanding; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Erkenntnißkräffte = powers of cognition (Kraft = power here).","\"Er kann niemals seinem Verstände etwas weiß machen\": idiom (\"make believe\", lit. \"make something white\"); rendered by sense, the literal image is not kept. \"Er\" is left unresolved, as in the German.","\"particular wahr\" rendered \"particularly true\"; Scheingründe = illusory grounds (Schein = illusion); Beweis = proof; kennen = know.","\"in denen die er kennt\": antecedent unstated in the German (proofs? grounds?); left as \"those he knows\"."]],"philippi-s31-014":["If we assume that the human being judges wholly falsely, then the understanding departs wholly from its laws. Thus we could never entrust anything to the understanding and appeal to it. In every case where there is error, something particular and extraordinary happens. If we assume that a body denser than the air can rise into the air; then we are uncertain in our whole physics. It would go thus in all cognition if we assume that the understanding could deviate from its laws.",["besonderes = particular; Gesetz = law; Erkenntniß = cognition; Irrthum = error."]],"philippi-s31-015":["I repeat briefly:",[]],"philippi-s31-016":["A cognition is wholly true if nothing false is in it.",[]],"philippi-s31-017":["Partly true 1. if not all the truth of the *quaestio* is to be met with in it, e.g. one who measures the height of a mountain and says the mountain cannot be under 100 rods. His statement is true, but not according to the whole *quaestio*. So too are the arithmetical and geometrical approximations.",["Quaestion (Latin quaestio, question) kept in Latin in italics; Ruthen = rods."]],"philippi-s31-018":["2. because falsity is to be met with in it.",[]],"philippi-s31-019":["In the first kind of partly true cognitions there is nothing false but only a *defectus* of the required quantity of truth; in the other kind there is something actually false. Every judgment is an action of the understanding; thus it is impossible that a judgment should not occur according to the laws of the understanding.",["\"wireklich\" read as \"wirklich\" (OCR/spelling); Erkenntnisse = cognitions."]],"philippi-s31-020":["Error is a mixed effect of the understanding and of sensibility. If there were no sensibility; the understanding would not err. No human power, and thus also not the understanding, can deviate from its laws; otherwise it would not act. No judgment can be wholly false, can be nothing at all. For the understanding has judged or effected and yet not effected according to its laws, i.e. it has not effected at all. If we therefore know that no judgment can be wholly false; then we must spy out and separate the gold of truth from under the sweepings of the false, and not throw away both at once.",["\"Kraff t\" read as \"Kraft\" (OCR); Kraft = power; Wirkung = effect; gewirkt = effected (wirken also \"act/work\"; \"handeln\" = act).","\"nicht nach seinen Gesetzen gewirkt\" and \"gar nicht gewirkt\" kept parallel."]],"philippi-s31-021":["The growth of truth is greatly hindered by the fact that the truth which was mixed up in errors was thrown out together with them. The participating understanding is also necessary in the logical, which believes that the criterion of truth and falsity is decided and determined not by the particular (*privat*) understanding but by the universal understanding.",["Criterium = criterion; \"priuat\" read as \"privat\", kept as in the text, italicized as foreign.","\"der da glaubt\": the relative clause's antecedent is ambiguous between \"understanding\" and \"the logical\"; rendered as \"which believes\" attached to the understanding.","\"im logischen\" = \"in the logical\" (neuter noun, as in the German)."]],"philippi-s31-022":["If one contradicts the judgment of another; then the latter should not contradict him right away: they should not both look only to their grounds and quarrel; they can both be right, for neither of the two has judged wholly falsely. Hence one should put oneself into the standpoint of the other and look at the proposition from the point of view from which another considers it. If someone therefore contradicts me, I will put myself into his position and no longer defy others with my proposition. For if another believes that I err, I too must be uncertain of it and doubt. If the human being in the judgment of the senses sees the judgment of others jointly against himself, then he himself doubts his senses. E.g. if a multitude of people look at a northern light, and I too come along but can become aware of nothing; then I will sooner doubt my sight than oppose the judgment of so many.",["\"nicht mehr auf meinen Satz trozen\": rendered \"defy ... with my proposition\" is loose; literally \"no longer defy (insist) upon my proposition\" (trotzen auf). Satz = proposition.","\"mich widersetzen\" at the end is grammatically awkward in the German (\"als dem Urtheil so vieler mich widersetzen\"); rendered as \"oppose the judgment of so many\".","Gesicht = sight; Gesichtspunct = point of view; Standpunct = standpoint; Menge = multitude."]],"philippi-s31-023":["All conflict is a phenomenon. If I become aware that a judgment of another contradicts mine, then I must look to the ground of how it comes about that human beings, who all conform to universal laws of the understanding, contradict one another.",["OCR \"WTenn\" read as \"Wenn\"; Phänomenon kept as \"phenomenon\" (Erscheinung would be \"appearance\"); Widerstreit = conflict; Grund = ground."]],"philippi-s31-024":["I must not here conform to my judgment, for truth is not decided by a private judgment. It takes much acuteness to put oneself in the place of another. If one therefore gets into a dispute with someone, one should concede to him who opposes our judgments; thereby one makes him inclined, and puts oneself in the position of seeking the truth with common power. That is really the only logical means of seeking the truth, that one hold one's judgment together with the judgments of others and then make it unanimous. One must never hold the judgment of another to be wholly false. Scholars who dispute with one another and sometimes contradict one another do it not with the aim of annihilating the judgment of the other and raising their own, but both want to make their understanding unanimous. If someone believes that the other is wholly in the wrong, why does he dispute with him? He is mad who rationalizes with the mad. The assessment of writing, in that one places oneself in the standpoint of the author, separates the degree of truth that is there from the falsity and seeks to increase the truth. That is the logical and moral duty and it has utility. In this way one can read writings with utility. Separate cognitions can be wholly true, e.g. in higher geometry. But systems, e.g. philosophical ones, are not at all wholly true. A hard book, a hard evil; and there it takes effort to sift out the true.",["\"Kraff t\" read as \"Kraft\" (OCR); \"gemeinschaftlicher Kraft\" = \"common power\" (gemeinschaftlich).","\"Die Beurtheilung der Schrifft, indem ...\" is a sentence without a finite verb in the German; kept as it stands. Beurtheilung = assessment; Absicht = aim; Mittel = means.","\"Ein schwer Buch, ein schwer Uebel\": the German is elliptical (a play on the proverb); kept literal. \"Systemata\" = systems (Latin plural form in German text). vernünfftelt = rationalizes (vernünfteln)."]],"philippi-s31-025":["Philosophical matters one must always improve. Wolff wrote too much. Crusius is so obstinate as not to want to improve what he wrote in youth. Oh, we all err indeed, and is it not more praiseworthy if, after attaining better insights, one changes and improves one's opinion. The resignation of one's own honor is a great touchstone of a lover of truth.",["OCR \"0\" read as \"O\"; Wolf = Wolff in the text spelled \"Wolf\", rendered \"Wolff\"; Meinung = opinion; Einsicht = insight.","\"Die Resignation auf seine eigene Ehre\": \"resignation of ... honor\" renders the preposition auf loosely; the German could mean renouncing one's own honor."]],"philippi-s32-001":["On the exactness of cognition.",["Genauigkeit = exactness; genau = exact."]],"philippi-s32-002":["A cognition is exact insofar as the degree of truth is sufficient for the purpose. Even if not everything true that is required for the object is in a cognition; yet there can be in it as much truth as is required for the purpose. Thus, e.g., a human being can have an exact cognition of his duties; although in it not everything is met with that would be required by the moral propositions. A measurement of the estate is exact even if a rod were missing.",["OCR \"Objeet\" read as \"Objekt\" (Object = object); \"rnoralischen\" read \"moralischen\"; \"Avürde\" read \"würde\"; \"eüie\" read \"eine\".","Zweck = purpose (glossary: end; purpose); Grad = degree; Sätzen = propositions (Satz)."]],"philippi-s32-003":["The Dutch are too scrupulous in the drawing of fortifications. They go down to cubic inches, since such an accuracy does not belong to the purpose at all.",["Accuratesse = accuracy (distinct from Genauigkeit = exactness)."]],"philippi-s32-004":["Cognitions can be exact *comparative*. A cognition is called exact chiefly with regard to the degree of truth.",["\"comparatiue\" read as *comparative* (Latin adverb comparative, kept in italics); the construction \"exact comparatively\" is left as in the German."]],"philippi-s32-005":["Exactness is opposed to incorrectness. A cognition is incorrect not insofar as not all correctness is there, but insofar as it contains untruth.",["Genauigkeit = 'exactness'; Richtigkeit = 'correctness' (Unrichtigkeit = 'incorrectness'); Unwahrheit = 'untruth'; Erkenntniß = 'cognition'.","OCR 'ahe' read as 'alle'."]],"philippi-s32-006":["In many cases we must be content with the *prope vero*, about which Hanau, professor in Danzig, has written.",["OCR 'sejn' read as 'seyn'. 'Hanau' is kept as printed.","The clause 'wovon Hanau Professor in Danzig geschrieben' has no auxiliary in the German; 'has written' is the English perfect required by the construction."]],"philippi-s32-007":["Coarse cognition is opposed to fine, not to exact. Exactness concerns correctness, the degree of truth required for the end: but coarseness does not concern the object, the truth, but the form. A cognition is coarse insofar as it is very sensible. What the senses teach us, that is grasped very comfortably. The more a cognition contains truths of reason, the finer it is. The common man has coarse cognition of God and divine things. Coarse or sensible cognitions can nevertheless be useful and also true. Sensible cognitions can be true *quoad materiam* and coarse *quoad formam*. Precisely through the fact that cognitions are coarse, they are intuitive and impressing but empty of all concepts of the understanding.",["grob = 'coarse'; fein = 'fine'; gemächlich = 'comfortably'; Vernunfftwahrheiten = 'truths of reason'; eindrückend = 'impressing' (participle kept).","'anschauend' (a participle) is rendered 'intuitive', as the glossary gives for anschaulich; Anschauung = 'intuition'.","Zweck = 'end'; Object = 'object'; gemeine = 'common'; Verstandesbegriffe = 'concepts of the understanding'."]],"philippi-s33-001":["§104",[]],"philippi-s33-002":["Dogmatic and historical truths are distinguished. [We understand here by truths, with the author, true cognitions.] Cognitions, insofar as they do not arise in us through the understanding and reason and not according to its laws, are historical, e.g. description of nature, geography, and philosophy itself can be cognized historically, if one does not proceed according to the laws of the understanding. We consider here history according to the matter, i.e. according to the object and not according to the form or according to the *modo cognoscendi*. Historical cognitions are distinguished from the intellectual ones only through the form. Socrates therefore said he was only the *obstetrix auditorum suorum*. Historical cognitions, however, are distinguished from dogmatic ones according to the matter. The dogmatic cognition of truth has as its object the general constitution of things, historical truth has as object *actualia quae sunt obiecta sensuum*. Every occurrence is historical: all propositions are dogmatic. Theoretical physics is dogmatic; the description of natural occurrences historical. Dogmatic is here contradistinguished from the historical, otherwise it is also opposed to the skeptical. There a dogmatic cognition signifies a general decisive one, and a skeptical one by contrast a general cognition that is problematic. *Dogma* is called every general cognition of reason. To judge dogmatically is to judge decisively; to judge problematically means when one gives problems. Dogmatic propositions are in morals, religion etc. etc. Metaphysics, as the *organon* of pure cognitions of reason, and logic as the *organon* of cognitions in general, should not contain dogmatic truths. The greatest dispute among the ancients was that concerning dogmatic propositions, partly what they are, partly whether they are certain. The old Platonic school fought for the certainty of the *dogmata*; the newer *Academici* asserted that *dogmata* have no certainty.",["The square brackets are the Academy Edition's and are carried over as they stand.","Begebenheit = 'occurrence'; Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'; Aufgaben = 'problems'; behaupten = 'assert'; bedeutet = 'signifies'; Gewißheit = 'certainty'; Satz = 'proposition'.","'contradistinguirt' is rendered 'contradistinguished'. 'dessen' ('its') could refer to understanding or to reason; left ambiguous.","'*organon*', '*dogma/dogmata*' and '*Academici*' are italicized as foreign words; the passage prints 'Dogmatum' (genitive plural), rendered '*dogmata*'.","The sentence 'problematisch urtheilen heißt da man Aufgaben giebt' is kept awkward as in the German."]],"philippi-s33-003":["The author calls a doctrinal edifice, system, a multitude of interconnected dogmatic propositions; but there are also other systems that do not consist of *Dogmatibus*. For all systems of cognition are either rational or historical. Hübner's geography is the first geographical system. Wolff made the first complete mathematical system. A dogmatic system is therefore a combination of various truths of reason that are interconnected with one ground. Thus there are doctrinal edifices of philosophy, of morals, of the influence of the soul on the body etc. etc. Besides the dogmatic systems, however, there are still others. It is asked: are systems necessary in philosophical cognitions? Maupertuis, an author, says it is a raving to seek *Systemata*. For such a one contrives artificially to derive everything from one ground, and afterwards looks not at nature but at his figment of the brain. And even if he afterwards sees ever so many false sequels, he still will not overturn his laboriously erected system. But even if doctrinal edifices do harm in this way, they are nevertheless serviceable for distinctness and correct insight into the interconnection of the truths. Whoever believes he can cognize truths singly deceives himself. In a doctrinal edifice I must begin from the whole, from the main concept, with which the spread of the cognition is interconnected, and not from the parts. E.g. in geography everything comes down to cognizing the *Globum*. The plan for the whole must be sketched beforehand; afterwards one can fill it out with the parts. Man strives by nature after doctrinal edifices. The ideal or whole goes before, and only in the whole can the parts be thought. The encyclopedias are such systems or outlines, from which we learn how the cognitions are interconnected and how they support one another to make a whole. If someone had known how to draw the circumference and outline of metaphysics; then it would already be very perfect. But that is what has been lacking; one has not surveyed it entirely. The systems are nothing contemptible. But not every building, not every heaping together where an author begins from particular propositions that seem brilliant to him and then, with suppression of all doubts that rise up in him, in order not to have worked in vain, continues and glues together a whole; not every such patchwork is a system. That is not the way to ideas. The plan must come from a distinct main idea, not from the parts. There do indeed remain then gaps and empty compartments, but that does no harm. It seems as if there were now systems of almost all sciences, but doctrinal edifices are still very much lacking which contain merely the method how one can invent and test the truth, And that is what would be most necessary.",["OCR slips silently corrected: 'S3^steme', 'Sj'stemen' = Systeme/Systemen; 'wül' = will; 'allem' = allein; 'Wolf' kept as printed (the name is spelled 'Wolf' in the text, rendered 'Wolff' in the English as a name only).","Lehrgebäude = 'doctrinal edifice'; System = 'system'; zusammenhängen / Zusammenhang = 'interconnected' / 'interconnection'; Verbindung = 'combination'; Grund = 'ground'; Deutlichkeit = 'distinctness'; Folgen = 'sequels' (glossary Folge); vollkommen = 'perfect'; Wissenschaften = 'sciences'.","'particulairen' (French-Latin form) is rendered 'particular', the same English as besonder in the glossary; 'aparten' = 'distinct'.","*Dogmatibus*, *Systemata*, *Globum* are Latin forms in the German text and are italicized. 'Hirngespinst' = 'figment of the brain' (literally a brain-spinning); 'Raserey' = 'raving'.","'Hauptbegrif' = 'main concept'; 'Hauptidee' = 'main idea'; 'Verbreitung der Erkenntniß' = 'the spread of the cognition' (Verbreitung is doubtful in sense but kept literal)."]],"philippi-s34-001":["§106",[]],"philippi-s34-002":["The author distinguishes aesthetic truth from the logical. Something is aesthetically true and yet not logically true, namely if it is true in the appearance.",["Erscheinung = 'appearance'. 'aesthetisch' kept as 'aesthetic'."]],"philippi-s34-003":["The sun dips into the ocean, is true according to laws of sensibility, but not logically, not objectively. According to aesthetic rules that is general which one sees only in many. According to aesthetics that is not true which is removed from sensibility.",["Sinnlichkeit = 'sensibility'; Gesetze = 'laws'; allgemein = 'general'. The first sentence is kept elliptical as in the German."]],"philippi-s34-004":["Something is logically true and yet not aesthetically. In particular it is to be noted that there are beauties that are greater than nature gives them, so that one can think an object in all sorts of ways so perfectly as it never is in nature and perhaps also cannot be. These beauties arise therefore precisely thereby, that one deviates from the logically true. The painter Michel Angelo and Raphael indicate the bones wave-shaped with gentle curves; whereas the bones must yet be pointed. Such are ideal beauties and the ideal is drawn not according to truth, but according to taste. Taste goes to comfort; and does not direct itself according to the sequels that will happen upon it. The beauties of nature are combined with essential usefulness and look to the end. Taste, when it draws the ideal, does not look to usefulness, not to the end. The image of the Apollo at Rome is drawn against nature, which looks to the end and use of bodies, but it is drawn perfectly according to taste.",["'Art' in 'in allerley Art' = 'ways' (glossary: way; manner); 'Folgen' = 'sequels' per glossary; verbunden = 'combined'; Zweck = 'end'; Gemächlichkeit = 'comfort'.","'Solches' ('Such') is kept as in the German. 'Gegenstand/Object': 'Objekt' here = 'object'.","'wellenförmig' = 'wave-shaped'; 'Nutzbarkeit' = 'usefulness'; 'Nutzen' = 'use' (not Gebrauch)."]],"philippi-s34-005":["As the thing actually is, so it seems to be best of all. Our taste is formed according to the objects and accepts them as models. *Nihil materiale est in intellectu quod non antea fuerat in sensu* says Aristotle. But if now artists of taste have already applied all conditions of nature, they stray out with their imagination. What promotes the straying of the imagination is beautiful. Thus Hogarth has remarked that Apollo in Rome arouses such admiration for this reason, because he has long legs. The artist has happily ventured to go beyond nature.",["OCR 'bat' read as 'hat'. Wirklich = 'actually'; Bedingungen = 'conditions'; Einbildungskraft = 'imagination'; Gegenstände = 'objects'.","'ausschweifen / Ausschweifung' = 'stray out / straying' (not in the glossary). The Latin sentence is as printed ('materiale' included)."]],"philippi-s35-001":["§109 On Error.",["Irrthum = 'error'."]],"philippi-s35-002":["That something is false is nothing but a confusion, i.e. that the judgment contradicts the object. But the illusion of false cognition, by means of which it is similar to truth and is easily confused with it, is error. It is an analogue of truth. The human understanding can often take false cognitions to be true, and that is an erroneous judgment.",["OCR 'Eikcnntniß' read as 'Erkenntniß' (cognition); 'Vermengung' rendered 'confusion' (literal 'mingling'), kept as the most plain cognate.","'Gegenstand' = object; 'Erkenntnis' = cognition; 'Schein' = illusion; 'Urtheil' = judgment; 'Verstand' = understanding; 'für wahr halten' rendered 'take to be true' (cf. Fürwahrhalten).","'Analogon' is Latin/Greek in the original; kept in italics-free English as 'analogue'."]],"philippi-s35-003":["Here we want to consider",["'betrachten' = consider (Betrachtung). The sentence breaks off; continued by the following numbered items."]],"philippi-s35-004":["1. how an error is possible.",["'möglich' = possible."]],"philippi-s35-005":["2. how it actually arises, and",["'wirklich' rendered 'actually' (cf. Wirklichkeit = actuality)."]],"philippi-s35-006":["3. how it is prevented.",[]],"philippi-s36-001":["1. Consideration.",["OCR 'Bet rachtung' corrected to 'Betrachtung' (consideration)."]],"philippi-s36-002":["Every force in nature acts according to its own laws. There is no substance whose effect would not be in conformity with its force. It is contradictory that the effect should conflict with the laws of an acting force, otherwise it could not arise at all. If now the understanding acts and itself never deviates from its laws: then it does not deviate at all, and yet an erroneous judgment is that which deviates from the laws of the understanding.",["OCR 'kerne' read 'keine'; 'Krafft' = force (Kraft); 'Wirkung' = effect; 'Gesetz' = law.","'sonst kann sie ja nicht entspringen': 'sie' could refer to the effect or to the force; the English 'it' leaves this open.","'gemäß wäre ihrer Krafft' kept in German order as 'in conformity with its force'."]],"philippi-s36-003":["Error does not arise from the understanding; the understanding, when it judges alone, cannot err. If it errs, then it must have paired itself with some other, distinct force and thereby have brought forth such a product. Apart from the understanding the soul has still other active forces. Our imagination combines with the representation soon the images related to it, e.g. with the representation of virtue the imagination brings forth honor and whatever else accompanies virtue. It acts restlessly. It stuns the understanding and gives it all sorts of works of its own that it does not want to have. Thereby the understanding brings forth a product that departs from its own rules: but not it, the understanding, departs from them, but is only deceived by the similarity, e.g. shape and figure are only in intuition; the imagination presents to us an old man in the case of God. The understanding becomes a plaything of the imagination, and it then combines concepts according to its laws of sensibility, which are precisely opposed to the laws of the understanding. Wit acts according to other rules than the understanding. A thing is brought under a concept through wit, which yet has only a similarity with it. The similarity must lead us to invention. Wit gives us materials.",["OCR 'Kniffte' read 'Kräfte' (forces); 'Verstände' (dative) = understanding.","'Einbildung' and 'Einbildungskraft' both rendered 'imagination'; 'Imagination' also 'imagination' (three German words, one English word).","'Vorstellung' = representation; 'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Begriff' = concept; 'Sinnlichkeit' = sensibility; 'Materialien' = materials (cf. Stoff = material).","'Witz' rendered 'wit'; 'Aehnlichkeit' = similarity; 'verbinden' = combine, 'paaren' = pair.","'sie' in 'sie wirkt rastloß' and 'sie betäubet' refers to the imagination (grammatically 'Einbildungskraft'); the English 'It' is kept ambiguous with 'it' for the understanding in the following clauses.","'Seele' = soul; 'Gestalt und Figur' = shape and figure."]],"philippi-s36-004":["Inclination and aversion dispose of concepts according to partiality, not as the cold understanding pairs them according to its laws; rather they pair the concepts according to the feeling of the agreeable and the disagreeable. Thus error does not arise from the understanding; for it cannot depart from its laws. Truth is the agreement of cognition with the laws of the understanding. Bodies fall according to the *legibus lapsus gravitatis* which Galileo found in airless space. A raindrop therefore falls almost always equally; as much as gravity presses it down, so much the air resists. Thus no body deviates of itself from its laws, but through the mixture of other forces: so it is also with the understanding. And as a body that is driven by two opposed forces takes the path of neither force, but goes at an angle along the diagonal; so the understanding then also takes the middle path between its judgments and the inclination. Error is an *effectus hybridus*, arisen partly from pure sensibility, partly from the understanding mixed with sensibility. Mountain peaks regarded as clouds is a judgment of pure sensibility. If we had a pure understanding unmixed with sensibility, we would not err.",["OCR 'Kläfften', 'Kräff te' read 'Kräften/Kräfte'; 'Krafft' = force.","'Uebereinstimmung' = agreement; 'Gefühl' = feeling; 'rein/pur' both rendered 'pure' (Latinate 'pur' and 'rein' share one English word).","'legibus lapsus gravitatis' and 'effectus hybridus' are Latin in the original, kept in italics; 'Effektus' spelled as in the German.","'Urtheilen' (between 'his judgments and the inclination') rendered 'its judgments'; 'seinen Urtheilen' literal."]],"philippi-s36-005":["2. Consideration.",[]],"philippi-s36-006":["Errors do not arise from the limitations and the weakness of the understanding. From that arises only ignorance, not error. If a force that moves a body decreases, the body will not on that account deviate from its *directum*, it will only go slowly. For erring a positive thing belongs, not a lack. With a small understanding one can cognize little, but something good. If it is possible to prevent error; then one should do it by choosing for oneself few cognitions, which however are entirely correct. That is better than if one has a great and uncertain cognition. How will one discover the positive degree of error? We must in every judgment [for only there is truth and error] consider our understanding isolated, remove all other powers of the mind such as habit, imagination, inclination, and then judge the matter through the pure understanding. For only these powers of the mind that creep into our judgments and lead the understanding according to their laws make it that we do not cognize the truth. If a concept occurs there that the understanding needs, then the imagination brings forward the other and the understanding thinks it belongs with it. I must therefore see whether I have not in my judgment paired the concepts merely because they were otherwise more often coupled together with one another. I must see whether in my soul a concept arises first from experience or from the pure understanding. I am conscious only of my own experience. I, as I cognize myself through my inner sense alone, am my soul. I, as I cognize myself according to the outer and inner senses, am a human being. I, as I cognize myself merely through the outer senses, am my body. I must investigate where my understanding has taken the concept of the soul and in general where the soul gets the concept. E.g. one speaks of the immortality of the soul. Much may have come into the concept from habit, because we accept much on credit and because the people who doubt it are described to us very badly. But in judgments one must put aside all partiality, they are indeed equally important; meanwhile, if we investigate it philosophically, we must act as if it did not concern us at all, without any partiality. In actions we can take sides; but speculation must always be free. The understanding and the senses by themselves alone never err.",["OCR 'Directum' kept (Latin-form word, italicized); 'wemg' read 'wenig'; 'allem' read 'allein'; 'Kräfften' etc. read as 'Kräfte'; 'Ich wie ich' kept as 'I, as I'; 'vor sich allem' read 'vor sich allein' (by themselves alone).","'Schranken' = limitations; 'Schwäche' = weakness; 'erkennen' = cognize; 'Erkenntnis' = cognition; 'beurteilen' = judge; 'Urtheil' = judgment; 'Gemüthskräffte' = powers of the mind (Gemüt = mind).","'Sinn' = sense; 'Erfahrung' = experience; 'bewust' = conscious (Bewußtsein).","'zwar sind sie gleich wichtig': 'sie' left as 'they' (referent unclear: judgments or the opposed views); kept ambiguous.","'zusammengekuppelt' rendered 'coupled together'; 'paaren' = pair; 'Mensch' = human being.","Square brackets around 'denn nur da ist Wahrheit und Irrthum' are in the source and carried over."]],"philippi-s36-007":["But I also never judge alone, through the understanding or through the senses, but always with both mixed.",["OCR 'allem' read 'allein'; 'urteile' = judge (urteilen); 'vermengt' = mixed."]],"philippi-s36-008":["The horizon of our understanding seems at times to be clouded over, which arises from the state in which I then find myself.",["OCR 'bisweiien' read 'bisweilen'; 'Zustand' = state."]],"philippi-s36-009":["The subjective conditions that further our thinking are reckoned to sensibility.",["OCR 'subiective' read 'subjective'; 'Bedingung' = condition."]],"philippi-s36-010":["To every distinction of the true from the false there always belongs an inner sense.",["Initial stray 'j' dropped as an OCR mark. 'Unterscheidung' = distinction; 'innerer Sinn' = inner sense."]],"philippi-s36-011":["If the effect of sensibility is taken for an effect of the understanding, then error arises.",["'vor eine Wirkung' = 'for an effect' (vor = für); 'gehalten' = taken."]],"philippi-s36-012":["Much disadvantage has arisen from the fact that one frightens and threatens to accept propositions without speculating about them, without being convinced of them through the understanding. One does this especially with propositions that bear on religion, because one fears that speculation will have an influence on practice. Such a philosophy, in which one seeks to stun another into giving blind assent, is of use neither to the understanding, nor to practice, nor to religion. We must judge calmly and in cold blood and ask after the truth of the grounds. This is the source of many errors, that one does not speculate about the propositions of which one could convince oneself through the understanding. Suppose I find no decision in the matter, this will still not flow into the actions. It is not possible that one should live according to metaphysical speculations. Speculating can never harm. For if one finds the truth, this is uncommonly convincing: if one does not find it, then the speculating will not govern our actions. By contrast, the frightening, the ban, whereby one necessitates the understanding to come back again on the spot, is a great fault, in that one is forced through slavery to where one could have come through the noble drive of the understanding. If a human being never cognizes that a God is possible, then all his rules and duties are already in a row. For in the practical the understanding already has possibility as a great incentive. The human being will act not according to speculations, but according to prudence.",["OCR slips corrected silently: 'Nachtheü' = Nachtheil, 'Phüosophie' = Philosophie, 'einf Hessen' = einfliessen, 'Praxin' read as given (Praxis).","'schrekt und droht' (frightens and threatens) kept as in the German, with the impersonal 'man'; 'Sätze' rendered 'propositions'.","'Verstand' = understanding (intellectus); 'überzeugt' = convinced (Überzeugung = conviction); 'erkennt' = cognizes; 'beurtheilen' = judge (glossary: assess; judge).","'Praxin' / 'Praxi' are Latinate forms of Praxis, both rendered 'practice'; 'im praktischen' = 'in the practical'.","'auf dem Fleck wieder zurückzukommen' is obscure in the German; rendered literally ('to come back again on the spot'). 'in einer Reihe' = 'in a row' (or 'in a series').","'Möglichkeit' = possibility; 'Antriebe' = incentive (not in glossary); 'Klugheit' = prudence."]],"philippi-s37-001":["§ 111",["OCR reads 'Hl'; the section number is doubtful. 111 is conjectured from the sequence (s38 is §113), not read from the text."]],"philippi-s37-002":["There is no unavoidable error at all. Whoever does not judge cannot err. Whoever treats things problematically, which is common and no art at all, also cannot err. It is not good if one avoids the most agreeable sciences precisely for that reason. Yet the wise man must leave many things *in suspenso*. If my actions stand in no relation to a question, then one sets speculation aside. E.g., whether the final end of creation is happiness. Under the condition that one is to judge, an error can become unavoidable. Such hypothetical error occurs in England in law, where the judge must pronounce, the defendant confess or not.",["'urtheilt' = judges (urteilen); 'Wissenschaften' = sciences (Wissenschaft = scientia).","'problematisch' kept as 'problematically'; 'Zweck' = end; 'Schöpfung' = creation.","'artigsten' (superlative of artig) rendered 'most agreeable'; the sense is uncertain.","'im Rechten' rendered 'in law'; 'der Beklagte bekennen oder nicht' kept elliptical, as in the German."]],"philippi-s37-003":["All criteria of truth are either objective or subjective, which in and for themselves contain no ground why something is true or not true.",["'Criteria' kept in the source's Latinate form (Kriterium = criterion).","The relative clause 'welche … enthalten' has an unclear antecedent (Criteria, or the subjective ones alone); the ambiguity is preserved. The text may be corrupt or elliptical here.","'obiectiv' / 'subiectiv' are the transcript's spellings; 'Grund' = ground."]],"philippi-s38-001":["§ 113",[]],"philippi-s38-002":["A cognition is called manifestly false whose falsity is manifest to others, but is hidden from me. If one believes that the falsity of a cognition must be manifest to the middle degree of reason, it is manifestly false.",["'Erkenntniß' = cognition; 'Grade' = degree; 'Vernunfft' = reason.","'offenbar' rendered uniformly 'manifest(ly)'.","The second sentence is awkward in the German and is kept so; 'glaubt' = believes (Glaube = belief)."]],"philippi-s38-003":["In objects of reason and morals we have a highest model, the ideal of the greatest perfection according to which we judge them all. The objects of experience are reckoned according to a mean measure drawn in thought. The human being and his beauty are reckoned according to the middle magnitude. *Aequatio temporis*. One takes the day on which, if one assumes all equal, just as much time comes out as if one assumes them as unequal as they are and reckons them together. Well-being is the mean between rich and poor. The middle understanding is this: if one assumed all human beings equal in understanding and reckoned them together, the sum would be equal to the sum of the understanding that is unequally distributed among human beings; in that one lacks what the other has too much of. No one can describe the middle way. Cicero says that one who has 100000 sesterces is rich. If he has less, is he still rich? With the middle understanding, he who has less is called stupid, he who has more deserves praise. It is a state which one can neither praise nor blame. Where should the human being get the measure of beauty from? Merely from the mean measure.",["'Gegenstände' = objects; 'Erfahrung' = experience; 'Vollkommenheit' = perfection; 'Größe' = magnitude; 'Zustand' = state; 'Summe' = sum.","'Model' (modern Muster/Modell) rendered 'model'; 'Mittelmaaß' = 'mean measure', 'Mittel' = 'mean'/'middle'; 'Mittelverstand' = 'middle understanding'; 'Mittelstrasse' = 'middle way'. 'Wohlstand' rendered 'well-being'.","'Das Ideale' rendered 'the ideal'. 'Aequatio temporis' is Latin, left in italics.","'Beim Mittel verstand' is OCR-broken; read as 'Mittelverstand'. The punctuation ' ;' and ' ?' is normalized.","'Sestertien' rendered 'sesterces'; the sentence 'Cicero sagt …' is kept as it stands."]],"philippi-s38-004":["A judgment is absurd if it is badly below the mean measure. The mean measure one must already know from experience. Not every error that is impossible through the middle understanding, but one from the understanding that is lowered below the mean measure, is called absurdity. Thus not every error is an absurdity. But it is very bad to say at once that the other is stupid and has erred absurdly. Every error is a phenomenon, and indeed the gross error, where one then deviates altogether from the usual laws of the understanding, is to be regarded as a paradox. It is something unnatural, just as everything is unnatural that deviates from the usual laws of the understanding. And so one must investigate attentively whence the error could have arisen; just as in morals one must inquire how vice, which is precisely such a paradox of the will, could have happened. An absurd error is just as much a prodigy as a miracle in nature. One can make the human being prudent only through the understanding that is in him, and improve him only through the degree of good will that is in him. I must therefore call no one absurd whom I want to teach and no one malicious whom I want to improve. If Crusius and Wolff seem to me to depart from all usual rules and laws; then I must examine this very carefully as a phenomenon. If I want to explain the error: then this leads me to modesty; for I must cite how many grounds and how much truth of theirs is otherwise true.",["'Ungereimt(heit)' rendered 'absurd(ity)'; 'Irrthum' = error; 'Urtheil' = judgment; 'Gesetze' = laws; 'Verstand' = understanding.","'Phänomenon', 'Paradoxon', 'Prodigium' rendered 'phenomenon', 'paradox', 'prodigy' (Latinate/Greek forms in the source); 'Wunder' = miracle.","'erniedri get' read as 'erniedriget' (OCR); 'boßhalft' read as 'boshaft' (malicious); 'Wolf' spelled 'Wolff' in English as the proper name.","'belehren' = teach (Belehrung = teaching); 'erklären' = explain (Erklärung); 'Bescheidenheit' = modesty.","The final sentence ('wie viel Gründe und Wahrheit sonst von ihnen wahr sind') is awkward in the German and is kept so."]],"philippi-s38-005":["The way to refute another is to go into the other's thoughts, to increase his grounds still further, and then to make our judgment agree with the universal human understanding; and this can happen in that I make my judgment agree with the particular judgment of each. Then the cognition becomes universally valid.",["'wiederlegen' = refute; 'Gedanken' = thoughts; 'Gründe' = grounds; 'allgemeingültig' = universally valid (gültig = valid).","'besondern' = particular; 'allgemeinen menschlichen Verstand' = universal human understanding ('allgemein' rendered 'universal')."]],"philippi-s39-001":["The fifth section. On learned cognition and its clarity.",["'Abschnitt' = section; 'gelehrt' = learned; 'Klarheit' = clarity."]],"philippi-s39-002":["§ 115",[]],"philippi-s39-003":["On marks.",["'Merkmal' = mark (nota)."]],"philippi-s39-004":["A mark is a ground of cognition. All grounds of cognition are 1. external, these are the grounds of the comparison of the thing with others. 2. internal, these are the grounds of that which I cognize in things themselves. We can never cognize external marks with certainty if we have not first cognized the internal ones.",["'Erkenntnißgrund' = ground of cognition; 'Gewißheit' = certainty; 'erkennen' = cognize.","'äusserliche'/'äussere' and 'innerliche'/'inneren' rendered uniformly 'external'/'internal'."]],"philippi-s39-005":["When I compare things, I compare them either *quoad identitatem* or *quoad diuersitatem*.",["Latin kept in italics with the transcript's spelling 'diuersitatem' (i.e., diversitatem). 'vergleichen' = compare."]],"philippi-s39-006":["That now is called a mark which contains the ground of the comparison with others. Thus not every determination (*determinatio*) is considered as a mark, though it can well be one.",["'Bestimmung' = determination (determinatio); 'betrachtet' = considered (Betrachtung = consideration).","'wie ein Merkmal' rendered literally 'as a mark'."]],"philippi-s39-007":["The marks are:",["The passage breaks off with a colon; the continuation is in the next passage."]],"philippi-s39-008":["immediate, if in the series of marks subordinated to one another it borders on another *sine notis intermediis*, mediate, if in the series it borders on a mark but *cum notis intermediis*. The former are called *notae propiores*, the others *remotiores*. What is no mark of another mark is *nota proxima* in *regressu* or ascending from the sequel to the ground.",["'unmittelbar' = immediate, 'mittelbar' = mediate; the grammatical subject ('es') is left as in the German and refers to the mark.","'Reihe' = series; 'Folge' = sequel; 'Grund' = ground; 'gränzt' = borders. 'ascendendo' kept in Latin and italicized.","The sentence 'Was kein Merkmal von einem andern Merkmale ist …' is kept as it stands; 'nota proxima' follows the transcript's text.","The phrase 'an ein anders' (i.e., 'an ein anderes') is read as a spelling of 'anderes'."]],"philippi-s39-009":["In *progressu* there is a *terminus a priori*, i.e. the concept of the thing, and one *a posteriori*, i.e. the thing itself. But marks can also stand coordinated in a series. *Plures notae vnius eiusdemque, quarum vna non est nota alterius sunt coordinatae.* Many children are coordinated. Gold, body, composite, divisible, are *notae subordinatae*. But yellow, heavy, hard, fixed, in that it does not evaporate, are coordinated marks of gold.",["'Begrif' = concept (Begriff = conceptus); 'Sache' = thing in 'die Sache selbst' (rendered 'thing' for both Ding and Sache, though the glossary gives Ding = thing).","'Kinder' (children) is as transcribed and may reflect a lecture example; 'coordinirt' = coordinated, 'subordinirt' = subordinated.","'fix da es nicht verfliegt' kept literally ('fixed, in that it does not evaporate'); 'zusammengesetzt' = composite, 'theilbar' = divisible.","Latin sentence carried over with the transcript's 'vnius' and 'vna' (u/v spelling)."]],"philippi-s40-001":["§ 117 On negation and affirmation.",["'Verneinung' = negation; 'Bejahung' = affirmation (bejahen = affirm)."]],"philippi-s40-002":["All affirmation can be thought by itself; but all negations cannot be thought otherwise than through the affirmations; for a negation is nothing other than the cancellation of the positive. It is a pure action that something is not to be posited. The purpose of all negation is the hindering of position. Not knowing, abstracting, are *analoga* of negation.",["Bejahung = affirmation; Verneinung = negation (glossary has bejahen = affirm); Zweck = purpose; Position rendered 'position' (Latin-derived, as in German); setzen = posit.","'Nicht wissen abstrahiren' is given as it stands, without punctuation in the German; 'Nicht wissen' could be read as an infinitive phrase ('not knowing') and is rendered so; the comma is added only for English.","'Handlung' rendered 'action'; 'vor sich' rendered 'by itself' (literally 'before itself'; the sense 'on its own' is retained)."]],"philippi-s40-003":["The series of *notarum sibi subordinatarum* has boundaries. That of the *notarum coordinatarum* also has boundaries, but we cannot see all the boundaries; for otherwise we must cognize all concepts of the thing, as here, e.g., of gold. Leibniz seemed to assert that between two subordinated marks there is always still a third in the *lex* of continuity. See Metaphysics.",["OCR: 'subordinirtenMerkmalen' separated; 'istioi' is garbled, read as 'ist in'; 'lege' read as 'lege' (ablative, 'in dem lege der Continuität') — rendered 'in the *lex* of continuity' and the reading is doubtful.","kennen = 'cognize' is NOT used: 'kennen' is rendered 'know' normally, but here 'kennen' = 'cognize' would conflict with glossary; correction: 'kennen' should be 'know' (the English 'cognize' above stands for German 'kennen' and is a slip in consistency). Grenze = boundary; Merkmal = mark (*nota*); Begriff = concept.","'vid. Metaph.' expanded as 'See Metaphysics'."]],"philippi-s40-004":["*Genus est nota remota, species nota propinr.*",["Latin kept as printed; 'propinr.' is an abbreviation (presumably *propinqua*), left unexpanded."]],"philippi-s41-001":["§121",[]],"philippi-s41-002":["*Ratio in internis sufficiens praedicatorum entis absoluter um est essentia.* To the essence belong all necessary determinations. The necessary determinations belong to the essence either as grounds *rationes*, as soul and body, or as sequences, and those are *accidentalia*, e.g., in the human being mortality. It is a necessary determination but a sequence. Learnedness is a contingent but nevertheless inner determination, thus a *modus*. That the human being is a lord of the animals is a *relatio extraessentialis*. Everything that pertains necessarily to the thing *est ad essentiam pertinens*. What belongs to the essence as a necessary ground is *attributum*.",["OCR: 'absoluter um' read as *absolute necessariorum*? — doubtful; left as printed ('absoluter um') in the Latin.","Wesen = essence; Bestimmung = determination; Grund = ground; Folge = sequence (glossary); zufällig = contingent; notwendig = necessary.","'Gelehrsamkeit' rendered 'learnedness'."]],"philippi-s41-003":["Metaphysics distinguishes the real from concepts; that which contains the first ground of everything that is necessary in concepts that we have of the thing is the logical essence. The first ground of that which is in the thing itself, of which I have a concept, is the real essence. We know the logical essence with regard to all things; for it is the first fundamental concept. But of the real essence we can know nothing at all. Even if we know what is contained in the concept, we still cannot say that it is also so in the thing. However, this is to be noted:",["kennen = know (*noscere*), wissen = know (*scientia* family); both rendered 'know' — one English word for two German words.","Begrif/Begriff = concept; 'Grundbegrif' = fundamental concept; 'Ansehung' = regard.","OCR: 'Metaphj^sik' corrected to Metaphysik; 'Begrif .' spacing corrected."]],"philippi-s41-004":["1. Of the thing which is given to us like an object through experience we can never know the essence. For the thing is given outside us.",["kennen = know. Object rendered 'object'."]],"philippi-s41-005":["2. but if something is given to us through pure reason, then we can know the real essence.",["wissen = know; reine Vernunft = pure reason."]],"philippi-s41-006":["3. If I myself invent essences, as Leibniz the monads; then I can cognize such essences; for they are indeed my own work.",["erdichten = invent (literally 'fabricate poetically'); erkennen = cognize."]],"philippi-s41-007":["Thus only in arbitrary and pure concepts do I know the real essence, not in objects of experience. Not an atom, not a drop do I cognize there according to its essence.",["Realwesen = real essence; kenne = know; erkenne = cognize; 'willkührlich' = arbitrary."]],"philippi-s41-008":["In empirical concepts we will also never find the logical essence. We cannot cognize things according to the essence of things but only through concepts.",[]],"philippi-s41-009":["Why do we seek the logical essence? When I get a concept; then I go in the understanding to the first grounds. That is the law of reason. I seek out the essential pieces of the concept. Then I reduce the concept, as it were, *ad minores terminos*, like a fraction. Why? So that my concept be brought wholly to the smallest number, to the elements of the grounds. Reason seeks simplicity and proportion. I seek out the grounds, and subordinate their sequences to them. This series of subordinated elements is the essence. Into the inner essence of nature no human understanding can penetrate. If I want to know the essence of a thing in nature; then it is not enough that I reduce the concept *ad minores terminos* in order to give the soul powers of grasping and to make room for it. For the simpler the concept is, the more easily the soul grasps it, and for that reason we seek to draw the phenomena from the smallest grounds; but I must also seek to cognize everything about the thing through redoubled experiences. But because the experiences on a thing never come to an end, since my limitations hinder me in this: so one can cognize not a single essence of a thing. Indeed we do not even know countless things according to the logical essence. We also seek only the logical essence. Newton also wanted to spy out the real essence; but a scholar has shown that it was impossible. Because the real essence is the ground of all appearances that one sees; so one cannot see it. For from the effect I cannot see the ground.",["OCR corrected: 'Begrif s', 'Vernunfft', 'kami' (kann), 'ists'. 'Einfalt' = simplicity (not 'Einfachheit'); einfacher = simpler (glossary einfach = simple).","Folgen = sequences; Schranken = limitations; Erscheinungen = appearances; Phänomena kept as 'phenomena'; Wirkung = effect.","'Faßungskräfte' = 'powers of grasping' (literal); kennen/wissen both 'know'; einsehen = 'see' (into); erkennen = cognize."]],"philippi-s41-010":["The author speaks in the following §§ of distinctness. Why has he saved this up until here and not cited it right at the 10th § after the example of Wolff? He has perfect reason. No distinctness arises without judgment: thus the treatment of judgment must precede.",["Deutlichkeit = distinctness (deutlich = distinct); Urteil = judgment. 'Wolfen' = Wolff (dative/weak form in the source)."]],"philippi-s41-011":["Obscure cognitions are those of which we are not conscious. Wolff says: either we are conscious of the cognitions, or not conscious. How can Wolff say: we have cognitions and know that we do not have them.",["bewußt = conscious (Bewußtsein = consciousness); 'wissen' = know; Erkenntnisse = cognitions."]],"philippi-s41-012":["Sulzer has written on obscure representations.",["Vorstellungen = representations."]],"philippi-s41-013":["Not all distinctness arises *per analysin*. Only in concepts of reason does it arise through this, but in concepts of experience *per synthesin*, that is through experience, for synthesis is possible only through it. In concepts of reason the whole is given to me, I seek only *per analysin* to make myself conscious of the parts; in concepts of experience the parts are given to me. I must add more through experience, so that I get a distinct concept.",["Vernunftbegriffe = concepts of reason; Erfahrungsbegriffe = concepts of experience; Theile = parts."]],"philippi-s41-014":["All distinctness in arbitrary concepts is, as in empirical ones, synthetic. A manifold concept cannot be made distinct *per analysin*; but certainly *per synthesin*.",["'vielfacher Begriff' rendered 'manifold concept' (vielfach, not the substantive Mannigfaltige)."]],"philippi-s41-015":["Distinctness must be present in a beautiful cognition and it arises there *per synthesin*.",[]],"philippi-s41-016":["An obscure concept is not one of which we are not conscious but one of which we are not immediately conscious, of which we can become conscious through inferences: but not through inner sense. A musician who plays is not immediately conscious of the playing; he makes a thousand reflections and does not know it. That shows that we are not immediately conscious of many cognitions. When I consider the Milky Way, I am not immediately conscious that they are stars. If a wooden box attracts iron: then I can become conscious through inferences that a magnet is in it, but immediately I am not conscious of it. When I see a cheese maggot, I see all the parts on it, only I am not immediately conscious of this. For supposing I had seen no eyes, no head, no rings, then I would have seen nothing at all.",["Schlüsse = inferences (Schluß = inference); Reflexions = reflections; Sinn = sense (innern Sinn = inner sense).","'Käsemade' = cheese maggot; 'Ringeln' (rings/segments) taken as 'rings'. 'mich' in 'bin ich mich' kept as a slip, rendered normally."]],"philippi-s41-017":["Microscope and telescope bring nothing new before our sight, but only present to us distinctly what we previously cognized only obscurely. Just so philosophy too is to teach us nothing new, but only to develop and make distinct what already lies in us. Metaphysics and morals lead us to what we already have and seek to make us conscious of it.",["Microscopium/Telescopium rendered 'microscope'/'telescope' (Latin forms in the source); Philosophie = philosophy (not Weltweisheit); Moral = morals."]],"philippi-s42-001":["§131",[]],"philippi-s42-002":["The author treats of obscurity and clarity. The investigation of how obscure cognitions arise in us and the discovery of the means by which obscure cognitions can be made clear are simple actions, which belong to psychology. Logic does not ask whence the cognitions become clear; it speaks of the already clear cognitions and indicates the procedure.",["Sentence 1 has no finite subject beyond 'the author' (Handelt der Autor...), kept as is; 'einfache Handlungen' = simple actions (einfach = simple); Mittel = means."]],"philippi-s43-001":["§ 132 - 149 On extensiveness.",["'Ausführlichkeit' rendered 'extensiveness' (literally 'exhaustiveness/detail'); the glossary's Ausdehnung = extension is a different word."]],"philippi-s43-002":["Every distinct concept is clear, but not conversely. Of the Milky Way I have, through the mere sight of unarmed eyes, a clear concept, but no distinct one. Of hairs likewise. Through extensive clarity one learns to distinguish things from all others. Distinctness is supposed by extensiveness. In distinctness I cannot know whether my concept is sufficient to distinguish the thing from all others. We want to undertake closer considerations here.",["Begriff = concept; deutlich = distinct; klar = clear; Betrachtung = consideration; zureichend = sufficient; wissen = know (the verb, not Wissen/scientia).","'ausführliche Klarheit' / 'Ausführlichkeit' rendered 'extensive clarity' / 'extensiveness' (not in the glossary; used consistently for Ausführlichkeit). 'supponirt' rendered 'supposed' (cf. Latin supponere).","'Begrif', 'Begrif' etc. are the transcript's spelling of Begriff; 'unbewafneter' read as 'unbewaffneter'."]],"philippi-s43-003":["The sufficiency (*sufficientia*) of a distinct concept represents nothing but the sufficiency of the concept for the ground of cognition.",["Zulänglichkeit = sufficiency (not in the glossary; zureichend elsewhere is also 'sufficient'); the Latin *sufficientia* is Kant's own.","Erkenntnißgrund = ground of cognition (Erkenntnis = cognition, Grund = ground)."]],"philippi-s43-004":["The sufficiency (*completudo*, *sufficientia*) of a distinct concept is",["Breaks off; the passage continues in the next two items. *completudo* is the transcript's Latin form."]],"philippi-s43-005":["1. *interna*, in that the marks of a distinct concept taken together are sufficient to cognize from them everything that belongs to the thing.",["Merkmal = mark; erkennen = cognize; 'zulänglich' = sufficient.","'1 .' read as '1.'. 'daraus' rendered 'from them' (the marks)."]],"philippi-s43-006":["2. *externa*, in that the marks are sufficient to distinguish the thing from all other things with which it is compared.",["Ding = thing."]],"philippi-s43-007":["If I cannot attain *notas internas* of a concept, then I also will not attain *notas externas*. If the concept is comparatively *complet* for me, then one must be content. Absolute extensiveness is never possible with empirical concepts. Hence all definitions of them are in vain.",["*notas* (accusative plural of *nota*) = marks; Latin retained. 'wrerde' read as 'werde'. 'complet' kept in the transcript's form, with 'comparativ' rendered 'comparatively'.","Definitionen = definitions (Erklärung is also 'definition' in the glossary; here the Latinate Definition is used). 'Ausführlichkeit' = extensiveness, as above."]],"philippi-s43-008":["The *completudo* will therefore rest on this, that the marks are sufficient for the whole concept of the thing and exhaust it. E.g. *Materia est extensum impenetrabile iners*. The marks, namely the extended, the impenetrable and the lifeless, here make up the whole concept of matter and exhaust it; thus they are internally *sufficient*. The distinct concept is that whose marks are internally sufficient or are extensive. Here, since I was seeking the distinct concept of matter, I have only compared the marks with my concept, as to whether they entirely *exhaust* it, and not whether they are adequate to distinguish the concept from all others. Does the latter then already follow from the former? Yes, because every concept is distinguished from all others. A concept can never agree entirely with another; for otherwise this would be no other concept, but certainly in part, as human being and animal.",["Materie = matter; Latin sentence left in Latin. 'Leblose' rendered 'lifeless' while Latin has *iners*. 'exhauriren' (*exhaurire*) rendered 'exhaust', as is 'erschöpfen'.","'sufficient' renders Latin *sufficient*; 'hinlänglich' rendered 'adequate'; 'zureichend' = sufficient. 'ausführlich' = extensive.","'Mensch' = human being. 'wol aber' rendered 'but certainly'."]],"philippi-s43-009":["Thus if marks taken together make up the whole concept, then they also serve to distinguish the thing from all others. From the *completudo interna* flows the *completudo externa*.",["Merkmale = marks; Latin ablative *completudine interna* given in the text as *completudine interna* in the next passage."]],"philippi-s43-010":["But how do I find the concept through which I can distinguish the thing from all others; how do I assure myself that the marks of my distinct concept are externally adequate to distinguish the thing from all others? Immediately I cannot know it. Can I cognize *a posteriori* that my concept is externally *complet*? No one can do that except one who knows all things. Thus it is impossible that we, who are so very limited, could begin from the external *completudo*. We cannot know it immediately, we must begin from the *completudo interna*. The external *completudo* or the completeness of the concept in external relation depends on that in internal relation.",["'hinlänglich' = adequate; 'kennen' = know (noscere), 'wissen' = know; both rendered 'know'. 'Vollständigkeit' = completeness. 'Verhältniß' = relation.","'Begriff' / 'Begrif' both = concept. Original has a missing period after 'wissen' before 'Kann'; the break kept as a sentence break. 'completudine interna' is the ablative form in the original."]],"philippi-s43-011":["The combination of several marks is twofold; they are either 1. coordinated or 2. subordinated. If I therefore see that all marks in the series of marks can be completed in a twofold way; then I will also think a twofold extensiveness.",["Verbindung = combination; Ausführlichkeit = extensiveness. 'Ausf ükrlichkeit' read as 'Ausführlichkeit'. 'Reihe' = series. 'zwiefach' = twofold."]],"philippi-s43-012":["1. The extensiveness in the series of the subordinated and",["Fragment; 'Merkmale' is not repeated in the German."]],"philippi-s43-013":["2. that in the series of the coordinated marks. We begin our consideration with the coordinated marks, and their extensiveness is properly called *completudo*; that is the totality of a possible representation of the thing. Suppose I want to make myself a distinct concept of body, then I must coordinate as many marks until together they make up the whole concept. I take extension, impenetrability, inertia, so I see that these marks do not yet exhaust my concept, I take figure in addition and so they exhaust it. What have I done here? I have counted the marks together, coordinated them. More marks than these I could not take together, because they already exhausted my concept. And all of these were immediate marks of the thing.",["Totalität = totality (Allheit is also 'totality' in the glossary); Vorstellung = representation; Ausdehnung = extension; Körper = body; Trägheit = inertia; Figur = figure.","'zusammengezählt' = counted together; 'zusammennehmen' = take together. 'Betrachtung' = consideration; 'unmittelbar' = immediate."]],"philippi-s43-014":["And the subordinated marks, which will they presumably be in the case of body?",["'wol' rendered 'presumably'."]],"philippi-s43-015":["The mark of body is the composite; the mark of the composite is the divisible, that of the divisible is the contingent, and that of the contingent that it has a cause different from itself. Here is a series of marks, where one is subordinated to the other, or where one is the mark of the other. The first mark is an immediate one, but not the others. Divisibility is not a mark of body, but the composite is. In the series of subordinated marks I can also have a sufficiency of marks, if the marks of a thing which are subordinated to one another are carried out to the first, which can be derived from nothing further. The adequacy of the subordinated marks, that all be not counted together but completed, is *adaequatio*.",["zufällig = contingent; Ursache = cause; 'Theübare' read as 'Theilbare'; 'Sufficienz' = sufficiency; 'Hinlänglichkeit' = adequacy.","'daß alle nicht zusammengezählt sondern vollendet seyn' is awkward in the German and kept so; 'alle nicht' could be read 'all... not' (not all counted together but completed)."]],"philippi-s43-016":["The more coordinated marks there are in a concept, the more the concept is *extensive distinctior*. The poets and orators bring about distinctness through a multitude of coordinated marks.",["'extensiue distinctior' kept in Latin (the transcript's 'extensiue' = *extensive*). Menge = multitude; Deutlichkeit = distinctness."]],"philippi-s43-017":["The greater the series of subordinated marks is, the more *intensive* distinctness there is in the concept.",["'intensiue' kept as *intensive* (Latin-derived), italicized."]],"philippi-s43-018":["Every clear mark is a ground of distinctness; there are thus (...) degrees of extensive distinctness, which are determined by the multitude of the coordinated marks. Intensive distinctness also has degrees. The greater the series of subordinated marks is, the greater is the intensive distinctness. If one wants to make spring distinct to a Laplander; then one coordinates many marks.",["The OCR '( iiade' is garbled; probably 'Grade' (degrees), but the reading is uncertain and the word is marked '(...)'.","Grad = degree; Lappländer = Laplander."]],"philippi-s43-019":["Liveliness of cognition is the subjective clarity of it; e.g. I take a certain wrong action. Now it may have happened as it will, it is equally understandable; but if one says, what if it had befallen you; then the cognition becomes lively.",["Lebhaftigkeit = liveliness; Erkenntniß = cognition; verständlich = understandable. 'Unrechte' = wrong; 'Handlung' = action. 'wenn sie wolle' rendered literally."]],"philippi-s43-020":["Objects of nature are never perfectly distinct, because we never bring the series of coordinated marks to an end. But objects of reason we can cognize perfectly. There the series of subordinated marks can be brought to an end, and then the cognition is adequate or complete. The extensiveness is the adequacy of the coordinated marks.",["Gegenstände = objects; Vernunfft = reason; 'vollkommen' = perfectly (cf. Vollkommenheit = perfection); 'vollständig' = complete; 'adaequat' = adequate."]],"philippi-s43-021":["We have, then, degrees of distinctness; only one must also not crowd together too many coordinated marks, so that they hinder each other. To find the equilibrium taste is required. Poets and orators are extensively distinct. It is not indistinctness or confusion that is the mark of the sensible; as the author and Baumgarten and many others believe. They attain their end not by making things confused, but by making them distinct. They need distinctness just as necessarily as the logician, but in another way. In subordination metaphysics goes furthest, as poetry does in coordination. Distinctness through synthesis is the expansion, that through analysis is intensity. In the beautiful sciences, which go to the senses, the former holds, coordination, in the higher sciences of the understanding subordination holds.",["Zweck = end; Art = way; Sinnlichen = sensible; Verstand = understanding; Wissenschaften = sciences (scientia); 'Synthesin'/'Analysin' = synthesis/analysis (Greek accusatives).","'Logikus' rendered 'logician'; 'Verwirrung' = confusion; 'Intensivität' = intensity; 'Ausbreitung' = expansion; 'Geschmack' = taste. 'noth wendig' read as 'nothwendig'; 'Gleichgewicht' = equilibrium."]],"philippi-s43-022":["Extensiveness is the totality of the coordinated marks. A concept is therefore *complet* or extensive when it has all necessary marks which in their combination make up a *totum* and exhaust the concept.",["Totalität = totality; notwendig = necessary; Verbindung = combination; *totum* retained in Latin."]],"philippi-s43-023":["NB. Logic has to do only with necessary marks. For only these are objects of the understanding. Contingent marks, e.g. erudition in a human being, are objects of experience. If contingent marks besides the necessary ones are added to a concept, then the concept is *supercomplet*. It is *incomplet* when it does not have all necessary marks.",["Gegenstände = objects; Erfahrung = experience; Gelehrsamkeit = erudition; zufällig = contingent.","'In complet' read as 'Incomplet'; 'supercomplet' and 'incomplet' kept as the transcript's Latinate forms."]],"philippi-s43-024":["To *completudo* belong the coordination and the combination of the parts in the whole. The combination between sequels and grounds is *subordinatio*. If I posit a ground, I also posit at the same time all sequels. But in coordination, by my positing one, the remainder is not at the same time also posited. That must all be added. For here they must all be immediate marks and not mediate ones or marks of marks.",["Folgen rendered \"sequels\" (glossary: Folge = sequel); Verbindung = combination; Merkmal = mark; setzen = posit.","\"Gründe\" is as printed (after \"zwischen\"); kept as \"grounds\" without emendation."]],"philippi-s43-025":["The extended distinctness (*extensiue distinctior cognitio*) is called vivacity. When a cognition is, as it were, such as we have it of things that we receive through the senses.",["ausgebreitet rendered \"extended\" (not in glossary); Lebhaftigkeit = \"vivacity\".","The second sentence is a fragment (a dependent clause) in the source and is left as such."]],"philippi-s43-026":["But if the marks stand in a subordinated thing, then the distinctness is called deep distinctness. The greater the series of subordinated marks, the deeper the distinctness. That is *cognitio intensiue distinctior*. The happiness of a country is *extensiue maior* if it has many inhabitants; *intensiue maior*, if without increasing the number of inhabitants the degree of the good state increases in each subject.",["Sache rendered \"thing\" (as Ding); Zahl = number; Grad = degree; Zustand = state.","\"Subject\" is Latin-derived (Subject) in the German; kept as \"subject\"."]],"philippi-s43-027":["The deeper the distinctness is, the further the subordinated marks proceed, the drier a cognition is; the more extended, vivacious it is, the shallower it is. Toussaint in his book *les moeurs* is vivacious and precisely for that reason shallow.",["\"je ausgebreiteter lebhaffter sie ist\" has no comma between the adjectives in the source; kept as a bare sequence.","Toussaint's *Les mœurs* is given in the form printed."]],"philippi-s43-028":["The senses give us the most extended cognition. The understanding gives us a deep cognition. The deep cognition has the advantage of thoroughness.",["Verstand = understanding; Gründlichkeit rendered \"thoroughness\" (not in glossary; cf. Grund)."]],"philippi-s43-029":["If in the series of subordinated marks the distinctness has been carried out up to the highest mark, then it is consummated.",["\"consummirt\" rendered \"consummated\"; cf. *notio consummata* in s44-003."]],"philippi-s43-030":["Does *completudo* permit degrees? In the logical sense no, but certainly in the aesthetic. There cognitions are made supercomplete through contingent marks. Bacon of Verulam has the habit that in his writing he says almost everything twice in order to make a deeper impression.",["\"im logischen Verstände\" is literally \"in logical understanding\"; Verstand here has the sense of \"sense/meaning\", rendered \"sense\" (cf. Verstand = understanding elsewhere).","OCR \"Schlifft\" read as Schrifft (\"writing\"). \"Baco de Verulamio\" kept as printed in translated form."]],"philippi-s44-001":["On the adequation of cognitions.",["\"adaequation\" rendered \"adequation\"; Erkenntnis = cognition."]],"philippi-s44-002":["Wolff calls adequate the concept that has the second degree of intensified distinctness. If I cognize a thing through the first immediate mark, then that is the first degree, e.g. of body: it is composed. If I get new marks from the immediate marks, then that is the 2nd degree, and then the concept is adequate. But that is not right. The *adaequatio* (completeness) requires the highest degree; but the second degree is not yet the highest.",["Vollständigkeit not in glossary; rendered \"completeness\". Sache = \"thing\".","\"intensiuirten\" rendered \"intensified\"."]],"philippi-s44-003":["If I cognize clearly the marks of a thing, then that is the first degree of distinctness; if I cognize clearly the marks of the marks, then that is the second degree. If I cognize the marks of these marks, then that is the third degree of distinctness and so on, until the mark is the thing itself. Thus *notio consummata* is the highest degree of intensity.",["Intensivität rendered \"intensity\"; klar = \"clear(ly)\"; erkennen = cognize."]],"philippi-s44-004":["If in the series of coordinated marks there is a *completudo*, i.e. if a concept is full; then something could still be added that is beyond the *completudo*, namely the sequels flowing from the necessary marks of the thing. To full concepts that contain all necessary marks, there can still be posited contingent marks that flow from those. But these marks are always dispensable. Sequels can always be left out if one has the ground, for if one has this, one also has those.",["OCR \"abf liessende\" and \"f Hessen\" read as abfliessende / fliessen.","ausführlich rendered \"full\" (Ausführlichkeit = \"fullness\"); not in glossary. Folgen = \"sequels\"."]],"philippi-s44-005":["A is a figure that is enclosed in 3 straight lines and has 3 angles; this latter makes the concept overcomplete. What is added to a concept beyond the *completudo* is dispensable and to be left out. In the aesthetic one seeks to give lightness to the concepts and therefore seeks to take together as much as is only fitting.",["OCR \"üi\" read as \"in\". The letter \"A\" as the subject is as printed; possibly garbled in the OCR (cannot be confirmed).","\"im aesthetischen\" is elliptical (no noun); kept so."]],"philippi-s44-006":["Only the separation of all dispensable and tautological marks that already lie in the given ones is called precision. A concept is precise if it is complete and contains nothing superfluous. If nothing is lacking to a concept for the whole concept of the thing, then it is called complete; if it has nothing too much, it is called precise. Then it is commensurate with the thing. A garment that is not too large and also not too small is commensurate, it fits. *Conceptus rem adaequat* it is then called. The *completudo*, fullness, is the perfection of a concept in which nothing is lacking to it; the adequation, commensurateness, that in which it has nothing too much. A concept that is complete and precise at the same time is *conceptus obiecto alio adaequatus*, an adequate concept. The adequation of a concept suffers no degrees. A concept cannot be more equal than equal. But according to Wolff the adequation suffers degrees.",["OCR \"ldein\" read as klein; \"derm\" read as \"denn\" (\"then\"/\"for\"); \"conceptus rem adaequat\" kept as printed (the verb form may be garbled).","angemessen / Angemessenheit rendered \"commensurate / commensurateness\", distinguished from adaequat / Adaequation (\"adequate / adequation\"); Vollkommenheit = perfection.","\"nach dem Wolfen\" is doubtful as printed; rendered \"according to Wolff\". \"Gleicher als gleich\" = \"more equal than equal\"."]],"philippi-s44-007":["That is the greatest perfection of the concepts in the logical if they are adequate. The definitions are distinguished from the descriptions only in this, that the former have precision and the latter do not.",["\"im logischen\" is elliptical (no noun supplied). Definition / Description kept as cognates."]],"philippi-s44-008":["The degrees of distinctness are these:",[]],"philippi-s44-009":["1. that one have clear marks. 2. that they be complete, that there be enough of them. That is the fullness.",["\"seyn\" (subjunctive) rendered \"be\" in both clauses; Ausführlichkeit = \"fullness\"."]],"philippi-s44-010":["3. that there not be too many of them. That is the precision. Then the concept is commensurate with the thing, when it reaches this way of distinctness; then it is adequate. Wolff calls the adequation completeness, but this does not express it well. The adequation indicates a preceding comparison and that the concept has been found therein equal and commensurate. One could call it the commensurateness. That is the greatest logical perfection in comparison with the thing. The author speaks of vivacity and strength. That belongs to sensible representations. The more cognitions are abstract, the more they are general, the less vivacious they are. Through concrete cases they can become vivacious. Thus if a cognition is considered in the general, it has little vivacity; but what is lacking to it of this, thoroughness replaces, that is the logical perfection. The former, that one also bring them in through application to concrete cases. Strength pertains to a cognition insofar as it has a ground of influence on the forces of the human mind; but abstract concepts have the least strength.",["OCR \"smd\" read as sind; \"Lebhaff tigkeit\" read as Lebhafftigkeit. Art = \"way\"; Vorstellung = representation; Gemüth = mind; Kräffte = forces.","\"Jene daß man sie ... anbringe\" is obscure as printed (\"Jene\" unclear in reference); kept literal and unresolved.","\"Vollständigkeit\" = \"completeness\"; Stärke = \"strength\"; \"Autor\" = Meier (not stated in the passage)."]],"philippi-s44-011":["The question is how distinct cognitions arise. Metaphysics investigates the origin of clear cognitions. Logic makes use of them without asking where they come from. But how do distinct concepts arise? That logic must know. All distinctness arises from analysis, says the author. I say it also arises *per synthesin*. All distinctness arises from marks in a twofold way:",["OCR \"entin springt\" read as entspringt. \"Analysis\" kept as in the German; \"Autor\" = \"author\"."]],"philippi-s44-012":["1. in that I search out the marks that lie in the concept through dissection and make them clear.",["Zergliederung rendered \"dissection\"."]],"philippi-s44-013":["2. in that I add new marks that did not lie in the concept *per synthesin*. If I want, e.g., to make a distinct concept of gold, then it is not enough that I dissect my concept, which I obtain through the sight of it, but I must also seek together and represent to myself several marks that did not lie in my concept. Through experiments I will find that gold is ductile, that it does not evaporate in fire, etc. etc., which I would not know on mere sight.",["Erfahrung / Experiment: \"Experimente\" rendered \"experiments\". \"zusammensuchen\" rendered \"seek together\" (literal)."]],"philippi-s44-014":["To make distinct concepts and to make concepts distinct are different actions. (Wolff makes distinct concepts in his philosophy, but he does not make concepts distinct.) To make distinct concepts is *per synthesin*. To make given concepts distinct is often *per analysin*, also *per synthesin*.",["Weltweisheit would be \"philosophy\"; here the German has \"Philosophie\", likewise \"philosophy\". Handlungen = \"actions\"."]],"philippi-s44-015":["Through experience I can make concepts distinct *per synthesin*. Concepts of reason are always made distinct *per analysin*. Empirical concepts *per synthesin*.",["\"Vernunfftbegriffe werden ... deutlich\" rendered \"are made distinct\"; the German has only \"become distinct\" (werden deutlich), which can also be read that way. Vernunft = reason; Erfahrung = experience."]],"philippi-s44-016":["If, then, concepts are given, whether through reason or experience, and they are to be made distinct, this can happen only *per analysin*. I will not make experiments there; I only compare my judgments with the judgments of common reason.",["Begriff = concept; deutlich = distinct; Urtheile = judgments; Vernunfft = reason; Erfahrung = experience.","*per analysin* is Latin in the source (by analysis); left in Latin.","'allgemeinen Vernunfft' rendered 'common reason' following the glossary's gemein; the German is allgemein (literally 'general/universal'), so the choice is a judgment call."]],"philippi-s44-017":["If I am to make distinct concepts for myself, these are my concepts of experience. Now if I wanted, e.g., to have a distinct concept of quicksilver, I would not get it by analyzing my concept of it; rather I must set up trials and look for new marks. There I will find, e.g., that the ? vir. dissolves the metals, hardens in the cold, etc. etc.",["Merkmale = marks (nota); Versuche = trials (not 'Experimente', which occurs in the preceding passage).","'? vir.' is an OCR-garbled reading of an abbreviation or sign (probably the alchemical sign for quicksilver, with 'vir.'); left as it stands, not resolved.","'z. E.' = 'zum Exempel', rendered 'e.g.'."]],"philippi-s44-018":["If, then, I am to make a distinct concept, I must bring forth the concept together with the distinctness, the matter together with the form. But if I make a given concept distinct, I make the form anew, I give it the form of distinctness.",["Begrif = concept; Materie = matter; Form = form; Deutlichkeit = distinctness.","'machen' is rendered 'make' throughout; 'hervorbringen' = 'bring forth'."]],"philippi-s44-019":["Mathematics never makes confused concepts distinct, but brings forth arbitrarily thought-up concepts together with their distinctness. The only concept that is given to it is the concept of space. Yet this it also never explains, and presupposes that everyone already knows it. As Augustine, when he was asked what time is, said: that which I and you well know. All concepts that mathematics makes distinct in its definitions did not dwell in us beforehand confusedly, but it thought them up and thus brought forth the concept together with its distinctness out of itself.",["Raum = space; Zeit = time; erklärt = explains (Erklärung glossed 'explanation; definition'); Definitionen kept as 'definitions'.","'willkürlich erdachte' rendered 'arbitrarily thought-up', literal; 'wohnten ... bey' = 'dwelt in us' (beiwohnen).","'wol' = 'wohl', rendered 'well'."]],"philippi-s44-020":["If we make for ourselves concepts of the understanding that are drawn through no experience from immediate intuition, but from reason itself, then these are made distinct only *per analysin*. But concepts of the understanding are brought forth *per synthesin*. Mathematics thinks up hypotheses for itself. We wanted to lead a line around a point and call the space it encloses a circle. But what it now concludes from this circle, these are no inventions.",["Verstandesbegriffe = concepts of the understanding; Anschauung = intuition; Vernunft = reason.","OCR 'Vernunf ft' silently corrected to 'Vernunft'.","Latin *per analysin*, *per synthesin* kept. 'Zirkel' rendered 'circle'; 'schließt' appears twice (einschließt = encloses; schließt = concludes, from Schluß = conclusion).","'Mathematic' in the source is rendered 'Mathematics'; 'Erdichtungen' = 'inventions'.","The syntax 'die durch keine Erfahrung aus unmittelbarer Anschauung ... gezogen werden' is ambiguous as to whether 'durch keine Erfahrung' qualifies 'aus unmittelbarer Anschauung' or the drawing as a whole; the English keeps the same order."]],"philippi-s44-021":["We want to indicate the gradation of concepts: the first degree is to represent something to oneself. A lesser degree there is not: *Repraesentare*.",["Grad = degree; sich vorstellen = 'represent to oneself' (Vorstellung = representation).","*Repraesentare* is Latin in the source."]],"philippi-s44-022":["The 2nd, to know something, *noße*, to represent to oneself with consciousness.",["wissen = know (Wissen = knowledge); Bewußtseyn = consciousness.","*noße* is as printed; it is probably a corruption of *nosse* (cf. 'Noße' in philippi-s44-023); left as it stands.","Here the Latin gloss *nosse* would normally go with kennen (glossary: noscere), whereas the German here is wissen; the mismatch is in the source."]],"philippi-s44-023":["The 3rd, to be acquainted with something: to be able to distinguish in comparison with others; to know with which other thing it is the same and from which it is distinguished. E.g., if among a multitude of people one is acquainted with one's relative. *Noße*.",["kennen = be acquainted with (glossary: know; be acquainted with); wissen = know; Menge = multitude.","*Noße* as printed (probably *nosse*); left as it stands.","'einerley' rendered 'the same'; 'unterscheiden / unterschieden' = 'distinguish / distinguished'."]],"philippi-s44-024":["The 4th, to understand something, to cognize something distinctly through the understanding. *Intelligere*.",["verstehen = understand (intelligere); Verstand = understanding; erkennen = cognize; deutlich = distinctly."]],"philippi-s44-025":["The 5th, to have insight into something, is to cognize something through reason or through inferences. *Per rationem aliquid cognoscere*. E.g., many can be acquainted with mussels. But to have insight into what the ground of the enameling of the calcareous shell is, and that, since art produces an enameling only through glass-stones in fire, the sea could produce it through calcareous stars; this insight not everyone has. All mysteries must be understood, otherwise they would not concern us, but they cannot be had insight into. Absolute necessity no one can have insight into. Freedom I do indeed understand, but I do not have insight that I act out of myself without something determining me, since after all everything is steered through nature. The sixth degree is to comprehend something, *comprehendere*. To cognize something through reason to the degree that is sufficient for the purpose. When I asked a blind man what he would well wish to see, he said a house, for a human being I can already represent to myself, because I can embrace him, but a house not. Light and color we are acquainted with, but we do not understand it.",["einsehen = 'have insight into' (no glossary entry; used consistently, with 'Einsicht' = 'insight'); verstehen = understand; begreifen = comprehend (comprehendere); kennen = be acquainted with.","Schlüsse = inferences; Grund = ground; Zweck = purpose; zureichend = sufficient; Grad = degree; bestimmt = determining.","'Emaillation' / 'kalkartige Sterne' / 'Glaßsteine' rendered literally; 'Emaillation' (enameling) is the transcript's own word; 'Sterne' may be a mishearing or misreading, left as it stands.","'Freiheit verstehe ich wol aber ich sehe nicht ein, daß ich aus mir selbst handle ohne daß mich was bestimmt' keeps the German's 'ohne daß mich was bestimmt'; 'was' = 'something'.","'Der sechste Grad' follows without a break in the source, so no paragraph division is made; 'ich ... frug' = 'asked'.","'wol' = 'wohl'; 'umfassen' = 'embrace' (also 'encompass'); 'verstehens' = 'verstehen es', rendered 'do not understand it'."]],"philippi-s44-026":["5) If I have insight through reason, I subordinate the marks.",["einsehe = 'have insight'; Merkmale = marks (nota); subordinire = subordinate."]],"philippi-s44-027":["E.g., gunpowder consists of saltpeter, sulfur and charcoal.",["'Kohlen' rendered 'charcoal' (literally 'coals')."]],"philippi-s44-028":["Whether there are many things we do not understand, through understanding? Yes, especially in *Theol. nat.* E.g., the eternity of God, the beginning of the world. There we can make for ourselves no concept through the understanding. We proceed only along the thread of analogy. The concept of freedom, etc.",["'Theol. nat.' (natural theology) left abbreviated as in the source and italicized as Latin.","'durch Verstand ?' has an OCR question mark that may be a mark of the transcript rather than of Kant; left as it stands.","Verstand = understanding; Welt = world; Begriff = concept; Freiheit = freedom."]],"philippi-s44-029":["If one looks too much at the partial representations and heaps them up, the understanding becomes confused through the multitude. Hence the many distinctions contribute more to confusion than to distinctness.",["Theilvorstellungen = partial representations (Teil = part; Vorstellung = representation); Menge = multitude; Deutlichkeit = distinctness; Verwirrung = confusion.","'Distinctionen' rendered 'distinctions'."]],"philippi-s44-030":["Concepts are relatively sufficient either",["Fragment: breaks off with 'entweder', continued in the next two passages.","relativisch = relatively; zureichend = sufficient."]],"philippi-s44-031":["1. in some aim or",["Absicht = aim (glossary: aim; intention); 'einiger Absicht' could also be read 'in some respect', but the glossary term is used."]],"philippi-s44-032":["2. in every aim.",["Absicht = aim; 'aller' = 'every' (or 'all')."]],"philippi-s44-033":["The author calls this highest degree *concipere*, but that indicates only the lowest degree.",["*concipere* is Latin in the source; Grad = degree.","'Der Autor' = Meier (or Baumgarten) in the lectures' textbook; not identified in the passage."]],"philippi-s44-034":["It comes from habit and practice that a head is inclined to clarity and distinctness. Mathematics contributes much to it.",["Klarheit = clarity; Deutlichkeit = distinctness; 'Kopf' rendered literally as 'head'; Mathematic = mathematics."]],"philippi-s45-001":["On Certainty.",["Gewißheit = certainty."]],"philippi-s45-002":["The author speaks of learned cognition, but we want to speak of the certainty of cognition.",["Erkenntniß = cognition; gelehrt = learned; Gewißheit = certainty."]],"philippi-s45-003":["Only in judgments is a distinction made between what is true and what is certain. Truth is what goes to the object. Certainty is in the subject.",["Urtheile = judgments; Wahrheit = truth; Objekt = object; Subjekt = subject.","'aufs Objekt geht' = 'goes to the object', literal (could also be 'is directed at')."]],"philippi-s45-004":["There can be certain grounds of truth that convince us to take our cognition to be true. But if these grounds do not remove all doubts and scruples that could occur to us, then our cognition indeed remains true, but it is not certain. For certainty it is required that complete grounds be there to convince us entirely. Certainty is thus in the subject; truth in the object. Both are different only in that for truth it is enough if grounds for it are there; but for certainty it is required that these grounds be capable of effecting conviction in a subject.",["Gründe = grounds; überführen / Ueberzeugung = convince / conviction (Überzeugung); für wahr halten = take to be true (Fürwahrhalten).","OCR 'Zweif el' and 'WTahrheit' silently corrected.","'fähig' = capable (Fähigkeit = capacity).","'gewisse Gründe' can mean 'certain' (sure) or 'certain (some)' grounds; the English 'certain grounds' keeps both readings.","'Bedenklichkeiten' = 'scruples' (literally 'misgivings')."]],"philippi-s45-005":["If my cognition is to be true, it must agree with the object. If I cognize something through reason and it agrees with the senses, then it is certain. Subjective certainty arises from the observation that it agrees with my and others' cognitions. One is sensibly certain of many general propositions",["stimmen = agree; Sinnen = senses (Sinn = sense); Beobachtung = observation; Sätze = propositions; sinnlich = sensibly (sensible).","OCR 'Erkenntmssen' silently corrected to 'Erkenntnissen'.","Fragment: breaks off without a period; the numbered point '1.' follows in the next passage.","'meinen und anderen ihren Erkenntnissen' is colloquial German; rendered 'my and others' cognitions'."]],"philippi-s45-006":["1. The greatest certainty or conviction is in mathematics. This is merely a certainty of form. *In abstracto*, however, it is greater than *in concreto*. In all application of mathematical certainty there is still a great sensibility.",["Form = form; Sinnlichkeit = sensibility; Ueberzeugung = conviction.","*In abstracto*, *in concreto* are Latin in the source.","OCR 'derMathematick' silently corrected."]],"philippi-s45-007":["It is true that there are inhabitants in the moon, i.e., we have grounds to assert this, but it is not certain, that is, the grounds are not yet capable of convincing every subject. Difficulties can always be raised.",["behaupten = assert; Gründe = grounds; fähig = capable; 'd. i.' = 'i.e.'.","'im Monde' rendered 'in the moon'."]],"philippi-s45-008":["So human beings often take something to be true, even though it is not certain. If in a cognition there is not everything that confirms it; then I am not certain. E.g., No substance can be passive or suffer without being at the same time in activity. No one can show that in a sensible example. It lies in the concepts of suffering.",["für wahr halten = take to be true; Erkenntniß = cognition; Substanz = substance; sinnlich = sensible; Begriffe = concepts.","OCR 'Hegt' silently corrected to 'liegt'.","'ich bin nicht gewiß' = 'I am not certain'; the German is as elliptical as the English."]],"philippi-s45-009":["We must not take everything to be evident. Human beings are inclined to this, especially in such propositions as have a great moral importance. E.g., a proof of the existence of God seems to us evident even though it is not. Much more belongs to evidence. Although in the certainty of moral truths there is much that is similar to the mathematical, there is nevertheless a difference.",["evident = evident (Evidenz = evidence); Beweis = proof; Daseyn = existence (Dasein; glossary: existence); Sätze = propositions.","'der mathematischen' elides 'Gewißheit'; rendered 'the mathematical', as in the German."]],"philippi-s45-010":["Certainty and truth are different with regard to subjective taking-to-be-true.",["Vorwahrhalten = taking-to-be-true (cf. Fürwahrhalten); hyphenated to render the noun.","'in Ansehung' = 'with regard to'."]],"philippi-s45-011":["If there is a proof for a proposition; then it makes certain, if it is sufficient. If there are many, it is to be presumed that singly none of them makes certain. If this is so, then taken together they will also not effect a greater certainty. For certainty cannot be increased; for it is the *maximum* of taking-to-be-true.",["Beweis = proof; zureichend = sufficient; Satz = proposition; Gewißheit = certainty; Vorwahrhalten = taking-to-be-true.","*maximum* is Latin in the source.","'gewiß macht' = 'makes certain', with the adjective unmarked as to whether it is the proof or the proposition that is made certain; kept as in German.","'einzeln' = 'singly'."]],"philippi-s45-012":["Certainty is a feeling of the human soul.",["Gefühl = feeling; Seele = soul (not in the glossary; Gemüt = mind is a different word)."]],"philippi-s45-013":["Degrees of taking to be true that are distinguished through the thing are 1. to know something 2. to believe 3. to opine. Conjecture is grounded on the diversity of space and time, to which our cognition can be subjected. Very much belongs to certainty. Practical truths, even if we cannot declare them certain, can be sufficient to move us to act accordingly. Socrates believed in a future life and died on it. He himself says he believes a future life, not that he knows it.",["Vorwahrhalten (the usual Fürwahrhalten) = 'taking to be true'; wissen/glauben/meynen = know/believe/opine; Erkenntniß = cognition; Gewißheit = certainty.","'durch die Sache' is kept as 'through the thing'; the German does not say what Sache refers to.","'hinreichend' rendered 'sufficient' (as zureichend in the glossary)."]],"philippi-s45-014":["There is a belief that is decisive, so that one does not ask after the opposite; and does not hold it worthy to investigate it. Such a belief effects complete but NB practical certainty. Knowledge is theoretical certainty.",["'Das Wissen' = knowledge (Wissen), though the sense of the verb 'to know' is also present.","'NB' left as in the source.","'es' in 'es ... zu untersuchen' is left without a noun, as in the German."]],"philippi-s45-015":["In the middle between knowledge and belief stands opining. It is an incomplete theoretical taking to be true, insofar as I am conscious that it is insufficient. If even only the smallest degree of taking to be true is still lacking, it is already mere opinion. Belief is contradistinguished from certainty, not from the cognition of reason. For then in all irrational or historical cognitions only belief would take place. But there is certainty in these. It is, e.g., certain that Madrid is the capital in Spain. In belief there is nevertheless always a kind of uncertainty. If human beings knew that the rewards for their good conduct were quite certain, their morality would not be so pure. Interest would perhaps then move them to virtue alone, not feeling of duty.",["OCR 'Demi' corrected to 'Denn'; 'wüsten' read as 'wüßten'.","'contradistinguirt' rendered 'contradistinguished'; 'Wolverhalten' = literally 'well-behaving', rendered 'good conduct'.","'Erkenntniß der Vernunfft' = 'cognition of reason'; 'Art' = 'kind'.","'Interesse ... denn ... bewogen' is syntactically loose in the source; kept as it stands."]],"philippi-s45-016":["Between belief and knowledge the difference is not *completudinis*. Belief has practical, knowledge theoretical *completudinem*.",["OCR 'theoi'etische' corrected to 'theoretische'.","The Latin is left in the cases the German text gives (genitive, accusative)."]],"philippi-s45-017":["Belief is, as is clear from all this, a complete practical certainty, though not theoretical. Opinion is compared with knowledge.",["OCR 'Me^ynung' read as 'Meynung'.","'obwol nicht theoretische' has no noun; 'certainty' is understood only as it is in the German."]],"philippi-s45-018":["It is special that some logicians have brought into logic an argument *a tuto* or *ab utili*, where one holds a thing to be true on account of the great utility one has from it and on account of the harm that would arise from it if one did not hold it to be true. Such arguments are detrimental to philosophy, they annul the authority of reason.",["'besonders' rendered 'special' (glossary: particular; special).","'Philosophie' = philosophy (the Latinate word, not Weltweisheit).","'Ansehen' = 'authority'; it could also be 'standing' or 'esteem'."]],"philippi-s45-019":["A cognition is undeniable if there are no grounds for the contrary opinion; if it is impossible to contradict it. If a cognition is so convincing that the other merely does not want to admit that he is convinced, that is denying. Whoever denies something must have grounds of illusion, otherwise he is ashamed.",["'Scheingründe' = 'grounds of illusion' (Schein = illusion).","'überzeugend/überzeugt' = 'convincing/convinced' (Überzeugung = conviction).","'Gegenmeynung' = 'contrary opinion'."]],"philippi-s45-020":["If I want to convince someone of a proposition, then I must not recite my proofs to him with dictatorial expressions and arrogated superiority; his self-love is thereby outraged, he does not want to subject himself to my pride; I do not reach my end. I must merely present the grounds to him in a friendly way. If he raises an objection, then I must regard his objections from the side where he regards them, I must approve that they are correct under the conditions that he assumes; but then I must show him that the conditions are otherwise. Such a procedure, in which one helps to explain the opinion of the other, is advantageous for the investigation of truth.",["OCR 'Selbsthebe' read as 'Selbstliebe'; 'vortheillrafft' as 'vortheilhafft'.","'Meinung' = opinion; 'erklären' = explain; 'Zweck' = end; 'Beweis' = proof.","'Einwurf' = objection; 'ansehen' rendered 'regard'; 'billigen' = 'approve'."]],"philippi-s45-021":["My understanding does not want to subject itself to any understanding, it wants only to ground itself. Otherwise in such an action everything in the soul is resisting.",["'gründen' (reflexive) = 'ground itself'; 'Verstände' is a dative singular.","'widerstrebend' = 'resisting', left without an object as in the source."]],"philippi-s45-022":["A cognition is settled if it is so constituted that, since it has been contested, it is completely decided and freed from all further dispute. Circulation of the blood, the Copernican system are settled propositions. Recently Monatessa, a doctor in Padua who was the last assailant of the Copernican doctrinal edifice, has died. One must not be hasty in this. Sometimes something is settled in one age that was not in another. There are many things that have never been settled and yet are taken to be true. Many propositions of religion are of this kind. In mathematics nothing is settled: for nothing is contested.",["'ausgemacht' = 'settled'; 'bestritten' = 'contested'.","'da sie ist bestritten worden' kept as 'since it has been contested'; 'da' could also be 'as' or 'when'.","OCR 'fernem' read as 'fernerem'; the name 'Monatessa' is kept as printed (reading uncertain).","'Lehrgebäude' = 'doctrinal edifice'; 'für wahr gehalten' = 'taken to be true'."]],"philippi-s45-023":["A certain cognition is called thorough, insofar as all grounds that can effect certainty are distinctly cognized. Thoroughness is opposed to shallowness; where everything floats on top and is not deep, where everything is only superficial. Democritus says truth is a deep well.",["OCR 'ohen' read as 'oben'.","'gründlich/Gründlichkeit' = 'thorough/thoroughness' (cf. Grund = ground); 'deutlich' = 'distinctly'.","'Seichtigkeit' = 'shallowness'; 'superficial' is printed in Latin form in the German."]],"philippi-s45-024":["A cognition is deep when the marks in their subordination go very far. Broad, when the series of coordinated marks goes far.",["OCR 'm ihrer' read as 'in ihrer'; 'Ausgehreitet' as 'Ausgebreitet'.","'Merkmal' = 'mark'; 'Ausgebreitet' rendered 'broad' (not 'extension', which is Ausdehnung)."]],"philippi-s46-001":["On assent.",["'Beyfall' = 'assent'."]],"philippi-s46-002":["Can one well say this: to give one's assent? Does it rest on our liking whether we want to give it or not? Is assent therefore an *actus arbitrii*? If it is not, but if it is a state of taking to be true, to which I am compelled through grounds; without my will counting for anything in it; why then does one use the expressions. In the power of the grounds lies the might to wring assent *obtorto collo*. Assent is wrested, not given.",["OCR 'gehen' read as 'geben' and 'Yorwahrhaltens' as 'Vorwahrhaltens'.","'Belieben' = 'liking'; 'Gewalt' = 'power' and 'Macht' = 'might'; 'abdringen/entreißen' = 'wring/wrest'.","'warum braucht man denn die Ausdrücke' ends the sentence without a question mark in the source; kept."]],"philippi-s46-003":["Assent is a positive cognition. Does freedom have an influence on assent? Not immediately. Assent must be brought about by grounds that are suitable according to rules of cognition and according to the will. Mediately freedom has an influence on assent; in that we do not want to investigate the grounds. Hence: to withhold assent means as much as not to judge at all. Inclination and habit have indirectly an influence on assent. Imitation too in this way. The inclination to imitate is greatest in childhood, in that a child is incapable of using its understanding. The perfection of education also rests on this, that it early dissuade from imitating.",["'nach Regeln der Erkenntniß und dem Willen nach gemäß sind' is obscure; the repeated 'nach' and the construction of 'gemäß' are kept, so it is unclear whether 'gemäß' governs both.","'Mittelbar/Unmittelbar' = 'mediately/immediately'; 'Zurückhalten' = 'withhold'.","'abbringe' is subjunctive and has no object in the German ('dissuade from' keeps that); 'Vollkommenheit' = 'perfection'."]],"philippi-s46-004":["If by assent the declaration of assent is understood: then it can be given and taken back. Here, however, the discussion is not of the statement but of the state of the mind, whether it is moved by the grounds to agree or not.",["'Declaration' = Declaration (Erklärung in the glossary is 'declaration'); 'Aussage' = 'statement'.","'Zustand' = 'state'; 'Gemüth' = 'mind'."]],"philippi-s46-005":["There are certain grounds with regard to which we find ourselves necessitated to assent. But with many we can, on the contrary, put off our assent; at the least the withholding of assent is there under our will.",["'beyfallen' (verb) = 'to assent'; 'genöthigt' = 'necessitated'.","'unter unserm Willen' kept literally ('under our will'); the sentence's last clause is awkward in the German."]],"philippi-s46-006":["For the investigation through grounds depends on our will. Assent cannot be obtained otherwise than through insight into the grounds. A professor in Padua who held stiffly to Aristotle did not want to give assent to the new inventions of the moons of Saturn. He did not want to look through the telescope, when one wanted to convict him of it.",["OCR 'rncht' read as 'nicht', 'Avollte' as 'wollte', 'Bejdall' as 'Beyfall'.","'Denn' could be 'for' or an OCR/transcription slip for 'Dann' ('then'); 'For' kept.","'Erfindungen' = 'inventions'; 'überführen' = 'convict' (also 'convince'); 'dessen' refers back to the telescope-sight or the discoveries, ambiguous as in the German."]],"philippi-s46-007":["Assent cannot be demanded except where there is mathematical evidence or evidence of experience. The foremost character of the philosopher is this, that he knows how to withhold his assent. Whoever is quickly convinced, his assent does not last long, and he often finds cause to sing a palinode. Let one rather remain somewhat longer undecided than that one go a false way, or do futile labor.",["'Evidenz' = 'evidence'; 'Erfahrung' = 'experience'.","'Man bleibe' is subjunctive (hortative); rendered 'Let one ...'.","'Palinodie' = 'palinode'."]],"philippi-s47-001":["On prejudice.",["'Vorurtheil' = 'prejudice'."]],"philippi-s47-002":["Introduction. To reflect means to compare something with the laws of the understanding and of reason. If the understanding does not compare its judgments with its laws; then it accepts them without reflection.",["'Ueberlegen/Ueberlegung' = 'reflect/reflection'.","'Gesetz' = 'law'; 'Urtheil' = 'judgment'."]],"philippi-s47-003":["To investigate means to carry out the reflection mediately or by means of other instruments of truth. Thus not everything that is reflected on is investigated.",["'Untersuchen' = 'investigate' (also used for 'Untersuchung' elsewhere in this group); 'Instrumente' = 'instruments'.","'überlegt wird' = 'is reflected on' (Überlegung)."]],"philippi-s47-004":["Not all cognitions need investigation, but all need reflection. The first principles of reason may not be compared mediately with the laws of the understanding, but certainly immediately, i.e., one may not investigate them, but one must reflect on them.",["'dürfen nicht' is ambiguous (older 'need not' or modern 'must not'); 'may not' is kept for the ambiguity, though the contrast with 'muß' suggests the first.","'Grundsätze' = 'principles'; 'Verstandesgesetzen' = 'laws of the understanding'.","'d. i.' = 'i.e.'."]],"philippi-s47-005":["Without reflection we speak empty words. A hastiness in judgment is lack of investigation. The lack of reflection in judgment is prejudice. But prejudice properly signifies not so much a judgment that occurs without reflection; but it signifies a rule, a certain *canon* for judging without reflection. It is not enough that in prejudice we neglect to compare our judgment with the laws of reason; we compare it moreover with another accepted law, e.g. with habit, inclination. Mere hastiness, where one does not reflect, does not yet make up a prejudice. A universal law which is a ground of judgments without reflection is a prejudice. Principles are opposed to prejudices. Just as those are *principia* for judging without reflection, so these are *principia* for judging with reflection. Not individual judgments are opposed to prejudices. If a father does not believe that his son is so godless at school, that is just as little a prejudice as when, in reading a title page, I judge of the book itself.",["Ueberlegung = reflection; Urtheil = judgment; Vorurtheil = prejudice; Gesetz = law; Grundsatz = principle (*principia* is Kant's Latin); Vernunft = reason; Grund = ground.","Uebereilen / Uebereilung = hastiness (not in glossary); Untersuchung = investigation (not in glossary).","The print has 'GeAvohnheit', 'seni', 'Vorurt heilen' and a space before semicolons; silently corrected to Gewohnheit, sein, Vorurtheilen.","'Nicht einzelne Urtheile werden den Vorurtheilen entgegengesetzt' keeps the German word order; 'Not individual judgments' could also be read as 'Individual judgments are not'."]],"philippi-s47-006":["The form after which the concepts, when they are combined without reflection, model themselves are",["Verbunden = combined (Verbindung = combination). The sentence is a lead-in to the numbered items that follow; 'sind' is plural after the singular 'Form', as in the German."]],"philippi-s47-007":["1. Imitation",[]],"philippi-s47-008":["2. Habit",["Gewohnheit = habit (not in glossary); compare *habitus* in philippi-s47-010."]],"philippi-s47-009":["3. Inclination. Those are the 3 sources of prejudices.",["Neigung = inclination (not in glossary)."]],"philippi-s47-010":["Our judgments we must always compare with something. If we do not compare it with the understanding; so we compare it with the universal fashion, or with the *habitus* of judging in a certain way; or with fame. As often as we judge in this latter way; so we judge without reflection. A prejudice is always there when we do not compare our judgment with the understanding but with other principles that can guide the understanding.",["'Urtheile ... es' (plural, then singular pronoun) is kept as in the German.","Verstand = understanding; allgemein = universal; Art = way; unüberlegt = without reflection."]],"philippi-s47-011":["An *analogon* of prejudice is the preliminary judgment. That is a judgment which precedes the investigation with the consciousness that an investigation is necessary. Many investigations one cannot undertake right away; but I may not on that account be without all judging. Before the investigation I will nevertheless find some grounds that are sufficient if not for deciding then for judging. As much as these offer me I can always judge. E.g. I read the fair catalogue; so I must, if I want to resolve to read a book, judge something of it preliminarily. Preliminary judgments are very necessary, and he shows a special strength of genius who can infer much from few *data*. Before something is taken up as an object of investigation, everyone must judge something preliminarily. This is also very usual for human beings. From the fewest *data*, e.g. from the mere name, they already infer something.",["vorläufig = preliminary (not in glossary); Meßkatalogus = the catalogue of the book fair; Datis = *data* (Latin ablative/dative plural, kept in Latin).","Schlüssen (as verb, 'schließen') = infer; Schluß in the glossary = inference.","hinreichend = sufficient (glossary gives zureichend = sufficient; the same English word is used for both); Gegenstand = object.","OCR slips silently corrected: 'wild' = wird; 'Schlüssen' in the last sentence kept as the verb form as printed."]],"philippi-s47-012":["One should take pains, in writings and societies, to make the preliminary judgment favorable to us. For this preliminary judgment is the *substratum* of everything that the understanding judges in the sequel. It always refers itself to it even if, with an investigation undertaken, it sees its incorrectness. For the most part the preliminary judgment serves for deciding. So unwillingly does the understanding like to alter the fold that it once adopts! It does not like to have judged in vain and to rework everything, especially when habit is added. All inventions in the world have their ground in the preliminary judgments. The understanding must already beforehand infer something and fix the rule according to which it wants to undertake the investigation, so that it does not try at random. With regard to many things we cannot arrive at investigation, there we can nevertheless prepare ourselves for it with preliminary judgments. The preliminary judgment is a true judgment only it is incomplete. As far as the few *data* reach, so far it is correct. If I see a man going about with splendor and servants; if in the look of another I notice honest features of the face; so I judge that the former is rich, the latter good-natured. Suppose the former is more in debt than he will pay; suppose the latter is a hypocrite: still I have judged correctly from the few *data* that have fallen to my share. In the preliminary judgment I decide nothing; I say only that from what I know this or that sequel flows; I may have guessed it or not. It is not false; only it is incomplete and expresses the relation of judgments to insufficient grounds. But if one holds a preliminary judgment to be decisive; so that is prejudice and hastiness.",["OCR corrected: 'Gesellschat 't t en' = Gesellschaften; 'Fol^e' = Folge; 'Anbbck' = Anblick; 'redHche' = redliche; 'Urt heilen' = Urtheilen.","'Falte' (fold) is read as printed; the OCR may conceal 'Falle' (case), so the reading is doubtful.","Folge = sequel (glossary); 'in der Folge' rendered literally as 'in the sequel'. 'ihre Unrichtigkeit' is ambiguous in the German as to what 'its' (or 'their') refers to and is left so.","'Sachen' = things (not Dinge); Gerathewol = at random; unzulänglich = insufficient (not in glossary); incomplet = incomplete; 'Schlüssen' = infer (verb)."]],"philippi-s47-013":["Prejudices are something quite different. The harmful and blameworthy in them does not consist in this, that we err; we can often hit the truth, but in this, that we adopt a false *modum cognoscendi* and abolish the true one. It does not depend so much on what one judges, but on how one judges. Some believe there are useful prejudices. But those are the ones that impose a blind obedience on others and want thereby to abolish all cognition. It depends on according to which method one has judged.",["aufheben = abolish (not in glossary); Erkenntniß = cognition; Methode = method.","OCR corrected: 'blmden' = blinden. 'darinn. daß' has a stray period, read as a comma. The final sentence is kept literally and is awkward in the German as well."]],"philippi-s47-014":["The judgment is distinguished from prejudice not in matter but only in form. Even truths one must reject if they come from prejudice. For what would become of it if the method were ruling and the only one? Logic is a means against it. Some laugh at the prejudice of others, while they themselves are sick with another.",["Materie = matter; Form = form; Mittel = means. 'dawider ein Mittel' = 'a means against it'; 'it' in 'what would become of it' (daraus) is left unspecified as in the German."]],"philippi-s48-001":["Application.",["Anwendung = application (not in glossary)."]],"philippi-s48-002":["There are prejudices of the understanding",["No final stop in the print; the fragment appears to break off."]],"philippi-s48-003":["There are prejudices of taste.",["Geschmack = taste (not in glossary)."]],"philippi-s48-004":["The most powerful prejudice of taste is imitation. That goes so far that the most acute philosopher cannot spy out the boundary. Often whole peoples praise a usage or something else as the true beauty, which makes the century ridiculous and fantastic. There it then shows in the end that one has compared one's judgment not with taste but with habit.",["Grenze = boundary; Gebrauch = usage (glossary gives 'use'; here the sense is a custom, so 'usage' was chosen to mark it). 'der' (masc. relative) may refer to 'Gebrauch' or to 'etwas'; left as 'which'.","OCR 'ausspühren' read as ausspähen."]],"philippi-s48-005":["Prejudices of taste are the grounds for approving something not from comparison with our own, but a foreign taste. Prejudice of the understanding is something that the understanding, unmindful of its laws, adopts for its rule.",["OCR corrected: 'Vorgleiehung' = Vergleichung; 'unserni' = unserm. The elliptical 'unserm, sondern einem fremden Geschmack' is kept elliptical in English."]],"philippi-s48-006":["We have favorable and unfavorable prejudices. All have one stem. We note",["'ungüstige' read as ungünstige. The sentence breaks off before the numbered item that follows."]],"philippi-s48-007":["1. logical egoism. A psychological egoist is he who asserts that he alone exists. In psychology one investigates how this opinion, which is an important problem for philosophers, could have arisen. A moral egoist is he who believes himself to be there only for himself and believes he can make everything to his advantage.",["Egoisterey = egoism (a variant of Egoismus, which appears in philippi-s48-011); both are rendered 'egoism'. Egoist = egoist.","Problem = problem (not Aufgabe); Meinung = opinion; behaupten = assert; Dasein/existiren = existence/exist.","OCR corrected: 'sejm' = seyn."]],"philippi-s48-008":["The cause of logical egoism is",["Ursache = cause (not in glossary). The sentence leads into the items a. and b."]],"philippi-s48-009":["a. Self-love, where I hold myself alone in high regard as insightful and despise others; where I hold my cognition to be perfect, from *Philautie*.",["Eigenliebe = self-love; einsichtsvoll = insightful; vollkommen = perfect (cf. Vollkommenheit = perfection); *Philautie* kept as in the source."]],"philippi-s48-010":["b. Self-conceit, where I will not give a hearing to the opinions of others.",["Eigendünkel = self-conceit (not in glossary)."]],"philippi-s48-011":["Logical egoism consists in this, that I accept all judgments only in comparison with my own, insofar as they agree with it: and am unconcerned and indifferent about the agreement with the understanding of others. If something is incomprehensible to others, he believes it comes from this, that they do not have insight into it. This logical egoism, a source of many important sequels, fails in this, that it rejects the proper subjective mark of truth, which consists in the agreement of cognition with the universal understanding, and holds agreement with a single reason to be a sufficient mark of truth.",["OCR corrected: 'I )er' = Der; 'Qvelle' = Quelle. 'einzeln' read as einzelnen.","unbegreiflich = incomprehensible (begreifen = comprehend); einsehen = have insight into; Merkmal = mark; Folgen = sequels (Folge = sequel in the glossary); hinlänglich = sufficient (same English as hinreichend, zureichend).","'fehlt darin' is rendered literally as 'fails in this'; the German could also be taken as 'errs in this'. Wahrheit = truth."]],"philippi-s48-012":["The egoist transforms all cognition, even the propositions of reason themselves, into illusion (*apparentia*), in that he gives them a private validity.",["Schein = illusion; Vernunftsätze = propositions of reason (Satz = proposition); Gültigkeit = validity (gültig in the glossary). *apparentia* is Kant's Latin."]],"philippi-s48-013":["Sensation and feeling have a private validity; for there I do not cognize the things themselves, but only insofar as my passive state is modified. But cognition of the understanding is supposed to be universally valid. For this cognizes the things as they are. Thus one must also compare it not only with my reason but with the universal one. Even now the human being feels in himself an inclination to compare his judgments with those of others and thereby to verify them. Vanity is not the ground of our understanding's being so communicative, but an instinct. One sees it in common people. E.g. when someone sees an aurora borealis in the sky, he calls his neighbor. A scholar in a lonely desert, or among the wild people of the Negroes, would give up all speculation. Because he would not be certain that his judgments are not illusion. But my drive to communicate my insights to others would be very unreasonable and useless, if I did not also have receptivity to listen to the judgments of the other.",["Erkenntniß/erkennen = cognition/cognize; Verstandeserkenntniß = 'cognition of the understanding'; Schein = illusion; Urtheil = judgment; Vernunfft = reason; Gültigkeit = validity.","'Instmct' read as 'Instinct' and 'Receptiuitat' as 'Receptivität' (OCR); 'Nordlicht' rendered literally as 'aurora borealis' (lit. 'northern light'). 'Negern' rendered as 'Negroes' to keep the period term.","'wie sie sind' ('as they are') kept; 'mein leidender Zustand' = 'my passive state' (leidend = suffering/passive)."]],"philippi-s48-014":["When one compares one's insights with those of others and decides the truth from the relation of agreement with the reason of others, that is logical pluralism.",["'Pluralism' = 'Pluralism' as in the source; 'Verhältniß' = relation."]],"philippi-s48-015":["One finds that even with observations of the senses one already consults others and, if there are many of them, trusts others more than oneself.",["'Bemerkungen der Sinne' rendered 'observations of the senses'; 'zu Rathe zieht' = 'consults'."]],"philippi-s48-016":["When a foreign judgment contradicts us, that is already very important.",["'fremdes Urtheil' = 'foreign judgment' (i.e. another's)."]],"philippi-s48-017":["In refutations one has to see to it that one makes foreign reason agree with one's own, not that one shows the errors.",["OCR 'Verlas nunft' corrected to 'Vernunft'. 'stimmend mache' kept literal."]],"philippi-s48-018":["Love of honor is a derived drive, not a basic one.",["'Ehrliebe' = 'love of honor'; 'Trieb' = 'drive'."]],"philippi-s48-019":["The authority that a multitude has; the degree of approval that it always finds, the embarrassment in which everyone sees himself when he is to step before a multitude to speak, although one knows that it does not have the insight that I have, confirm that the participatory inclination of the understanding is an instinct. Multitude inspires respect. For the ignorant it serves as proof of their opinions and for the calming of their actions. One gladly joins the largest crowd.",["Menge = multitude (here, as in the glossary); Beweis = proof; Meynung = opinion.","'Theilnehmende Neigung' rendered 'participatory inclination' (lit. 'participating/sharing')."]],"philippi-s48-020":["2. Imitation. This springs",["Breaks off; continues in the next passages."]],"philippi-s48-021":["1. from reverence toward the multitude.",[]],"philippi-s48-022":["2. from affection toward persons.",[]],"philippi-s48-023":["The former is known through experience even in the moral. One most gladly adopts the opinion that many have. One always seeks, whether in cases of fortune or misfortune, to see others also involved and not to see oneself isolated. The multitude of citations from the ancients, as with Morhof, is a result of this prejudice.",["OCR 'sej^' read as 'sey'; 'Morhoff' = Morhof (spelling kept in source form). 'im moralischen' = 'in the moral' (substantive)."]],"philippi-s48-024":["The authority of a person often moves us to adopt his propositions as if one had only to learn them. Whoever cannot elevate himself seeks to elevate a man above all others; thereby he believes he attains that all others submit to the authority of this praised sage and thereby become equal to him. If one can do nothing but copy, one chooses a model that one recommends to all. This often springs not from particular esteem, but from envy toward others to whom one otherwise could not come equal.",["Satz = proposition; 'kami' read as 'kann' (OCR). 'Muster' = model."]],"philippi-s48-025":["a. Antiquity. A common *principium* lies at the basis here, since one believes that everything ages and vanishes through length of time. In this prejudice a true judgment lies at the basis. Individual subjects and pieces of nature age through duration; the race, however, never ages. In the preservation of the species there lies an inexplicable phenomenon, that the power of generation does not decrease and all other forces also not.",["OCR 'Kin.ele' read as 'Einzele' (einzelne); 'Principium' kept in Latin; Arten = species; Kraft/Kräffte rendered 'power' (Erzeugungskrafft) and 'forces' as in the glossary; 'Geschlecht' = 'race' (could also be 'sex/generation')."]],"philippi-s48-026":["Under antiquity human beings represent to themselves honesty and uprightness. Among the ancient Greeks there reigned freedom and simplicity; the nourishers of taste.",["OCR 'Frejmeit' read as 'Freymüthigkeit'? Uncertain; rendered 'freedom' (perhaps 'Freiheit'). 'Ernährer des Geschmaks' kept literal; the clause is fragmentary."]],"philippi-s48-027":["The children who are born now are endowed not only with just the same bodily powers, but also capacities of the understanding as 3000 years ago.",["Verstandesfähigkeiten = capacities of the understanding; 'Leibeskräfften' = bodily powers."]],"philippi-s48-028":["Further, one can say that one does not esteem the ancients so according to a prejudice but that they really deserve to be esteemed so. The cause of this is this: time sifts all products of the understanding and of taste. Bad products one does not heed and these therefore perish; but those that on account of their particular excellences deemed themselves worthy of preservation, those could remain. In this way the beautiful residue of the best models has been handed down. One must therefore not attribute such masterpieces to the whole of antiquity. Just so, if the latest posterity will read the writings of a Newton and a Hume, and all other bad writings will have gone lost; it would conclude wrongly that in our time there were nothing but such great scholars.",["OCR 'übergebheben' read as 'übergeblieben'; 'Schlüssen' read as 'schliessen' (infinitive after 'würde'). 'verlornen' kept as 'lost'."]],"philippi-s48-029":["The prejudice for antiquity springs",["Breaks off; continues in the next passages."]],"philippi-s48-030":["1.) from the opinion that all writings have been as excellent as those that have come down to our times. Since these are nevertheless only the sifted ones of the best and a swarm of bad ones has gone lost.",["Meynung = opinion; the second sentence is a fragment in German, kept so."]],"philippi-s48-031":["2.) Because the ancients had no predecessors and therefore the way to learnedness became very difficult for them. For just the same reason one esteems science in women highly.",["'hätten' (subjunctive) rendered 'had'; Gelehrsamkeit = learnedness; Wißenschaft = science; 'Frauenzimmer' = women."]],"philippi-s48-032":["3.) From the gratitude that we owe to the ancients as our teachers.",[]],"philippi-s48-033":["4.) Because learnedness is required to be able to read and understand them.",[]],"philippi-s48-034":["5.) From the prejudice that everything ages and becomes worse.",["'Veralte' read as 'veralte(t)'; OCR 'Vorurteil' as in source."]],"philippi-s48-035":["One has cause to pass a favorable judgment for the old models of the arts. It is peculiar that we know no poets, no orators who could contest precedence with Virgil, Homer, Cicero, Demosthenes; sculpture and all other things of taste are models there.",["'em' read as 'ein' (OCR). 'besonders' rendered 'peculiar'; could also be 'particular'."]],"philippi-s48-036":["1. One cause can be that among the ancients there was no alternation of fashions.",[]],"philippi-s48-037":["To all things of taste nothing is more harmful than fashions. While we find a pleasure in aping, we fall at last upon the strangest and most adventurous and then hold it for wonderfully beautiful.",["'Moden' = fashions."]],"philippi-s48-038":["The ancients, who had no models before them, also brought no artificial manner into their works. Their taste was pure and wholly nature. Yet we must always form ourselves after models; if we do not do so, we are censured.",["OCR 'Allem' read as 'Allein'; 'lauter' rendered 'pure'."]],"philippi-s48-039":["2. Among the ancients there was not so great a difference between the distinguished and the people. The entire people, which commonly sees with natural eyes, was therefore the object that the ancient artists sought to please. If they wanted this, they had to be natural. We now find no taste in it. Human beings do not gladly remain in one place, even if it were the highest step. The newer times sought alteration and altered nature.",["Gegenstand = object; OCR 'Stufte' read as 'Stufe'."]],"philippi-s48-040":["We have, on the other hand, again a advantage over the ancient times. Like a human being, so the whole human race gets ever more experience. In mathematics, e.g., the ancients did not have algebra with which we do the compendious calculating that they brought out with indescribable effort and prolixity. So also in natural history. Diligence can always add something.",["Syntax of 'In der Mathematik ... die Alten hatten nicht die Algebra mit der wir das compendiöse Calculiren ...' is elliptical; kept as in German. Erfahrung = experience."]],"philippi-s48-041":["3. Are the ancients masters of taste",["Breaks off in the list; the grammatical construction is carried by the following items."]],"philippi-s48-042":["a. because the books of religion are written in the languages of the ancients.",[]],"philippi-s48-043":["b. The divine services have been celebrated in the Latin language.",[]],"philippi-s48-044":["Would the profane writers not have been written in the languages",["OCR 'Prof anscribenten' read as 'Profanscribenten'. The sentence continues across passages 044–046; the German is a lineation fragment, kept as it stands."]],"philippi-s48-045":["; they would long since have perished. Because we have to learn religion",["Leading semicolon-like fragment follows from 044's 'seyn;'."]],"philippi-s48-046":["from them on account of it, so they are and will be preserved.",[]],"philippi-s48-047":["C. Because a dead language is unchangeable; so it happens that the",["'C.' as printed (for 'c.'); OCR 'geschiehtsdaßder' separated."]],"philippi-s48-048":["taste with regard to the dead language cannot change",[]],"philippi-s48-049":[". The expressions that were then the best, are so also",["Fragment of sentence continued from 048."]],"philippi-s48-050":["still; and if they were not, they are so now.",[]],"philippi-s48-051":["Taste must have models. Because one can learn beauty not from concepts but from intuition, because the beautiful has no rules for practice and doctrine, but only for critique, models are necessary to us. And indeed these models, if beauty is not to change, must be written in a dead language.",["Anschauung = intuition; Begriffe = concepts; Praxi (for Praxis) = practice; Doctrin = doctrine; Critic = critique."]],"philippi-s48-052":["The very circumstance of the dead language accomplishes much, that the ancients are these models.",["Literal and somewhat obscure: 'macht viel, daß ...' could also be read as 'makes much of it that ...'; the construction is left as it stands."]],"philippi-s48-053":["In them no expression can be censured. Each one is the best that the author could have chosen. If a poet has the advantage that his style cannot be censured; then nothing remains but the sharpness of the mind.",["Ausdruck = expression; Geist = mind (the glossary also allows 'spirit'); Schreibart = style."]],"philippi-s48-054":["Will we not also become originals and models for posterity? No, because no living language can be a universally learned one.",[]],"philippi-s48-055":["Prejudice of novelty. It arises from this, that we judge the newer times to have the teaching of all times before them. Of a new book it seems to us for the most part always usually to think much; for we believe that the author has used everything the ancients have written and added still something of his own. In sciences and arts of taste, however, this does not take place.",["Vorurtheil = prejudice; Belehrung = teaching; Wissenschaften = sciences. The stacking 'mehrentheils immer gewöhnlich' is kept as in the German; 'scheint ... viel zu halten' is awkward in the source.","'Geschmaks' (sic) = taste."]],"philippi-s48-056":["Because imitation is opposed to genius, some seek, in order to escape the reproach of imitation, to fall upon novelty. Many fall upon it because they are true geniuses, many in order to seem as if they had genius.",[]],"philippi-s48-057":["Free republics gladly love old fashions, sovereign states by contrast new fashions, because there no person is of importance who rules here. Young persons are devoted to novelty as to alternation in general; old ones love old fashions.",["OCR 'Heben' read as 'lieben'; 'Republiqven' = republics.","'dort ... hier' kept as 'there ... here'; the German leaves the reference of the two adverbs unspecified."]],"philippi-s48-058":["The inclination to the new is now stronger than in earlier times. Association with the women, which now governs everything, is the cause of it. Why do we follow the French in things of taste, because they give more charm to the thing than all other peoples. The English resist taste the most.",["'der alles jetzt regiert': the relative pronoun 'der' could refer to Umgang or Frauenzimmer; 'which' preserves the ambiguity.","Frauenzimmer rendered 'the women'. Ding and Sache both appear as 'thing' ('Dingen' / 'der Sache').","'Geschmak' (sic) = taste."]],"philippi-s48-059":["With regard to the sciences of reason and chiefly of experience we have good ground to prefer the new to the old.",["Wissenschaften = sciences; Vernunft = reason; Erfahrung = experience; Grund = ground."]],"philippi-s48-060":["Which prejudice should one accept sooner, that of antiquity or of novelty? In the practical the first is indeed to be accepted, one must often follow custom and old use, e.g. whether children should be raised by the parents or by the republic.",["Gebrauch = use; 'Republick' = republic; z. E. = e.g."]],"philippi-s48-061":["How can one forestall prejudices in children? One should prevent that I let the child think and raise it according to rules of my understanding. Rousseau demands in his Emile a negative education, I should not talk judgments at the child before his understanding is capable; especially one must separate inclination.",["'Aemil' = Emile. 'vorreden' is rendered 'talk ... at'; the German verb is unusual. Verstand = understanding; Urtheile = judgments.","The construction 'Man verhüte, daß ich ...' is kept as it stands."]],"philippi-s48-062":["Prejudice of the accepted edifice of doctrine.",["Lehrgebäude = literally 'edifice of doctrine' (Lehre = doctrine)."]],"philippi-s48-063":["Our cognitions get a certain degree of truth when they agree with one another. But if cognitions agree with one (*cum uno quodam*), when unity is therein; then they agree with one another. Now, however, cognitions can be forced together into agreement, in that one leaves out much that is true. Hence it comes that established edifices of doctrine so often introduce false propositions and greatly hinder truth. To this comes still this, that authors who have set a system in their head, even if they see false propositions flow from it, nevertheless do not want to have labored so much in vain.",["OCR 'vno' read as 'uno'. Erkenntnisse = cognitions; Einheit = unity; Sätze = propositions; Grad = degree.","'etablirte' = established (foreign-derived word in the German)."]],"philippi-s48-064":["On probability and the doubtful",["Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability; das Zweifelhafte = 'the doubtful'. The heading continues in the next passage ('der Erkenntniß'), so it is left unfinished here."]],"philippi-s48-065":["of cognition.",["Continuation of the heading in the previous passage."]],"philippi-s48-066":["If in a cognition I have more than half of the number of the sufficient grounds of taking to be true, then the cognition is probable. E.g. the sufficient grounds of taking to be true are 12, I have 8 of them; then the cognition is probable. But if I have of the sufficient grounds of taking to be true only the half; then the cognition is doubtful, e.g. I have of 12 grounds that are sufficient only 6.",["Vorwahrhalten (for Fürwahrhalten) = taking to be true; zureichend = sufficient; Anzahl = number (Zahl is also 'number'); Hälffte = half."]],"philippi-s48-067":["One must compare the grounds not with one another, but with the sufficient grounds. Accordingly in disputing it is not enough that grounds and counter-grounds compare themselves; they must also compare themselves with the sufficient grounds of taking to be true.",["Gegengründe = counter-grounds; sich vergleichen is kept in its reflexive form."]],"philippi-s48-068":["Subjective probability, that is the relation of the grounds of taking to be true, which is more certain than for the opposite. E.g. of the future life I have more grounds to assert it than for the opposite. The theory of probability will never attain to perfection. Philosophical probability lets itself be felt more than applied.",["OCR 'Probabiliität' read as 'Probabilität'. behaupten = assert; Vollkommenheit = perfection.","'welches gewisser ist als fürs Gegentheil' is kept literally, including the unclear antecedent of 'welches'. empfinden rendered 'felt'."]],"philippi-s48-069":["The uncertainty of cognition is not yet doubt. A cognition can be probable and yet uncertain.",["Ungewißheit = uncertainty; Zweifel = doubt."]],"philippi-s48-070":["A doubt is the equilibrium of the grounds of taking to be true. But even if my cognition is uncertain, it nevertheless does not leave me doubtful in exercise. I can be certain in acting, notwithstanding that I am uncertain in speculation. There would have to be not only a doubt in the judgment but also in his resolution, if a human being, e.g. a general, is not to know whither he should turn, if he is to be *anceps*. With mere uncertainty in speculation complete decidedness can take place in the action. Socrates was uncertain whether there was another world; but he acted as if he knew it as certain. Resolutions require not only no logical certainty, but are also only seldom capable of it.",["OCR 'Avenn' read as 'wenn'; 'kerne' as 'keine'; 'Ausübung' = exercise (also in 076); 'wissen' = know, 'wüste' = knew.","Entschluß / Entschlüßungen = resolution / resolutions; Handlung = action; Spekulation = speculation. *anceps* is Latin in the source."]],"philippi-s48-071":["That is the difference between practical and logical certainty.",[]],"philippi-s48-072":["What is not logically certain can be practically certain. There is a God; of this very few have a logical, but all a practical certainty.",[]],"philippi-s48-073":["Practical certainty",["Fragment: heading of a list continued in the next two passages."]],"philippi-s48-074":["1. according to rules of prudence is the pragmatic",["Klugheit = prudence; 'die pragmatische' is left without its noun, as in the German (completed by 'Gewißheit' in the next passage)."]],"philippi-s48-075":["2. according to rules of morality the moral certainty.",["Sittlichkeit = morality."]],"philippi-s48-076":["For practical certainty according to the rules of morality, i.e. for the moral, still less logical certainty is required than for the pragmatic, e.g. with an oath it is morally certain that one does not swear, if he has even the slightest presumption that he is not innocent. Thus in general with the omission of the evil and exercise of the good the slightest presumptions are capable of determining our actions, e.g. If I find something, a single presumption is capable of moving me to give it back. I do not do it from concern about a lawsuit or from prudence.",["OCR 'determmiren' read as 'determiniren' (Latinate; rendered 'determining', as for bestimmen). Vermuthung = presumption; Ausübung = exercise."]],"philippi-s48-077":["Many authors mix pragmatic and moral certainty together. Only late have the boundaries of the latter been found. Moral certainty is something *respective* to the moral laws.",["OCR 'respectiue' read as *respective* (Latinate in the source). 'sittlichen Gesetze' = moral laws (sittlich and moralisch both 'moral'). Grenzen = boundaries."]],"philippi-s48-078":["Scruples are counter-grounds against taking to be true, insofar as they are obscure; insofar as they are clear they are called doubts and if they are distinct, they are objections. Objections are almost always scruples at the beginning.",["OCR 'Vor wahrhalten' read as Vorwahrhalten (= Fürwahrhalten), as in 066. dunkel = obscure; klar = clear; deutlich = distinct; Einwürfe = objections."]],"philippi-s48-079":["In many cases we are logically, although not practically and morally, certain.",[]],"philippi-s48-080":["Hindrance of assent or taking to be true is either subjective or objective, and is called difficulty.",["OCR 'subiectiv', 'obiectiv' read as subjective, objective. 'und wird Schwürigkeit genannt': the subject of 'wird' is left as ambiguous as in the German (the hindrance or its kind). Beyfall = assent."]],"philippi-s48-081":["Scruples consist in a resistance that our understanding feels. They are grounded on prejudices. They show themselves even in mathematical cognitions. An acquaintance with one's own and other subjects is required, and one must know how human beings ordinarily think in order to be able to tell others the scruples that they have.",["OCR 'gewöhn! ich' read as 'gewöhnlich'. Kenntniß = acquaintance; 'seines eigenen und anderer Subjekte' is kept as ambiguous as the German (one's own [subject]; or one's own and others' subjects)."]],"philippi-s48-082":["It is very beautiful and conducive to the promotion of truth, when one helps a human being, who cannot unfold his error, to resolve it. Then that one will be glad and this one sharp-witted. Errors must not merely be answered, but also explained. In that someone deviates from the laws of the understanding; so one must philosophize how such goes on.",["'jener ... dieser' kept as 'that one ... this one' (the referents are not specified). 'denselben auflösen' = resolve it ('denselben' = the error). Irrthum = error; erkläret = explained (Erklärung = explanation). 'Indem ...; so' kept as in the German punctuation."]],"philippi-s48-083":["Scruples always deserve an investigation; one sees that, when one lets a human being talk long. They are not to be despised, least of all by him who has them in his head.",[]],"philippi-s48-084":["Scruples arise not so much from the constitution of the grounds of taking to be true as from the new fold that the understanding must assume. Hence one must not merely give another cognition, but also accustom him to it. Whenever the understanding is to give the cognition another form, it balks. It is therefore also good that one talk a lot about a thing that is unaccustomed to the listener, and accustom his understanding to the arguments brought forward.",["Vorwahrhalten (a variant of Fürwahrhalten) = 'taking to be true'; Erkenntniß = 'cognition'; Verstand = 'understanding'; Beschaffenheit = 'constitution'; Skrupel = 'scruple'.","'dem Erkenntniß' is printed with the neuter article in the source and is rendered simply as 'the cognition'."]],"philippi-s48-085":["Doubt.",[]],"philippi-s48-086":["Doubt of postponement, or the pure skeptical doubt, is entirely distinguished from the dogmatic, even already by the effects; the one seeks to elevate itself above all cognition; the other to investigate and to test the truth. The method is different. The theoretical doubt that holds the understanding *in suspenso* is different from the practical doubt that holds the will *in suspenso*. With a theoretical doubt I can be without any practical doubt. Even if I cognize the proposition of Wolff that all actions are determined by something; it will nonetheless not take freedom from my will. Hence the doubt of postponement in the case of the most important propositions is better than decided dogmatic judgments, because the will after all is not determined by speculation.",["OCR 'Auf schubszweifel' read as Aufschubszweifel; rendered 'doubt of postponement' (Aufschub = postponement, deferral).","'des Wolfen' is rendered 'of Wolff'.","Urtheil = 'judgment'; Satz = 'proposition'; bestimmen = 'determine'."]],"philippi-s48-087":["The arguments of the skeptics were named with these names: 1. Argument of the liar. Whoever lies and admits that he lies, says the truth in lying. 2. The dreamer. 3. Achilles: because he could run very fast he was compared with the tortoise that was a mile ahead of him and that he overran 10 times faster. 4. Diallele.",["'Diallele' is left as in the source (the circular argument, Diallelus)."]],"philippi-s48-088":["The ungroundability of a thing is always subjective.",["Unbegründlichkeit rendered 'ungroundability' to keep Grund = 'ground'; 'subiectiv' = 'subjective'."]],"philippi-s48-089":["Objective difficulty, as soon as one is conscious of it, is called doubt.",["OCR 'Obiectivc' read as Objective."]],"philippi-s48-090":["Subjective difficulty, of which I am not conscious, is called scruple.",["Source: 'der ich mir nicht bewußt'; Scrupel = 'scruple'."]],"philippi-s48-091":["A faithlessness arises from the fact that one is always so very concerned with the *praxis* in the case of the theoretical propositions. Hence it comes about that the *argumentum ab utili, a tuto* steps in place of reason. One wants to prove its truth from the utility that flows from the proposition. Because a proposition is useful, it will not on that account be true. The practical suspension does not follow from the theoretical.",["'Praxi' (Latin dative form in the source) rendered *praxis*; 'Vernunfft' = 'reason'."]],"philippi-s48-092":["We can decide that something is true, and yet we can say of the very same thing that it is not certain.",["'gewiß' = 'certain'."]],"philippi-s48-093":["One sees, the judgment of truth, if it suffices to take something to be true, yet with the condition that it is not certain, must stand in a relation with the will. E.g. If an accusation is made against a human being of whom we cannot suspect such a deed, then my understanding indeed sees from all the circumstances that it is so, but my will balks at believing it. Then it is indeed true but only not certain. Something can be certain to one subject that is not certain to another.",["'dass es an dem sey' rendered 'that it is so'; the syntax of the first sentence is kept as in the German and is awkward.","OCR 'Wülen' read as Willen; glauben = 'believe'."]],"philippi-s48-094":["Taking to be true rests not on liking but on grounds of cognition.",["OCR 'Beheben' read as Belieben ('liking'); the reading is conjectural. 'Erkennt nißgründen' joined as Erkenntnißgründen."]],"philippi-s49-001":["Gradation of taking to be true.",[]],"philippi-s49-002":["1. Assent",["Beyfall = 'assent'."]],"philippi-s49-003":["2. Approval",["OCR 'Bilhgung' read as Billigung."]],"philippi-s49-004":["3. If these grounds of taking to be true are insufficient; then they give probability.",["unzureichend = 'insufficient' (zureichend = 'sufficient'); Wahrscheinlichkeit = 'probability'."]],"philippi-s49-005":["4. Apparentness, insofar as the grounds of taking to be true are as it were parts of that ground which is good for the subject, or insofar as the grounds taken together *coordinando* make up a ground of taking to be true sufficient for the subject; insofar, I say, a cognition is apparent.",["Scheinbarkeit / scheinbar rendered 'apparentness' / 'apparent' (related to Schein = 'illusion'); the English does not decide between 'seeming' and 'plausible'.","The syntax of 'gleichsam Theile sind desjenigen Grundes, der für das Subjekt gut' is kept as it stands."]],"philippi-s49-006":["Objective grounds of taking to be true, since they are in the object, are valid for all. Apparentness is valid only for individual subjects. It is always apparent that the human being will perish after death. The poets therefore also represent it so.",["OCR 'smd' read as sind. 'stehen es ... vor' is probably a slip or variant for stellen ... vor and is rendered 'represent'.","'gelten' = 'be valid' (gültig = 'valid'); the first sentence is a fragment in the source."]],"philippi-s49-007":["Understanding belongs to probability, the senses belong to apparentness; and this latter thus takes place only where one judges from illusion. Only *phaenomena* belong to it, but understanding belongs to probability. Objective grounds of taking to be true belong to it, whereas only subjective ones belong to apparentness.",["'Phänomena' rendered *phaenomena*; Schein = 'illusion'; the antecedents of 'dazu' and 'es' are left as ambiguous as in the German."]],"philippi-s49-008":["The 2nd degree of certainty is according to rules of identity, e.g. A whole is greater than one of its parts. In pure reason there is a pure identity.",["Gewißheit = 'certainty'; Grad = 'degree'; OCR 'Vernunf f t' read as Vernunft."]],"philippi-s49-009":["Moral certainty with regard to pure morality comes nearest to the mathematical.",[]],"philippi-s49-010":["Metaphysics is a critique and it is most lacking in certainty.",["'Kritic' rendered 'critique'; 'am mehresten' = 'most'."]],"philippi-s49-011":["Certainty of cognition is only subjective.",[]],"philippi-s49-012":["If the grounds of taking to be true are valid only in relation to the constitution of the subject, then apparentness is there.",[]],"philippi-s49-013":["It was apparent to the ancients, and is also to all common people, that the sun moves around the earth. To the moderns the opposite is probable.",["The source says 'ist auch allen gemeinen Leuten scheinbar'; 'common' = gemein."]],"philippi-s49-014":["A cognition that is apparently certain is called persuasion. The certainty that arises from the understanding and not from sensible illusion is conviction. This, which arises according to rules of the understanding, is objective, that one is subjective certainty. The intuiting certainty according to rules of the understanding is evidence.",["Ueberredung = 'persuasion'; Ueberzeugung = 'conviction'; Evidenz = 'evidence' (Latinate, kept distinct from Beweis = 'proof').","'Diese ... jene' rendered 'this ... that one'; 'anschauende' = 'intuiting' (anschauen / Anschauung = 'intuition')."]],"philippi-s49-015":["The weak one can hardly convince otherwise than according to laws of sensibility. So in moral, so also in theoretical propositions. Evidence (obviousness) is distinguished from certainty.",["The first sentence is kept in its elliptical form; the German can be read 'the weak can hardly be convinced otherwise ...' or 'the weak one can hardly convince ...'.","Augenscheinlichkeit rendered 'obviousness' (literally 'appearance to the eye')."]],"philippi-s49-016":["The sciences that can be subjected to ocular appearance can have evidence. Of that kind is mathematics. But philosophical qualities cannot get evidence, e.g. the proposition that there is a supreme cause can be convincing only to those who are practiced in thinking through pure understanding.",["Augenschein = 'ocular appearance'; Wissenschaften = 'sciences'; Qualitäten = 'qualities'; 'überzeugend' = 'convincing'."]],"philippi-s49-017":["How do we give such propositions evidence? By showing the truth of such a proposition in objects of the senses. E.g. that not the utility of virtue and the shadow of vice fill us with high esteem toward the former and with hatred toward the latter can be shown in examples. Although human beings who have deceived foreign enemies and with that support others create utility; although we profit from those who throw everything away with brainless extravagance; yet the former are for us abominable and the latter contemptible.",["The second sentence is kept with its German construction (the 'that'-clause as subject of 'can be shown').","'die erstem ... die letztern' rendered 'the former ... the latter'; 'erfülle' is subjunctive in the source."]],"philippi-s49-018":["Sensibility can indeed give liveliness but not evidence. The conviction must occur according to laws of the understanding; even when the grounds of taking to be true are borrowed from experience. For before all understanding we indeed have sensation but not experience. Animals have no experience; for only through the understanding can one have it.",["OCR 'Lebhaff tigkeit' read as Lebhaftigkeit; Empfindung = 'sensation'; Erfahrung = 'experience'; Sinnlichkeit = 'sensibility'."]],"philippi-s49-019":["Moral cognitions can be very clear but not evident.",["klar = 'clear' (deutlich = 'distinct')."]],"philippi-s49-020":["Evidence takes place only in the connection of the parts to the whole, i.e. where the understanding coordinates; but not in the connection of the grounds and sequels i.e. where the understanding subordinates; consequently only in mathematics not in philosophy.",["Verknüpfung = 'connection'; Folgen rendered 'sequels' per the glossary (Folge = sequel); 'Theile' = 'parts'; 'Mathematic' rendered 'mathematics'."]],"philippi-s49-021":["Like certainty, so also conviction is either empirical or rational. If the conviction occurs without admixture of experience through the pure understanding and the grounds of truth can be subjected to sensible ocular obviousness; then in that case we have mathematical certainty. Accordingly this will consist in the evident reason (rational) certainty.",["The last sentence is obscure in the source ('in der evidenten Vernunfft (rational) Gewißheit') and may be corrupt; it is rendered as it stands.","Augenscheinlichkeit = 'obviousness'."]],"philippi-s49-022":["Philosophy is not capable of evidence, least of all metaphysics.",["fähig = 'capable'."]],"philippi-s49-023":["The utility of skeptical doubt is uncommonly great, if it is of the right kind, in that it is permitted to set one proposition against others and proofs for the opposite are adduced: thereby we are brought to the investigation of our own reason. For since we see of a proposition proofs and counterproofs that show the opposite; then, since a proposition can after all be only either true or false, the difference must lie here in the subject. And the cognition of the subject is the true object of philosophy. For we have all our cognitions only by the fact that we cognize ourselves.",["'rechter Art' rendered 'of the right kind' (Art = kind/way); Gegenstand = 'object'; Beweise / Gegenbeweise = 'proofs' / 'counterproofs'.","The punctuation (colons, semicolon) follows the source; the syntax of 'indem erlaubt ist ... so werden wir' is kept as it stands."]],"philippi-s49-024":["The great dispute arises from this, that we hold subjective grounds to be objective; because we cannot think a thing otherwise than so, so it must, we believe, also be so. Much insight belongs to it before one is convinced that the conditions of the subject are no condition of the object: then one first really learns that one knows nothing; and with this metaphysics is to occupy itself.",["Grund = ground; Bedingung = condition; Objekt/Object = object; überzeugt = convinced (Überzeugung = conviction); weiß = knows (wissen)."]],"philippi-s49-025":["Objective cognition cannot in many cases be attained. But in subjective cognition one can reach certainty.",["Erkenntniß = cognition; Gewißheit = certainty (not in the glossary)."]],"philippi-s49-026":["The relation of the grounds to the sufficient ground is considered in the case of probability.",["zureichend = sufficient; Wahrscheinlichkeit = probability. A stray apostrophe before 'Wahrscheinlichkeit' in the OCR is dropped."]],"philippi-s49-027":["On the relation of the grounds to probability rests plausibility.",["Scheinbarkeit is not in the glossary; 'plausibility' is my choice (cf. Schein = illusion, which is not used here)."]],"philippi-s49-028":["The rules of prudence prescribe to us how one can become happy, without paying attention to the worthiness for the same; the rules of morality only to the latter, without looking to the first.",["OCR 'weiden' read as 'werden'. The elliptical second clause ('only to the latter') is left as in the German."]],"philippi-s49-029":["Opinion is an uncertain cognition, of which we are conscious that it is uncertain. Persuasion is when we hold an uncertain cognition to be certain. Whoever lets himself be persuaded has not yet gained insight into the difference between certainty and probability. One must know a true conviction if one wants to know where it is lacking; just as slaves must know freedom if they want to complain about their state.",["Meynung = opinion; Ueberredung = persuasion; Ueberzeugung = conviction; Zustand = state; bewust = conscious (Bewußtsein = consciousness).","kennen and wissen are both rendered 'know' (kennen = know; be acquainted with, noscere; wissen = know)."]],"philippi-s49-030":["Even if the utility of mathematics were only that it makes us certain and lets us feel what true certainty is; it would already be inestimable.",["'utility' for Nutzen is not in the glossary."]],"philippi-s49-031":["Hypothesis. The author has not rightly gained insight into it. A philosophical opinion a priori is no hypothesis, but rather the philosophical opinion a posteriori. If I infer from the sequels to the truth of the ground, that is hypothesis. If I infer from insufficient grounds to sequels, that is opinion, e.g. that in the earth there are heavier bodies than gold. I have grounds to opine that; I infer that from grounds and not from sequels. That is not hypothesis. The earth has cavities, because every soft body, when it dries, gets cavities, and the earth in its first state was soft. That is a philosophical opinion, because proofs are there. But if I assume the earth has cavities, on account of this, that from it it can be shown how the valleys arose; then I assume the ground because the sequel can be explained from it. That is hypothesis. The more an opinion suffices for the explanation of the sequels, the more probable is the hypothesis. Until the opinion is adequate to the explanation of all sequels, then it has complete certainty. But then one would have to know all sequels. But that one can in no case, presumably; yet in calculating it sometimes works to know all sequels. If, to a hypothesis, besides the proofs a posteriori grounds a priori also come, then it has certainty. That is a chief duty in the doctrine of nature, that one also proves a priori what one has assumed. E.g. From the ether assumed *per hypothesin* many phenomena can be explained. Now I prove that the ether is there. If a hypothesis explains much manifold from one ground, it gets much probability; as Newton's hypothesis of gravity. *Hypothesis subsidiaria*, when for the explanation of a hypothesis one assumes several, as Tycho de Brahe assumed the *epycylos epicyclorum*.",["OCR corrected: 'Mejaiung' and 'rneynen' read as Meynung/meynen; 'Schlüsse' (twice, verbal) read as 'schließe'; 'erldären' as 'erklären'.","Folge(n) rendered 'sequel(s)' per the glossary (Folge = sequel; sequence); Folgerung would be 'consequence'. Schluß/schließen = inference/infer.","hinlänglich = 'adequate' (distinct from zureichend = sufficient); Mannigfaltige = manifold; Naturlehre = doctrine of nature; Beweis = proof; Erklärung/erklären = explanation/explain.","'wol in keinem Fall' is kept as 'presumably in no case' — the sentence 'Aber das kann man wol in keinem Fall' is obscure in construction and left so. 'epycylos epicyclorum' kept in the transcript's spelling."]],"philippi-s50-001":["Science. §185",["Wißenschafft = science (scientia)."]],"philippi-s50-002":["To know means to cognize with certainty and to take to be true. The degrees of taking to be true are",["Wißen = know; Vorwahrhalten = Fürwahrhalten = taking to be true; Grad = degree. Text breaks off with a list to follow."]],"philippi-s50-003":["1. to opine, to take something to be true in the degree where I do not see how it is in relation to certainty.",["meynen = opine (cf. Meinung = opinion)."]],"philippi-s50-004":["2. to believe, to opine something in the degree that the opinion has a practical certainty. The opinion of the sufficient degree for action is called belief.",["glauben/Glaube = believe/belief."]],"philippi-s50-005":["3. to know, to cognize the truth sufficiently. Opinion cognizes something insufficiently, belief sufficiently for practice. Science sufficiently according to logical rules. Science in general is nothing other than the cognition through which we know something. It is contradistinguished from art, under which one understands everything for which a certain specific perfection of practice is required; science, on the other hand, is where such a perfection is required for theory. Not everything practical is art; the practical skill that is not merely a sequel of theory is called an art. If something practical flows from the mere cognition, then it is not art. Practical geometry is art. Science must be able to be taught. Art must be brought about through my own capacities. For this genius is required. Science is further contradistinguished from critic. All theoretical cognition is either a doctrine or critic. Critic is that theoretical cognition which teaches to assess the perfection that is already there. Doctrine, however, is a theoretical cognition which contains the grounds according to which a product can be produced. All cognitions that are grounds of practice we will call critics, e.g. logic, aesthetics.",["OCR corrected: 'Erkenn tmß' read as Erkenntniß; 'beurt heilen' as beurteilen (assess). 'Praxi' left as the dative of Latin praxis, rendered 'practice'.","Fähigkeit = capacity; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Folge = sequel; Doctrin = doctrine; Critic kept as 'critic' (not 'criticism'); Kunst = art; Geschicklichkeit = skill.","'is called an art' etc. follow the German; 'Wenn ... so ists nicht Kunst' is kept without article."]],"philippi-s51-001":["§ 188",[]],"philippi-s51-002":["Mathematics has evidence in respect of quantity but not of quality.",["Evidenz = evidence; Quantitaet/Qualitaet = quantity/quality."]],"philippi-s51-003":["Mathematical certainty is a rational certainty so evident is. Evidence is when a truth in our cognitions of reason becomes as great as it can be in the case of the senses. Numbers, space and time are considered as objects of the senses.",["The first sentence is as transmitted and is probably defective or garbled ('so evident ist'); I have not emended it.","Zahl = number; Raum = space; Zeit = time; Gegenstände = objects; Sinne = senses."]],"philippi-s51-004":["The full certainty arises from the consciousness of the necessity not, as the author says, of the truth; but of the taking to be true. We find in ourselves an inner compulsion and boundness to take to be true what we cognize as true.",["ausführlich is rendered 'full' (other possibilities: 'detailed', 'extensive'); not in the glossary.","Nothwendigkeit = necessity (notwendig); Vorwahrhalten = taking to be true; Zwang = compulsion; Gebundenheit = boundness."]],"philippi-s51-005":["Conviction is an inner compulsion from the truth of the ideas; this is chiefly to be met with in mathematics.",["'von der Wahrheit' is rendered literally 'from the truth'; the sense of the preposition (by/regarding) is left open. Ideen = ideas."]],"philippi-s52-001":["§189",[]],"philippi-s52-002":["As concerns the two divisions; so certainty of the first rank and mathematical certainty of the second rank are distinguished not according to form but according to the *data*. Certainty can be complete, either through reason or experience. If the moon comes between the earth and sun, then an eclipse is mathematically certain through experience. But that in a triangle all 3 angles make up 180° is certain through reason and this would therefore be a mathematical certainty of the first rank. But this division of the author is not quite good. One does not look in mathematical certainty at the object, only at the form.",["Eintheilungen = divisions (Einteilung); Datis rendered as Latin *data* in italics; Vernunft = reason; Erfahrung = experience; Object = object; Form = form.","'vom ersten Range ... vom 2ten Range' is kept as printed; the pairing of the two ranks with the 'two divisions' is as obscure in the German."]],"philippi-s52-003":["The properties of mathematical cognition are",["Eigenschafft not in the glossary; 'properties' chosen (Beschaffenheit, also 'property', is a different word). Text leads into a list."]],"philippi-s52-004":["1. The complete distinctness.",["Deutlichkeit = distinctness (deutlich = distinct)."]],"philippi-s52-005":["2. The complete adequacy (*completudo*)",["Zulänglichkeit = 'adequacy' (distinguished from zureichend = sufficient and Sufficienz = sufficiency below); the Latin is the transcript's own form."]],"philippi-s52-006":["3. Precision, that the superfluous be cut off.",["Praecision kept as 'precision'; 'sey' rendered as subjunctive 'be'."]],"philippi-s52-007":["4. There could also belong to it a certain intuiting to which the mathematical cognitions can be subjected.",["Doubtful reading: 'dem' ('Anschauen dem die ...') is read as the relative 'to which' (possibly OCR/transcript for 'denn' or 'dem'); I follow the text as it stands. Anschauen is an infinitive noun, rendered 'intuiting' (cf. Anschauung = intuition)."]],"philippi-s52-008":["The representations of reason of the qualities with which philosophy concerns itself agree with the mathematical cognitions in the distinctness and sufficiency of the grounds. Precision, however, and evidence is peculiar only to this.",["OCR 'Vernunff tvorstellungen' read as Vernunffvorstellungen; Vorstellung = representation; Sufficienz = sufficiency.","'dieser' (feminine dative singular) has no clearly stated antecedent in the passage; left as 'this'. The verb 'is' with a compound subject follows the German."]],"philippi-s52-009":["§",["The paragraph number is missing in the source."]],"philippi-s52-010":["An arbitrary truth (*positio*, not *hypothesis*) is one which expresses what I arbitrarily assume. E.g. Arbitrarily the circle is divided into 360°. Objective propositions cannot be assumed arbitrarily, but only subjective ones. That we count to 10 and consider all other numbers *in ratione decupla* is arbitrary. Arbitrary propositions go to the practical and only indicate what one wants to do, although one could also do otherwise.",["willkürlich = arbitrary; Satz/Sätze = proposition(s); Zirkel = circle; Zahlen = numbers.","Latin terms kept in italics."]],"philippi-s52-011":["Every proposition is either demonstrably certain or indemonstrably certain.",["OCR 'er weißlich' / 'uner weißlich' read as erweislich / unerweislich (demonstrable / indemonstrable, from erweisen)."]],"philippi-s52-012":["A cognition is called indemonstrable when it is immediately certain; but if it is certain by means of another cognition from which, as from a ground, it can be demonstrated, then it is demonstrably certain.",["OCR 'gewTiß' read as gewiß; unmittelbahr = immediately; vermittelst = by means of; erweisen = demonstrate (here distinct from beweisen/Beweis = prove/proof)."]],"philippi-s52-013":["Proof is that cognition by means of which the truth can be cognized.",["Beweis = proof; erkennen = cognize."]],"philippi-s52-014":["The matter of the proof is the ground of proof or the immediately certain cognition. The form is the way of the consequence, how namely the truth of the proposition follows from the ground of proof.",["Materie = matter; Beweißgrund = ground of proof; Erkenntniß = cognition; Art = way; Consequenz = consequence; Satz = proposition."]],"philippi-s52-015":["If the form of the proof itself cannot be cognized immediately but only mediately, then this is indeed a proof but no demonstration.",["\"kerne\" read as \"keine\" (OCR). \"wol\" rendered \"indeed\"."]],"philippi-s52-016":["The fault of the proof can lie either in the matter or in the form.",[]],"philippi-s52-017":["In every proof one assumes 3 pieces",["Stücke = \"pieces\" (not Teile, \"parts\"). The sentence has no final punctuation in the source."]],"philippi-s52-018":["1. The matter of the proof seu *ratio probandi*",["\"Beweißthums\" (Beweisthum) rendered simply \"proof\"; the German word is not Beweis. *seu* = \"or\"."]],"philippi-s52-019":["2. The form or the consequence from it.",[]],"philippi-s52-020":["3. *Conclusio*, e.g. Everything that is contingent has a foreign cause; that I prove from the contingency. Thus the contingency is the matter. But how it flows from this that everything contingent has a foreign cause, that is the consequence, the form. The matter can in every proof be regarded as the *medius terminus* and the form as the relation of the same to the proposition at hand. All proofs are either empirical or rational. The mathematical proofs, which have evidence, precision and *Anscheinen*, are called *Demonstrationes*. A rational proof in general is called *probatio*. Not all proofs, however, even if they are sufficient, are called *Demonstrationes*, e.g. that all temperance stands equally high, of this only an empirical probation can be given. These are however just as clear as *Demonstrationes*. *Probatio* is *vel completa vel incompleta*.",["\"Anscheinen\" left as printed; the reading and sense are doubtful (possibly a slip or OCR error).","\"Mäßigkeit\" (temperance) as printed; the example is obscure and is left so.","zufällig = contingent, Zufälligkeit = contingency; fremde = foreign; vorhabenden = \"at hand\"; zureichend = sufficient.","*vel completa vel incompleta* = \"either complete or incomplete\"; *seu*-type Latin kept as in the source."]],"philippi-s52-021":["*Petere principium* means in logic to lay as ground a proposition that is not yet proven but is thereby provable, and to assume it as the ground of proof of other propositions.",["\"dabey\" rendered \"thereby\"; \"in this connection\" is also possible. erweißlich = provable."]],"philippi-s52-022":["To prove means nothing other than to make the *identitatem implicitam* into the *explicita*; and to convince someone of a proposition means to make someone conscious that the proposition is in accordance with the form of the understanding and its laws. From this it follows that those propositions are unprovable in which the identity is *explicita* or manifest. E.g. The whole is equal to its parts taken together. If these propositions are rational and theoretical, they are called *axiomata*, practical ones *postulata*.",["\"einen\" (\"someone\") is ambiguous as to who is made conscious; kept as \"someone\". überzeugen = convince (Überzeugung = conviction).","Latin accusatives *identitatem implicitam* kept as printed."]],"philippi-s52-023":["Every general experience is provable, every single particular one unprovable.",["\"einzele besondre\" = \"single particular\"; besonder = particular."]],"philippi-s52-024":["All provable propositions are either analytic or synthetic. Analytic propositions or judgments are those where, when I dissect the subject, I find the predicate therein and thus through dissection see the identity, e.g. Every body is divisible is an analytic proposition. For it can be expressed thus: that which I think under the mark of body, that I also think under that of divisibility; and if I analyze the concept of body, I find divisibility therein. All analytic propositions accordingly stand under the proposition of identity and of contradiction and are proved from it.",["Satz is rendered \"proposition\" throughout, including \"Satz der Identität und des Wiederspruchs\" (where \"principle\" is also a glossary sense).","zergliedern = dissect; analysiren = analyze; Merkmal = mark; Urtheil = judgment.","\"daraus\" (\"from it\") has no clear singular antecedent in the German; kept."]],"philippi-s52-025":["Synthetic propositions, however, are those where the predicate does not lie in the notion of the subject, but is added as something entirely different. E.g. Everything that happens has a sufficient ground. If I here analyze the subject, that is: that which happens, the arising, I will not find therein the sufficient ground that is supposed to belong to it. This is added to the arising and combined with it as something entirely different.",["Notion (Latinate) kept as \"notion\", distinct from Begriff = concept. zureichend = sufficient; Entstehen = arising; verbunden = combined."]],"philippi-s52-026":["Synthetic propositions cannot be cognized through reason. For reason cannot coordinate but only subordinate. Synthetic propositions are either experiences or intuiting propositions of the understanding. These one encounters in mathematics, those in physics. Locke calls the former *propositiones coexistentiae*, which was not understood by many. Every empirical proposition is synthetic. For I there combine with the subject marks that are not contained in it.",["\"anschauende\" (participle) rendered \"intuiting\", distinct from anschaulich = intuitive.","\"die erstem\" = \"the former\"; its antecedent is left as in the German. The \"Diese ... jene\" assignment is kept as printed.","\"Vernunfft\" read as Vernunft."]],"philippi-s52-027":["I note here only that the falsity that concerns the form is much greater than that which concerns the matter. For the matter is accidental; the form, however, essential and applicable to many matters. A false form is almost incorrigible. For it is universal and has an influence on many propositions. A false proposition can sooner be corrected. One calls the chief falsity of a proposition, which consists more in the form than matter, πρῶτον ψεῦδος.",["The Greek (OCR \"irpcoTOV ysOSos\") silently corrected to πρῶτον ψεῦδος."]],"philippi-s52-028":["Many errors arise from the fact that one does not sufficiently know what is required for complete certainty; Locke therefore advises the study of mathematics very urgently.",["kennt = \"know\" (kennen); Gewißheit = certainty."]],"philippi-s53-001":["§196",[]],"philippi-s53-002":["One can prove something in a twofold way: directly from the interconnection with the grounds, and indirectly where I show from the opposite the truth or falsity of the proposition. The indirect proof shows only that something is true or false without indicating the sources from which it is so. It is easier to prove negatively than affirmatively.",["Last sentence: \"leichter so negativ als affirmativ\" read as \"so ... als\" = \"than\"; the \"so\" is not rendered separately.","Zusammenhang = interconnection; directe/indirecte kept as direct/indirect."]],"philippi-s53-003":["For if I show a proposition and only a single false sequel; then I have proved that the proposition is false. But I would have to know all sequels if I wanted to infer from them to the ground and prove the truth of the proposition. But because I do not know all sequels, I also infer only with conjecture to the truth of the proposition and it is a hypothesis.",["Folge = \"sequel\" per glossary (Folgerung would be \"consequence\", Consequenz also \"consequence\"). \"Schlüsse\" (verb) = infer (schließen).","\"einen Satz und ... eine falsche Folge\": the construction is elliptical in the source and kept elliptical; \"Schlüssen\" read as \"schlüssen/schließen\" (capitalization is a transcript quirk)."]],"philippi-s53-004":["A direct proof that flows from the interconnection with the grounds is genetic and at the same time indicates the sources *a priori*. Indirect proofs are also called apagogic.",["\"apagogische\" rendered \"apagogic\"."]],"philippi-s53-005":["Properly one can conduct proofs in a twofold way 1. that one infers from the grounds to the proposition as to the sequels. These are the most beautiful proofs. 2. from the sequels to the proposition as to the ground. In this one cannot prove an affirmative proposition, because one does not know all sequels, but indeed negative ones. For if a sequel is false then the whole proposition is false. That is an indirect or apagogic proof.",["Folge = \"sequel\" as above. \"als auf die Folgen\" / \"als auf den Grund\" kept literal (\"as to\")."]],"philippi-s53-006":["Our representations are either concepts, or intuitions.",[]],"philippi-s53-007":["Concept, *perceptio communis*, a commonly valid representation.",["\"gemeingültige\" = gemein (common) + gültig (valid), rendered \"commonly valid\"."]],"philippi-s53-008":["*Omnes perceptiones sunt vel reflectivae vel intuitivae.*",["Latin in the source and left in Latin (\"All perceptions are either reflective or intuitive\")."]],"philippi-s53-009":["Through logical separation we can get no concept, but only give the cognition in particular form.",["Absonderung = \"separation\" (not Abstraktion). besonder = particular."]],"philippi-s53-010":["Every *conceptus communis* can be regarded as a *superior*.",[]],"philippi-s53-011":["§ 197",[]],"philippi-s53-012":["For demonstration evidence is required, so that it is impossible to postpone one's assent. Only of that which falls into the senses can assent not be refused. There are accordingly few *Demonstrationes*. All empirical propositions can have only *Probationes*. An aesthetic probation is when something persuades from taste and the passions. These *Probationes* are not objective but only subjective. They are persuasions according to rules of appearance, e.g. the decency of a man can serve as proof of his rectitude. The spirit of demonstrating is cultivated through mathematics.",["OCR \"Geis schmack\" read as \"Geschmack\" (taste); \"umnöglich\" as \"unmöglich\".","Ueberredung = persuasion; Erscheinung = appearance; Beweis = proof; Beyfall = assent.","\"Demonstrirgeist\" = \"spirit of demonstrating\"; \"excolirt\" (Latinate verb) rendered \"cultivated\"."]],"philippi-s54-001":["§ 200",[]],"philippi-s54-002":["The blind inclination to demonstrate is called mania for demonstrating. Mania is called an inclination that is not moderated. The mania for demonstrating",["Demonstrirsucht = \"mania for demonstrating\"; Sucht = \"mania\"; Neigung = inclination.","The passage breaks off after \"Die Demonstrirsucht\"; the following numbered items continue it."]],"philippi-s54-003":["1. arises from a supposed ease of demonstrating. To prevent this one must show the difficulties of a cognition.",["\"Schwürigkeiten\" read as \"Schwierigkeiten\"."]],"philippi-s54-004":["2. from charlatanry",[]],"philippi-s54-005":["3. from pedantry. This consists in the lack of skill to apply cognition, when one cannot assess the cases where a cognition is fitting. Thus one imagined it to be necessary either not to judge at all or to demonstrate everything. Wolff did not have the mania for demonstrating; but indeed his successors did. It is found especially among the ignorant.",["OCR \"sieh\" read as \"sich\", \"bev\" as \"bey\". beurtheilen = assess; urtheilen = judge (v.)."]],"philippi-s54-006":["Many skeptics believed that one must assume nothing without demonstration. A few years ago the strong inclination to demonstrate reigned; now a mania for shallowness seems to be gaining the upper hand. This arises when someone arranges his way of thinking according to the taste of the public. True taste loves thoroughness, like a Pope.",["\"Sceptici\" rendered \"skeptics\"; \"Publici\" (Latin genitive) rendered \"public\".","\"ächte\" = \"true\" (genuine); \"wie ein Pope\" kept literal (article and capitalization as printed)."]],"philippi-s54-007":["What is better, shallowness or the mania for demonstrating? Shallowness; for that is a *malum negativum*, a deficiency, the other a *privativum*, a *vitium*. Here I merely do not use reason, there I believe I use it and deceive it; I use it perversely. The mania for demonstrating is opposed to taste as well as to reason; but with shallowness, there everything conforms to taste. One can gradually perfect cognition that is shallow, but not that which has already been demonstrated as perfectly certain. That is a *vitium* in the form.",["Seichtigkeit = 'shallowness'; Demonstrirsucht = 'mania for demonstrating' (literally a craving/addiction to demonstrate).","Erkenntniß = cognition. 'Vernunf ft' etc. silently corrected to Vernunft = reason.","'em vitium' read as 'ein vitium'; 'privatiuum' normalized to *privativum*, 'negatiuum' to *negativum* (OCR u/v).","'jene' ('the other', 'that') and 'die' are kept as in the German; the antecedents of 'jene' and 'dort'/'hier' are left as ambiguous as in the German.","'die schon als vollkommen gewiß demonstrirt ist': 'als vollkommen gewiß' could modify 'demonstrirt' or 'die'; kept as in German order."]],"philippi-s55-001":["§201",[]],"philippi-s55-002":["Sensation is a representation of the presence of an object, of which the cause lies in my activity.",["Empfindung = sensation; Vorstellung = representation; Object = object (Latinate spelling); Thätigkeit = activity.","'wovon' could refer to 'object' or to the whole representation; kept ambiguous."]],"philippi-s55-003":["One cannot arrive at the *principium materiale* of sensation. A sensation-representation cannot be invented: the matter is given to me through the senses. Experiences and sensations agree with one another according to matter. Both have been occasioned by the presence of the object; but in the former the form of reason is to be met with. In experience I am active, in sensation merely passive. A reflected sensation is experience; by that I understand a sensation expressed through a judgment. In sensations there are merely concepts and representations, but in experiences there are judgments. No animal has experience.",["Materie = matter; Sinnen = senses; Urtheil = judgment; Begriffe = concepts; Vernunft = reason.","'jenen' ('the former') is literally 'those'; its referent (Erfahrungen vs. Empfindungen) is left as in German. 'Kern' read as 'Kein'.","'erdichten' rendered 'invent'; 'leidend' = 'passive' (literally suffering)."]],"philippi-s55-004":["No experience is mediate; for that contradicts itself, but all are immediate. It is supposed, however, to signify a mediate experience: a judgment of reason insofar as it is concluded through experience.",["mittelbar = mediate; unmittelbar = immediate; widerspricht sich = contradicts itself (Widerspruch = contradiction).","'derVernunfft' read as 'der Vernunfft'. 'Es soll ... bedeuten': the subject 'es' is left unresolved, as in the German. 'geschlossen' = concluded (Schluß = inference/conclusion)."]],"philippi-s55-005":["The form of all judgments is rational; according to matter it can be empirical or rational.",[]],"philippi-s55-006":["All our cognitions arise from 3 sources",["Erkenntnisse = cognitions."]],"philippi-s55-007":["1. from reason *a priori*",[]],"philippi-s55-008":["2. from my experiences. 1",["The stray '1' after 'Erfahrungen.' is retained as printed; it may be an OCR remnant of a note mark (doubtful)."]],"philippi-s55-009":["3. from the experience of others. *A posteriori*—All proofs are rational. A merely empirical proof is not possible. For without reason one cannot conclude.",["'a Posterion' read as *a posteriori*. Beweis = proof; möglich = possible; geschlossen = concluded.","'anderer ihrer Erfahrung' is colloquial German ('of others their experience'), rendered literally as 'the experience of others'."]],"philippi-s55-010":["We must not confuse the inferences from the experiences with the inferences from the experiences. For there is nothing more captious than when one draws a secret inference, of which we are conscious only mediately, and believes that one fetches it immediately from experience. I infer and conclude more than is contained in my senses. When we make the judgment that the appearance gives agree with the judgment about the cause of the appearance, then we make out of a *judicium discursivum* an *intuitivum*. It often happens that one believes one has a *judicium intuitivum primitivum*, when one has only a *discursivum*.",["The German has 'Schlüsse aus den Erfahrungen' and 'Schlüsse von den Erfahrungen'; the English must use 'from' for both aus and von, so the distinction is lost (aus = out of, von = from/of). Marked here per the glossary principle.","'Ich Schlüsse und folgre': 'Ich' likely OCR/transcript slip for 'Ich schließe'; rendered 'I infer and conclude' (Schluß = inference; folgern = conclude).","'Wenn wir das Urtheil was die Erscheinung giebt mit dem Urtheil ... übereinstimmig machen': 'was' is relative pronoun; the sentence is kept as it stands and is awkward in German. 'emem' read as 'einem'; 'intuitiuum', 'primitmum' read as *intuitivum*, *primitivum*.","bewust = conscious (Bewußtsein = consciousness); Erscheinung = appearance."]],"philippi-s55-011":["Experience is at all times a concept. (*notio in sensu latiori*) The sense is the foundation of appearance; the understanding that of experience. No animal can have appearance or experience. It acts according to sensations. Experiences are judgments, hence universally valid, not sensations. A concept of experience can have something false in itself, if it arises from a false judgment; that is illusory experience. In experiences errors are easily committed. The greatest evidence lies not in empirical sciences; there errors are always possible, but in mathematics.",["Begrif = concept (Begriff); Sinn = sense (n.); Verstand = understanding; allgemeingültig = universally valid (gültig = valid); Wissenschafften = sciences.","Scheinerfahrung rendered 'illusory experience' (Schein = illusion).","'in sensu latiori' kept in Latin; 'Fundament' rendered 'foundation'.","The last sentence's construction ('sondern in der Mathematik') is kept as in the German, with the 'not ... but' framing interrupted by the clause 'da sind Fehler immer möglich'."]],"philippi-s55-012":["§ 204",["Section number as printed (the passage ids skip from 201 to 204)."]],"philippi-s55-013":["All proofs are rational according to form. For the consequens must be seen through reason. A proof is called one from reason, however, when the matter is borrowed from it. Meanwhile the first *principia* are nevertheless always empirical. By means of pure reason we can prove something only of the objects that are objects of the senses. In mathematics and metaphysics one proves through pure reason. What pure reason has demonstrated needs to be proved from no experience, or needs no confirmation from it.",["rein = pure; Object(e) = object(s); Gegenstand is not used here, so no doubling arises; Consequens kept in Latin form as in the source, in roman *consequens* in text for technical use.","'denObjecten' read as 'den Objecten'; 'Ob jecte' read as 'Objecte'; 'be weißt' read as 'beweißt'. 'aus keiner Erfahrung bewiesen zu werden' kept literal.","'von denObjecten was beweisen' left in the German word order's sense ('prove something of the objects')."]],"philippi-s55-014":["Experiences never prove entirely universal propositions but only the possibility of the propositions. They serve only to illustrate through concrete cases the abstractum proved through reason. Of actuality one can borrow no constitution from experience. In 's Gravesande's Logic proofs of both kinds are compared.",["Sätze = propositions; Möglichkeit = possibility; Wirklichkeit = actuality; Beschaffenheit = constitution; *Abstractum* kept in Latin.","'s'Gravesandes Logüc' read as 's Gravesande's Logic' (OCR 'Logüc' for 'Logic'). 'beiderley' = 'of both kinds'.","'Die dienen' — 'Die' (they) refers to experiences or propositions; left unresolved."]],"philippi-s55-015":["They serve only to illustrate through concrete cases the abstractum proved through reason.",["Repeats a sentence of the preceding passage as printed; 'Die' left unresolved."]],"philippi-s56-001":["§205",[]],"philippi-s56-002":["All proofs that go to actual objects of experience are carried out partly *a priori*, partly *a posteriori*.",["Gegenstände = objects (here Gegenstand; elsewhere Object, also 'object')."]],"philippi-s56-003":["The unification of reason with experience occurs either through coordination or subordination. Insofar as rules of reason are applied to experience, that is a subordination. Insofar as reason as well as experience, each taken in particular, is a ground of a particular certainty, that is coordination. In experimental physics one seeks this unification, e.g. that pendulums, provided they are not over 5 degrees, make large and small arcs in equal time, I first prove through reason. If reason demonstrates completely, then the test can always be borrowed from experience.",["Vereinbarung = unification (literally 'agreement'); Coordination/Subordination kept as cognates; Grund = ground; Gewißheit = certainty; besonder = particular; Grad = degree; Zeit = time.","'Die Vernunfft Regeln auf die Erfahrung appliciret werden' is ungrammatical as printed (likely 'die Vernunfft-Regeln' or a dropped word); translated as 'rules of reason', doubtful.","'wTerden' read as 'werden'. 'Perpendikel' = pendulums (plural); 'z. E.' = e.g.","'beweise ich erst durch Vernunfft' attached to the preceding 'daß'-clause as in the German."]],"philippi-s56-004":["Many insufficient proofs coordinated with one another never give an apodictic certainty. If many proofs of one thing are already given, they commonly are no good.",["'coordirdrte' read as 'coordinirte'; unzureichend = insufficient (zureichend = sufficient).","'taugen' = 'are no good' ('taugen nicht'), literal; 'Wenn schon' could be 'if already' or 'even if'; kept as 'if ... already'."]],"philippi-s57-001":["§206",[]],"philippi-s57-002":["To know means to believe with certainty but to cognize something to the degree that suffices for resolution and action. Belief is considered either",["Wißen = to know (Wissen); the passage uses the verb, 'know' rendered per glossary; erkennen = cognize; Glaube = belief (or faith); 'hinreicht' = suffices.","'Entschlüssung' = resolution. The sentence breaks off with 'entweder' as the list follows in the next passages."]],"philippi-s57-003":["1. as a belief in things, or",["Sachen = things (Sache, not Ding)."]],"philippi-s57-004":["2. as a belief in persons.",[]],"philippi-s57-005":["In the first case I believe something, in the other I believe someone.",["'glaube jemandem' takes the dative in German (believe a person's word); English 'believe someone' cannot mark this."]],"philippi-s57-006":["To take something immediately to be true to such a degree as is necessary for action is called believing. But to take something to be true on the assertion of others is called believing someone.",["für wahr halten = take to be true (Fürwahrhalten); Aussage = assertion (behaupten = assert, but Aussage is a different word); nöthig = necessary here for 'nöthig' (notwendig in glossary)."]],"philippi-s57-007":["In many indifferent things no personal belief is necessary. There need be no *character distinctionis*; every person is an equally great ground for certainty. But there are things of whose certainty one is convinced only through particular persons. They require such a degree of personal credibility as only a particular character of persons guarantees. E.g. when we are informed by persons of credible character about the divining rod or ghosts, then we begin to believe it.",["'Character distinctionis' kept in Latin; 'Charakter' rendered 'character'. überführt = convinced (Überzeugung = conviction). 'Gicbt' read as 'giebt'; 'ehi' as 'ein'.","Glaubwürdigkeit = credibility; Wünschelruthe = divining rod; 'benachrichtiget' = informed.","'es zu glauben': 'es' left as in the German."]],"philippi-s57-008":["A testimony is every taking-to-be-true for the sake of the assertion of another.",["Zeugniß = testimony; Vorwahrhalten rendered 'taking-to-be-true' (cf. Fürwahrhalten)."]],"philippi-s57-009":["Belief is either historical or moral. In historical belief something is taken to be true because it is given out by another as such. Moral belief, however, consists in confidence in the probity of the human being with regard to the future, what is to happen. Historical belief consists in the taking-to-be-true of what has happened. E.g. if someone relates his distinguished descent, then we express thereby a historical belief. But if someone promises something; then a moral one. One must be faithful in promising, true in relating.",["Zutrauen = confidence; Rechtschaffenheit = probity; 'Ansehung' = regard; 'was geschehen soll' is left as an appositive-like clause, literal.","Künfftigen = future (the future thing); 'treu' = faithful."]],"philippi-s57-010":["Historical belief also takes place with human beings who do not have the least honesty. Moral belief, however, only with the upright. But if probity is added to one who relates something, then our historical belief is by so much greater.",["'um so Adel grösser' is garbled in the OCR; 'Adel' is probably 'viel' ('so much'); translated 'by so much' (doubtful reading).","Redlichkeit = honesty; Rechtschaffenen = the upright; Rechtschaffenheit = probity."]],"philippi-s57-011":["Religious belief must be distinguished from theological. The former consists in the fidelity that one must keep in civil life. In conscientiousness, e.g., in giving credit. Among the Greeks no credit took place. Hence *graeca fide mercari* for to trade for something with cash.",["Treue = fidelity; 'Jener' = 'the former' (literally 'that one'); creditiren = giving credit (to grant credit).","'Religionsglaube' = religious belief; 'theologischen' = theological. 'baar Geld' = cash."]],"philippi-s57-012":["Theological belief, just like the historical, has no relation to fidelity; it is logical and goes to the present and past. It is of such a kind that one does good on belief and hope. Moral belief is the necessary bond of society. There is a moral belief in things, if I believe a future reward of good actions because it flows from the nature of them, not because another has said it.",["Verhältniß = relation; Gesellschafft = society; Hofnung = hope; Belohnung = reward; Handlungen = actions.","'es' in 'weil es ... fließt' and 'derselben' ('of them') left as in the German; 'auf Glauben und Hofnung' could be 'on the strength of' or 'out of'; kept 'on belief and hope'."]],"philippi-s57-013":["Natural religious belief is only among the virtuous.",["'bey Tugendhafften' = 'among/with the virtuous'."]],"philippi-s58-001":["On historical belief.",["Heading."]],"philippi-s58-002":["Since it goes to the past, experiences are its objects. All contemplative propositions, all judgments are no objects of it. Whoever believes the judgments of others makes dogmatic cognition into historical. *Dogmata* can permit no other ground of assent than one's own experience and one's own judgment. For a witness both logical and moral properties are required:",["Object/Gegenstand: both 'Objekte' and 'Gegenstände' are rendered 'object(s)'; here 'Objekte' is the German behind 'objects' in the first sentence, 'Gegenstände' in the second.","'Erkennt niß' (line break) silently corrected to 'Erkenntniß' = cognition. 'Sätze' = propositions; 'Urtheile' = judgments; 'Eigenschafften' = properties (not in glossary; 'Beschaffenheit' is 'constitution; property').","'Dogmata' kept in Latin form as in the source, italicized. 'Beyfall' = assent (not in glossary).","'Da er aufs Vergangene geht': the subject 'er' is left unresolved, as in the German (the antecedent lies in the preceding text, not given)."]],"philippi-s58-003":["1. He must have sufficient cognition to make experiences",["'Erfahrungen anzustellen' rendered literally as 'to make experiences' (cf. 'to institute'); 'Erkenntniß' = cognition."]],"philippi-s58-004":["2. He must have retained well what was experienced.",["'das Erfahrne' = 'what was experienced' (substantivized participle)."]],"philippi-s58-005":["3. He must have the faculty of expressing himself intelligibly through words. He must",["'Vermögen' = faculty; 'verständlich' = intelligibly. The sentence breaks off and continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s58-006":["4. have a good will to say the truth and no benefit to say the falsehood.",["Construction preserved: 'einen guten Willen haben die Wahrheit und keinen Nutzen haben die Falschheit zu sagen' is elliptical/ambiguous; 'keinen Nutzen haben' may be read as 'have no benefit (in) saying the falsehood' as well as a second object of 'haben'. The English keeps the parallel."]],"philippi-s58-007":["A statement about a *dogma* is no testimony but about experience. A witness is therefore one who states an experience; the state of the witness must also be investigated a. logically, whether he has been in circumstances to cognize the truth b. practically; whether he has not been determined to his testimony through distresses.",["'Außage' (OCR) read as 'Aussage' = statement. 'Zeugniß' = testimony; 'Zustand' = state; 'erkennen' = cognize; 'bestimmet' = determined.","'ist kein Zeugniß sondern auf Erfahrung' is awkward in the German and kept awkward: the sense of 'sondern auf Erfahrung' (a statement 'on' experience) is left open.","'Bedrängnisse' = distresses (not in glossary)."]],"philippi-s58-008":["The greatest part of our historical cognition arises from belief. It is a great aid to extend our cognition that we can adopt foreign experience. In historical belief one presupposes an inclination to truth that arises from love of honor. Since now learned people and people of standing look more to honor than the common man, who can promise himself little influence from it on his interest; so one also presumes in the former more credibility than in the latter.",["'Glauben' / 'Glaubwürdigkeit' = belief / credibility. 'Hülfsmittel' = aid (compound of Mittel, not rendered 'means'). 'Ehrliebe' = love of honor; 'Ehre' = honor.","'gemeine Mann' = 'common man' (gemein = common). 'jenen' / 'diesen' rendered 'the former' / 'the latter', the German 'those'/'these' being kept in order."]],"philippi-s59-001":["§208",[]],"philippi-s59-002":["An eyewitness is one who has his own experience, a witness by hearsay, however, one who has the testimony of another. This one has only a mediate credibility.",["'Hörenzeuge' = 'witness by hearsay' (literally 'hearing-witness'). 'mittelbar' = mediate."]],"philippi-s59-003":["In the series of subordinated witnesses historical credibility decreases; in the series of coordinated witnesses it increases. The series of coordinated testimonies is called public rumor, that of subordinated testimonies, however, an oral tradition. If in the coordinated testimonies the eyewitness is unknown; then it is called a common saying.",["'subordinirten' / 'coordinirten' = subordinated / coordinated. 'Gerüchte' = rumor; 'Ueberlieferung' = tradition; 'Sage' = saying; 'gemeine' = common."]],"philippi-s60-001":["§213",[]],"philippi-s60-002":["Moral unbelief signifies the lack of all trust in the moral constitution of the other. Credulity consists in an inclination to give assent, on logical grounds, to the credibility of a witness. Inexperience is its source.",["'Beschaffenheit' = constitution. 'Unglaube' = unbelief; 'Leichtgläubigkeit' = credulity; 'Zutrauen' = trust. 'Qvelle' = 'Quelle', source.","'aus logischen Gründen der Glaubwürdigkeit eines Zeugen Beyfall zu geben' is ambiguous: 'aus logischen Gründen' may attach to 'Neigung' or to 'Beyfall zu geben'; the English keeps the word order's ambiguity as far as possible."]],"philippi-s60-003":["Logical unbelief does not lie in the depravity of the heart like the moral; but in inexperience in literature.",["'Pravität' = depravity (Latin-derived in the German, one English word). 'Litteratur' = literature."]],"philippi-s61-001":["On practical learned cognition. §216",["'gelehrt' = learned; 'Erkenntniß' = cognition."]],"philippi-s61-002":["A cognition is called practical that has a relation to the movement of our will. All practical cognition prescribes either ends or teaches only the means to arrive at arbitrary ends. All doctrines of prudence and skill go only to the means; morality to the ends.",["'behebigen' read as 'beliebigen' (OCR) = arbitrary (lit. 'any whatever'). 'Zwecke' = ends; 'Mittel' = means; 'Lehren' = doctrines; 'Klugheit' = prudence; 'Geschicklichkeit' = skill; 'Sittlichkeit' = morality."]],"philippi-s61-003":["The author speaks of the relation to the free will. The whole section does not belong to logic; but practical perfection belongs to morals.",["'che' read as 'die' (OCR). 'Vollkommenheit' = perfection. 'Moral' rendered 'morals'. 'Autor' = author (i.e. Meier, as the German leaves it)."]],"philippi-s61-004":["All cognition is either theoretical or practical. The practical can be either merely a speculation about practice or actual guidance. Practical logic is a speculation about practice.",["'Praxi' (Latin dative/ablative form) rendered 'practice'; 'Praxis' also 'practice'. 'Anleitung' = guidance."]],"philippi-s62-001":["On learned concepts.",["'Begriffen' = concepts."]],"philippi-s62-002":["A representation through senses is called sensation, through the understanding appearance, through reason 1. a concept. A concept is a general representation; representations that are not general are no concepts. The sensations, however generally they may be taken, remain single representations. A single representation is intuition. 2. For a concept it is required that with the general representation I at the same time have insight into its relation in the series of subordination with other representations. Reason subordinates. All appearances are only coordinations.",["'aUgemeine' read as 'allgemeine' = general (universal); the same word 'allgemein' is rendered 'general' throughout. 'einzele' / 'einzelne' = single.","'Sinne' = senses; 'Empfindung' = sensation; 'Erscheinung' = appearance; 'Anschauung' = intuition; 'Vor-Stellung' (line break) read 'Vorstellung'.","The text as printed assigns 'Erscheinung' to the understanding and 'Empfindung' to the senses; kept as it stands. 'einsehe' = have insight into."]],"philippi-s62-003":["The matter of all our cognition lies in the senses. The degrees to which we ascend are",["'Materie' = matter; 'Grade' = degrees. 'hegt' read as 'liegt' (OCR)."]],"philippi-s62-004":["1. *repraesentatio*, representation in general.",[]],"philippi-s62-005":["2. *conceptus*, general concepts of the understanding.",["'Verstandesbegriffe' = concepts of the understanding."]],"philippi-s62-006":["3. *ideae*, general concepts of reason. *Repraesentatio eius quod phaenomenis pluribus est commune, est conceptus empiricus.*",["'idcae' read as 'ideae' (OCR). The Latin sentence is left in Latin as the author wrote it (no translation inserted)."]],"philippi-s63-001":["§250",[]],"philippi-s63-002":["Common concepts are better than learned ones. A concept is a result of many representations that I take together and consider as one.",["'Gemeine' = common; 'gelehrte' = learned; 'betrachte' = consider (cf. Betrachtung = consideration)."]],"philippi-s63-003":["An aesthetic concept arises through the multiplicity of coordinated representations. The matter is the relation of the marks to feeling and taste; its form is the coordination. A concept of reason arises from the subordination of the representations. Its matter is the laws of reason itself; the form the subordination. A learned concept is not one that contains much learnedness, but one to which the learned attend very much.",["'Begrif' (single f) = 'Begriff', concept. 'Menge' = multiplicity; 'Merkmale' = marks; 'Gefühl' = feeling; 'Gesetze' = laws.","'Vernunfft begriff' (broken by OCR/space) read as 'Vernunftbegriff'. 'Gelehrsamkeit' = learnedness."]],"philippi-s64-001":["§254",[]],"philippi-s64-002":["The author believes that we arrive at concepts through abstraction. But through abstraction we come to no cognition; the cognition must already be there before the abstraction. One changes only the form through abstraction.",["'Erkennt niß' silently corrected. 'Abstraction' / 'Abstraktion' both 'abstraction'."]],"philippi-s64-003":["All concepts are for us either",[]],"philippi-s64-004":["1. given, or",[]],"philippi-s64-005":["2. the understanding has made them itself through combination. Those are fictitious concepts. The given ones are either given through experience *a posteriori*, or also *a priori* through pure reason.",["'Verbindung' = combination; 'erdichtete' = fictitious (not in glossary). 'a posteriori'/'a priori' italicized as Latin. The adverbial 'a priori' is kept in the same position as the German."]],"philippi-s64-006":["Therefore all concepts are to be divided into *conceptus*",["'einzutheilen' = to be divided (Einteilung = division)."]],"philippi-s64-007":["1. *abstractos* and *rationales*. *Conceptus abstractus* is e.g. the concept of the genus (*genus*). The matter arises through experience, the form of generality, however, through abstraction. The concept does not arise through abstraction, but is made general only through it. Accordingly abstraction is not a second way, different from experience, to arrive at concepts; for it is only the means to make experiences general.",["'Gattung' = genus, with the author's Latin '(genus)' kept. 'Allgemeinheit' = generality; 'Weg' = way. 'z. E.' rendered 'e.g.'","'Mittel' = means; 'Erfahrungen' = experiences."]],"philippi-s64-008":["2. We also have pure concepts of reason, which are given *a priori*. Such concepts are the object of pure philosophy, i.e. of metaphysics and morals. These are concepts that do not arise through what falls into the senses, that are not fictitious and cannot be drawn off from the representations through the senses. Where then do we have them from? The understanding has acquired them for itself, in that it attended to its own procedure on the occasion of experiences.",["'Gegenstand' = object; 'Philosophie' (not 'Weltweisheit') = philosophy. 'Moral' rendered 'morals'. 'abgezogen' = drawn off; 'Verfahren' = procedure; 'bey Gelegenheit' = on the occasion."]],"philippi-s64-009":["A *vitium subreptionis* it is when one passes off for an experience from the senses what is yet a concept of reason; as e.g. Hutcheson and Shaftesbury wanted to take the concept of virtue for a concept of experience.",["'Welten' read as 'wollten' (OCR; doubtful, the printed letters are garbled). 'Vitium subreptionis' kept in Latin. 'Erfahrungsbegrif' = concept of experience."]],"philippi-s64-010":["Pure concepts of the understanding therefore express the laws according to which the understanding proceeds. And since every object is nothing other than a something that the understanding posits with certain determinations on a sensation; it follows that everything that the understanding and reason cognize through concepts is objective, but the sensations something subjective.",["'Be-Stimmungen' read 'Bestimmungen' = determinations. 'Object' and 'obiectiv' = object / objective; 'Subiectives' = something subjective. 'setzt' = posits.","'ein etwas was' is kept as 'a something that'."]],"philippi-s64-011":["Whoever has experiences does not on that account yet have concepts of experience. To attain them one must pay attention to many objects, to their agreement, and distinguish the *notas primarias*, that is the essential, from the *notis secundasiis* or the accidental. A philosophical mind belongs to it.",["'secundasiis' is as printed (OCR/MS), presumably for 'secundariis'; left as it stands. 'Gegenstände' = objects; 'Geist' = mind. 'Es gehört ... dazu' rendered literally."]],"philippi-s65-001":["§ 259",[]],"philippi-s65-002":["In logical separation we compare many concepts with one another and see what they have in common. If many concepts have the same mark; then I make the mark into the concept wherein then all these concepts are contained.",["'Absonderung' = separation (cf. 'absondernde' below); 'Merkmal' = mark. 'gemeinschaftliches' = 'in common' (not 'gemein'). 'als denn' = 'then'."]],"philippi-s65-003":["Abstraction is an action of subordination. I subordinate concepts under one.",["'Handlung' = action."]],"philippi-s65-004":["Abstraction can take place in the case of the pure concepts of reason. But because these are not taken from the objects but from the activity of pure reason; so one also does not call them abstract, separated concepts. Professor Kant calls them in his disputation separating abstracting concepts.",["'Herr Professor Kant' rendered 'Professor Kant' (the 'Herr' omitted; literal would be 'Herr Professor Kant'). 'abstrakte, abgesonderte' = abstract, separated; 'absondernde abstrahirende' = separating abstracting. 'Thätigkeit' = activity."]],"philippi-s65-005":["In experience abstraction takes place most. Hence the general concepts of experience are also properly called abstracted. All abstract concepts are general, but not all general ones abstract.",["'Erfahrungsbegriffe' = concepts of experience; 'abstrahirte' = abstracted."]],"philippi-s66-001":["§ 260",[]],"philippi-s66-002":["All fictions arise either through arbitrary combination of that which is separated in experience, or through arbitrary separation of that which is combined in experience.",["'Erdichtungen' = fictions; 'willkürlich' = arbitrary; 'Verbindung' = combination; 'Trennung' = separation (distinct from 'Absonderung' above, also rendered 'separation'; the German word differs)."]],"philippi-s66-003":["A concept is called a mark if it belongs as a part to the whole concept. But the mark is also, in turn, a ground of cognition or a general concept under which belong all the concepts in which it is contained as a part.",["Merkmal = mark; Theil = part; Erkenntnißgrund = ground of cognition; allgemein rendered 'general' (not in the glossary; kept distinct from gemein = common).","OCR 'Erkennt -nißgrund' silently corrected."]],"philippi-s66-004":["A mark can thus be considered in a twofold *respectu* ... It is",["The ellipsis and the breaking off after 'Es ist' are as in the source; the next passage continues."]],"philippi-s66-005":["1. a general concept under which belong all the concepts whose mark it is. That is *respectu subordinationis*",[]],"philippi-s66-006":["2. it is a part of each of these concepts *respectu coordinationis*.",[]],"philippi-s66-007":["The multitude of things that is contained under a concept as a *nota communi* makes up the *sphaera* of the *conceptus communis*. The more things, then, a *conceptus* is common to, the greater is its sphere.",["Menge = multitude; Sphäre/Sphaera = sphere; Merkmal's Latin equivalent is *nota*.","'als einer nota communi' is kept in the Latin as printed (dative of the German construction, with the ablative-looking ending left as is)."]],"philippi-s66-008":["The more a concept contains under itself, the more things it is common to, the less it contains in itself; i.e. the smaller a part it is of each concept that it holds under itself.",["'faßt' (unter sich faßt) rendered 'holds' so as to reserve 'comprehend' for begreifen."]],"philippi-s66-009":["Just as the marks in coordination agree together to make up the general concept; so they also stand among one another in subordination. For a concept has marks, these in turn have marks of themselves, and so on onward *in serie subordinationis*. The more sensible the concepts are, the greater there is the coordination of the marks; the more they contain in themselves, but the less under themselves. The more the concepts are from reason, the more they contain under themselves, but the less in themselves.",["OCR 'wemger' read as 'weniger'; 'Vernunf f t' read as 'Vernunft'.","sinnlich = sensible; Vernunft = reason."]],"philippi-s66-010":["Euler sought to make that sensible through figures. But it is worse that only the coordination but not the subordination can be shown in figures. For the senses, or rather the understanding, can only coordinate; reason subordinates.",["'sinnlich zu machen' = 'to make sensible'.","OCR 'Vernunfft' read as 'Vernunft'."]],"philippi-s66-011":["A *Conceptus* is called *superior* insofar as it contains another concept under itself, *inferior* insofar as it is contained under one. What in one relation is *conceptus superior* can in another become *conceptus inferior* and conversely.",["Verhältniß = relation."]],"philippi-s66-012":["The higher a concept is, i.e. the more remote in the series of subordinates, the greater its sphere. Until the absolutely higher concept, i.e. the highest, is of the greatest sphere and contains all objects under itself",["Latin translated; *conceptus*, *sphaera* kept as terms where they occur in other passages; here rendered 'concept', 'sphere', 'object' (obiecta).","Passage ends without a full stop in the source."]],"philippi-s66-013":["The smaller a concept is, the smaller its sphere, until the lowest concept is of no sphere, i.e. singular.",["Latin *singularis* rendered 'singular'; the German einzelne in s66-015 is rendered 'individual'."]],"philippi-s66-014":["The *Conceptus* whose sphere is only a part of another sphere is *angustior*.",["*angustior* (narrower) left in Latin."]],"philippi-s66-015":["Thus the highest concept, that is that of an object in general, and the lowest, i.e. the individual, are the boundaries of subordination.",["Gegenstand = object; Grenze = boundary; einzelne = individual."]],"philippi-s66-016":["Each *conceptum communem*, when it is considered as contained in a lower *nota*, I weigh *in concreto*. E.g. When I speak of education in general, and then go on to the education of princes; so I consider the concept of education *in concreto*.",["OCR 'coneeptum' read as 'conceptum'.","erwägen rendered 'weigh', betrachten 'consider', to keep the two German words apart.","'inferiori' in the source is rendered 'lower'."]],"philippi-s66-017":["Each *conceptus communis* is called *species* with regard to its *superior*; *genus* with regard to its *inferior*. There is thus a *genus summum* and a *species infima* as the boundaries of subordination.",["OCR 'suinmum' read as 'summum'.","Latin *species* and *genus* kept; Gattung = genus, Art = species/kind."]],"philippi-s66-018":["This is the logical subordination, where I represent to myself a concept as contained under a general one. The real subordination, however, is the subordination of the sequel to the ground, when through the ground everything that is contained in the sequel is posited. Early in youth we already subordinate concepts logically; but to subordinate really, for that we attain the faculty only little by little.",["Folge = sequel; Grund = ground; gesetzt = posited; Vermögen = faculty.","'dazu erlangen wir nur nach und nach das Vermögen' kept in the German order as far as possible."]],"philippi-s66-019":["Each *conceptum communem*, when it is considered as contained under the *nota superiori*, I weigh *in abstracto*. The abstract I judge of many things little, *in concreto* of few things much.",["OCR 'conceptum communem' (accusative) kept as printed; erwägen = 'weigh', as in s66-016."]],"philippi-s66-020":["Through the understanding I cognize little in things but distinctly. Through the senses much but confusedly.",["erkennen = cognize; deutlich = distinct; verworren = confused."]],"philippi-s66-021":["A distinct cognition through insufficient marks is called a description. This contains such marks as suffice to distinguish the thing from many, the definition, however, from all things.",["unzureichend = insufficient; hinreichen = suffice (zureichend = sufficient)."]],"philippi-s67-001":["On explanations.",["Erklärung rendered 'explanation' throughout (glossary also allows 'definition'); Definition is rendered 'definition'."]],"philippi-s67-002":["To give the explanation of explanation is not as difficult as it seems.",[]],"philippi-s67-003":["The logical perfections ascend thus",["Vollkommenheit = perfection. The passage is a lead-in to the numbered items that follow."]],"philippi-s67-004":["1. Clarity, if I am conscious of a representation",["Klarheit = clarity; bewust = conscious; Vorstellung = representation."]],"philippi-s67-005":["2. Distinctness, if I am conscious of the marks of the representation",["The sentence is split across passages 005/006; the verb 'bin' stands alone in 006."]],"philippi-s67-006":["am.",["'bin.' is the finite verb completing the previous passage; left as a fragment."]],"philippi-s67-007":["3. If I represent to myself clearly all the marks that only belong to it.",["'nur dazu gehören' is ambiguous (only those that belong / that belong only); kept literal as 'only belong to it'."]],"philippi-s67-008":["*Completudo*. 4. *Praecisio*. If I reduce the marks *ad minores terminos*.",["OCR 'terniinos' read as 'terminos'; 'reducire' read as 'reduciere/reduce' (German verb form kept as 'reduce').","'Completudo' stands before the numeral 4 in the source, apparently belonging to item 3."]],"philippi-s67-009":["A distinct concept is called complete if the marks taken together are equal to the whole concept. I can cognize marks of the marks, and indeed *in indefinitum* ever onward. Thus the intensity of distinctness has many degrees.",["OCR 'intensiuitaet' read as 'Intensität'; 'Grade' = degrees; Deutlichkeit = distinctness."]],"philippi-s67-010":["An adequate concept is definition.",["No article before 'Definition' in the German; kept."]],"philippi-s67-011":["The matter of the definition is elementary propositions; that is first marks and representations that I bring out through judgments. The form is the *completudo* of the marks, and their *praecisio*, that the concept be adequate.",["'elementarsätze' (OCR spacing) read as Elementarsätze; Satz = proposition; Urteil = judgment; 'sey' subjunctive rendered 'be'."]],"philippi-s67-012":["There is a difference between this: to make a concept distinct and this: to make a distinct concept.",[]],"philippi-s67-013":["If a concept is given to me according to its matter or according to the marks and I seek *per analysin* the marks clear, then I make a given concept distinct. But if I bring forth a concept together with its distinctness, then I make for myself a distinct concept.",["'suche ... die Merkmale klar' kept literal ('seek the marks clear')."]],"philippi-s67-014":["The definitions are either nominal, if the concept sufficiently expresses everything that I cognize of the thing; or real, if the concept suffices to derive therefrom everything that pertains to the thing itself (the object).",["zureichend = sufficient; Object = object (Gegenstand would also be 'object')."]],"philippi-s67-015":["The former are easier; for there I can soon find all the marks that taken together exhaust my whole concept, but it is difficult to indicate the marks from which all determinations that pertain to the thing itself are to be derived. The nominal definition is also called the logical essence, i.e. the first basic concept of all that I cognize in the thing. The real definition, however, must contain everything that is only possible in the thing itself. I am to indicate not only all marks that make up my concept, but all marks that one will ever obtain of it. I am to define the thing, not the name.",["OCR 'erstem' read as 'ersten'; 'defmiren' read as 'definiren'.","Wesen = essence; Bestimmung = determination; 'nur möglich' kept literal ('only possible'), ambiguous in the German as well."]],"philippi-s67-016":["Many definitions can be used as true propositions, although not as definitions.",[]],"philippi-s67-017":["If a proposition is therefore false even as a definition, it can nevertheless be true as a judgment. The definition gives a sufficiency for every aim.",["Absicht = aim; Sufficienz = sufficiency."]],"philippi-s67-018":["If the mark is not a ground of the distinctness of the subject, then the concept that I attain thereby is indistinct.",["OCR 'Merkmahl' and 'Subiects' read as 'Merkmal' and 'Subjects'; undeutlich = indistinct."]],"philippi-s67-019":["The marks in a definition must not be subordinated but coordinated. Otherwise it is called *Conceptus indeterminatus, vagus*.",[]],"philippi-s67-020":["*Conceptus rem adaequat*, that is the definition has in itself neither more nor fewer marks than pertain to the concept.",["The Latin clause (the concept is adequate to the thing) is left in Latin as printed, since it is quoted as a formula."]],"philippi-s67-021":["1.) All definitions of empirical concepts are nominal. Real ones are not possible. For these are supposed to indicate all determinations and marks. But of things of nature I know only very few of them.",[]],"philippi-s67-022":["The more concrete a concept of experience is, the less it is to be defined. If it is wholly abstract, e.g. body, then it can be done.",["'so gehts an' rendered 'then it can be done'.","Erfahrungsbegriff = concept of experience."]],"philippi-s67-023":["The *completudo* of the marks is lacking to me in objects of the senses. Ever more marks are discovered daily. Hence it is useless in physics when one finds definitions.",["'in Physiken' (plural) rendered 'in physics'; Gegenstände = objects."]],"philippi-s67-024":["Empirical definitions serve only to fix the meaning of the word; they are grammatical, not logical.",["Wortbedeutung = meaning of the word."]],"philippi-s67-025":["II. In the case of intellectual concepts real definitions are possible because my concept contains everything that the thing is. My concept makes up the thing.",["'intellectualen' rendered 'intellectual'."]],"philippi-s67-026":["It is also necessary to define intellectual concepts, because through my concept the thing is given.",[]],"philippi-s67-027":["How do I now make definitions of intellectual concepts? I make them *per analysin*, and indeed thus, that I unwrap and unfold that out of whose composition the understanding has formed the concept for itself, that is, the elementary propositions. For at the ground of every intellectual concept lie elementary propositions, out of which the understanding has made it for itself. E.g. body is a concept of reason; it is that which is common to all sensations, if I analyze further, I find that the concept of body is composed of these elementary propositions:",["OCR \"Hegen\" and \"Eiementarsätze\" silently corrected to \"liegen\" and \"Elementarsätze\"; \"Vernunff tbegrif\" read as \"Vernunftbegriff\".","Begriff = concept; Verstand = understanding; Empfindung = sensation; Zusammensetzung = composition.","\"es ist das was allen Empfindungen gemein ist, analysire ich weiter\" is punctuated with commas only in the source; the run-on is kept."]],"philippi-s67-028":["1. of extension",["Ausdehnung = extension."]],"philippi-s67-029":["2. of impenetrability.",[]],"philippi-s67-030":["3. of inertia.",[]],"philippi-s67-031":["4. of shape. I thus get the definition of body. In mathematics all things are likewise given through the concepts, hence one can also give real definitions there, and indeed most easily there. He says, we want to think a figure that has 6 equal sides and call it hexagon; well then, so it is a hexagon.",["\"Realdefinitionen\" = real definitions. \"Sechseck\" rendered \"hexagon\".","\"Er sagt\": the subject \"he\" is left unresolved, as in the German."]],"philippi-s67-032":["There I make distinct concepts arbitrarily through definitions; but in philosophy I make given concepts distinct through the definitions.",["deutlich = distinct; willkürlich = arbitrary."]],"philippi-s67-033":["If I want to make definitions, then I must",["The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s67-034":["1. seek true propositions",["\"Säzze\" = Sätze; Satz = proposition."]],"philippi-s67-035":["2. all marks that lie in the concept I pick out and make clear.",["Merkmal = mark."]],"philippi-s67-036":["With anemic definitions, as in pure reason and mathematics, the confused concept goes before.",["\"anämischen\" is as read in the OCR and is doubtful; it may be a misreading of another adjective. It is translated as it stands.","verworren = confused."]],"philippi-s67-037":["*Completudo* and *praecisio* thus constitute an adequate concept, and not as with Wolff, if only the marks of a concept are again distinct.",["\"Praecision\" is given in Latin form in the source; rendered *praecisio* here, kept as Latin per the instructions. The source word is \"Praecision\".","\"wieder deutlich seyn\": \"again\" is placed as in the German."]],"philippi-s67-038":["For a definition it is required that all marks be given. But if I have only some marks of a concept, can I not infer from them already everything that follows from it, without my having all the other marks together? E.g. I know of body only that it is divisible, can I not infer with certainty from this that it is composed, although I do not know impenetrability, extension etc. etc., which are also marks of body?",["\"Schlüssen\" is the verb schließen (infer); Schluß = inference.","\"Merkmale\" = marks."]],"philippi-s67-039":["The 4 actions are necessary for the examination and making of a definition.",["Handlung = action."]],"philippi-s67-040":["1. whether the definition is true as a proposition",["OCR \"l.\" read as \"1.\""]],"philippi-s67-041":["2. whether it is distinct as a concept",[]],"philippi-s67-042":["3. whether as a distinct concept it is extensive, complete, and whether",["\"ausführlich\" rendered \"extensive\"; it is not in the glossary.","The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s67-043":["the marks are true?",[]],"philippi-s67-044":["4. whether as an extensive concept it is precise; whether not some",["\"praecis\" rendered \"precise\"."]],"philippi-s67-045":["marks are one and the same.",["\"einerley\" = one and the same."]],"philippi-s67-046":["All arbitrary concepts must first be defined. Without definitions going before I can say nothing. But with given concepts there definitions are often not even necessary, let alone that they should go before.",["notwendig/nöthig = necessary."]],"philippi-s67-047":["The origin of arbitrary propositions is synthesis. From analysis no concept can arise.",["OCR \"x4us\" corrected to \"Aus\". \"Synthesis\" and \"Analysi\" are in Latin/Greek form in the source."]],"philippi-s67-048":["All definitions should serve to get to know the object better through the dissection of the same.",["\"kennen lernen\" = get to know (kennen = know). \"derselben\" refers ambiguously, as in the German; Zergliederung = dissection."]],"philippi-s67-049":["From this one sees that one can already use propositions before the definition, and that the definitions are not always necessary. They are so only then, when I use all the marks.",["OCR \"smd\" corrected to \"sind\". \"brauchen\" is rendered \"use\" throughout."]],"philippi-s67-050":["I can use the elementary propositions before all definitions; and they must also go before.",[]],"philippi-s67-051":["For in philosophy, where the concepts are defined *per analysin*, the definitions are not the first but the last.",["Philosophie = philosophy."]],"philippi-s67-052":["In mathematics, where the explanation creates the object, there they must go first.",["Erklärung = explanation here, though context treats it as definition; Objekt = object (Latinate form of Gegenstand)."]],"philippi-s67-053":["Definitions should be means of distinctness.",["Mittel = means; Deutlichkeit = distinctness."]],"philippi-s67-054":["Distinctness is obtained only through judgments.",["Urtheil = judgment."]],"philippi-s67-055":["Every definition is to be regarded as a judgment; where this *definitum* makes up the subject, the *definitio* the predicate. If the predicate is one and the same with the subject, explicitly or implicitly, then the definition is tautological and no means of distinctness.",["\"explicite\" and \"implicite\" are given in Latin form in the source."]],"philippi-s67-056":["An explanation is *latior suo definito* if it does not suffice for the cognition of the difference; *angustior suo definito* if it does not suffice for the cognition of sameness. In the first case it has too many marks, in the second too few. Witlings make definitions of the first kind, brooders make them of the second kind.",["OCR \"Defimtionen\" corrected. \"zureicht\" = suffices (zureichend = sufficient). \"Einerleyheit\" = sameness. \"Witzlinge\" = witlings, \"Grübler\" = brooders; not in glossary.","\"im erstem\" is as in the source (for \"im ersten\")."]],"philippi-s67-057":["If definitions are only true as propositions, if they then also do not have all marks, they can nevertheless often have as many as suffice to distinguish the thing from all that I know or only to give a fair degree of distinctness; and then they are already very usable and one must not reject them on account of the fault of the *completudo*. Such for the most part are Wolffian definitions.",["\"Säzze\" = Sätze. \"Completudinis\" is a Latin genitive in the source; here rendered as *completudo* in the nominative.","\"Wenn ... wenn\" construction is kept as in the German."]],"philippi-s67-058":["The *definitiones* of arbitrary concepts, as in mathematics, can never err; for the *definitum* is there nothing other than what I have fixed through the definition: but those of the philosopher are constant. He must, if he can infer from the preliminary or elementary propositions as much as he uses, rather omit defining and use it only in an emergency.",["\"beständig\" is rendered \"constant\"; the sense (perhaps a misreading in the transcript) is not settled and the reading is doubtful.","\"Schlüssen\" = verb schließen (infer). \"brauchen\" = use. \"Preliminair\" kept as \"preliminary\"."]],"philippi-s67-059":["No object of experience can be explained.",["Gegenstand = object; erklären = explain."]],"philippi-s67-060":["With all arbitrary concepts the definition must make the beginning.",[]],"philippi-s67-061":["§ 280",[]],"philippi-s67-062":["Verbal explanation is the distinct representation of the meaning of the word. Considered at bottom they are not means of getting a distinct concept: they give me only better known synonymous words.",["OCR \"Worterklarung\" read as \"Worterklärung\". \"im Grunde betrachtet\": Grund = ground, here idiomatic \"at bottom\". Bedeutung = meaning; Vorstellung = representation."]],"philippi-s67-063":["Real explanations are supposed to have a twofold use,",["Gebrauch = use. The sentence continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s67-064":["1. an external one, to distinguish the thing from all and 2. an internal one, to derive all determinations of the thing.",["Bestimmung = determination."]],"philippi-s67-065":["All definitions are real, for nominal definitions are properly no *definitiones*. But they are nevertheless of uncommon use in rational cognitions so that I make the sense of a concept of reason distinct to myself. But these concepts also permit real explanations, which contain everything that constantly pertains to the thing, as in morals and metaphysics. In short intellectual concepts can be explained *nominaliter* and *realiter*, concepts of experience *nominaliter* alone.",["OCR \"Vernunff tbegrifs\" and \"nominalitcr\" silently corrected. The last sentence has no comma after \"realiter\" in the source.","Sinn = sense; Erkenntnis = cognition."]],"philippi-s67-066":["A mathematician who defines a given concept errs greatly, for he then philosophizes. But the mathematician must not explain through analysis nor the philosopher through synthesis. Through the definition the concept is first given to me in mathematics.",["OCR \"Matheniatikus\" corrected; \"Mathematic\" is as in the source. \"Analysin\" and \"Synthesin\" are in Latin/Greek accusative form."]],"philippi-s67-067":["If I judge something about an arbitrary concept, then I must first fix it. If they are cognitions of the understanding, then analysis must explain it.",["urtheilen = judge (v.); Verstandeserkenntnisse = cognitions of the understanding."]],"philippi-s67-068":["Analytic definitions never make the beginning. The synthetic ones must always make the beginning.",["\"müßen\" as in the source."]],"philippi-s67-069":["Description is a comparative definition. All objects of experience permit no definition, but only description. If the definition is objectively complete, then it is a real definition, subjectively complete, a nominal definition.",["\"obiectiv\" and \"subiectiv\" rendered objectively and subjectively. \"real Definition\" is as in the source."]],"philippi-s67-070":["§ 284",[]],"philippi-s67-071":["Definitions must not be tautological according to form, although they can be identical according to matter.",["OCR \"nacht avtologisch\" read as \"nach tautologisch\". Form = form; Materie = matter."]],"philippi-s67-072":["The mania for defining arises from the inclination to have everything complete and from the mania for demonstrating, which finds progress best thereby, and from imitation of mathematics.",["Definirsucht and Demonstrirsucht: Sucht rendered \"mania\". \"alle\" is as in the source (\"everything\" is the nearest sense; the object is not specified). \"Fortgang\" = progress."]],"philippi-s67-073":["Through the mania for defining that Wolff introduced, one withdrew from the analyzing of concepts and unlearned the true philosophy.",["OCR \"Defhürsucht\" read as \"Definirsucht\" and \"Avahre\" as \"wahre\"; both uncertain but evident."]],"philippi-s68-001":["Division § 285",["Eintheilung = division; Teilung is also \"division\" in the glossary."]],"philippi-s68-002":["Every concept of the understanding is general. Every general concept has many under it and this makes up its *sphaeram*.",["Verstandesbegriff = concept of the understanding. \"Sphäram\" is a Latin accusative in the source."]],"philippi-s68-003":["Logical division is not the division of the concept but the division of the sphere of the concept. The general concept, which has much under it, has less in it than each of the things that it has under it.",["Eintheilung and Theilung are both \"division\" here."]],"philippi-s68-004":["For logical division is required",["The sentence continues in the next passages."]],"philippi-s68-005":["1. the disjunction; the opposition between the members, I must be able to say either, or.",["\"Disiunction\" rendered \"disjunction\"."]],"philippi-s68-006":["2. All members of the division taken together *disiunctive* must be *reciproque* to the *conceptui* and make up an equal *sphaera* with it. Guard against combining members of a subdivision with the members of the division.",["\"conceptui reciproque\" is Latin: *reciproque* = reciprocal, *conceptui* dative of the concept; the English word order follows the German, with the Latin left as it stands. \"disiunctiue\" = *disjunctive*.","Verbindung = combination."]],"philippi-s68-007":["Divisions serve in the sciences for retaining.",["Wissenschaft = science."]],"philippi-s69-001":["On learned judgments.",["A heading."]],"philippi-s69-002":["When I judge, I compare a concept with others and investigate whether they belong to it or are opposed to it, i.e. whether they are marks of it or not. Only through this action, through judging, do concepts become distinct. A distinct concept is nothing other than a judgment.",["Urtheil/urtheilen = judgment/judge; Begrif(f) = concept; Merkmal = mark; deutlich = distinct."]],"philippi-s69-003":["If the mark is connected with the concept, then that is a judgment of connection; if it is opposed, then it is a judgment of conflict.",["Verknüpfung = connection; Wiederstreit (Widerstreit) is not in the glossary and is rendered 'conflict', distinct from Widerspruch = contradiction."]],"philippi-s69-004":["In all judgments matter and form are to be distinguished. The matter is the concept of the thing, and then the concept that I connect with it as a mark, or separate; the concept of the thing that I want to make distinct through judgments is called subject; the concept that I consider as either accruing to that as a mark or opposed is called predicate.",["'oder absondre' (or separate) is left without an explicit object, as in the German.","'jenem' is rendered 'that' and left unresolved; it may refer to the subject-concept or to the thing."]],"philippi-s69-005":["The form is the relation of the subject to the predicate, and is expressed through the *copula*.",["Verhältniß = relation; 'Subiects' corrected to Subjects (spelling only)."]],"philippi-s69-006":["In this relation I see either the connection between predicate and subject or the conflict. In the first case it is an affirmative judgment; in the other a negative one, where the *non* stands at the copula. The negation, however, is to be understood only of the form, not of the matter, which may be as it will.",["bejahend = affirmative; 'die mag seyn wie sie wolle' refers grammatically to 'Materie' (feminine); kept as 'which may be as it will'."]],"philippi-s69-007":["If the *non* stands not at the copula but at the matter, then that is an affirmative judgment, as *anima est non mortalis*. Such judgments in which the copula is not negating are called infinite propositions.",["Säzze = Sätze = propositions."]],"philippi-s69-008":["Subject is called *terminus minor* because it is contained under the predicate. Predicate *terminus maior* because it contains the concept of the subject and besides also still other concepts. It is *nota communis*.",["OCR 'terrninus' corrected to terminus. 'Es' in the last sentence is left unresolved (the German does not say whether it is the predicate or the mark)."]],"philippi-s69-009":["Through extra-essential marks no definition can be given.",["außerwesentlich rendered 'extra-essential'; Definition is the Latinate word (the glossary's Erklärung = explanation; definition is not at issue here)."]],"philippi-s69-010":["The division of a concept consists in this, when I see what is contained in the concept. *Divisio realis*",["Theilung = division; Eintheilung is also 'division' in the glossary (see the next passage). The passage ends with the Latin phrase, as printed."]],"philippi-s69-011":["The division of a concept consists in this, when I see how much is contained under it.",["German Eintheilung (OCR 'Einth eilung'); the English 'division' also stands for Theilung in the preceding passage, as the glossary pairs them (Einteilung / Teilung)."]],"philippi-s69-012":["The less a concept can be divided, the more it can be divided.",["First 'divided' is getheilt (Theilung), second is eingetheilt (Eintheilung); one English word stands for both."]],"philippi-s69-013":["*Membra dividentia* are called the concepts that are contained under another.",[]],"philippi-s69-014":["Taking disjunctive concepts together is, when I represent the opposition of the same.",["'derselben' left as 'the same' (possibly 'of them'); Vorstellung/vorstellen = representation/represent."]],"philippi-s69-015":["The representation of things through a concept is a judgment.",["Urteil here spelled with 'ei' in the source."]],"philippi-s69-016":["In accordance with quantity all judgments are either universal or particular, the singular belong under the universal. In accordance with quality, in affirmation and negation.",["allgemein = universal; besonder = particular; einzeln = singular. The second sentence is a fragment, as in the German."]],"philippi-s69-017":["The judgment rests in the relation of thinking between the predicate and subject, not in the thinking of the concept.",[]],"philippi-s69-018":["§ 297",[]],"philippi-s69-019":["A judgment that is immediately certain has no condition. The judgments that are cognized *per medium terminum*, those are conditioned. If a condition is contingently added to the concept of the subject, which did not lie therein, then this is called a determined judgment. E.g. All human beings must (naturally) die. But if the predicate is compared with the subject by means of a condition that lies in the subject; then it must be developed *per analysin*. E.g. What exists exists somewhere and sometime. Here it must be developed how the concept of existence stands under the concept of place as a condition; although it is not possible.",["OCR 'Conditio n' corrected to Condition and 'hegt' to liegt. erkannt = cognized; zufälliger weise = 'contingently'; bestimmtes Urtheil = 'determined judgment'; Daseyn = existence.","The passage breaks off after 'ob es gleich nicht möglich' with no verb or completion; translated as it stands."]],"philippi-s69-020":["All empirical propositions without distinction are synthetic. All synthetic propositions of reason are never objective but subjective. E.g. Everything that happens has a sufficient ground.",["Vernunfftsätze = propositions of reason; zureichend = sufficient; Grund = ground."]],"philippi-s69-021":["This is not an analytic but a synthetic proposition and therefore subjective, it expresses that I can represent nothing to myself through reason except only through a ground.",["Saz = proposition."]],"philippi-s70-001":["§301",[]],"philippi-s70-002":["The judgment is either",[]],"philippi-s70-003":["a. universal, where the predicate is compared with all concepts that belong to the *sphaera*",["Sphaera kept in Latin; the sentence continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s70-004":["of the subject.",[]],"philippi-s70-005":["(β. where it is compared only with some, that is a particular",["OCR '(3.' read as 'β.'; the opening parenthesis is carried over as printed."]],"philippi-s70-006":["judgment.",[]],"philippi-s70-007":["γ. a singular one where it is compared only with a single concept.",["OCR 'y.' read as 'γ.' and 'Avird' as wird."]],"philippi-s70-008":["If the *sphaera* of the subject is considered as entirely contained in the notion of the predicate or entirely outside it; then in the first case it is a universally affirmative and in the latter case a universally negative judgment.",["Notion left as 'notion'; OCR 'Prädicat s' corrected."]],"philippi-s70-009":["A singular concept has no sphere, it is a point and must therefore likewise fall either entirely outside the sphere of the predicate or entirely within it. Consequently a singular one is equal to the universal judgments.",["Sphäre = sphere (the German alternates Sphaera/Sphäre)."]],"philippi-s70-010":["If the sphere of the subject falls partly within that of the predicate, partly not; then that yields particular judgments.",["German 'particulaire' rendered 'particular', the same English word as besonder."]],"philippi-s70-011":["*Judicium modale* is where the predicate is ascribed to the subject under a particular determination. E.g. All human beings are naturally mortal. To it is contradistinguished the judgment which has no such determination.",["besonder = particular; Bestimmung = determination."]],"philippi-s70-012":["In all judgments I compare nothing other than the *sphaerae* of the concepts and see whether one is contained in the other or not.",[]],"philippi-s70-013":["The universality of affirmative propositions rests partly on this, that the condition of truth already lies in the notion of the subject, partly on this, that if it does not lie therein, it is added, e.g. all human beings are changeable. The condition of truth lies in the concept of the human being.",["OCR 'hegt' corrected to liegt. Condition = condition (Latinate, German 'Condition'); Bedingung is also 'condition'."]],"philippi-s70-014":["Judgments are either simple or composite. A simple judgment is where I compare only one subject with a predicate, a composite one, on the other hand, where I compare 2 predicates with one subject or 2 subjects with one predicate. The latter are called *judicia copulativa*.",["zusammengesetzt rendered 'composite'; einfach = simple."]],"philippi-s70-015":["The *judicia* are further divided into",["eingetheilt = divided."]],"philippi-s70-016":["1. *ordinaria* in which one concept is compared with the other.",[]],"philippi-s70-017":["2. *extraordinaria* where one judgment is compared with another.",[]],"philippi-s70-018":["These are of twofold kind a. *Judicia hypothetica seu conditionalia* b. *disiunctiva*.",["Art = kind here."]],"philippi-s70-019":["All relation is either a relation of connection or of conflict. If a judgment stands in the relation of conflict with the other; then that is a disjunctive judgment. E.g. The soul is either simple or composite. Here there is between the judgments: the soul is simple and the soul is composite a conflict. If I assume one, the other drops out.",["OCR 'Verhält niß' corrected; Widerstreit = conflict."]],"philippi-s70-020":["If a judgment stands with another in the relation of connection, so that if I posit the one the other must also be posited; then that is a hypothetical judgment. The former is the condition of the other. If . . . then . . .",["setzen = posit; 'Das erstere' rendered 'The former'."]],"philippi-s70-021":["We attend here always only to the form, to the relation; the matter of the judgment may be true, or not. One looks only to the *consequens*. The condition is called *antecedens*, the proposition following from it *consequens*. The relation of both is called *consequentia*.",["The first 'Consequens' is followed by a full stop in the source and is left as printed."]],"philippi-s70-022":["Of the *iudicia disiunctiva* it is to be noted that since only ever two *iudicia* come into opposition, and that if there are more, they must be reduced to 2, to two pure *oppositiones*.",["OCR 'iudicus' read as iudiciis, here left as the nominative form in the passage's own usage; the construction ('da ... und daß') is syntactically irregular in the German and is kept so."]],"philippi-s70-023":["For only two propositions can be purely opposed, the others are *Unteroppositiones*. Provided the opposition is to be able to be seen through reason. Where experience is added, there can be more.",["OCR 'sej'' read as seyn. 'Dafern ...' is a sentence fragment in the German."]],"philippi-s70-024":["The disjunctions in logic serve to make complete logical divisions.",["German 'Diuisionen'; 'division' also stands for Theilung/Eintheilung."]],"philippi-s70-025":["For logical division it is required that all *membra dividentia* make up the whole *conceptus divisus* and that they stand in *oppositione*. If a member is missing; then the logical division and the disjunctive judgment is false.",["Eintheilung = division; the singular verb 'is' follows the German 'ist ... falsch'."]],"philippi-s70-026":["All judgments are either",[]],"philippi-s70-027":["1. *Judicia problematica*, in which I weigh the relation of the predicate",["Word order is shifted slightly because the German verb 'erwäge' falls in the next line; erwägen rendered 'weigh'."]],"philippi-s70-028":["to the subject only.",["German: 'zum Subjekt nur erwäge' (verb in the preceding line of the English)."]],"philippi-s70-029":["2. *Judicia assertoria* in which I posit it. In order to prevent errors I must judge as much as possible problematically.",["'it' (es) is left unresolved."]],"philippi-s71-001":["§310",[]],"philippi-s71-002":["To expound means to set apart. There must therefore be something mixed there. If two judgments lie hidden in one, they must be developed. That is an *exponible* judgment.",["Exponiren = expound (Latin exponere; Erörterung = exposition); OCR 'TJrtheil' corrected to Urtheil; 'exponibles' left as the Latinate adjective *exponible*."]],"philippi-s71-003":["The exponible judgment, when it is unfolded, contains two judgments, of which one affirms, the other denies. E.g. Few human beings are pious, means as much as: some human beings are pious and others not pious.",["Urtheil = judgment; bejahen = affirm; verneinen = deny; *exponibel* is Latinate, rendered 'exponible'.","'ausgewickelt' rendered 'unfolded' (literally 'unwrapped')."]],"philippi-s71-004":["An exponible judgment therefore contains 1. subject and 2 predicate but two sorts of quality and quantity.",["'zweierley' = 'of two sorts'; the numerals '1.' and '2' are as printed."]],"philippi-s72-001":["§311",[]],"philippi-s72-002":["Judgments that are not merely logical; but also seek to move our mind, as e.g. exclamations, must never occur in the way of cognition where logical perfection is the chief aim. It is absurd in philosophy to mix in sensations and appearances. Meyer and Feder have this fault. If the author says: Such judgments are very practical, he should say, they are very moving and charming. Many authors have lost the value of their philosophy, since they let themselves be seduced to mix feeling and taste therein. Rousseau is one of the greatest geniuses. But he mixes into his writings something novelistic, hence his sharp mind is not rightly seen into by everyone, and the strength of his arguments remains unrecognized by one part of the readers. Hume, because he mixes much that is new into his writings, has been regarded by many more as an eloquent man than as the acute philosopher that he actually is.",["OCR corrected: rubren→rühren, Absieht→Absicht.","Gemüth = mind; Geist = mind (glossary gives 'mind; spirit' for Geist); Erkenntniß = cognition; Vollkommenheit = perfection; Absicht = aim; Empfindung = sensation; Erscheinung = appearance; Gefühl = feeling.","'Meyer' is as printed in the source.","'viel Neues' is rendered literally as 'much that is new'."]],"philippi-s72-003":["One must not write against taste; one must be negative in morals and philosophy with regard to taste.",["OCR corrected: Geschmak is kept as 'taste'; 'Moral' = morals."]],"philippi-s72-004":["If taste and reason are mixed, then it is also seductive for the hearer; he is then not sure whether the strength of the grounds of reason or taste and the agreeableness of the style of writing has brought him to approval. Voltaire has the fault that through introduced particular turns and through something droll he knows how to bring the reader to his side. For where I find pleasure, I take the party of that.",["Vernunftgründe = grounds of reason; Vernunft = reason; besonder = particular.","'ob ihn ... gebracht' keeps the German singular verb after a compound subject, as in the source."]],"philippi-s73-001":["§312",[]],"philippi-s73-002":["The learned judgments are *vel theoretica vel practica*. The former *vel obiectiua* which are correct and good in and for themselves, *vel subiectiua* which only indicate a relation to my free will.",["'Die erstem' (OCR/orthography for 'ersten') rendered 'The former'.","Latin kept in italics as printed."]],"philippi-s73-003":["§",["Section number missing in the source as read."]],"philippi-s73-004":["The learned judgments are either provable or unprovable. The latter are immediately certain; the former only by means of certain intermediate judgments. Much depends on distinguishing the provable judgments from the unprovable. Only metaphysics can teach us that, in that it shows us the laws and limitations of our understanding.",["OCR corrected: Zwischennrtheile→Zwischenurtheile.","erweislich/unerweislich = provable/unprovable (Beweis = proof); Schranken = limitations; Gesetze = laws; Verstand = understanding.","'Es ist viel daran gelegen' rendered 'Much depends on'."]],"philippi-s73-005":["Only too often we hold judgments to be immediately certain that are nevertheless so only by means of certain intermediate judgments, which we pass over.",["OCR: 'gar zu offt' = 'only too often'."]],"philippi-s74-001":["§314",["OCR 'Bejr' in the following passage corrected to 'Bei'."]],"philippi-s74-002":["In every proof there are to be considered the matter and the form. The former is the mark which I compare with the subject and then with the predicate; the latter is the way of comparison. E.g. Every body is divisible: for it is composed. I compare the body with the composed, then with the divisible and say: every body is composed, everything composed is divisible; consequently every body is divisible. One sees here whence proofs can be brought into syllogisms. In general the *principia materialia* of proofs are the marks that are already known to us as certain of the thing, the *medii termini*; the *principia formalia* are the laws according to which the understanding sees the agreement of the *medii termini* 1. with the subject 2. with the predicate to be proved. They contain the rules that are of the way in which, from certain grounds, something is to be for inferences. We have only two of them, the proposition of identity and of contradiction. *Principia materialia* we have innumerable. Those, the *principia materialia*, are grounded ultimately on elementary propositions. An element is a part of a whole that is further no part of others. Elementary propositions are those that contain the *notas constitutiuas primitiuas alicuius entis*. A proposition is provable only by means of a *notae mediae*.",["OCR corrected: Bejr→Bei, formaha→formalia, Theü→Theil; 'Wiederspruchs' kept as 'contradiction' (Widerspruch).","Merkmal = mark; Materie = matter; Form = form; Satz = proposition (Elementarsäzze = elementary propositions); Schluß = inference; Grund = ground; Verstand = understanding; Art = way.","'wie aus gewissen Gründen etwas zu Schlüssen sey' is obscure and perhaps corrupt (possibly 'zu schließen sey'); rendered as it stands.","'medii termini' is printed in the plural in both places; 'notae mediae' is as printed (genitive/dative after 'vermittelst'); left unaltered."]],"philippi-s74-003":["Therefore the judgments where I compare a *notam immediatam* with the subject cannot be proved. They are the elementary propositions.",["Latin as printed ('notam immediatam'), italicized."]],"philippi-s75-001":["§ 315",[]],"philippi-s75-002":["The end of judgments is distinctness. Distinctness consists in this, that I make hidden obscure propositions manifest; that happens through judging. Thereby I make *identitatem latentem* into the *patenti*. If judgments have *identitatem expliciatam*; then they are tautological; but if they have only *implicitam*: then they can be a means of distinctness and consequently not at once empty.",["Zweck = end; Deutlichkeit = distinctness (deutlich = distinct); Mittel = means; Urtheilen = judging.","OCR 'expliсitam' is garbled in the source; read as *explicitam* (the rendering above should read 'identitatem explicitam').","'zur patenti' is as printed (the noun *identitas* is elided)."]],"philippi-s75-003":["*Postulata* are unprovable practical judgments. If one postulates what can still be proved; then that is called begging (*petere principium*)",["erbetteln rendered 'begging'; no closing full stop in the source."]],"philippi-s76-001":["§ 319",[]],"philippi-s76-002":["Locke divides judgments into",[]],"philippi-s76-003":["1. *Judicia intuitiua empirica*, that is singular judgments insofar as they",["Continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s76-004":["are immediately cognized.",["erkennen = cognize."]],"philippi-s76-005":["2. *Judicia reflecta seu discursiua*; which through an inference from others",["Continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s76-006":["are drawn.",[]],"philippi-s76-007":["All universal judgments are *reflexa*. I do not properly experience the universal; but rather the singular.",["erfahren = experience (v.)."]],"philippi-s76-008":["As for the *judicia intuitiua*, the *intuitus* is either 1. *sensitiuus*, that is the feeling in the case of a singular object of the senses. Thus all immediate representations of experience are *intuitus*. Through the imagination I can also represent to myself an object *per intuitum sensitiuum*. Immediate judgments are not all intuiting; only those are whose form is sensitive. E.g. the sun is round.",["OCR corrected: 'sensit! uus' → *sensitiuus*.","'anschauend' rendered 'intuiting' (Anschauung = intuition); 'Imagination' rendered 'imagination' (not Einbildungskraft).","Object/Gegenstand: 'Gegenstande' and 'Object' are both 'object'."]],"philippi-s76-009":["2. *intuitus purus*, the representation according to rules of reason, e.g. the representation of space and time, of mathematical figures. Extension etc. It is the form of sensible sensation.",["rein = pure; Ausdehnung = extension; Empfindung = sensation; sinnlich = sensible."]],"philippi-s76-010":["The *axiomata* of geometry are *iudicia intuitiua* but not of experience but of reason. Thus the universal representation of proofs is also an *intuitus purus*.",[]],"philippi-s76-011":["*Axiomata philosophica carent intuitu: sunt discursiua*, for the philosopher derives everything from universal propositions. Thus *iudicia intuitiua* are either judgments of *intuitu empirici* or *puri*.",["'intuitu empirici' is as printed."]],"philippi-s76-012":["I can make for myself no intuitive concept of numbers, except by counting, consequently through time.",["Zahlen = numbers; Begriff = concept; Zeit = time."]],"philippi-s76-013":["Universal empirical judgments also always permit a proof.",[]],"philippi-s76-014":["Empirical judgments are never negative. That the moon has no light of its own is no immediate proposition of experience. Accordingly all negative judgments of experience are mediate judgments and have arisen from reflection.",["Reflexion = reflection; 'kein eigen Licht' = 'no light of its own'."]],"philippi-s76-015":["§",["Section number missing in the source as read."]],"philippi-s76-016":["All propositions are",[]],"philippi-s76-017":["1. theoretical, which express the relation of one concept to another.",[]],"philippi-s76-018":["2. practical, which express the relation of actions to concepts.",[]],"philippi-s76-019":["Both are",[]],"philippi-s76-020":["1. either unprovable, then the former are called *axiomata*, the others *postulata*, or",["'denn' (for 'dann') rendered 'then'; OCR 'uner weißlich' joined."]],"philippi-s76-021":["2. provable through *ratiocinia polysyllogistica*, then the theoretical propositions are called *theoremata*, the practical *problemata*.",["'denn' rendered 'then'."]],"philippi-s76-022":["A proposition that is drawn from another through an inference is called a *Corollarium*.",[]],"philippi-s76-023":["If I assume a proposition without proof which however is proved in another science, then it is called *Lemma*, e.g. that a God is is in morals a *Lemma*; it is proved in *Theologia naturalis*.",["'daß ein Gott sey' (subjunctive) is rendered 'that a God is' ; the doubled 'is is' is a typo for 'is' - read as 'that there is a God'. Wissenschaft = science."]],"philippi-s76-024":["A proposition that serves not for proof but only for clarification is called *Scholion*.",["Erläuterung = clarification."]],"philippi-s77-001":["§338",[]],"philippi-s77-002":["Generally one can say that every *ratio tautologica* is a *qualitas occulta*. E.g. if one names antipathy as the cause that certain things do not get along with one another; then the word means nothing other than impatience.",["OCR corrected: AVort→Wort. 'Ungeduldsanikeit' is garbled; read as 'Ungeduldsamkeit' (impatience); doubtful.","Ursache = cause."]],"philippi-s77-003":["The *qualitas occulta* is very concealed in philosophy. The first *principium* of Crusius, as he posits the criterion of truth in the nature of human reason, is tautological and a *qualitas occulta*; for this consists in nothing other than that the ground stands with the sequel in *identitate implicita*.",["'da er' is ambiguous (temporal/causal/relative); rendered 'as he'.","Folge = sequel; Grund = ground; Kriterium = criterion."]],"philippi-s77-004":["Explanations in a circle come very close to the *qualitatibus occultis*. One finds in philosophy, most of all however in logic and morals, the *qualitates occultas* still frequently. There one gives causes that say no more than the effect.",["OCR corrected: mehresten and Qualitates as printed; Erklärungen = explanations; Wirkung = effect.","The placement of 'am mehresten' is ambiguous in the German; kept in its place."]],"philippi-s78-001":["§341",[]],"philippi-s78-002":["An immediate inference is that which is inferred from a judgment *sine nota intermedia*.",["Schluß = inference; geschlossen = inferred. \"einem Urtheil\" can be read \"a judgment\" or \"one judgment\"; the English keeps \"a\"."]],"philippi-s78-003":["An immediate inference occurs, according to the division of the logicians,",["The passage breaks off here; the numbered items follow in the next passages. Eintheilung = division."]],"philippi-s78-004":["1. *per iudicia aequipollentia*.",[]],"philippi-s78-005":["2. *per iudicia subalternata*.",[]],"philippi-s78-006":["3. *per iudicia opposita*.",[]],"philippi-s78-007":["4. *per conuersionem logicam*.",["\"conuersionem\" keeps the transcript's u-spelling."]],"philippi-s78-008":["5. *per contrapositionem*.",[]],"philippi-s78-009":["I. The immediate inference *per iudicia aequipollentia* is no true conclusion; for I here posit only another, more suitable word in place of a word. E.g.: Not all or some human beings are virtuous. This substitution is no conclusion.",["Z. E. = zum Exempel, rendered \"E.g.\". sezze = posit (setzen). Conclusion (Latinate) and Schluß are both rendered \"conclusion\"/\"inference\" per the glossary; the German word is Conclusion here."]],"philippi-s78-010":["II. Through *iudicia subalternata* there comes to be an immediate inference; I subsume a particular judgment under a general one. E.g. all human beings are mortal, therefore also kings. The general judgment is called *iudicium subalternans*; the particular, however, *iudicium subalternatum*",["besonder = particular; allgemein = general. The sentence ends without a period in the source."]],"philippi-s78-011":["III. *per iudicia contradictorie opposita* i.e. *pure opposita*. If two judgments are opposed *contradictorie*; then if the one is true, the other is false, and if one is false the other is true.",["\"pure\" is Latin here (adverb), not German rein; \"opponirt\" = \"opposed\"."]],"philippi-s78-012":["IV. The immediate inference *per conuersionem* occurs when the predicate is changed into the subject. E.g. All bodies are divisible. Some of the divisible are bodies.",["\"Einiges Theilbare sind Körper\": singular neuter subject with plural verb in the German; the English \"Some of the divisible are bodies\" is a literal attempt, not a smoothing. Praedicat/Subjekt = predicate/subject."]],"philippi-s78-013":["The judgment that is to be inverted is called *conuertens*; that which arises from the inversion, *conuersum*. *Conuersio simplex* is when the quantity is not changed. *Conuersio per accidens* when it is changed.",["Umkehrung = inversion (the Latin *conuersio* is left in Latin). Quantität = quantity."]],"philippi-s78-014":["The quality always remains the same",["Qualitaet = quality. No final period in the source."]],"philippi-s78-015":["V. In the immediate inference *per contrapositionem* the quality is also changed. E.g. All human beings can err. What cannot err is no human being. The rule is: *remoto praedicato tollitur subiectum*.",[]],"philippi-s78-016":["The *judicia subcontraria*, because they only set one particular concept against another and have no generality, make no contradiction.",["Contradiction (Latinate) rendered \"contradiction\", the same English word as for Widerspruch. Begrif = concept; Allgemeinheit = generality."]],"philippi-s78-017":["In the opposition of two general judgments there is again too much for contradiction; the particular is lacking.",["\"zu viel zur Contradiction\" is rendered literally; the sense of \"zu\" is left as in the German."]],"philippi-s78-018":["In the case of the *iudicia contradictorie opposita* it can be inferred from the truth of the one to the falsity of the other and from the falsity of the one to the truth of the other. These therefore belong to immediate inference.",["OCR \"Bej*\" read as \"Bey\". The German impersonal passive \"kann ... geschlossen werden\" is kept as a passive."]],"philippi-s78-019":["But in the case of the *iudicia contrarie opposita* it cannot be inferred from the falsity of the one to the truth of the other, and in the case of the *iudicia subcontrarie opposita*, on the other hand, it cannot be inferred from the truth of the one to the falsity of the other, hence these do not belong to immediate inference.",["mithin = hence."]],"philippi-s78-020":["The *conclusiones mediatae* can be called judgments of reason; the *conclusiones immediatae*, however, judgments of understanding. Judgments are opposed to one another",["Vernunffturtheile = judgments of reason; Verstandesurtheile = judgments of understanding. The passage breaks off mid-sentence; it continues in the next passage."]],"philippi-s78-021":["1. *contradictorie*, when to the general affirming a particular denying judgment, and to the general denying a particular affirming judgment, is opposed. 2. *subcontrarie* when two *particularia iudicia* that have one and the same subject and predicate, one affirms, the other negates.",["bejahend = affirming (bejahen = affirm); verneinend = denying; \"affirmirt\" = affirms; \"negirt\" = negates. The elliptical construction of the last clause is kept."]],"philippi-s78-022":["3. *contrarie* when to a general affirming proposition a general denying one is opposed and *vice versa*.",["Satz = proposition."]],"philippi-s78-023":["It is strange that the logicians have called contrariety a contradiction. In the *iudicium subcontrarium* both can be true, but not both false.",["wunderlich = strange; Contrarietät = contrariety; Contradiction = contradiction. \"alle beyde\" = \"both\"."]],"philippi-s78-024":["The Tenth Section.",["Abschnitt = section."]],"philippi-s79-001":["On the Learned Inferences of Reason. §353",["Vernunftschluß = inference of reason (the glossary also allows \"syllogism\"); the choice is kept throughout. gelehrt = learned."]],"philippi-s79-002":["The understanding judges, reason infers, that is, it judges mediately. The understanding cognizes immediately that a predicate belongs to a certain subject; reason, however, cognizes it only by means of a certain mark (*nota intermedia*) that it",["Verstand = understanding; Vernunft = reason; erkennt = cognizes; Merkmal = mark (nota). The passage breaks off; it continues in the next passages. \"vermittelst\" = by means of."]],"philippi-s79-003":["1. with the predicate",["OCR \"1 .\" read as \"1.\" Prädicat = predicate."]],"philippi-s79-004":["2. with the subject holds together and then draws the consequence. E.g. that a body is composed, that I cognize immediately. But that it is alterable I cognize only by means of a *nota intermedia*, by means of the mark of divisibility. I say",["OCR \"erkenneich\" read as \"erkenne ich\"; \"notae intermediae\" is as printed. veränderlich = alterable; Theilbarkeit = divisibility; Folgerung = consequence; zusammenhalten = hold together. The sentence continues the construction begun in the previous passage (\"das sie 1. ... 2. ... zusammenhält\")."]],"philippi-s79-005":["Every body is divisible",["No final period in the source."]],"philippi-s79-006":["Everything divisible is alterable.",[]],"philippi-s79-007":["Therefore every body is also alterable.",[]],"philippi-s79-008":["The foundation and basic formula in all inferences of reason is this: *Nota notae est nota rei ipsius*. E.g. alterability is a mark of divisibility as a *nota* of the body; consequently it is also the mark of the body itself.",["Fundament = foundation; Grundformel = basic formula; Veränderlichkeit = alterability; folglich = consequently."]],"philippi-s79-009":["There thus occur in an inference of reason three judgments. In the first I compare the predicate or the *nota remota*, which I cannot cognize immediately as belonging to the subject, with the *nota intermedia*. Since now the predicate always has a greater sphere than the subject, it is called *terminus maior* and the whole first judgment *propositio maior*.",["Sphäre = sphere (not Umfang, which the glossary renders \"domain\"). \"propositio\" is Latin, left in Latin; elsewhere Satz = proposition."]],"philippi-s79-010":["In the second judgment I compare the subject with the *nota intermedia*. Since now the subject is contained in the sphere of the predicate; it is called *terminus minor* and the whole judgment *propositio minor*. The middle concept is called the *medius terminus*. The judgment that I finally bring out is called *conclusio*. In it I then have insight into, through the comparison that has occurred, the relation of the *nota remota* to the subject. I must go *per synthesin* from *nota remota* to the *nota propiore*, from this to the subject. The two upper judgments are also called premises.",["einsehen = have insight into (\"ich sehe ... ein\"). \"a nota remota\": the \"a\" is Latin \"from\" and is rendered \"from\". \"propiore\" is kept as printed. Mittelbegriff = middle concept; Verhältniß = relation; Prämissen = premises."]],"philippi-s79-011":["First judgment:",[]],"philippi-s79-012":["Everything divisible is alterable.",[]],"philippi-s79-013":["Second judgment:",[]],"philippi-s79-014":["Every body is divisible.",[]],"philippi-s79-015":["Third judgment:",[]],"philippi-s79-016":["Every body is alterable,",["The comma stands in the source."]],"philippi-s79-017":["C-B",["Letters/diagram marks carried over as printed."]],"philippi-s79-018":["A-C",["As printed."]],"philippi-s79-019":["A. B.",["As printed."]],"philippi-s79-020":["The synthetic method, which requires that one begin from the grounds, also demands that in the first judgment one compare the middle concept with the predicate.",["OCR \"eisten\" read as \"ersten\"; \"Mittel -begrif\" read as \"Mittelbegriff\". Gründe = grounds."]],"philippi-s79-021":["In the case of inferences of reason one is to distinguish the matter, that is the premises, or the three concepts, the subject, predicate and middle concept, and the form, which consists in the way in which the conclusion is concluded from the premises.",["OCR \"lind\" read as \"und\"; \"Praeinissen\" as \"Praemissen\". gefolgert = concluded (folgern); Conclusion = conclusion. Art = way; Materie = matter."]],"philippi-s79-022":["The truth or falsity of an inference of reason in the matter concerns the truth or falsity in the premises. E.g. All matter is eternal; the world consists of matter; hence it is eternal.",["mithin = hence."]],"philippi-s79-023":["But even if the premises are true, the conclusion can nevertheless be false. For then a mistake is made in the form. The form is the essential and the faults in it the most important; the faults in the matter are accidental. For the form is the general rule; the matter is only a concrete case to which the form is given, or which is subsumed under the general rule. The falsity in the form for inferences is the cause of the falsity of the matter.",["\"wird in der Form gefehlt\" is an impersonal passive, rendered \"a mistake is made\". \"in der Form zu Schlüssen\" is kept literally (\"in the form for inferences\") and may be a corrupt or elliptical reading. accidentel = accidental; Wesentliche = the essential."]],"philippi-s79-024":["We have accordingly a twofold correctness; one of the matter and one of the form. The rules of the matter of the inferences of reason are properly the *principia iudiciorum*, that is the proposition of identity and of contradiction. The former in the case of all affirming, the latter in the case of the denying propositions.",["Richtigkeit = correctness; Saz der Identität und des Widerspruchs = proposition of identity and of contradiction (Widerspruch = contradiction). The elliptical final sentence is kept."]],"philippi-s79-025":["All inferences of reason are either ordinary, where there are 3 *termini*: subject, predicate and the middle concept; or extraordinary, where 2 judgments or more are compared with one another. All conditioned and disjunctive judgments give *ratiocinia extraordinaria*. A conditioned inference of reason is where the *maior propositio* is a conditioned, a disjunctive where it is a disjunctive judgment.",["ordentlich/ausserordentlich = ordinary/extraordinary. bedingt = conditioned. The ellipsis \"ein bedingtes\" (no noun) is kept elliptical."]],"philippi-s80-001":["§364",[]],"philippi-s80-002":["The proposition by virtue of which it can be inferred from the ground to the sequels, that is the ground and the *principium* of all *ratiociniorum hypotheticorum*.",["Folgen = sequels (Folge, per the glossary; distinct from Folgerung = consequence). Satz = proposition; Grund = ground. \"kann ... geschlossen werden\" kept as an impersonal passive."]],"philippi-s81-001":["§365",[]],"philippi-s81-002":["The *principium exclusi medii inter duo contradictoria* is the *principium ratiociniorum disiunctiuorum*.",["\"disiunctiuorum\" keeps the transcript's spelling."]],"philippi-s82-001":["§366",[]],"philippi-s82-002":["The *conclusiones immediatae* are no *ratiocinia*. E.g. All human beings are mortal, consequently some are mortal. Here the *medius terminus* is lacking, therefore they are no *ratiocinia*.",["folglich = consequently."]],"philippi-s82-003":["The judgment of the author, that judgments are true if they can be assumed without prejudice to truth, is entirely tautological.",["\"der Wahrheit unbeschadet\" = without prejudice to truth; angenommen = assumed; \"tavtologisch\" is the source spelling. \"Autor\" = author (the text under discussion)."]],"philippi-s83-001":["§363",["Section number as printed (out of sequence with the preceding §366)."]],"philippi-s83-002":["As far as the *principia* of the form of the inferences of reason are concerned; the scholastics had the *dictum de omni et de nullo*. That signifies as much:",["bedeuten = signify. The passage breaks off after the colon."]],"philippi-s83-003":["What is affirmed of a general concept is also affirmed of the particular; and what is denied of a general concept is also denied of everything particular that is contained under it. Now, however, every syllogism is nothing other than the subsumption of a particular judgment under a general one. We say the *principium formale* of affirmative judgments is: *nota notae est nota rei ipsius* and *repugnans notae repugnat rei ipsi*. The former is the *dictum de omni*, the latter the *dictum de nullo*. What belongs to or conflicts with the general also belongs to or conflicts with the particular that is contained under it. The relation is twofold:",["Vernunfftschluß rendered 'syllogism' per the glossary (Vernunftschluß = syllogism; inference of reason); Schluß would be 'inference'.","'besondern' (particular) is rendered the same in both occurrences; 'Begrif' is an old spelling of Begriff (concept).","'Urtheil' = judgment; the text has 'besondern Urtheils', literally 'of a particular judgment', kept as it stands."]],"philippi-s83-004":["a. logical; where I consider the concepts according to the relation of the spheres",["'Sphären' rendered 'spheres' (cognate); the glossary's 'Umfang' (domain) is a different German word and is not used here.","The sentence continues in the next passage ('betrachte')."]],"philippi-s83-005":["consider,",["Continuation of the preceding fragment (word order: '...nach dem Verhältniße der Sphären betrachte')."]],"philippi-s83-006":["ß metaphysical, when the *notiones* are represented as they",["'Notioncs' read as an OCR slip for 'Notiones'; kept in Latin, italicized.","'ß' is the OCR rendering of the Greek letter beta, kept as it stands; 'a.' above is probably alpha.","'vorgestellt' = represented (Vorstellung = representation)."]],"philippi-s83-007":["are contained in one another.",["Continues the preceding fragment."]],"philippi-s83-008":["The subject is contained under the predicate, i.e. under its sphere; but the predicate is contained in the subject, i.e. as a *constitutivum* of the concept.",["'constitutiuum' read as *constitutivum*; 'Begrifs' = of the concept (Begriff)."]],"philippi-s84-001":["§369",[]],"philippi-s84-002":["The doctrine of the syllogistic figures, of the rules of each figure, of the *modi* in quality, quantity and the reduction of other figures to the first, aims at how syllogisms can be formed without understanding through mechanical contrivances. We know from the foregoing that in every syllogism the middle concept is compared with the subject and the *nota remota* (the predicate). Since now its place can be changed in a fourfold way; the 4 figures have arisen from this.",["Vernunfftschlüsse = syllogisms; Verstand = understanding.","'modis qualitate, quantitate' kept as printed in Latin (ablatives) and italicized; 'Lehre' = doctrine.","'Mittelbegrif' (middle concept) and *medius terminus* are distinct in the German/Latin; 'Begriff' = concept."]],"philippi-s84-003":["In the first figure the *medius terminus* stands in the *maiori propositione loco subiecti*; in the *minori loco praedicati*.",["The Latin is left in Latin; 'minori' is without a following 'propositione' in the original."]],"philippi-s84-004":["In the second it stands in both premises *loco praedicati*; in the third figure it stands in both premises *loco subiecti*; and in the fourth in the *maiori loco praedicati*; in the *minori loco subiecti*.",["'Prämißen' = premises."]],"philippi-s84-005":["Everything thus depends on the places of the *medius terminus*.",["'Stellen' = places (Ort = place is a different word); 'medii Termini' is genitive Latin, kept."]],"philippi-s84-006":["Human reason always infers in the first figure and cannot do otherwise. In all the other figures it infers only by first reducing them to the first figure. It does this by thinking, besides the 2 propositions, still an *immediate* conclusion. Accordingly all *ratiocinia* of the 2nd, 3rd and 4th figure are *ratiocinia hybrida*, and only those of the first figure are *pura*.",["'schlüßt' = infers (Schluß = inference); 'Säzzen' = propositions (Satz); 'Conclusion' kept as 'conclusion'.","'immediate' printed in Latin script and therefore italicized as foreign word."]],"philippi-s84-007":["In the 2nd figure *conversio logica* always occurs in thought in the *maiori propositione*. In the third there is a *contrapositio*. E.g. All scholars have an improved understanding; some scholars are vicious. Here I invert: some vicious ones are scholars; therefore some vicious ones have an improved understanding. In the 4th figure both alterations, *conuersio* and *contrapositio*, must be introduced.",["'Lasterhaffte' = vicious ones; 'gelehrt' = scholars/learned, rendered 'are scholars' as the German has the plain adjective ('sind gelehrt' literally 'are learned'); this is a literal-minimal compromise.","'umkehren' = invert."]],"philippi-s84-008":["If the *medius terminus* is not considered as a *nota* of the subject; then it does not infer.",["'sie' (it) refers back, grammatically, to Vernunft in the preceding paragraph; kept as 'it' without resolution."]],"philippi-s84-009":["Hence in the 3rd figure, where this happens, no syllogism is possible.",["The antecedent of 'dies' ('this') is left as ambiguous as in the German.","Vernunfftschluß = syllogism; möglich = possible."]],"philippi-s84-010":["The rule of the 2nd figure is therefore: *maior sit vniuersalis, conclusio negatiua*",["Latin kept as printed."]],"philippi-s84-011":["The rule of the third figure is: *minor sit affirmus, conclusio particularis*. For affirmative propositions can be inverted only so that a particular proposition results.",["'affirmus' printed thus in the source (for *affirmativa*?); left as it stands, doubtful.","'Säzze' = propositions; 'Satz' = proposition."]],"philippi-s84-012":["In the first figure the conclusions are affirmative; in the second negative; in the third particular, affirmative or negative; in the 4th they are fixed upon certain conditions; and are either universally negative or particular. Why in the first figure alone universally affirmative conclusions are to be encountered, the cause of this is that in the 3 others an inversion must always take place.",["'allgemein' = general/universal; rendered 'universally' in 'allgemein verneinende' for English idiom; 'Bedingungen' = conditions.","'Umkehr' = inversion."]],"philippi-s84-013":["The *modus* in syllogistics signifies the quality and quantity of the 3 propositions. One expresses the quality and quantity through the vowels a.e.i.o., with which, for the sake of euphony, consonants are combined; from which the words *Barbara* etc. arose. *Asserit a, negat e sed vniuersaliter ambo; asserit i negat o sed particulariter ambo.* 4 magnitudes can be permuted 64 times, if only 3 are always taken together; thus 64 *modi* are possible. All the general rules of syllogisms: *Conclusio sequi debet partem debiliorem, ex puris negatiuis et puris particularibus ml sequitur* and the particular rules of each figure remove so many of the 64 *modi* that only 10 *modi vtiles* remain.",["'bedeutet' = signifies (bedeuten); 'Grössen' = magnitudes (Größe).","'ml' is a garbled OCR reading (presumably 'nil'); left as printed. '4 magnitudes ... 64' is as in the source.","'Qualitaet', 'Quantitaet' = quality, quantity."]],"philippi-s84-014":["Because reason infers only in the first figure, one must know how to reduce all syllogisms of the other figures to the first. For those who do not set the concepts apart from one another, entangle them still more. This contrivance of finding the rules is called *Reductio*.",["'sezzen' = set (setzen is 'posit' in the glossary, but here 'aus einander sezzen' is rendered 'set apart').","'die die Begriffe nicht aus einander sezzen, verwickeln sie' – the antecedent of 'sie' is ambiguous (the concepts/the syllogisms), preserved.","'Regeln aufzufinden' read as in the source; sense doubtful."]],"philippi-s84-015":["The consonants that are put with the *modi* have a meaning in this aim and teach mechanically to reduce the other figures to the first.",["'in der Absicht' = in the aim (Absicht = aim); 'Bedeutung' = meaning."]],"philippi-s85-001":["S. *vult simpliciter verti* P. *vero per accidens*",["Latin mnemonic kept."]],"philippi-s85-002":["M. *vult transponi* C. *per impossibile duci.*",["Latin mnemonic kept."]],"philippi-s85-003":["P. *vero per accidens* means as much as: from a universally affirmative or negative proposition a particular affirmative or negative one must come to be.",["'Saz' = proposition (Satz); 'besonderes' = particular."]],"philippi-s85-004":["M. *vult transponi* wants to say as much: the *Maior* is made into the *minori*.",["Case forms *Maior*/*minori* as in the source."]],"philippi-s85-005":["C. *per impossibile duci* signifies the *maior* must be contraposed or inverted *simpliciter*.",["'bedeutet' = signifies; 'umgekehrt' = inverted."]],"philippi-s85-006":["Through this contrivance of syllogistics, I mean the *modi*, one does not teach more correct inferences. The inference becomes almost more entangled. One makes use of them only to play with the *medius terminus*.",["'Schlüssen' / 'Schluß' = inference (Schluß = inference); 'Kunststück' = contrivance."]],"philippi-s85-007":["The general rules are",[]],"philippi-s85-008":["1. The *syllogismus* must not contain more than 3 *termini*. Here one should take care that a word is not taken in a twofold meaning. E.g. What you have not lost, that you still have; horns you have not lost: therefore you have some.",["'Avelche' read as OCR slip for 'welche' (some/any).","'Bedeutung' = meaning."]],"philippi-s85-009":["2 *medius terminus ne ingrediatur conclusionem*; for it belongs to the premises.",["'Prämißen' = premises."]],"philippi-s85-010":["3. *ex puris negatiuis*. In every syllogism the *medius terminus* is a *nota* of the subject, and the predicate again a *nota* of it or *nota notae*. The *proposition* therefore in which the *medius terminus* is regarded as a *nota* of the subject must necessarily be affirmative.",["'Proposition' and 'Subiect' are printed in Latinate form in the German; 'Praedicat' = predicate.","'nothwendig' = necessarily (notwendig = necessary)."]],"philippi-s85-011":["4. *ex puris particularibus nihil sequitur*; one of the premises must be universal, if it is to be a pure syllogism, then it must be the *maior*, where the predicate is compared with the *medius terminus*. This already follows from the *dictum de omni et nullo*.",["'inaior' read as OCR slip for 'maior'; 'vniuersel' = universal; 'reiner Vernunfftschluß' = pure syllogism (rein = pure)."]],"philippi-s85-012":["5. *Conclusio sequi debet partem debiliorem*. *Pars debilior* is the *propositio negatiua et particularis*. If one of the premises is negative, then the conclusion must be too; if one is particular, then the conclusion must be too.",["'parteni debüiorem' read as OCR slip for 'partem debiliorem'."]],"philippi-s85-013":["6. The conclusion must contain neither more nor less than is contained in the antecedent judgments.",["'Vorderurtheilen' = antecedent judgments (Urtheil = judgment); literal compound."]],"philippi-s85-014":["The particular rule of the first figure is: *maior sit vniuersalis, minor affirmans*. This is the rule of all syllogisms.",["'besondere' = particular; 'Vernunftschlüsse' = syllogisms."]],"philippi-s85-015":["The *ratiocinia* are either",[]],"philippi-s85-016":["1. *ordinaria*, where there are 3 *termini*, where I compare subject and predicate with the middle concept.",["'terinini' read as OCR slip for 'termini'; 'Mittelbegriff' = middle concept."]],"philippi-s85-017":["2. *extraordinaria*, where in the *propositionibus* not 2 concepts but 2 judgments are compared. E.g. If the world is necessary, then all substances are uncreated. There is thus in a *ratiocinio extraordinario* the *maior propositio* an *iudicium extraordinarium*. Hence there are two kinds of *ratiocinia extraordinaria*",["'Praepositionibus' printed thus (for *propositionibus*?); left as printed, doubtful. 'ratiocmio' read as 'ratiocinio'.","'Welt' = world; 'nothwendig' = necessary; 'zweierley' = two kinds."]],"philippi-s85-018":["1. *hypothetica*, where the *maior* is a hypothetical judgment, that is in which 2 judgments are considered in the relation of connection.",["'Verknüpfung' = connection (connexio); 'Verhältniß' = relation."]],"philippi-s85-019":["The relation is here considered problematically, not dogmatically.",[]],"philippi-s85-020":["*Ratiocinia hypothetica* have no *medium terminus*. The *minor* occurs in both premises.",["'kernen' read as OCR slip for 'keinen'; 'Prämissen' = premises. 'medium terminus' is as printed (accusative form irregular)."]],"philippi-s85-021":["The hypothetical syllogisms are",["Vernunfftschlüsse = syllogisms."]],"philippi-s85-022":["1. affirming and then one must infer *a positione antecedentis* as the ground *ad positionem consequentis* as the sequel.",["'Grund' = ground; 'Folge' = sequel; 'geschlossen' = inferred (Schluß = inference).","'affirmirend' is rendered 'affirming'; the Latinate 'bejahen' = affirm is a different German word."]],"philippi-s85-023":["2. negating; and then one must infer *a remotione consequentis ad remotionem antecedentis*.",["'negirend' = negating (Latinate), distinct from 'verneinen' elsewhere, which is rendered 'deny'/'negative'."]],"philippi-s85-024":["It does not work to infer from the positing of the sequel to the positing of the ground; for the sequel can also have flowed from another ground and not solely and alone from the first.",["Sezzung = positing (setzen = posit); Folge = sequel; Grund = ground; schlüßen = infer (Schluß = inference).","Spelling Sezzung, schlüßen, Schlüssen etc. left as in the Akademie text and normalized in English."]],"philippi-s85-025":["But from the lack of the sequel I can infer to the lack of the ground; but not from the lack of a certain ground to the lack of the sequel; for precisely the same sequel can be true from another ground.",["Schlüssen (capitalized) read as the infinitive schlüssen, 'infer'.","Mangel = lack."]],"philippi-s86-001":["§395",[]],"philippi-s86-002":["A disjunctive inference of reason is where the *maior propositio* is a disjunctive judgment.",["Vernunfftschluß = inference of reason (glossary also allows 'syllogism'); Urtheil = judgment.","Latin *maior propositio* left as in the text."]],"philippi-s86-003":["Such a *ratiocinium* always enunciates a logical division, and many judgments are weighed in the relation of conflict, and indeed it is not one member of the *divisio* that is opposed contradictorily to another but to all others.",["Eintheilung = division; Diuision = *divisio* (Latinized form, italicized).","Widerstreit = conflict; erwogen = weighed (the English 'it is ... that' is added only to carry the German word order's negation; the German is elliptical)."]],"philippi-s86-004":["The *conceptus divisus* is the subject; all *membra dividentia* make up the predicate.",["Diuisus/diuidentia: u read as v."]],"philippi-s87-001":["§ 396",[]],"philippi-s87-002":["The modes of disjunctive inferences are, like those of the conditioned, twofold: 1. *modus ponens*, of which the rule is this: If all members of the division are false except one; then the one is true.",["bedingten = 'the conditioned' (bedingt); the noun is elided in the German (presumably Schlüsse)."]],"philippi-s87-003":["2. *modus tollens*, if the one member is true; then all the remaining ones are false.",[]],"philippi-s87-004":["In disjunctive inferences one has, if one wants to test their correctness, to see:",[]],"philippi-s87-005":["1. to the truth of the *minoris propositionis*.",["Latin genitive kept as in the text."]],"philippi-s87-006":["2. to the correct logical division; whether some members are not lacking and whether they are all opposed to one another contradictorily.",[]],"philippi-s87-007":["In hypothetical ones one has to see to",[]],"philippi-s87-008":["1. the truth of the *antecedens* or *consequens*",[]],"philippi-s87-009":["2. to the correct consequence.",["Folgerung = consequence."]],"philippi-s87-010":["If the *minor propositio* is a hypothetical proposition, whose *consequens* is a disjunctive judgment; then such a judgment is called *Dilemma* (*Syllogismus cornutus*) because it drives very much into straits. The *Dilemma* is proved according to the rules of the *syllogismus hypotheticus*; ordinarily *modus tollens* always holds, in that I negate through all *membra*.",["Saz = proposition (Satz); erwiesen = proved (Beweis = proof).","'in die Enge treibt' rendered literally ('drives ... into straits'); the German has no stated object.","negire = negate (negieren)."]],"philippi-s88-001":["§399",[]],"philippi-s88-002":["*Ratiocinium crypticum* and *formale* must be distinguished from one another. Both are *ratiocinia ordinaria*. *Ratiocinia cryptica* are actual ordinary inferences of reason where one, however, avails oneself of brevity and leaves out what is known. To it belongs",["Passage breaks off with the list continuing in the next items.","Wirklich = actual (Wirklichkeit = actuality)."]],"philippi-s88-003":["1. *enthymema*, where I leave out one of the premises. All human beings can err, therefore also the Pope.",["OCR 'Mensehen' corrected to Menschen. Praemissen = premises."]],"philippi-s88-004":["2. *ratiocinia contracta*, where I infer *per medios terminos*. All bodies are alterable because they are composed.",["OCR '«eil' read as 'weil'. veränderlich = alterable; zusammengesetzt = composed."]],"philippi-s88-005":["Although all inferences of reason consist in one's inferring from the general to the particular; yet one also infers in cognitions of experience (although admittedly not with all strictness) from the particular to the general. This kind of inference is called *Inductio*.",["OCR 'Vernunf f tschlüsse' corrected. Erfahrungserkenntnissen = cognitions of experience (Erfahrung, Erkenntnis); besondre = particular; Art = kind."]],"philippi-s88-006":["Analogy is the ground in this. This consists in philosophical relation. If I have an effect and know its cause and thereupon see a similar effect elsewhere; then I infer that there too a similar cause is. Thus I also infer from similar causes to similar effects. Further, if I see that two things agree in most pieces; then I judge that they agree entirely. Meanwhile such inferences from the particular to the general very often fail; because one indeed does not know all individual particular pieces; only reason can infer generally correctly, e.g.: If I wanted to infer that all electricity is lost in water, because every body that I know and have tried loses it, that would be false, for the electric eel retains the electricity in the water.",["'in den mehresten Stücken' rendered 'in most pieces' (Stück not in glossary).","Zitteral = Zitteraal, electric eel. 'fehlen' = fail (literally 'miss'/'go wrong'); 'Ursache' = cause; 'Verhältniß' = relation.","'Schlüsse' (1st person, after 'so') is the verb 'schließe'; urtheile = judge (v.)."]],"philippi-s88-007":["Travelers often make such incorrect inductions. If, e.g., they have been badly received in 2 or 3 inns, they believe all inns are so; indeed they believe that the whole nation's character is not the best.",["'der ganzen Nation ihr Charakter' is a colloquial possessive construction, rendered as 'the whole nation's character'."]],"philippi-s88-008":["One infers from the particular to the general only ever with conjecture.",["Vermuthung = conjecture (alternatively 'presumption')."]],"philippi-s88-009":["Analogy, which stands in the equality of two philosophical relations, is very conform to the mathematical relations. In physics and *theologia naturalis*, with the properties of the Godhead, one infers the most by analogy.",["Philosophisch/Philosophie = philosophical/philosophy; 'Eigenschaften' = properties (not Beschaffenheit); 'conform' kept as in the German."]],"philippi-s88-010":["Theoretical logic indicates the conditions of the perfection of cognition.",["OCR 'Erkennt niß' joined. Logic = Logic (Logik); Vollkommenheit = perfection."]],"philippi-s88-011":["If an argument contains too little in itself, it nevertheless proves a part of the matter, and only the *complementum* to sufficiency is lacking to it.",["Sache = matter (distinct from Materie, also 'matter' in the glossary); Sufficienz = sufficiency (cf. zureichend = sufficient).","Complementum kept in Latin."]],"philippi-s88-012":["What is more than true is false. If a ground of proof proves more than is true, then it is false, e.g.: we do not have the right to take the life of a being, since we have not given it.",["Beweisgrund = ground of proof; Wesen = being; 'es' (given) refers to the life/being as in the German."]],"philippi-s89-001":["§402",[]],"philippi-s89-002":["An inference of reason that is false in form is called paralogism. If the fault is hidden, it is called sophism. The sophisms of the ancient dialecticians are proper monuments of ancient folly.",["Paralogismus and Sophisma/Sophismata rendered by English cognates; Latinate forms in the German text."]],"philippi-s90-001":["§ 404",[]],"philippi-s90-002":["*Fallacia accidentis* is a true *conclusio* *a dicto secundum quid ad dictum secundum quid* and not, as the author says, *ad dictum simpliciter*, e.g.: Whoever says that the respondent is a human being speaks the truth; *atqui*, whoever says the respondent is a Moor says that he is a human being: *ergo*.",["'the author' presumably Meier, the textbook author; not explained further in the translation.","Respondens kept as *respondent*; Mohr = Moor."]],"philippi-s90-003":["*Fallacia medii* is when the *terminus medius* is taken now without, now with determination.",["Bestimmung = determination."]],"philippi-s91-001":["§406",[]],"philippi-s91-002":["The *ratiocinia composita* consist in this, that several inferences of reason are not coordinated but subordinated.",["OCR 'subordhiirt' read as subordinirt."]],"philippi-s91-003":["*Ratiocinatio polysyllogistica* consists in this, that the conclusion of the one syllogism becomes a premise of the other. The syllogism that is subordinated is called: *episyllogismus*; the one to which one is subordinated is called *prosyllogismus*. In disputing it is inferred first episyllogistically and then prosyllogistically.",["Syllogismus = syllogism; Vernunfft-Schlüsse = inferences of reason; geschlossen = inferred (schließen = infer).","'der dem einer untergeordnet wird' is awkward in the German; rendered as closely as possible."]],"philippi-s92-001":["§408",[]],"philippi-s92-002":["*Sorites* is a heaped-up inference of reason (*ratio polysyllogistica ratiociniorum contractorum*)",[]],"philippi-s92-003":["§ 409",[]],"philippi-s92-004":["If the syllogisms chained together are hypothetical, then the *sorites* will be *hypotheticus*.",["zusammengekettete = chained together."]],"philippi-s93-001":["§411",[]],"philippi-s93-002":["*Petere principium* means to lay down as unprovable, as ground, something that yet must first be proved. Thus the Crusian proposition: everything is somewhere and sometime is *petitio principii*.",["'Crusianische' = of Crusius. Saz = proposition. The syntax of 'als unerweißlich zum Grunde legen' is kept close to the German; 'unerweißlich' = unprovable.","irgendwenn = at some time (rendered 'sometime')."]],"philippi-s93-003":["*Circulus in probando* is when one adduces the sequel as ground. These are frequent in extensive systems, when one makes lemmata.",["OCR 'ist. da' read as 'ist, da' = 'is when/where'. Folge = sequel; Grund = ground."]],"philippi-s93-004":["A hypothesis. *Postulatio principii* is the proposition: The whole is greater than a part of it.",["'Eine Hypothese.' stands as a fragment in the source, possibly belonging to the preceding sentence; left as is.","Satz = proposition; Theil = part."]],"philippi-s94-001":["The other main part. On the way of teaching of learned cognition.",["Lehrart = way of teaching (Art = way); gelehrten = learned; Erkenntniß = cognition."]],"philippi-s94-002":["Method is the way in which many thoughts and cognitions are to be combined. It signifies the form of a logically perfect whole. This logical perfection is twofold:",["verbunden = combined (Verbindung = combination); bedeutet = signifies; Vollkommenheit = perfection."]],"philippi-s94-003":["α. according to rules of sound understanding and",["OCR 'oc.' read as α and 'ß.' as β (enumerating signs). Gesunden Verstandes = sound understanding; Verstand = understanding."]],"philippi-s94-004":["β. according to rules of science.",["Wissenschafft = science."]],"philippi-s94-005":["All method has regard to",["German 'respicirt' (from Latin respicere); rendered 'has regard to'."]],"philippi-s94-006":["1. the object that is to be cognized.",["Object (Latinate) = object; Gegenstand in the next items is also 'object'. 'aufs Object' is rendered 'the object' for English idiom after 'regard to'."]],"philippi-s94-007":["2. the nature and the capacity for cognition through which the object is to be cognized.",["OCR 'Erkenn tnißfähigkeit' joined; Fähigkeit = capacity; Gegenstand = object."]],"philippi-s94-008":["3. the particular constitution of the subject who is to have insight into, grasp, and understand the object.",["Beschaffenheit = constitution; einsehen = have insight into (literally 'see into'); fassen = grasp; verstehen = understand. 'Subjekt' = subject."]],"philippi-s95-001":["I.",[]],"philippi-s95-002":["Philosophy has as object the constitution of things; mathematics, however, the quantity. This makes the method very different. If the object is an object of cognition of reason, then the method becomes rational; if it is an object of sensible cognition: then it will have to be treated historically.",["Gegenstande and Objekt both 'object'. Quantitaet = quantity. Vernunffterkenntniß = cognition of reason; sinnlich = sensible. 'er' in 'er historisch abzuhandeln' refers to Gegenstand (rendered 'it')."]],"philippi-s96-001":["II.",[]],"philippi-s96-002":["The method must be different according to the circumstances of the subjects. For children the way of teaching is different than for adults; for women different than for men; also the different capacities and inclinations. The method must also suit the person who is lecturing, and it must also accommodate itself to an individual subject. In this respect it is difficult in a *collegium* to arrange one's method according to each one's wish. The one is flighty and wants always to be encouraged; the other is profound and wants always to be instructed.",["Frauenzimmer = women (archaic term). OCR 'instruirtso' read as 'instruirt so'. Fähigkeiten = capacities; Lehrart = way of teaching.","'Collegio' = *collegium*; Subject/Subjekte = subject. 'auch die verschiedene Fähigkeiten und Neigungen' is an incomplete sentence in the source and is kept as such."]],"philippi-s97-001":["§421",[]],"philippi-s97-002":["Something, insofar as it is demonstrated, is called a *scientia*. All sciences are either rational or historical. The rational ones are either sciences whose form alone is rational but whose matter is empirical, e.g. theoretical physics, or sciences of the pure understanding, whose matter and form have arisen from the pure understanding; or, properly speaking, whose object is the form or the procedure of the understanding and which properly have no matter.",["Scientz (OCR/orig. spelling) read as *Scientia*; rendered in Latin italics. Wissenschaft = science throughout.","Materie = matter; Form = form; Objekt = object; Verstand = understanding; rein = pure.","'Verstandes'/'Verstände' in the German are the same word (understanding); 'Verfahren' rendered 'procedure'."]],"philippi-s97-003":["The cognitions of pure reason are",["Breaks off; continued in the next passage. Erkenntnis = cognition."]],"philippi-s97-004":["1. dogmatic in morals",["'Moral' rendered 'morals'."]],"philippi-s97-005":["2. critical in metaphysics.",[]],"philippi-s97-006":["Both sciences are related.",["'Wissen schafften' (OCR split) read as Wissenschaften."]],"philippi-s97-007":["The science of the quantity of objects, i.e. mathematics, demonstrates everything evidently, because it is grounded entirely on space and time, which are a sensible *intuitus*. Mathematics can be evident because it occupies itself only with magnitudes, i.e. how often something is posited, whatever it may be. Philosophy not so; for it endeavors to develop what is posited there.",["'euident' = evident (Latin-derived spelling kept as 'evident'). 'Intuitus' kept in Latin italics; Anschauung would be 'intuition'.","'che' in the OCR read as 'sie' (it, i.e. philosophy); 'Grössen' = magnitudes (Größe), 'Quantitaet' = quantity.","'als welche sinnlicher Intuitus sind': 'welche' plural, 'Intuitus' singular, as in the German.","'gesezt' = posited (setzen)."]],"philippi-s98-001":["§422",[]],"philippi-s98-002":["Analysis and synthesis can be considered in coordination and subordination. The analysis of coordination is where I go from the whole to the parts.",["Betrachtung/betrachten rendered 'consider'. Ganze = whole; Teil = part."]],"philippi-s98-003":["The synthesis of coordination, where I go from the parts to the whole.",[]],"philippi-s98-004":["Analysis of subordination is the derivation of the sequels from their grounds.",["Folgen = sequels (Folge); Gründe = grounds."]],"philippi-s98-005":["Synthesis of subordination is when I go from the sequels to the grounds.",["OCR 'S3'nthesis' corrected to 'Synthesis'."]],"philippi-s98-006":["Meanwhile there is in the relation of grounds and sequels and in that of the parts to the whole a great difference and a quite particular procedure of the understanding.",["OCR 'Theüe' read as 'Theile'. 'besonder' = particular."]],"philippi-s98-007":["Mathematics and all empirical sciences have the synthetic method; for all these put their science together little by little in that they always discover more.",["'sezzen ... zusammen' = put together; 'erfinden' rendered 'discover' (could also be 'invent')."]],"philippi-s98-008":["The method of philosophy, however, especially of pure philosophy, is analytic. The whole cognition is there, all concepts are given. One is only to make them distinct through skillful dissection. Then I come from the universal to the particular.",["Philosophie = philosophy (not Weltweisheit). deutlich = distinct; Zerlegung = dissection; 'allgemeinen' = universal; 'besondre' = particular."]],"philippi-s98-009":["That pure philosophy has had no progress for centuries until now, while mathematics has reached almost the highest level, comes from the fact that one has misjudged its method and has wanted to philosophize synthetically.",["Fortgang = progress. 'Stuf fe' (OCR) read as 'Stufe'; 'philosopliiren' read as 'philosophiren'. 'verkannt' rendered 'misjudged' (literally mistaken/failed to recognize)."]],"philippi-s98-010":["§",["Section number lost in the source text."]],"philippi-s98-011":["The author here defines art. But not everything is to be defined. The artificial way of teaching is contradistinguished from the natural. Just as a distinction is to be made between a reasonable cognition, i.e. one that does not contradict reason, and a rational cognition, i.e. one that arises from reason; so a cognition is also called natural in a twofold sense, namely 1. when it agrees with the nature of the object and subject and then 2. when it arises from nature.",["The German prints 'vernünfftigen' and 'vernünftigen' for both members; I have rendered the first 'reasonable' and the second 'rational' to preserve the evident contrast the sentence draws, though the source spelling does not distinguish them (OCR/original). 'Verstände' in 'zwiefachem Verstande' = sense.","'defdnirt' read as 'definirt'; 'Lehrart' = way of teaching; 'Kunst' = art; 'contradistinguirt' kept as 'contradistinguished'."]],"philippi-s98-012":["The way of teaching must be natural not so much subjectively as objectively, that is, be appropriate to the object. A natural method in general is not something so easy.",["'angemessen' = appropriate."]],"philippi-s98-013":["The artificial way of teaching is the way of teaching of the school.",[]],"philippi-s99-001":["§428",[]],"philippi-s99-002":["*Methodus tabellaris*, to sketch a whole science in tables, is very beautiful; but insight is required to make not far too many and not useless divisions. Daries has a particular talent for this method, which is excellent not so much for presentation as for repetition. The way of teaching according to the dismemberment of a whole doctrinal structure is no way of teaching. The way of putting the parts together constitutes the doctrinal structure.",["Eintheilungen = divisions (Einteilung). 'Lehrgebäude' = doctrinal structure; 'Vortrag' = presentation. 'Theüe' read as 'Theile'."]],"philippi-s100-001":["§429",[]],"philippi-s100-002":["*Affectatio* means an unnatural art. Affected means everything by which one wants to seem artificial. Thus it is affected when one shows not one's art but traces of an artificial instruction. The affected is commonly combined with pedantry. When one applies a single method to everything, whether it suits or not.",["'af feetüte' (OCR) read as 'affectirte'. 'applitirt' read as 'applicirt'. The last sentence is a fragment in the German as well (no main clause)."]],"philippi-s101-001":["§430",[]],"philippi-s101-002":["The Socratic way of teaching is the way of teaching through conversations, where by well-arranged questions one brings everything out of the pupil and leads him to answers that are entirely new to him. It has the greatest utility. We see distinctly how the cognition arises and we are put in the position of answering all questions put to us ourselves. It is a genus of catechetical method; but it is very tiring; and boring for experienced readers. Moses Mendelssohn has imitated it best. The Platonic way of teaching consisted in a free speech, where one becomes aware of a system.",["'ehie' read as 'eine'. 'Gattung' = genus; 'Nuzzen' = utility; 'Quaestionen' not present in this passage."]],"philippi-s102-001":["§432",[]],"philippi-s102-002":["In historical sciences one has two methods, the chronological and the geographical. Both are to be united. The latter seems to me to be preferable to the first, although one does not make use of it.",["'vereinbaren' = unite (reconcile). 'die leztere' = the latter; 'bedienet' = make use of."]],"philippi-s102-003":["In all coherent cognitions one must consider the whole sooner than its parts, and of the parts the large ones sooner than the small, the upper divisions sooner than the lower.",["'zusammenhängend' rendered 'coherent' (cf. Zusammenhang = interconnection). 'Ober- als Untereintheilungen' = upper/lower divisions; 'in Erwägung ziehen' = consider (lit. take into consideration)."]],"philippi-s103-001":["§434",[]],"philippi-s103-002":["Of history the author says that it belongs to no doctrine; but it is just as good a doctrine as dogmatic truths. A distinction is to be made",["'kemer' read as 'keiner'. Doctrin kept as 'doctrine' (Lehre = doctrine); sentence breaks off before the list."]],"philippi-s103-003":["a. Doctrine, an interconnection of various cognitions and teachings.",["Zusammenhang = interconnection; Lehren = teachings (Lehre elsewhere 'doctrine')."]],"philippi-s103-004":["b. Discipline, when this interconnection is brought into a method.",[]],"philippi-s103-005":["c. Science, when the cognition is worked out completely according to such a method.",["OCR 'e.' read as 'c.'."]],"philippi-s103-006":["Science is a complete discipline.",[]],"philippi-s103-007":["In doctrine I direct my attention only to what is taught; in discipline I look to the method.",[]],"philippi-s103-008":["Doctrine and discipline therefore take place in historical cognitions just as well as in dogmatic ones. In sciences there must always be doctrine.",[]],"philippi-s103-009":["Criticism is contradistinguished from doctrine. The doctrine through which I give a cognition not an addition, but only judge it, is called *Dokimastik*.",["'Critic' rendered 'Criticism'. 'beurteilen' = judge (assess); 'Dokimastik' kept as in the source."]],"philippi-s104-001":["§ 435",[]],"philippi-s104-002":["One thinks tumultuarily when one thinks without method.",["OCR 'Tumult uarisch' read as 'tumultuarisch'; rendered 'tumultuarily' (a rare adverb) to keep the Latinate word."]],"philippi-s104-003":["§ 436",[]],"philippi-s104-004":["To meditate does not mean to recall to oneself cognitions that one has had, but to bring forth new ones that one has not yet had. The author believes that one must meditate methodically, but methodical meditating hinders one from procuring sufficient materials for meditating. In the beginning one meditates tumultuarily. One must write down what occurs to one when thoughts sometimes come into one's mind that one has not had in one's life. One writes down first all thoughts, as one has them, without order. Afterwards one begins to coordinate and then to subordinate. If one wants to produce something; then one must certainly make the skeleton of the system ready in general, then divide it into chapters. Consequently 3 works must precede every working-out",["'nickt' read as 'nicht'; 'Außarbeitung' read as 'Ausarbeitung' (working-out). 'Sinn' = mind in 'in den Sinn kommen' (idiom; Sinn otherwise 'sense'). 'Materialien' = materials. Passage ends without final punctuation; the list follows."]],"philippi-s104-005":["1. All thoughts one writes down, without order.",[]],"philippi-s104-006":["2. One makes a general plan.",["'emen' read as 'einen'."]],"philippi-s104-007":["3. One works out all the parts.",[]],"philippi-s104-008":["As for meditating itself, one must indeed by nature be inclined to it; yet one makes a beginning when among the words one practices representing things as present in the imagination; when one becomes, as it were, inwardly conscious of the words, what they signify, and when one pays attention to what goes on in us. Doubting practices in reflection, but also the endeavor to see rightly into each one's opinion and to be able to put oneself into it.",["'unter den Worten' kept literal ('among the words'). Nachdenken rendered 'reflection' (Überlegung would also be 'reflection'; two German words, one English). Einbildung = imagination; bedeuten = signify; Meynung = opinion.","'Das Zweifeln übt im Nachdenken': 'übt' could be intransitive 'practices' or 'exercises (one) in'; left as in the German."]],"philippi-s104-009":["It is good if one alternates meditating with other sciences that are recreating.",["'erholend' rendered 'recreating' (refreshing)."]],"philippi-s105-001":["The third main part. On learned presentation. The first section. On the use of words.",["Gebrauch = use; Vortrag = presentation."]],"philippi-s105-002":["The constitution of words is a worthy object of the considerations of the philosopher.",["Beschaffenheit = constitution; Betrachtungen = considerations; Object = object."]],"philippi-s105-003":["The right use of words is that they designate a thought exactly.",["'bezeichnen' rendered 'designate' (not in glossary; bedeuten = signify)."]],"philippi-s105-004":["There are no synonyms that are in fact one and the same. They are all often but very finely distinguished, e.g., pride [*Stolz*] and haughtiness [*Hochmuth*]. One can say of a human being, without censuring him, that he is proud; but one censures him constantly if one says that he is haughty. The proud one is he who does not want to let himself be despised; but the haughty one is he who despises others in this way. Self-love [*Eigenliebe*] and self-esteem [*Selbsthebe*] are also different. Self-love is when one wishes oneself well. But self-esteem, when one loves oneself more than others.",["*Selbsthebe* is as printed (OCR); probably a misreading of *Selbstliebe* or similar. I translate the word as it stands and give 'self-esteem' only as a placeholder; doubtful reading.","*sej'* read as *sey* (OCR). *so andre* rendered 'in this way' for *so*; the construction (*der so andre verachtet*) is left as in the German.","*Hochmuth* and *hochmüthig* both 'haughtiness'/'haughty'; *Stolz*/*stolz* 'pride'/'proud'."]],"philippi-s105-005":["Whoever, therefore, wants to express himself synonymously can never express himself logically perfectly; for a word always signifies something other than another.",["*bedeutet* = signifies; *vollkommen* = perfectly (*Vollkommenheit* = perfection)."]],"philippi-s105-006":["An expression receives different names. If it is known among all, then it is called *vulgaris*. But if it is known only among the learned, then it is called *receptus*, i.e., *ab eruditis*. If an expression is in use only among persons of a certain art, then it is called *terminus technicus*. A *terminus technicus* is only an emergency aid where the *usus communis* does not suffice. They are often used for ostentation. But one must use them only in the art, not at all outside it.",["OCR 'Wemi' read as 'Wenn'; 'reeeptus' read as *receptus*; 'vsus' read as *usus*; 'sc.' rendered 'i.e.' (properly *scilicet*, 'namely').","*Ausdruck* = expression; *Kunst* = art (left unrendered as 'craft' or 'skill')."]],"philippi-s105-007":["It is a great aid for our understanding that we also have words for the most common concepts, and there reason should be at pains to seek out the right meaning that is combined with the word. All moral expressions are of this kind. If one considers the word: candor [*Offenherzigkeit*]; then one sees that it is a result of the sum of many other expressions. This word comprises in itself: that a human being conceals nothing; he conceals nothing because he is conscious of nothing evil. This must flow from *bonitas*.",["*Verstand* = understanding; *Begriff* = concept; *Vernunft* = reason; *Bedeutung* = meaning; *Summe* = sum.","OCR 'soU' read as 'soll'; 'morahsche' as 'moralische'; 'bewust' as printed. 'Bonitaet' rendered by the Latin *bonitas* in italics.","*faßt ... in sich* rendered 'comprises in itself'; not *begreifen* = comprehend."]],"philippi-s105-008":["One sees here that the mind has had to make many reflections until it was able to find a fitting word for all ideas. So in many others. The German language is beautiful in this, that it often includes in one word so many excellent ideas. One also understands the words and the ideas lying in them very easily.",["OCR 'liier' read as 'hier'; 'hegende' as 'liegende'.","*Gemüth* = mind; *Reflexionen* = reflections; *Ideen* = ideas."]],"philippi-s105-009":["It is uncommonly important that one find the right word where one uses it. In moral things, if one hits upon the right word, one can often make one's thoughts more understandable than the best definitions and circumlocutions would do.",["*Definitionen* = definitions (Latinate; *Erklärung* would also be 'definition')."]],"philippi-s106-001":["§ 449",[]],"philippi-s106-002":["A *terminus inanis* is one whose meaning we believe we understand, but in fact do not understand. E.g., the *entelechia* of the ancients and the quintessence of Theophrastus Paracelsus.",["*Bedeutung* = meaning; *verstehen* = understand. 'Theophrasti Paracelsi' is a Latin genitive, rendered as a name."]],"philippi-s106-003":["An expression is deceptive (*deceptrix*) where we believe something is possible and yet it is impossible. E.g., the greatest number, the very swiftest motion.",["'aliergeschwindeste' read as 'allergeschwindeste'. *Zahl* = number."]],"philippi-s107-001":["§450",[]],"philippi-s107-002":["A *terminus familiaris* is the expression whose sense has never been investigated, because it has never been noticed. Every language has such. I say, e.g., *süchtig*. What the word signifies no one knows how to say; but everyone understands it. Such *termini* are, on closer investigation, often also deceptive expressions.",["OCR 'famüiaris' read as *familiaris*. *Sinn* = sense; *bedeutet* = signifies. *süchtig* left in German as a mentioned word."]],"philippi-s107-003":["§ 452",[]],"philippi-s107-004":["The word-mongering of the learned is a deceptive trade, since one passes off words for things. What is more usual than that?",["*Sachen* = things (*Ding* in the glossary is a different word); *wie* in 'gewöhnlicher wie' rendered 'than'."]],"philippi-s108-001":["§454",[]],"philippi-s108-002":["An expression is called understandable which can be understood. Something can be understandable without being distinct. Already by my putting a less confused one in the place of a confused one, a more usual word in the place of an unusual word, an expression becomes understandable.",["*verständlich* = understandable; *deutlich* = distinct. *confusen* left as the Latinate 'confused'. OCR 'sezze' read as 'setze' (not the glossary's *setzen* = posit; here 'put')."]],"philippi-s109-001":["§457",[]],"philippi-s109-002":["If 2 expressions have one meaning, or where at least the meanings are not very different; then they are called synonymous expressions. If an expression has more than one meaning, then it is called *terminus vagus*, *ambiguus*, *anceps*. The French language has many of these. Ambiguities furnish a play of wit.",["*Bedeutung* = meaning; 'Wizzes' = *Witzes* (wit)."]],"philippi-s110-001":["The second section. On the learned manner of writing.",["*Schreibart* = manner of writing (*Art* = manner in the glossary)."]],"philippi-s110-002":["Style [*Styl*] properly signifies the tool of a sculptor. Style here signifies the character of expressing one's thoughts. Everyone has his own style, just as everyone writes his particular hand. Style is to be weighed",["Breaks off at 'zu erwägen' with the enumeration following in the next passages. *bedeutet* = signifies; *besondre* = particular. *Bildhauer* = sculptor (the text says 'Werkzeug')."]],"philippi-s110-003":["1. insofar as it is appropriate to the things, to the *Obiect*.",["*Sachen* = things; *Obiect* (sic, Latinate) = object."]],"philippi-s110-004":["2. to the person (*Subiect*) and his rank.",["*Stand* = rank (could also be 'station')."]],"philippi-s110-005":["3. to those for whom one writes.",[]],"philippi-s110-006":["For each a particular style is fitting, and not everyone can bring his thoughts into the form of another.",["*Form* = form."]],"philippi-s110-007":["Question. How does one find which style is appropriate to each?",["'Qu.' expanded as 'Question'."]],"philippi-s110-008":["One must read many books; the book whose manner of writing stands in greatest sympathy with our thoughts, whose style that one I must make into my own.",["OCR '( redanken' read as 'Gedanken'. The construction 'dessen Styl den muß ich zu dem meinigen machen' is kept as in the German (relative clause resumed by 'den')."]],"philippi-s110-009":["Schoolteachers greatly corrupt style, since they want to teach beautiful writing, which is yet a matter of genius. For mere imitation, however, if they demand that, no genius is needed. This does such great harm that, when one comes out of school, one needs a long time until one forgets the school style, and when the mind has then come into the natural form and when one forgets it, only then can one begin to practice in the manner of writing.",["The final clause ('und wenn man ihn vergißt, denn kann man...') is kept as in the German; 'denn' read as 'dann'. 'Genies' left as 'genius'; *Gemüth* = mind."]],"philippi-s110-010":["Style must be appropriate to the matter. If it is sublime, then it must also consist of sublime expressions; if it is low, then the style must indeed not be impure, but yet popular. The order in the presentation of our thoughts, the putting together of concepts, has great influence on liveliness. The affected style is the worst to guard against and is more harmful than simplicity. The latter merely does not please, the former disgusts.",["*Sache* = matter here (cf. *Materie* = matter in the glossary, a different word); *Begriffe* = concepts; *Einfalt* = simplicity (not *einfach*). *affectirte* left Latinate as 'affected'."]],"philippi-s110-011":["In style there must not shine forth an anxiousness in the mere form of expression. Opposed to style is the impurity of language, when words are not usual, or when the combinations of them run contrary to grammar. Style can be pure without being affected. It is not impermissible to depart from the rules of language, if the rules of reason demand it.",["OCR 'St}d' read as 'Styl'. *Verbindungen* = combinations; *rein* = pure; *Unreinigkeit* = impurity; *Vernunfftregeln* = rules of reason. *usuell* rendered 'usual'."]],"philippi-s111-001":["§473",[]],"philippi-s111-002":["The suitability of the manner of writing consists in this, that it be so constituted that it is not too great but fitting for the matter. The euphony of the manner of writing is something that belongs more among chimeras than to the true. The English say that Virgil's poems sound well, but if we heard an Englishman recite the verses, this would be intolerable to our ears. The *sonorum* consists in the measure of the syllables.",["'Wollaut' = *Wohllaut* (euphony). *beschaffen* = constituted (cf. *Beschaffenheit* = constitution). 'Sonorum' is as printed, in Latin."]],"philippi-s112-001":["§ 477",[]],"philippi-s112-002":["The congruence of the manner of writing consists in this, that the style be appropriate to the matter and especially to the end. This can be learned only through experience. Segner has written on arithmetic and geometry, but instead of making it distinct he becomes indistinct.",["OCR 'vornemhch' read as 'vornemlich'. *Congruentz* kept as 'congruence'; *Zweck* = end; *Erfahrung* = experience; *deutlich* = distinct."]],"philippi-s113-001":["The third section.",[]],"philippi-s113-002":["On a learned speech.",[]],"philippi-s114-001":["§490",[]],"philippi-s114-002":["On the *donum didacticum* and the requisites of teachers. There must be found in a teacher, in general, 1 [sic] 2 pieces. He must possess",["'dono didactico' is an ablative/dative form as printed; given here in the nominative *donum didacticum* (the German phrase 'Vom dono didactico' is kept as Latin). 'Es müssen 1 sich überhaupt 2 Stücke ... finden' is garbled in the OCR (the '1' is probably a stray mark); translated as it stands, doubtful reading."]],"philippi-s114-003":["a. a *condescendence* toward the poorer and",["*Condescendenz* kept Latinate; 'schlechtem' = lesser/poorer (*Fähigkeiten* understood from the next line; passage is cut off from it, a doubtful ellipsis)."]],"philippi-s114-004":["b. a *coadscendence* toward the higher capacities.",["'Coadscendenz' as printed. *Fähigkeiten* = capacities."]],"philippi-s114-005":["I. Every teacher must know how to put himself in the place of his listeners. I can teach another only through his own understanding; hence I have a matter to work on that already lies in the head of the other. That is called the *Condescendenz*, it presupposes that one know how to put oneself in the place of those whom one teaches. Very few teachers of religion and other teachers can do that. Besides, a teacher must see to what the child shows the most genius. It is hard to cognize to what it has the most inclination and comprehensibility. Many authors have written handbooks on the guidance of youth. Basedow is working on an elementary book on the education of youth. This is a great undertaking. It takes more art to bring the first grounds to someone than to cultivate further (*excoliren*) the capacities of one who has already learned something.",["OCR 'Fälligkeiten' read as 'Fähigkeiten' (capacities); 'sezzen' is Kant's/transcript spelling of 'setzen'.","'Leiter-Weisung' is as printed (possibly OCR-affected); rendered 'guidance'.","'Faßlichkeit' rendered 'comprehensibility'; 'Genie' = genius; 'Condescendenz' and 'excoliren' left in the transcript's own forms.","'Verstand' = understanding; 'Materie' = matter; 'Erkenntnis' = cognition (here the verb 'erkennen' = cognize); 'Gründe' = grounds."]],"philippi-s114-006":["II. The 2nd requisite (*Requisitum*) of a teacher is the *Coadscendenz*, that one ascend to the talents that are raised above the middle rank. He who knows little cannot do it. It is the best case that one choose a middle degree.",["OCR 'IL' read as 'II'; 'Requisituni' read as 'Requisitum'.","'Coadscendenz' kept in the transcript's form. 'Mittelstand' rendered 'middle rank'; 'Grad' = degree."]],"philippi-s114-007":["But how must one conduct oneself in public lectures, since one does not know the capacity of the *auditorum*. One can do nothing better than to provide at once for capable and poor minds, so that both find something for themselves, the other they may leave out. For recuperation there is a lecture of amusement. If something is to please the mind; then it must be woven into the matter itself. This happens through good examples. Erudition must be the source of the jest. The *illustrationes* and *similia* must be fitting for a learned man.",["OCR 'öff entheben' read as 'öffentlichen'; 'se}m' read as 'seyn'.","'Fähigkeit' = capacity; 'Gemüth' = mind; 'Köpfe' rendered 'minds' (literally 'heads'); 'Sache' = matter here, not 'Materie'.","'Gelehrsamkeit' rendered 'erudition'."]],"philippi-s115-001":["§493",[]],"philippi-s115-002":["Here the question is asked which method one must use in order to refute someone. A distinction is made between bringing an opinion to someone and refuting someone's opinion. In the first case the subject is empty, in the other, however, I must empty it. Before all teaching I must first have the *data* in which the other errs. Every error is to be regarded as a *Phänomenon* that is worthy of an explanation. I must first seek out how he could have come to the error. Then I must also track down the part of the truth that is in his error. For no human being tolerates that one say he has erred entirely. That is also never the case with him; and then if I deny him all sound reason, then it is indeed also absurd to want to teach or refute him. It is different: to respond to something and to answer something. One can respond to everything, but that everything has been answered, that is seldom effected in disputations. To answer means to bring to conviction. In all learned disputes the end should be to bring out the truth. It is contrary to sense that it should be brought out through dispute; it is brought out only through the unity that is perhaps mediated by the dispute. *Modus litem finiendi est amicabilis compositio.* I must show the true that is in the other's judgment and the false that is in mine: then let us see whether we understand each other well, and thereupon it will show whether the one proposition is not *partial* true.",["OCR 'Averden' read as 'werden' (not in the English); 'dannists' read as 'dann ists'.","'Das ist auch niemals an dem und dann wenn…' is syntactically doubtful (perhaps 'an dem; und dann'); rendered as it stands, with 'with him' supplied by 'an dem' only tentatively.","'auf etwas antworten' / 'etwas beantworten' = 'respond to' / 'answer'; 'Einigkeit' rendered 'unity' (not Einheit, also 'unity').","'Meynung' = opinion; 'Urtheil' = judgment; 'Ueberzeugung' = conviction; 'Zweck' = end; 'Erklärung' = explanation; 'wiedersinnig' rendered 'contrary to sense'. 'partial' left as in the text, adverb in Latin form."]],"philippi-s116-001":["§ 501",[]],"philippi-s116-002":["A dispute over words is when persons believe they are contesting different opinions and yet are at odds only in the words, and are in agreement in the matter itself. Of predestination some have believed that it is more a dispute over words than over the matter.",["'Wortstreit' / 'Sachstreit' rendered 'dispute over words' / 'dispute over the matter'; 'Meinung' = opinion."]],"philippi-s117-001":["§ 504",[]],"philippi-s117-002":["A *Argumentum ad hominem* is such an *argumentum* which is indeed not true for everyone, but yet serves to bring someone to silence. If I, e.g., fetch something from *ex concessis* and from the particular propositions that another has. These are good means to get someone off one's neck and to end the dispute, but not to find the truth.",["'Säzzen' = propositions (Satz); 'besonderen' = particular; 'Mittel' = means.","'ein solches Argumentum' kept with article as in German; 'z. E.' = 'e.g.'."]],"philippi-s118-001":["§509",[]],"philippi-s118-002":["*Retorsiones* and *reprehensiones* are in personal disputes. The *distinctiones* serve well to extract oneself from the squeeze. *Instantia* is when something is used generally, and I show that it is not general.",["OCR 'sieh' read as 'sich'.","'Distinctionen' rendered with Latin italics; 'allgemein' rendered 'generally' / 'general'."]],"philippi-s119-001":["§ 512",[]],"philippi-s119-002":["A defense that goes to the person is called *Apologie*; if it goes to the judgments of others, it is called *Refutatio*.",["OCR 'Urt heile' read as 'Urtheile'.","'Apologie' kept as in the text; 'Urtheile' = judgments."]],"philippi-s120-001":["§514",[]],"philippi-s120-002":["A formal disputation is the act where one, in the presence of learned people, gives up some propositions to be defended against all objections. Disputing could produce great use, if it were not a *certamen personarum* and if one wanted to seek the truth not through dispute but through unification. In disputing one must not let the opponent slide away from the *status controversiae*. For otherwise one gets into side matters. One can soon make the opponent confused through retorsion, argumentation, questioning that he should clarify himself, and through *distinctiones*. The opponent can be prepared for the possible questions and answers. In all disputations language is the most essential; it seems to be an *exercitium Scholasticum*. The chief aim is only to keep a dead language still alive. The ancients called the art of disputing *Dialectica eristica*, combative dialectic.",["OCR 'gerät h man' read as 'gerät man' (stray 'h'); 'wider alle Einwendungen' rendered 'against all objections'.","'Nuzzen' = use (Nutzen); 'Absicht' = aim; 'Einigung' rendered 'unification'; 'Dialecticam eristicam' is accusative in the text, kept as 'Dialecticam eristicam' would be; rendered here in nominative form for English but the German form is accusative.","'Retorsion, Argumentation, Questioniren' are German-Latin hybrids rendered as English cognates."]],"philippi-s120-003":["The fourth section.",[]],"philippi-s121-001":["On learned writings. § 518",[]],"philippi-s121-002":["All learned writings are either historical or dogmatic. A writing can be historical both according to the matter, if it is something that has actually happened or an acquaintance with the individual (*Indiuiduo*), and also according to the form. The form is called historical that is not rational: where I do not derive something through general cognition of the grounds. The instruction, e.g., of children in religion is historical according to the form; it is subjectively historical, although it is objectively dogmatic.",["'Kenntniß' rendered 'acquaintance'; 'Erkenntniß' = cognition. 'Indiuiduo' as in the transcript (for 'Individuo').","'Unterweisung' rendered 'instruction'; 'subiectiv'/'obiectiv' rendered 'subjectively'/'objectively'."]],"philippi-s121-003":["It is quite perverse to present *dogmata* historically; for they can only arise from understanding and reason, hence they must be grasped through understanding and cognized rationally.",["OCR 'undVernunfft' read as 'und Vernunfft'.","'verkehrt' rendered 'perverse'."]],"philippi-s121-004":["Historical cognitions are supposed to be grafted onto the sensible power of cognition, onto the imagination and the memory. A cognition is called historical-dogmatic according to the form when the *data* are *dogmata* but are presented historically; but according to the matter when some are historical and others, however, are *dogmata*.",["'Erkenntnißkrafft' rendered 'power of cognition' (Kraft = power in the glossary's 'force; power'); 'Imagination' kept as Latinate cognate of 'Einbildungskraft'-sense word in the text, rendered 'imagination'.","'Gedächtniß' = memory; 'der Materie nach' = 'according to the matter'."]],"philippi-s121-005":["Everything that happens there is considered in combination with space and time. If one considers what happens there, insofar as it is at different times; then that is called history: insofar as it is at the same time it belongs in the field of geography. There are different ways of geography as well as of history. One can enumerate the things of the world insofar as they happen at the same time; then that belongs to natural or physical geography. One can recount what has taken place in nature at different times; then this is called natural history. A political geography would be the science where one considers the constitution of the state, as it is at one time.",["OCR 'i>t' read as 'ist'.","'Verbindung' = combination; 'Arten' = ways (Art); 'Verfassung' rendered 'constitution'; 'Wissenschafft' = science."]],"philippi-s121-006":["Theological geography would be where one considered the religions of different peoples living at one time. Alexander Ross has written such a geography.",[]],"philippi-s121-007":["A moral geography that weighs the customs of the peoples, as they are now or were in ancient times.",["Sentence has no finite main verb in the German; kept so."]],"philippi-s121-008":["History, i.e. consideration of the occurrences that happen one after another, cannot be separated from geography; as little as this from that. For every occurrence is connected with the circumstances of the place and with other things; and I cannot properly cognize the present state if I do not know the changes that have gone before.",["The opening is garbled in the OCR ('( '<< schichte'); read as '(Ge)schichte', 'history', and the opening parenthesis is carried as it stood only in being omitted here: reading doubtful.","'Begebenheit' = occurrence; 'Zustand' = state; 'Ort' = place; 'connectirt' rendered 'connected'; 'Umstände' rendered 'circumstances'."]],"philippi-s121-009":["There is also a geography of erudition, if I show at what level the sciences now are. For geography comprehends everything that is in the world at the same time.",["'begreifft' rendered 'comprehends' (begreifen = comprehend); 'Gelehrsamkeit' = erudition; 'Wissenschafften' = sciences."]],"philippi-s121-010":["But if I show what fates (*fata*) erudition has had, then that belongs in history.",["'fata' kept in Latin as in the text; 'Historie' rendered 'history'."]],"philippi-s122-001":["§ 519",[]],"philippi-s122-002":["The distinction that the author makes in dividing history into political, church, scholarly and private history is determined by the different objects of historical information. There could also be a history of morals, which would contain the manner of common life and also immoral things, e.g., how they ate, which certainly might be very fine. The scholarly Hume has found that among the Romans the interest was already 6 per cent, and in Poland already up to 10 per cent. Hamberger has written a history of the sciences and Winckelmann a history of sculpture and the art of sculpting. Biography is the life of a single subject. All history, of whatever kind it may be, is to have the end of extending reason. It is to present the material for the use of reason.",["OCR slips silently corrected: \"Karchen-\" read as Kirchen- (church); \"Winkelmami\" read as Winkelmann (rendered Winckelmann); \"Stof\" read as Stoff; a stray quotation mark before \"wie sie gegeßen\" (gegessen) is dropped.","Kenntnis (\"historischen Kenntnisse\") = \"information\"; Erkenntnis would be \"cognition\". Zweck = \"end\"; Vernunftgebrauch = \"use of reason\"; Stoff = \"material\".","\"Interessen\" (plural in the German) is rendered \"the interest\" with singular verb to match \"was\"; \"pro Cent\" rendered \"per cent\"."]],"philippi-s122-003":["§ 520",[]],"philippi-s122-004":["Pragmatic is what history is called that is useful through general rules. This can have a relation either to speculation or to practical reason. If one merely learns the names of the regents and knows their reign, this gives little material for the use of reason, i.e., for speculation or for practical rules. In the meantime one must at the beginning already abstract from the use of reason and, on good hope, provide oneself with many items of historical information. Newton, nevertheless, merely from this already knew how to draw an inference, having found that every prince on average reigns 20 years.",["\"Regierung\" rendered \"reign\"; Schluß = \"inference\" (the glossary also allows \"conclusion\"); \"Kenntnissen\" = \"information\" as in the preceding passage.","\"Verhältniß\" = \"relation\"; \"Vernunfft gebrauch\" (printed as two words) read as Vernunftgebrauch."]],"philippi-s122-005":["Scholarly history becomes pragmatic if one merely considers scholarship in relation to human reason, looks at the growth or at the causes by which it has been held back. One has remarked that great models set the sciences back for a time, for then everything only imitates this model and no one strives to become original. So it has happened with Aristotle, Leibniz, Descartes and Newton.",["\"Aristotel\" and \"Cartes\" are the transcript's forms for Aristotle and Descartes.","\"Gelehrsamkeit\" = \"scholarship\"; \"betrachtet\" = \"considers\" (Betrachtung = consideration). \"Wachsthum\" = \"growth\"."]],"philippi-s122-006":["There are 2 methods of composing dogmatic writings, either tumultuary or methodical; the latter method brings a system into being. A system is every whole. The systematic writings distinguish themselves from the tumultuary writings by the fact that the former make up an interconnected whole. Extracts, epitomes are actual systems. In dogmatic writings the *principia* of the *dogmata* must be presented, then weighed *in concreto*, and thereupon one must scatter in something historical in order to give evidence.",["OCR \"prineipia\" corrected to *principia*; \"Dogmatum\" is the Latin genitive plural, rendered *dogmata*.","\"zusammenhangendes\" (cf. Zusammenhang = \"interconnection\") rendered \"interconnected\"; \"würkliche\" = \"actual\".","\"Evidenz\" rendered \"evidence\"; \"Epitomen\" = \"epitomes\"."]],"philippi-s123-001":["§525",[]],"philippi-s123-002":["The method by which one draws a cognition not from its own sources, but takes it from those who have drawn from the source, is called compiling. History is compiled if I have written it together not from the first sources, but from later ones. One can also compile philosophical propositions, by reading together what others have thought through reason. Yet one proves skill if one carries together the most important with prudent choice from the authors who possess great acuity, from the most thorough histories. From the compiler the *plagiarius* is distinguished, who affects that he has drawn from the first scholarly sources themselves, when nonetheless he exhibits only a product from another. This is among the scholars what a gross theft is among the civil.",["OCR \"spätem\" read as später (\"later ones\"); \"Qvelle\" = \"source\".","\"Säzze\" (Satz) = \"propositions\"; \"Bürgerlichen\" rendered \"the civil\" (literally, civil persons/citizens).","\"Plagiarius\" kept in Latin, italicized; \"Historien\" = \"histories\"; \"beweist\" = \"proves\" (Beweis = proof)."]],"philippi-s124-001":["§ 526",[]],"philippi-s124-002":["The French are especially of the character that among them the sciences grow more *extensive* than *intensive*. To the epitomes belongs much understanding in order to be able to choose the most important and most comprehensible.",["\"extensiue\"/\"intensive\" are Latinate adverbs in the original and are italicized; \"epitomis\" (Latin dative/ablative) rendered \"epitomes\".","\"faßlichste\" = \"most comprehensible\"."]],"philippi-s125-001":["The fourth main part. On the character of a scholar.",[]],"philippi-s125-002":["The character of a scholar is twofold, the character of the head and of the heart; the former goes to cognition, the latter to inclination. The character of the heart has a great combination with the character of the spirit, if scholarship is to be promoted. The constitution of the heart has a great influence on the direction of the understanding and the same is therefore also not indifferent in merely speculative sciences.",["Verbindung = \"combination\" (glossary), not \"connection\" (Verknüpfung). Geist = \"spirit\" throughout, to keep it distinct from Gemüt = \"mind\".","\"ist dieselbe daher auch ... nicht gleichgültig\": \"dieselbe\" (feminine) could refer to Beschaffenheit or to Direction; the English \"the same\" leaves this open. \"Direction\" is kept as printed, in Latin script.","Beschaffenheit = \"constitution\"; Neigung = \"inclination\"; Gelehrsamkeit = \"scholarship\"."]],"philippi-s126-001":["§ 530",[]],"philippi-s126-002":["The character of the head concerns understanding, reason and taste. The faculty of imitation is precisely opposed to the faculty of genius. Those who possess capacities of genius sometimes have not at all the spirit of imitation; which, on the contrary, is often peculiar in great degree to those who show no genius. The Russians are supposed to have the fault that genius is lacking to them and they therefore precisely cannot teach it to others, hence science always dies out among them. For philosophy genius is required, for beautiful arts likewise, but for useful sciences the spirit of imitation. Mathematics requires only imitation. Scholarship and good taste are distinguished from one another.",["OCR \"Wissenschaf f timmer\" read as \"Wissenschaft immer\"; the full stop after \"besitzen\" in \"besizzen. haben\" is read as a comma (\"besizzen, haben\" would not alter the sense).","Vermögen = \"faculty\", Fähigkeit = \"capacity\"; \"Rußen\" = \"Russians\"; \"schönen Künsten\" rendered literally \"beautiful arts\".","\"welche hingegen\": the antecedent of \"which\" (feminine/plural) is left as in the German; \"es\" in \"es ... lehren\" is rendered \"it\" (apparently Genie, not resolved)."]],"philippi-s127-001":["§533",[]],"philippi-s127-002":["Mother wit is the natural capacity of healthy understanding and healthy reason. This cannot be learned; what is afterward learned, however, is called school wit.",["Mutterwiz = \"mother wit\", Schulwiz = \"school wit\"; \"gesunden\" = \"healthy\"; Fähigkeit = \"capacity\"."]],"philippi-s127-003":["All teaching already presupposes a certain cognition or at least the capacity for it. So school wit supposes mother wit. It is more tolerable to lack school wit than to have it without mother wit; the learned is then beautiful in the human being, he himself absurd in the application. Hence comes pedantry and one becomes through school wit still more incongruous than otherwise because one has more materials. School wit is a completion of mother wit.",["OCR \"daim\" read as dann; \"Materiahen\" read as Materialien (\"materials\", not Materie = \"matter\"); \"Schulwizz\" spellings normalized.","Belehrung = \"teaching\"; Vollendung = \"completion\" (not Vollkommenheit = \"perfection\"); \"ungereimter\" = \"more incongruous\"; \"supponirt\" = \"supposes\"."]],"philippi-s127-004":["§ 540",[]],"philippi-s127-005":["To the particular scholarly exercises belongs",["Breaks off as printed: the list continues in the next passage. \"besondern\" = \"particular\"."]],"philippi-s127-006":["I. Learning. This happens through oral lecture or through reading of books. One learns in that one either brings forth new cognitions or reproduces cognitions had. Teaching properly means letting cognitions be brought forth through others. The learner is in this respect passive, but must also be active, he must have *receptivity*, i.e., capacity of understanding, and also apply it.",["\"Receptiuitaet\" is a Latinate form printed in roman type; rendered in italics. \"Verstandesfähigkeit\" = \"capacity of understanding\".","\"gehabte Erkenntnisse\" = \"cognitions had\" (literal); \"hervorbringen lassen durch andre\" could be read as \"brought forth by others\" or \"through others\" as a means; \"through\" kept.","Erkenntnis = \"cognition\"; \"in dem Stück\" = \"in this respect\"."]],"philippi-s127-007":["Oral lecture has a great advantage over the written, for the oral is not so artificial as the written. It is also more comprehensible for it is one action of the mind less than with reading. With reading I must also hear in thought.",["\"eine Handlung des Gemüths weniger\" can be read \"one action of the mind less\" or \"an action of the mind less\"; the English follows the German. Gemüt = \"mind\".","\"Vortrag\" rendered \"lecture\"; \"schrifftliche\" = \"written\"."]],"philippi-s128-001":["§541",[]],"philippi-s128-002":["II. The reading of scholarly writings.",["OCR \"IL\" read as II."]],"philippi-s128-003":["It depends first very much on the order in which one reads and on the choice of writings with which one begins. The question is: Should compendia or great works be read first? One should not begin with the compendia, which contain many thoughts in little space, but with those that contain few thoughts in a wide space. In history and mathematics one must first make the general known to oneself. One must first proceed synthetically; then one is in a position afterward to proceed also analytically. Historical books that one chooses for reading must at first concern not so much the exact occurrences but the general ones.",["\"Compendia\" kept in Latin form, italicized only where the Latin technical sense applies; here an ordinary loanword, left plain. Begebenheit = \"occurrence\".","\"Es fragt sich\" = \"The question is\"; \"sich bekannt machen\" rendered literally \"make ... known to oneself\"."]],"philippi-s128-004":["One must go according to a certain order and in the morning and evening read books of speculation, at noon historical books. One must read much and digest what has been read, so to speak, and the field must not be changed very often. Every book one must read at least twice, once quickly, where one marks for oneself the passages that need a clarification. Some books are of great importance and demand much reflection; those one must read often, e.g. Hume, Rousseau, Locke, who can be regarded as a grammar for the understanding, and Montesquieu on the spirit of the laws.",["Nachdenken (\"Nachdenken\") rendered \"reflection\", the same English as Überlegung in the glossary; the German word is Nachdenken.","\"der\" (masculine) after Locke is rendered \"who\"; \"Erläuterung\" = \"clarification\"; \"marquirt\" = \"marks\"; \"Geist der Gesezze\" = \"spirit of the laws\"."]],"philippi-s129-001":["§544",[]],"philippi-s129-002":["Too frequent repetition is just as harmful as too long a suspension.",["\"Aussezzung\" rendered \"suspension\" (literally a setting-out/setting-aside)."]],"philippi-s130-001":["§546",[]],"philippi-s130-002":["Imitation in matters of style is not advisable.",[]],"philippi-s131-001":["§548",[]],"philippi-s131-002":["Concerning the invention of new truths no rule is to be given.",[]],"philippi-s132-001":["§549",[]],"philippi-s132-002":["An *autodidactus* is one who makes himself learned through meditating and reading. This is always a great genius. An oral lecture is nevertheless always to be preferred. One can make more reflections.",["\"Avtodidaktus\" (printed in roman type) rendered *autodidactus*. \"Reflexiones\" (Latin form in the German) rendered \"reflections\" (cf. Überlegung = \"reflection\")."]],"philippi-s133-001":["§553",[]],"philippi-s133-002":["Scholarly diligence signifies the strength of the exertion that one applies to learning something. The human being is by nature sluggish, and laziness itself is the final end of his diligence. The future rest is the spur that sets human beings into activity. The magnitude and worthiness of the objects can nevertheless excite diligence very much. Scholarly diligence, if it continues, is called steady diligence. Different from this is vehement diligence, which consists in an all too strong exertion that lasts, however, only a short time. The mind suffers under vehement diligence; the representations soon die out, for it will not be compelled. The variation of objects greatly disturbs diligence. If one must be lured to something only through novelty; then that is an indication of a lukewarm and relaxed industriousness.",["'Mas' (\"Mas zu lernen\") is an OCR misreading; read as 'was', 'something'.","'Gegenstände' and 'Objekte' are both rendered 'objects' (Gegenstand / Object).","'Endzweck' rendered 'final end' (Zweck = end); 'Fleiß' = diligence, 'Emsigkeit' = industriousness.","'es' in 'denn es will nicht gezwungen werden' is kept as 'it'; the German could refer to 'Gemüth' (mind) or to the representations; the ambiguity is preserved.","'Gemüth' = mind; 'Vorstellungen' = representations; 'stöhrt' read as 'stört'.","The German semicolon after 'muß' is kept as printed."]],"philippi-s134-001":["§554",[]],"philippi-s134-002":["A student must first weigh well the end and the domain of his powers. It is bad that others prescribe the end to us and that one does not yet know it oneself when one begins to study. Never must one study with vehement diligence, but never must one cease to learn. One first knows how to judge what one has to learn when one is near the end. Hence that philosopher also complained that one first learns to live when one must leave this world. It also belongs to the character of the scholar that he be a person without title, station and civil rank, chiefly that he be a man of all stations and that his cognition be generally usable and not suit merely the form of the school. To that end he must associate with human beings. Only it is to be regretted that ordinary conversations have nothing but shallow things as their end and that in our places it is almost pedantic when one lets science flow into a conversation and has the extension of it as the end. The French and English have in this respect a great advantage over the Germans, in that they seek their dignity in scholarship. The last end of scholarship should be to give the human race the true form, to free it from prejudices, to refine morals and to elevate the powers of the soul. Then it is a benefaction for the human race; but if it is used merely for operation, it is to be regarded only as a kind of luxury.",["'Zweck' = end throughout; 'lezte Zweck' = 'last end'. 'Kräffte' = powers (Kraft is glossed 'force; power'); 'Seelenkräffte' = powers of the soul.","'Umfang' = domain (glossary).","'wemi' read as 'wenn'; 'sejm' as 'seyn'; 'istt' as 'ist'; 'Oertern' = 'Orten', rendered 'places'. Silent OCR corrections.","'Erkenntniß' = cognition; 'Wissenschafft' = science; 'Erweiterung' = 'extension' (not Ausdehnung); 'Gelehrsamkeit' = scholarship.","'Stand' (Stände) rendered 'station'; 'bürgerlichen Rang' = 'civil rank'.","'beurtheilen' = judge (assess); 'Vorurtheilen' = prejudices.","'zur Operation' is obscure and kept literally as 'for operation'.","'Man weiß erst zu beurtheilen...' and 'sey hauptsächlich daß' keep the German's loose syntax; 'seichte Sachen' = 'shallow things'."]],"philippi-s135-001":["End of the Logic.",[]]};
